window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"HE-1":{"unit":"HE-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-01-01","title":"Blood Cell Histology, Origin, and Maturation","subject":"Hematologic System (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter teaches how to recognize each blood cell on a stained smear, where those cells come from, and how each lineage matures in the marrow. It then applies that knowledge to the findings boards use most: a high reticulocyte count, a left shift, extramedullary hematopoiesis with splenomegaly, platelet granule defects, and the location of B and T cells in lymph node and spleen. If you can explain what each stain color means and what each maturation step adds or removes, most morphology and lineage questions become reasoning rather than recall.","prerequisites":["Basic cell structure: nucleus, ribosomes, mitochondria, granules, and lysosomes","Acid and base properties of dyes and of cell components (DNA, RNA, basic proteins)","Cell cycle and the difference between DNA replication and cell division","Overview of innate and adaptive immune cells and their roles","Oxygen delivery and the stimulus for erythropoietin release from the kidney"],"connections":[{"to":"Blood cells and plasma (HE-2)","how":"Normal counts and the function of each cell in this chapter are the reference against which every abnormal blood count is judged."},{"to":"Hemoglobin and hematologic biochemistry (HE-5)","how":"Heme and globin synthesis happen during the erythroblast stages described here, so defects in either change how precursors mature."},{"to":"Diseases of blood cell production (HE-9-A)","how":"Marrow failure, myelofibrosis, and ineffective erythropoiesis are disorders of the lineages and sites described in this chapter."},{"to":"Lymphoid organ histology and function (IM-2, IM-8)","how":"The B and T zones of node and spleen are where adaptive responses are organized, so zone depletion reveals the type of immunodeficiency."},{"to":"Clotting abnormalities (HE-9-C)","how":"Platelet granule contents explain adhesion, aggregation, and the bleeding pattern of storage pool disorders."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hematopoietic stem cell (HSC)","def":"A marrow cell that can both renew itself and give rise to every blood cell lineage."},{"term":"Progenitor","def":"A cell descended from the stem cell that has lost self-renewal and is committed to one or a few lineages."},{"term":"Extramedullary hematopoiesis","def":"Blood cell production outside the bone marrow, usually in the liver and spleen."},{"term":"Ineffective erythropoiesis","def":"Death of erythroid precursors inside the marrow before they mature, so marrow activity is high but red cell output is low."},{"term":"Reticulocyte","def":"A newly released, anucleate red cell that still contains residual ribosomal RNA for about one day in the circulation."},{"term":"Polychromasia","def":"A blue-gray tint of young red cells on Wright stain, caused by residual RNA mixed with hemoglobin."},{"term":"Left shift","def":"An increase of bands and earlier neutrophil precursors in the blood, reflecting accelerated marrow release."},{"term":"Leukoerythroblastic smear","def":"A blood smear with both nucleated red cells and immature granulocytes, indicating marrow infiltration, fibrosis, or extreme stress."},{"term":"Endomitosis","def":"Repeated DNA replication without cell division, which makes the megakaryocyte large and polyploid."},{"term":"Azurophilic (primary) granule","def":"A lysosome-like neutrophil granule containing myeloperoxidase that is made at the promyelocyte stage."},{"term":"Periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS)","def":"The cuff of T cells around central arterioles in the splenic white pulp."},{"term":"High endothelial venule (HEV)","def":"A postcapillary venule with cuboidal endothelium in the lymph node paracortex through which lymphocytes enter from blood."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-01-01-C01","obj":"HE-1.1","title":"Identifying blood cells on a Wright-stained smear","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Wright stain is a Romanowsky stain that combines eosin, an acidic dye, with methylene blue and its oxidized azure derivatives, which are basic dyes. Acidic dyes bind basic (positively charged) proteins and stain them orange-pink; this is called eosinophilia. Basic dyes bind acidic material such as DNA, RNA, and sulfated proteoglycans and stain it blue to purple; this is called basophilia. Every color on the smear therefore tells you about chemistry: hemoglobin-filled red cells are pink, nuclei are purple, and cytoplasm rich in ribosomes is blue."},{"type":"p","text":"Three features identify almost every cell: size compared with a red cell (about 7 to 8 µm), nuclear shape, and granule color. Use the red cell as a built-in ruler. A small lymphocyte has a nucleus roughly the size of a red cell, a monocyte is about two to two and a half red cells across, and a platelet is a fraction of one."},{"type":"table","title":"Cells of the normal peripheral smear","cols":["Cell","Size","Nucleus","Cytoplasm and granules","Share of white cells"],"rows":[["Red cell","About 7 to 8 µm","None","Pink biconcave disc; central pallor about one third of the diameter","Not a white cell"],["Segmented neutrophil","About 12 to 15 µm","3 to 5 lobes joined by thin strands","Fine pale pink to lilac granules","About 40 to 70%"],["Band","About 12 to 15 µm","Curved, horseshoe shape, no thin strands","Same as neutrophil","Up to about 5%"],["Eosinophil","About 12 to 17 µm","Usually two lobes","Large, uniform, bright orange-red granules that do not hide the nucleus","About 1 to 4%"],["Basophil","About 10 to 14 µm","Bilobed or S-shaped, often obscured","Coarse dark blue-purple granules over the nucleus","Under 1%"],["Small lymphocyte","About 7 to 10 µm","Round, dense, fills most of the cell","Thin rim of pale to sky blue cytoplasm","About 20 to 40%"],["Monocyte","About 12 to 20 µm, largest","Folded or kidney-shaped, lacy chromatin","Abundant gray-blue cytoplasm, fine granules, sometimes vacuoles","About 2 to 8%"],["Platelet","About 2 to 3 µm","None","Pale blue rim with central purple granules","Not a white cell"]],"caption":"Normal adult white count is about 4,500 to 11,000/µL and platelet count about 150,000 to 400,000/µL. Percentages are approximate and vary slightly by laboratory."},{"type":"p","text":"The granule colors follow from their contents. Eosinophil granules contain major basic protein, a strongly cationic protein in a crystalloid core, so they take up eosin. Basophil granules contain heparin and histamine; heparin is a sulfated, strongly acidic molecule, so the granules bind the basic dyes and turn dark purple. Neutrophil specific granules are close to neutral and stain only faintly, which is why the neutrophil cytoplasm looks pale lilac."},{"type":"p","text":"The most common errors involve similar pairs. Lymphocytes and monocytes are both mononuclear, but the lymphocyte is small with a round dense nucleus and a thin rim of cytoplasm, while the monocyte is large with a folded nucleus and gray cytoplasm. A band has a curved but continuous nucleus without the thin strands that join segments, while the earlier metamyelocyte has only a shallow kidney-shaped indent. Large granular lymphocytes (natural killer cells and cytotoxic T cells) carry a few azurophilic granules and should not be mistaken for monocytes. Finally, a platelet lying on top of a red cell can mimic an inclusion; it sits in a different focal plane, while a true inclusion lies within the cell."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-01-01-C02","obj":"HE-1.2","title":"Hematopoiesis from stem cell to mature lineages","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"All blood cells descend from the hematopoietic stem cell, a rare marrow cell identified in humans by the surface marker CD34. Its defining properties are self-renewal, so the pool lasts for life, and multipotency, so it can produce every lineage. Most stem cells are quiescent at any moment. When one divides, its daughters either remain stem cells or begin to differentiate into progenitors, which proliferate rapidly but have lost self-renewal and become committed to fewer and fewer lineages."},{"type":"p","text":"The first major branch point produces two progenitors. The common myeloid progenitor gives rise to a megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitor (red cells and platelets) and a granulocyte-monocyte progenitor (neutrophils and monocytes), and also to eosinophils, basophils, and mast cells. The common lymphoid progenitor gives rise to B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells. Monocytes and mast cells finish maturing in tissues, and T cell precursors leave the marrow to complete development in the thymus."},{"type":"table","title":"Lineages and the signals that drive them","cols":["Progenitor","Mature cells","Key growth factor and its source"],"rows":[["Megakaryocyte-erythroid","Red cells","Erythropoietin, from peritubular interstitial cells of the renal cortex"],["Megakaryocyte-erythroid","Megakaryocytes, then platelets","Thrombopoietin, made mainly by the liver"],["Granulocyte-monocyte","Neutrophils","G-CSF (granulocyte colony-stimulating factor)"],["Granulocyte-monocyte","Monocytes, then macrophages","M-CSF (macrophage colony-stimulating factor)"],["Common myeloid","Eosinophils","IL-5"],["Common myeloid","Basophils and mast cells","IL-3; stem cell factor for mast cells"],["Common lymphoid","B, T, and NK cells","IL-7 (lymphoid precursors, especially T cells), IL-15 (NK cells)"]],"caption":"Early-acting factors such as stem cell factor, IL-3, and GM-CSF support several lineages; late-acting factors such as erythropoietin, thrombopoietin, and G-CSF act mainly on one."},{"type":"p","text":"Commitment is controlled from inside the cell by lineage-specific transcription factors, which switch on one lineage program and suppress the alternatives. GATA-1, for example, drives erythroid and megakaryocyte genes, while PU.1 drives myeloid and lymphoid genes. Once a cell expresses the receptor for a late-acting growth factor, that factor controls its survival, proliferation, and maturation, so circulating hormones adjust output of each lineage separately according to demand."},{"type":"p","text":"The level at which a disease starts determines which cells it affects. Injury to the stem cell itself, as in aplastic anemia, lowers all three myeloid outputs and causes pancytopenia. A mutation in a myeloid stem or early progenitor cell, as in the myeloproliferative neoplasms, can raise red cells, granulocytes, and platelets together. In chronic myeloid leukemia the BCR-ABL fusion arises in a pluripotent stem cell, which is why the Philadelphia chromosome can be found in several lineages and why blast crisis may be myeloid or lymphoid. Loss of one lineage-specific signal, such as low erythropoietin in chronic kidney disease, affects only that lineage."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-01-01-C03","obj":"HE-1.3","title":"Sites of hematopoiesis and extramedullary hematopoiesis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hematopoiesis needs a niche: stromal cells, extracellular matrix, and local growth factors that hold stem cells and let them divide. The location of that niche changes during development. Blood islands in the extraembryonic mesoderm of the yolk sac produce the first, mostly erythroid, cells from about week 3. Definitive stem cells, which arise within the embryo itself, then colonize the liver, which becomes the main site of blood formation from about the second month, with a smaller contribution from the spleen. As bones develop marrow cavities, stem cells settle there, and the marrow becomes the dominant site in the last trimester and the only normal site after birth."},{"type":"table","title":"Approximate timeline of hematopoietic sites","cols":["Site","Approximate period","Notes"],"rows":[["Yolk sac blood islands","About week 3 to week 8","Primitive, mainly nucleated erythroid cells with embryonic hemoglobins"],["Liver","About week 6 to birth; dominant mid-gestation","Main fetal site; makes fetal hemoglobin (HbF)"],["Spleen","Minor contribution in mid-gestation","Smaller role than the liver"],["Bone marrow","Begins mid-gestation; dominant from the last trimester","At birth nearly all marrow is red (active)"],["Adult red marrow","Lifelong","Vertebrae, sternum, ribs, pelvis, skull, proximal femur and humerus"]],"caption":"Sites overlap in time; textbooks give slightly different week ranges. In childhood, active marrow in the distal limb bones is gradually replaced by fatty yellow marrow."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver and spleen keep the ability to support blood formation after birth. When the marrow is replaced or cannot meet demand, circulating stem cells settle there again and produce blood cells, a process called extramedullary hematopoiesis. The result is hepatosplenomegaly, sometimes with paraspinal masses of hematopoietic tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"In primary myelofibrosis, a clonal myeloproliferative neoplasm, abnormal megakaryocytes release fibrogenic factors such as PDGF and TGF-beta that stimulate non-clonal fibroblasts to lay down collagen. The scarred marrow cannot hold hematopoiesis, so it moves to the spleen, which may become massive. Red cells forced out through distorted marrow and released from extramedullary sites appear as teardrop cells (dacrocytes), and the poorly regulated release puts nucleated red cells and immature granulocytes into the blood, a leukoerythroblastic smear."},{"type":"p","text":"Beta thalassemia major shows the other route to the same outcome. The marrow is not scarred; it is overworked but ineffective, because precursors die before they mature. Symptoms appear at about 6 to 9 months of age, when the switch from gamma to beta globin removes the protection of HbF."},{"type":"flow","title":"Beta thalassemia major: from globin imbalance to splenomegaly","steps":["Beta globin synthesis is absent or severely reduced","Unpaired alpha chains precipitate inside erythroid precursors","Precursors die in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis)","Anemia and tissue hypoxia raise erythropoietin","Marrow expands: crew-cut skull, frontal bossing, maxillary overgrowth","Liver and spleen resume hematopoiesis","Spleen also enlarges from clearing damaged red cells"],"caption":"Splenomegaly in beta thalassemia major has two causes: extramedullary hematopoiesis and work hypertrophy from removing abnormal red cells."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with myelofibrosis have a leukoerythroblastic smear, while a patient with iron deficiency anemia of the same severity does not?","explain":"In myelofibrosis, hematopoiesis takes place in fibrotic marrow and in the spleen and liver, which lack the normal marrow-blood barrier and release controls, so nucleated red cells and immature granulocytes escape into the blood. In iron deficiency the marrow architecture is normal and only hemoglobin synthesis is limited, so release stays orderly.","check":"The myelofibrosis paragraph and the definition of extramedullary hematopoiesis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-01-01-C04","obj":"HE-1.4","title":"Erythroid maturation and the reticulocyte count","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Erythroid maturation has one goal: to fill a small, flexible cell with hemoglobin and then remove everything else. It takes about 5 to 7 days in the marrow. The proerythroblast is large, with open chromatin, nucleoli, and deep blue cytoplasm full of ribosomes ready to make globin. With each division the cell gets smaller, the chromatin condenses, and hemoglobin accumulates while ribosomes decline, so the cytoplasm passes from blue through gray to pink."},{"type":"table","title":"Erythroid maturation stages","cols":["Stage","Nucleus","Cytoplasm on Wright stain","Key event"],"rows":[["Proerythroblast","Large, fine chromatin, nucleoli","Deep blue","Committed; begins globin synthesis"],["Basophilic erythroblast","Chromatin coarsening","Intensely blue (most ribosomes)","Active protein synthesis; dividing"],["Polychromatophilic erythroblast","Smaller, condensed","Gray-blue (hemoglobin plus RNA)","Last stage capable of division"],["Orthochromatic erythroblast","Small, dense (pyknotic)","Mostly pink","Nucleus is extruded and engulfed by a marrow macrophage"],["Reticulocyte","None","Faint blue-gray; slightly larger than a mature cell","Keeps ribosomal RNA and some mitochondria; about 1 to 2 days in marrow, about 1 day in blood"],["Erythrocyte","None","Pink with central pallor","No organelles; glycolysis only; lifespan about 120 days"]],"caption":"Hemoglobin accumulates while RNA and nucleus are lost, so cytoplasm turns from blue to pink."},{"type":"p","text":"Developing erythroblasts cluster around a central macrophage (an erythroblastic island) that supplies iron and engulfs the extruded nuclei. Because the reticulocyte still has ribosomes and mitochondria, it continues to make a little hemoglobin; the mature cell has neither, so it cannot make protein and depends on anaerobic glycolysis for ATP. The spleen removes any residual nuclear fragments, which is why Howell-Jolly bodies appear after splenectomy."},{"type":"p","text":"On Wright stain, reticulocytes appear as slightly large, blue-gray cells (polychromasia). A supravital stain such as new methylene blue, applied to living cells, precipitates the RNA into a dark network, which gives the reticulocyte its name and allows it to be counted. Because each reticulocyte stays in the blood about one day, the count is a direct readout of how many red cells the marrow released recently. The normal reticulocyte count is about 0.5 to 1.5% of red cells."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Erythropoietin feedback loop","steps":["Low oxygen delivery to the kidney (anemia, hypoxemia)","HIF stabilizes in renal peritubular interstitial cells","Erythropoietin secretion rises","Erythroid progenitors survive, divide, and mature faster","Reticulocyte count rises after about 3 to 5 days","Hemoglobin and oxygen delivery recover, lowering erythropoietin"],"caption":"Erythropoietin acts mainly by preventing apoptosis of committed erythroid progenitors, so output rises within days."},{"type":"p","text":"With anemia, a high reticulocyte count means the marrow is healthy and answering the loss, which points to hemolysis or bleeding. A low count with anemia means underproduction: iron, vitamin B12, or folate deficiency, marrow failure or infiltration, or low erythropoietin in kidney disease. The raw percentage must be corrected because anemia lowers the number of mature cells in the denominator, and because erythropoietin releases reticulocytes early, so each one stays in the blood and is counted for longer than one day."},{"type":"example","title":"Correcting the reticulocyte count","text":"A patient has a hematocrit of 25% and a reticulocyte count of 9%. First, correct for anemia: 9% x (25 / 45) = 5%. Second, correct for early release using the maturation time in blood, about 1 day at a hematocrit of 45%, 1.5 at 35%, 2 at 25%, and 2.5 at 15%. At 25% the factor is 2, so the reticulocyte production index (RPI) is 5 / 2 = 2.5. An RPI above about 2 indicates an adequate marrow response, consistent with hemolysis or blood loss. An RPI below about 2 points to underproduction, even when the raw percentage looks high."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-01-01-C05","obj":"HE-1.5","title":"Granulocyte maturation and the left shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neutrophil maturation takes about two weeks in the marrow and proceeds through six recognizable stages. Two processes define it. First, granules are made in a fixed order, so the granules present date the cell. Second, the nucleus condenses and then indents and segments, so nuclear shape tracks maturity. The cell can divide only through the myelocyte stage; from the metamyelocyte on it only matures, which creates a large marrow storage pool of nondividing metamyelocytes, bands, and segmented neutrophils ready for release."},{"type":"table","title":"Neutrophil maturation stages","cols":["Stage","Nucleus","Granules","Divides?"],"rows":[["Myeloblast","Round, fine chromatin, nucleoli","Few or none","Yes"],["Promyelocyte","Round or oval, nucleoli","Azurophilic (primary) granules made: myeloperoxidase, defensins, elastase","Yes"],["Myelocyte","Round, condensing, no nucleoli","Specific (secondary) granules appear: lactoferrin, lysozyme, collagenase","Yes, last dividing stage"],["Metamyelocyte","Kidney-shaped indent","Specific granules predominate","No"],["Band","Curved horseshoe, no thin strands","Mature granule complement","No"],["Segmented neutrophil","3 to 5 lobes joined by thin strands","Mature granule complement","No"]],"caption":"The promyelocyte is the largest cell of the series. Auer rods in a myeloblast are fused azurophilic granules and indicate acute myeloid leukemia, not normal maturation."},{"type":"p","text":"Blood neutrophils exist in two pools of roughly equal size: a circulating pool and a marginated pool rolling along venule walls. A white count measures only the circulating pool. Neutrophils remain in the blood only about 6 to 8 hours and then enter tissues, where they live for a day or two."},{"type":"p","text":"In acute bacterial infection, IL-1, TNF, and G-CSF accelerate release from the storage pool faster than cells can finish maturing, so bands and sometimes metamyelocytes and myelocytes appear in the blood. This is a left shift, named for the old practice of recording differentials with immature cells in the left-hand columns. It is often accompanied by toxic changes: toxic granulation (prominent azurophilic granules), Dohle bodies (blue patches of rough endoplasmic reticulum), and cytoplasmic vacuoles. Stress, epinephrine, and glucocorticoids raise the neutrophil count differently: they move marginated cells into the circulating pool and slow their exit into tissues, so the count rises within hours without a left shift."},{"type":"flow","title":"From bacterial infection to a left shift","steps":["Bacteria activate tissue macrophages","IL-1 and TNF released; G-CSF production increases","Marrow storage pool released faster","Bands and earlier forms enter the blood","Neutrophilia with left shift and toxic granulation"],"caption":"A left shift reflects accelerated release, not a different lineage."},{"type":"p","text":"Two distinctions are often tested. A very high count with a marked left shift and toxic changes in a person with infection is a leukemoid reaction; chronic myeloid leukemia instead shows a full spectrum of myeloid cells with basophilia and no toxic changes. A right shift, meaning hypersegmented neutrophils with six or more lobes, reflects impaired DNA synthesis in vitamin B12 or folate deficiency and is not a sign of infection."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a left shift from the differential","text":"White count 18,000/µL with 62% segmented neutrophils, 18% bands, 3% metamyelocytes, 12% lymphocytes, and 5% monocytes. The absolute neutrophil count includes segmented cells and bands: (0.62 + 0.18) x 18,000 = 14,400/µL, above the usual upper limit of about 7,500/µL. Bands alone are 0.18 x 18,000 = 3,240/µL, and at 18% of the count they far exceed the usual upper limit of about 5%; metamyelocytes are also present. The marrow is releasing its storage pool early, the pattern of acute bacterial infection. If the same count had less than 5% bands and no metamyelocytes, demargination from stress or glucocorticoids would be more likely."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-01-01-C06","obj":"HE-1.6","title":"Platelet formation and platelet granules","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Platelets are not cells but fragments of megakaryocytes, the largest cells in normal marrow (about 50 to 100 µm). A megakaryocyte replicates its DNA repeatedly without dividing, a process called endomitosis, and reaches a ploidy of 16N or more within a single lobulated nucleus. The extra DNA supports a very large cytoplasm. An internal demarcation membrane system divides the cytoplasm into future platelet territories, and the megakaryocyte, lying next to a marrow sinusoid, pushes long proplatelet extensions through the sinusoid wall. Blood flow then sheds the platelets, and one megakaryocyte releases thousands."},{"type":"p","text":"Thrombopoietin, made at a fairly constant rate by the liver, drives megakaryocyte growth and endomitosis. It is cleared by binding to its receptor (c-Mpl) on platelets and megakaryocytes, so when platelet mass falls, less thrombopoietin is removed, its level rises, and production increases. Platelets circulate for about 7 to 10 days, and about one third of the total platelet mass is held in the spleen, so an enlarged spleen lowers the blood platelet count."},{"type":"p","text":"Each platelet has a glycocalyx-coated membrane, a marginal bundle of microtubules beneath it that maintains the disc shape, an open canalicular system connected to the surface, a dense tubular system that stores calcium, and central granules. Because platelets lack a nucleus, they cannot make new protein in response to need; their granules are preloaded."},{"type":"table","title":"Platelet granules","cols":["Granule","Main contents","Function of contents"],"rows":[["Alpha granules (most numerous)","Von Willebrand factor, fibrinogen, fibronectin, factor V, platelet factor 4, PDGF, TGF-beta; P-selectin in the membrane","Adhesion, fibrinogen bridges for aggregation, coagulation, and wound repair"],["Dense (delta) granules","ADP, ATP, calcium, serotonin","Recruitment of more platelets (ADP), coagulation cofactor (calcium), vasoconstriction (serotonin)"],["Lysosomes","Acid hydrolases","Breakdown of engulfed material"]],"caption":"Alpha granules hold large proteins; dense granules hold small molecules and ions."},{"type":"p","text":"After a vessel is injured, platelets adhere through glycoprotein Ib binding von Willebrand factor on exposed collagen and then release their granules. Secreted ADP binds the P2Y12 receptor on nearby platelets, which activates glycoprotein IIb/IIIa so that it binds fibrinogen and links platelets together. Two contents are commonly misplaced. Serotonin is not made by platelets; they take it up from plasma, where it comes mainly from intestinal enterochromaffin cells. Thromboxane A2 is not stored in granules at all; it is synthesized on activation from arachidonic acid by cyclooxygenase 1. Because platelets cannot make new enzyme, irreversible acetylation of that cyclooxygenase by aspirin lasts for the life of the platelet."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has a normal platelet count and normal von Willebrand factor but bleeds after minor surgery; platelets aggregate poorly to collagen and show a weak second wave of aggregation. Which granule type is most likely missing, and why?","explain":"Dense granules. Without stored ADP, activated platelets cannot amplify their own signal and recruit neighbors, so the secondary wave that depends on released ADP is blunted. Missing alpha granules would instead give large gray-looking platelets on the smear.","check":"The granule table and the role of ADP in recruitment."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-01-01-C07","obj":"HE-1.7","title":"B and T cell zones in lymph node and spleen","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Secondary lymphoid organs bring together antigen, antigen-presenting cells, and rare lymphocytes that can recognize it. Each organ samples a different fluid: the lymph node filters lymph, and the spleen filters blood. Chemokines keep B and T cells in separate zones. T cells express CCR7 and are drawn to CCL19 and CCL21 in T zones, while B cells express CXCR5 and are drawn to CXCL13 made by follicular dendritic cells."},{"type":"h","text":"Lymph node"},{"type":"p","text":"Afferent lymphatics enter the convex surface and empty into the subcapsular sinus, then cortical sinuses, then medullary sinuses, and leave through the efferent lymphatic at the hilum. The outer cortex contains follicles of B cells. Primary follicles hold naive B cells on a network of follicular dendritic cells; after antigen exposure they become secondary follicles with a pale germinal center, where B cells proliferate and undergo selection, surrounded by a mantle zone. The paracortex (deep cortex) holds T cells, mostly CD4, together with dendritic cells that have carried antigen from tissues. Naive lymphocytes enter the node from blood through high endothelial venules in the paracortex. The medulla contains medullary cords, rich in plasma cells and macrophages, separated by medullary sinuses."},{"type":"flow","title":"From antigen in lymph to antibody in the medulla","steps":["Dendritic cells carry antigen through afferent lymph","Antigen presented to T cells in the paracortex","Activated helper T cells meet B cells at the follicle edge","Germinal center forms in a secondary follicle","Plasma cells settle in medullary cords and secrete antibody"],"caption":"The layout of the node follows the sequence of an antibody response."},{"type":"h","text":"Spleen"},{"type":"p","text":"Central arterioles branching from trabecular arteries are wrapped by the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath, a T cell zone. B cell follicles sit along the sheath and together they form the white pulp. The marginal zone, at the border between white and red pulp, receives blood first; its macrophages and marginal zone B cells trap blood-borne antigens and respond quickly to polysaccharide antigens of encapsulated bacteria. The red pulp consists of splenic cords (of Billroth), rich in macrophages, and venous sinusoids. In the open circulation, blood released into the cords must squeeze through narrow slits between the sinusoid endothelial cells; old, rigid, or antibody-coated red cells cannot pass and are phagocytosed, and inclusions such as Howell-Jolly bodies are removed (pitting)."},{"type":"table","title":"Lymphoid zones and what depletes them","cols":["Organ and zone","Main cells","Depleted or abnormal in"],"rows":[["Lymph node follicles and germinal centers","B cells, follicular dendritic cells","X-linked agammaglobulinemia (absent follicles); enlarged in follicular hyperplasia"],["Lymph node paracortex","T cells, dendritic cells, HEVs","DiGeorge syndrome (thymic aplasia); expanded in viral infections"],["Lymph node medullary cords and sinuses","Plasma cells, macrophages","Sinus histiocytosis in nodes draining a tumor"],["Splenic PALS","T cells","DiGeorge syndrome"],["Splenic follicles and marginal zone","B cells, marginal zone B cells, macrophages","Asplenia: weak defense against encapsulated bacteria"],["Splenic red pulp cords and sinusoids","Macrophages, red cells","Expanded in hemolytic anemias and extramedullary hematopoiesis"]],"caption":"Which zone is depleted tells you whether the defect is in B cells or T cells."},{"type":"p","text":"These zones explain clinical findings. Loss of the spleen (surgery or the autosplenectomy of sickle cell disease) removes the marginal zone and cord macrophages, so people are vulnerable to Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, and Neisseria meningitidis, and their smears show Howell-Jolly bodies and target cells."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and histology explain where blood is made and why the liver and spleen can make it again: they were fetal blood-forming organs and keep a supportive stroma. Biochemistry explains morphology. The blue of a young cell is ribosomal RNA, the pink of a mature red cell is hemoglobin, eosinophil granules are orange because major basic protein is cationic, and basophil granules are purple because heparin is sulfated. Heme and globin synthesis during the erythroblast stages connect this chapter to disorders of hemoglobin, and the dependence of DNA synthesis on vitamin B12 and folate explains hypersegmented neutrophils."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and pathology build on the same map. A left shift, a leukemoid reaction, and a leukoerythroblastic smear differ in where and why immature cells leave the marrow. Lymph node and spleen zones reveal whether a defect affects B or T cells. Morphology has limits: cell identification from a smear is a pattern judgment, reference ranges vary by laboratory and age, and a definitive lineage or clonality diagnosis needs flow cytometry, cytogenetics, or marrow biopsy."}],"summary":["Wright stain colors reflect chemistry: basic dyes stain DNA, RNA, and heparin blue to purple; eosin stains hemoglobin and major basic protein pink to orange.","Identify white cells by size relative to a red cell, nuclear shape, and granule color; lymphocytes are small with round nuclei, monocytes large with folded nuclei.","The CD34 positive stem cell self-renews and gives common myeloid (red cells, platelets, granulocytes, monocytes) and common lymphoid (B, T, NK) progenitors.","Blood forms in the yolk sac, then liver (with spleen), then bone marrow; liver and spleen resume production when marrow is fibrotic or ineffective.","Myelofibrosis gives massive splenomegaly, teardrop cells, and a leukoerythroblastic smear; beta thalassemia major gives marrow expansion and hepatosplenomegaly from ineffective erythropoiesis.","Erythroid precursors shrink, gain hemoglobin, lose RNA, and extrude the nucleus; the reticulocyte keeps RNA for about one day in blood.","A reticulocyte production index above about 2 in anemia means the marrow is responding (hemolysis or bleeding); below about 2 means underproduction.","Neutrophils gain azurophilic granules at the promyelocyte and specific granules at the myelocyte, the last dividing stage; a left shift is early release of bands and younger cells.","Megakaryocytes undergo endomitosis and shed platelets; alpha granules hold von Willebrand factor and fibrinogen, dense granules hold ADP, calcium, and serotonin.","B cells occupy node follicles and splenic follicles; T cells occupy the node paracortex and splenic PALS; plasma cells gather in medullary cords."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Blood"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Hemopoiesis"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Section on blood and blood vessel formation"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders; Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Cells and tissues of the immune system"}]},"HE-2":{"unit":"HE-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-02-01","title":"Blood Cells and Plasma","subject":"Hematology (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers what blood is made of and what each part does: the proteins and electrolytes of plasma, albumin and the Starling forces that keep fluid in the vessels, the red cell as a gas transport machine, the erythropoietin loop that sets red cell mass, the complete blood count, the jobs of each white cell, and the ABO and Rh systems. Board questions rarely ask for a value alone. They give a CBC, an edematous patient, a polycythemic patient, or a transfusion and ask you to explain the mechanism or predict the next finding, so each section is built around the reasoning behind the numbers.","prerequisites":["Body fluid compartments and the capillary wall as a semipermeable barrier","Hemoglobin structure, the oxygen dissociation curve, and the Bohr effect","Glycolysis and the pentose phosphate pathway","Overview of hematopoiesis and the myeloid and lymphoid lineages (HE-1)","Antibody classes, especially IgM and IgG, and complement activation"],"connections":[{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"The kidney senses oxygen delivery and makes erythropoietin, and nephrotic syndrome is the classic cause of edema from albumin loss."},{"to":"Respiratory physiology","how":"Red cell carbonic anhydrase and band 3 carry most CO2 to the lungs as bicarbonate, linking red cells to ventilation and acid-base balance."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal and liver physiology","how":"The liver makes albumin, fibrinogen, and most other plasma proteins, so cirrhosis lowers oncotic pressure and prolongs clotting tests."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"Leukocyte functions and the IgM and IgG antibodies of the ABO and Rh systems apply innate and adaptive immunity to the blood."},{"to":"Anemias and hemolysis (later HE units)","how":"The CBC framework built here is the entry point for every specific anemia and hemolytic disorder."}],"terms":[{"term":"Plasma","def":"The fluid portion of unclotted blood, obtained by centrifuging blood drawn into an anticoagulant, which keeps fibrinogen and all clotting factors."},{"term":"Serum","def":"The fluid left after blood has clotted, which lacks fibrinogen and the clotting factors consumed in clotting."},{"term":"Oncotic pressure","def":"The osmotic pressure exerted by plasma proteins that cannot easily cross the capillary wall, which draws fluid into the capillary."},{"term":"Starling forces","def":"The hydrostatic and oncotic pressures inside and outside a capillary whose balance sets net fluid filtration."},{"term":"Chloride shift","def":"Exchange of red cell bicarbonate for plasma chloride through band 3 as CO2 is loaded in tissues, reversed in the lungs."},{"term":"Erythropoietin (EPO)","def":"A glycoprotein hormone, made mainly by renal peritubular interstitial cells in response to hypoxia, that drives red cell production."},{"term":"Hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF)","def":"A transcription factor whose alpha subunit is degraded in normal oxygen and stabilized in hypoxia, switching on the EPO gene."},{"term":"Mean corpuscular volume (MCV)","def":"The average volume of a red cell in femtoliters, used to classify anemia as microcytic, normocytic, or macrocytic."},{"term":"Red cell distribution width (RDW)","def":"A measure of variation in red cell size; a high value means the cells are of mixed sizes."},{"term":"Reticulocyte","def":"A young red cell, released from marrow about one day before full maturity, that still contains residual ribosomal RNA."},{"term":"Left shift","def":"An increase in immature neutrophils (bands and sometimes metamyelocytes) in blood as the marrow releases cells early, typically in bacterial infection."},{"term":"Isohemagglutinins","def":"The naturally occurring anti-A and anti-B antibodies, mostly IgM, present in people who lack the corresponding antigen."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-02-01-C01","obj":"HE-2.1","title":"Composition of plasma and the difference between plasma and serum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blood is about 7% of body weight, roughly 5 L in an adult, and when it is centrifuged it separates into red cells at the bottom (the hematocrit, about 40 to 45%), a thin buffy coat of white cells and platelets, and plasma on top (about 55%). Plasma is the extracellular fluid of the blood. It is about 92% water, and its solutes are electrolytes, proteins, nutrients, hormones, dissolved gases, and wastes such as urea and creatinine."},{"type":"p","text":"Electrolytes make up most of the osmotically active particles. Sodium and its accompanying anions (chloride and bicarbonate) account for most of the plasma osmolality of about 275 to 295 mOsm/kg, so plasma sodium is the main determinant of water movement between plasma and cells. Plasma proteins contribute only about 1 mOsm/kg, yet because they stay in the vessel they generate the oncotic pressure that holds fluid in the circulation (C02)."},{"type":"p","text":"Total plasma protein is about 6 to 8 g/dL. Albumin, about 3.5 to 5 g/dL, is the most abundant. The globulins are a mixed group separated by electrophoresis: alpha-1 (for example alpha-1 antitrypsin), alpha-2 (haptoglobin, ceruloplasmin, alpha-2 macroglobulin), beta (transferrin, complement C3, LDL with its apolipoprotein B), and gamma (immunoglobulins). Fibrinogen, about 200 to 400 mg/dL, is the soluble precursor of fibrin. The liver makes albumin, fibrinogen, and most globulins; immunoglobulins are the main exception, coming from plasma cells. This is why liver failure lowers albumin and clotting factors while immunoglobulins are often high."},{"type":"table","title":"Main components of plasma","cols":["Component","Approximate adult value","Main role"],"rows":[["Water","About 92% of plasma","Solvent; carries heat and solutes"],["Sodium","135 to 145 mmol/L","Main determinant of plasma osmolality and ECF volume"],["Potassium","3.5 to 5.0 mmol/L","Sets resting membrane potential"],["Chloride","About 98 to 106 mmol/L","Main anion; exchanges with bicarbonate in red cells"],["Bicarbonate","22 to 28 mmol/L","Main extracellular buffer; carries most CO2"],["Calcium (total)","About 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL","About half ionized (active), about 40% protein bound"],["Albumin","3.5 to 5.0 g/dL","Oncotic pressure; transport"],["Globulins","About 2 to 3.5 g/dL","Transport, protease inhibition, complement, antibodies"],["Fibrinogen","About 200 to 400 mg/dL","Converted to fibrin in clotting; acute phase reactant"]],"caption":"Reference ranges vary slightly by laboratory. Proteins are few in number of particles but dominate oncotic pressure."},{"type":"h","text":"Plasma versus serum"},{"type":"p","text":"Plasma is obtained from blood drawn into an anticoagulant tube and centrifuged before it can clot. Citrate and EDTA work by binding calcium, which several clotting steps require, and heparin works by enhancing antithrombin. Serum is the fluid squeezed out after blood clots in a plain tube. Clotting converts fibrinogen to insoluble fibrin and largely consumes prothrombin and factors V, VIII, and XIII, so serum is plasma minus fibrinogen and those factors. Clotting also activates platelets, which release potassium and serotonin, so serum potassium runs slightly higher than plasma potassium."},{"type":"p","text":"These differences decide which sample a test uses. Clotting tests (PT and PTT) need citrated plasma, because the factors must still be present and calcium can be added back to start the reaction. Serum protein electrophoresis uses serum so that fibrinogen does not appear as an extra band near the beta and gamma region, where it could be mistaken for a monoclonal protein."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-02-01-C02","obj":"HE-2.2","title":"Albumin: oncotic pressure, transport, and edema","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Albumin is a small (about 66 kDa), highly negatively charged protein made only by the liver, with a half-life of about 3 weeks. Because it is abundant and crosses the normal capillary wall poorly, it supplies about 75 to 80% of the plasma oncotic pressure of about 25 to 28 mmHg."},{"type":"h","text":"Transport"},{"type":"p","text":"Albumin carries unconjugated bilirubin from splenic macrophages to the liver; this bilirubin is insoluble in water, so binding keeps it in plasma and out of tissues. When binding sites are saturated or a competing ligand such as a sulfonamide displaces it in a newborn, free unconjugated bilirubin can cross into the brain (kernicterus). Albumin also carries free (nonesterified) fatty acids released from adipose tissue to muscle and liver, along with many hormones and drugs."},{"type":"p","text":"About 40% of plasma calcium is protein bound, mostly to albumin; about 50% is ionized and about 10% is complexed with anions. Only ionized calcium is physiologically active and regulated by parathyroid hormone. In hypoalbuminemia, total calcium falls but ionized calcium stays normal, so there are no symptoms of hypocalcemia. Alkalosis does the reverse: fewer H+ ions compete for binding sites on albumin, more calcium binds, and ionized calcium falls even though total calcium is unchanged, which explains tingling and tetany during hyperventilation."},{"type":"h","text":"Starling forces and edema"},{"type":"p","text":"Fluid movement across a capillary follows the Starling relationship: net filtration equals Kf times [(Pc minus Pi) minus sigma (pi c minus pi i)], where Pc and Pi are capillary and interstitial hydrostatic pressures, pi c and pi i the capillary and interstitial oncotic pressures, sigma the reflection coefficient for protein, and Kf the filtration coefficient. Normally a small net outward filtration is returned by lymphatics. When albumin falls, pi c falls, the inward force weakens, and filtration rises. Lymph flow can increase several fold and interstitial protein is washed out, which buffers the change, but once filtration exceeds these safety factors, fluid accumulates as pitting edema, often generalized (anasarca) and with ascites or pleural effusions."},{"type":"flow","title":"From low albumin to edema","steps":["Albumin loss or underproduction","Plasma oncotic pressure falls","Inward oncotic force weakens; net filtration rises","Lymph flow rises; interstitial protein washes out","Safety factors exceeded","Interstitial fluid accumulates: pitting edema, effusions"],"caption":"Lymphatic and interstitial safety factors delay edema until albumin falls well below normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Albumin falls when it is lost in urine (nephrotic syndrome, proteinuria above 3.5 g/day) or through the gut (protein-losing enteropathy), or when the liver cannot make it (cirrhosis, where portal hypertension also raises splanchnic hydrostatic pressure and favors ascites) or lacks amino acids (kwashiorkor). Inflammation lowers it too, because albumin is a negative acute phase reactant."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: how much does low albumin change filtration?","text":"Use illustrative values for a capillary midpoint: Pc 25 mmHg, Pi 0 mmHg, pi c 25 mmHg, pi i 5 mmHg, sigma close to 1. Net pressure is (25 minus 0) minus (25 minus 5) = 5 mmHg outward. Now albumin falls by about half in nephrotic syndrome, and pi c drops to about 15 mmHg. Net pressure becomes (25 minus 0) minus (15 minus 5) = 15 mmHg outward, three times the original. Hydrostatic pressure never changed; the edema is purely oncotic."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has a total calcium of 7.6 mg/dL with an albumin of 2.0 g/dL and no symptoms. What would the ionized calcium most likely be?","explain":"Normal, because the drop is in albumin-bound calcium, not the regulated ionized fraction, so parathyroid hormone has nothing to correct.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on calcium binding and the effect of alkalosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-02-01-C03","obj":"HE-2.3","title":"Red cell design for oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The mature red cell is a biconcave disc about 7.5 to 8 micrometers across and about 2 micrometers thick at the rim, with a volume (MCV) of about 90 fL. It is little more than a membrane bag of hemoglobin, which makes up about a third of its contents by weight (MCHC about 34 g/dL). Each feature of its design serves gas transport, and each has a cost."},{"type":"table","title":"Red cell features and what they achieve","cols":["Feature","Functional advantage","Consequence or cost"],"rows":[["Biconcave disc","Large surface area for its volume; no point is far from the membrane, so gases diffuse quickly","Surplus membrane lets it fold through splenic slits and capillaries narrower than itself, but only while the skeleton stays flexible"],["Flexible spectrin skeleton","Deformability without rupture","Loss of membrane (spherocytes) or rigid hemoglobin (sickle cells) leads to splenic trapping"],["No nucleus or ribosomes","More space for hemoglobin","Cannot make new proteins or repair; lifespan about 120 days"],["No mitochondria","Does not consume the oxygen it carries","ATP comes only from anaerobic glycolysis; enzyme defects (pyruvate kinase) cause hemolysis"],["Glycolytic side pathways","Rapoport-Luebering shunt makes 2,3-BPG; pentose phosphate pathway makes NADPH","2,3-BPG lowers Hb oxygen affinity; NADPH keeps glutathione reduced (fails in G6PD deficiency)"],["Carbonic anhydrase and band 3","Fast conversion of CO2 to bicarbonate and its export to plasma","Most CO2 travels as plasma bicarbonate"]],"caption":"The red cell trades self-maintenance for maximal hemoglobin carriage and rapid gas exchange."},{"type":"h","text":"Carbon dioxide transport and the chloride shift"},{"type":"p","text":"Tissues produce CO2, which diffuses into the red cell. Carbonic anhydrase (mainly isoform II) speeds the reaction of CO2 with water to carbonic acid many thousand fold, and carbonic acid dissociates into H+ and bicarbonate. Without the enzyme this reaction would take far longer than the roughly one second a red cell spends in a capillary. Plasma has very little carbonic anhydrase, which is why the conversion happens inside the cell."},{"type":"p","text":"Two steps keep the reaction running forward. Deoxygenated hemoglobin is a better buffer than oxyhemoglobin and binds the H+, so releasing O2 in tissues helps the blood take up CO2 (the Haldane effect), while the H+ and CO2 lower hemoglobin's oxygen affinity and help unload O2 (the Bohr effect). Bicarbonate leaves the cell through band 3, an anion exchanger, in exchange for chloride entering, the chloride shift. Venous red cells therefore contain more chloride and, having gained osmotically active particles, more water, so they are slightly larger than arterial red cells. In the lungs every step reverses: bicarbonate re-enters in exchange for chloride, is converted back to CO2, and CO2 is exhaled."},{"type":"flow","title":"CO2 loading in a systemic capillary","steps":["Tissue CO2 diffuses into the red cell","Carbonic anhydrase: CO2 plus H2O to H2CO3","H2CO3 dissociates to H+ and HCO3-","Deoxyhemoglobin buffers H+ (Haldane effect)","Band 3 exports HCO3- in exchange for Cl- (chloride shift)","HCO3- carried in plasma to the lungs, where the steps reverse"],"caption":"About 70% of CO2 travels as bicarbonate, about 20% as carbamino compounds on hemoglobin, and about 10% dissolved."},{"type":"p","text":"The design explains several clinical findings. Hereditary spherocytosis cells have lost membrane, so they are less deformable and are destroyed in the spleen, and because the same hemoglobin sits in a smaller cell, MCHC is high. Because red cells depend wholly on glycolysis and the pentose phosphate pathway, deficiencies of pyruvate kinase or G6PD shorten their survival, a link developed in the hemolytic anemia units."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-02-01-C04","obj":"HE-2.4","title":"Erythropoietin and the oxygen-sensing loop","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Red cell mass is regulated by oxygen delivery, not by red cell number itself. About 90% of erythropoietin (EPO) comes from fibroblast-like peritubular interstitial cells in the renal cortex and outer medulla, and about 10% from the liver, which is the main source in the fetus. The kidney is well suited as the sensor: its blood flow is high relative to its metabolic need, and its oxygen use is tied to sodium reabsorption, so tissue oxygen tension there reflects the oxygen content of arterial blood rather than changes in renal blood flow."},{"type":"h","text":"Molecular mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"In normal oxygen, prolyl hydroxylase enzymes use O2 to hydroxylate hypoxia-inducible factor alpha (HIF-alpha, mainly HIF-2 alpha for EPO). The von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) protein recognizes the hydroxylated HIF-alpha and targets it for proteasomal destruction. In hypoxia, hydroxylation fails, HIF-alpha accumulates, pairs with HIF-beta in the nucleus, and turns on the EPO gene. EPO binds its receptor on late erythroid progenitors (CFU-E) and early erythroblasts, activating JAK2 and STAT5, which prevents apoptosis and lets more cells mature. EPO rises within hours of hypoxia, reticulocytes increase within about 3 to 5 days, and hemoglobin rises over weeks."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Erythropoietin feedback loop","steps":["Low O2 delivery (anemia, hypoxemia)","Renal interstitial cells: HIF-alpha stabilized","EPO secreted","Marrow: erythroid progenitors survive, mature","Red cell mass and O2 content rise","Renal O2 restored; HIF degraded, EPO falls"],"caption":"Negative feedback on oxygen delivery to the kidney. EPO responds to O2 content, so anemia and hypoxemia are equally strong signals."},{"type":"h","text":"Predicting EPO in disease"},{"type":"p","text":"The rule is that EPO tracks renal oxygen supply unless the producing cells are lost or a mutation bypasses the hormone. In chronic kidney disease, fibrosis destroys the peritubular interstitial cells, so EPO is inappropriately low for the degree of anemia and the result is a normocytic, normochromic anemia with a low reticulocyte count. At high altitude, low inspired PO2 lowers arterial saturation, EPO rises, and red cell mass increases over weeks (secondary polycythemia); EPO peaks within the first days and then falls partway back as oxygen delivery improves. In polycythemia vera, a myeloproliferative neoplasm, an acquired JAK2 V617F mutation in about 95% of cases leaves JAK2 switched on, so progenitors proliferate without EPO. The extra red cells raise oxygen delivery, and feedback suppresses EPO to low levels."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting EPO from the mechanism","cols":["Condition","Arterial O2 saturation","EPO","Red cell mass"],"rows":[["Chronic kidney disease","Normal","Low for degree of anemia","Low (anemia)"],["High altitude","Low","High","High (secondary)"],["Chronic lung disease or cyanotic heart disease","Low","High","High (secondary)"],["EPO-secreting tumor (renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma, hemangioblastoma)","Normal","High (inappropriate)","High (secondary)"],["Polycythemia vera","Normal","Low","High (primary)"],["Dehydration (relative polycythemia)","Normal","Normal","Normal; hematocrit high only because plasma volume is low"]],"caption":"Measuring saturation and EPO separates primary, secondary, and relative causes of a high hematocrit."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A smoker with a hematocrit of 56% has a normal measured arterial saturation and a high EPO. What category of polycythemia is this, and what lesion should you suspect?","explain":"Secondary polycythemia with inappropriate EPO, because saturation is normal yet EPO is high; this suggests an EPO-secreting tumor such as renal cell carcinoma. (Carboxyhemoglobin can also raise EPO while pulse oximetry reads normal; CO-oximetry separates the two.)","check":"Compare with the EPO table and the rule that EPO tracks oxygen delivery."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-02-01-C05","obj":"HE-2.5","title":"Reading the CBC to classify anemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Anemia is a reduced hemoglobin concentration, below about 13.5 g/dL in men and 12 g/dL in women. Classification uses two questions: what size and hemoglobin content are the cells (the indices), and is the marrow responding (the reticulocyte count)."},{"type":"table","title":"CBC indices","cols":["Index","How it is derived","Approximate adult range","What it tells you"],"rows":[["Hemoglobin","Measured","Men 13.5 to 17.5 g/dL; women 12 to 16 g/dL","Defines anemia"],["Hematocrit","MCV times RBC (millions/microL) divided by 10","Men 41 to 53%; women 36 to 46%","Red cell share of blood volume"],["MCV","Hct times 10 divided by RBC (millions/microL)","80 to 100 fL","Cell size: microcytic, normocytic, macrocytic"],["MCH","Hb times 10 divided by RBC","About 27 to 33 pg","Hemoglobin per cell"],["MCHC","Hb divided by Hct, times 100","32 to 36 g/dL","Hemoglobin concentration; low in iron deficiency, high in spherocytosis"],["RDW","Variation in cell size","About 11.5 to 14.5%","High when cells are of mixed sizes"],["Reticulocytes","Percent of red cells","About 0.5 to 2.5%","Marrow output"]],"caption":"Ranges vary by laboratory; a mixed population can average to a normal MCV."},{"type":"p","text":"Microcytosis (MCV below 80 fL) means hemoglobin synthesis failed, because the developing cell keeps dividing until it reaches a set hemoglobin concentration, so a shortage of iron, heme, or globin means extra divisions and smaller, paler cells. Causes include iron deficiency, thalassemia, some anemia of chronic disease, and sideroblastic anemia (including lead poisoning). Macrocytosis (MCV above 100 fL) is megaloblastic when DNA synthesis lags behind cytoplasmic growth (vitamin B12 or folate deficiency), or nonmegaloblastic with alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, or a high reticulocyte count, since reticulocytes are larger than mature cells. Normocytic anemia includes acute blood loss, hemolysis, chronic kidney disease, anemia of chronic disease, and marrow failure."},{"type":"p","text":"RDW refines the picture. It rises in iron deficiency as new small cells mix with older normal ones, and it is usually normal in thalassemia trait, where every cell is uniformly small and the red cell count is normal or high."},{"type":"h","text":"Is the marrow responding?"},{"type":"p","text":"The reticulocyte percentage overstates marrow output in anemia, because the denominator (total red cells) is low and severe anemia releases reticulocytes early, so they stay in blood longer. The corrected reticulocyte count equals the percentage times the patient's hematocrit divided by 45. The reticulocyte production index (RPI) then divides by the maturation time in blood: about 1 day at a hematocrit of 36 to 45%, 1.5 at 26 to 35%, 2 at 16 to 25%, and 2.5 at 15% or less. An RPI above about 2 to 3 indicates an appropriate marrow response, pointing to hemolysis or blood loss; an RPI below 2 indicates underproduction. Hemolysis is supported by high LDH, high unconjugated bilirubin, and low haptoglobin, each released or consumed when red cells break down."},{"type":"flow","title":"Classifying an anemia from the CBC","steps":["Confirm low hemoglobin","MCV: under 80, 80 to 100, or over 100 fL","Microcytic: check RDW and iron studies","Macrocytic: check smear for megaloblastic changes, B12, folate","Normocytic: calculate corrected reticulocytes and RPI","RPI above 2 to 3: hemolysis or blood loss","RPI below 2: underproduction"],"caption":"Size points to the failed step; the RPI separates loss or destruction from underproduction."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: interpreting a CBC","text":"A 32-year-old woman has hemoglobin 8.4 g/dL, hematocrit 28%, RBC 3.9 million/microL, RDW 17%, and reticulocytes 1.0%. MCV is 28 times 10 divided by 3.9, about 72 fL, so the anemia is microcytic. MCHC is 8.4 divided by 28, times 100, which is 30 g/dL, so the cells are hypochromic. The corrected reticulocyte count is 1.0 times 28/45, about 0.6%, and at a hematocrit of 28% (the 26 to 35% band) the maturation factor is 1.5, so the RPI is about 0.4: underproduction. A high RDW and a low red cell count favor iron deficiency over thalassemia trait. Low ferritin would confirm it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-02-01-C06","obj":"HE-2.6","title":"White cell functions and what a raised count means","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The normal white cell count is about 4,500 to 11,000 per microliter. The differential shows which cell line has changed, and each change reflects a cytokine signal: granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF) and IL-1 for neutrophils, IL-5 for eosinophils, and antigen-driven expansion for lymphocytes. Absolute counts (percentage times total white count) are more reliable than percentages, because a fall in one line raises the percentage of the others."},{"type":"table","title":"Leukocytes: structure, function, and causes of increase","cols":["Cell (share of WBC)","Key features","Main function","Raised in"],"rows":[["Neutrophil (about 40 to 70%)","3 to 5 lobed nucleus; myeloperoxidase and lysozyme granules; circulates about 6 to 8 hours","Phagocytosis and killing of bacteria and fungi via NADPH oxidase respiratory burst","Bacterial infection, tissue necrosis, glucocorticoids, catecholamines and stress"],["Lymphocyte (about 20 to 40%)","Round nucleus, scant cytoplasm; B, T, and NK cells","Antibody (B), cell-mediated immunity and help (T), killing of infected cells (NK)","Viral infection (EBV, CMV), pertussis, CLL"],["Monocyte (about 2 to 8%)","Large, kidney-shaped nucleus; becomes macrophage in tissue","Phagocytosis, antigen presentation, IL-1 and TNF release","Chronic infection (tuberculosis, endocarditis), recovery from infection"],["Eosinophil (about 1 to 4%)","Bilobed nucleus; granules with major basic protein","Kill helminths with granule proteins; modulate allergic inflammation","Helminths (tissue invasive), allergy and asthma, adrenal insufficiency"],["Basophil (under 1%)","Dense granules with histamine and heparin; IgE receptor (FcepsilonRI)","Release histamine and leukotrienes in allergic reactions","Myeloproliferative neoplasms, especially CML"]],"caption":"Percentages are approximate adult values; rows are in order of frequency."},{"type":"h","text":"Neutrophilia and bacterial infection"},{"type":"p","text":"Bacteria trigger macrophages to release IL-1, TNF, and G-CSF, which stimulate marrow production and release of stored neutrophils. Early release brings immature band forms into blood, a left shift, and the cells may show toxic granulation and Dohle bodies. Not every neutrophilia is infection. About half of blood neutrophils normally adhere to vessel walls (the marginated pool); glucocorticoids and catecholamines reduce adhesion, so cells move into the circulating pool and the count rises within hours without a left shift. Glucocorticoids at the same time lower eosinophils and lymphocytes."},{"type":"h","text":"Eosinophilia and lymphocytosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Helminths are too large to be engulfed, so Th2 cells release IL-4 (driving IgE) and IL-5 (driving eosinophil production). IgE coats the worm, and eosinophils bind and degranulate onto it, releasing major basic protein (antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity). Tissue-invasive helminths cause the strongest eosinophilia; protozoa usually do not. Allergy and asthma use the same Th2 pathway. Lack of cortisol in adrenal insufficiency removes the normal suppression of eosinophils, so counts rise."},{"type":"p","text":"Viruses live inside cells, where antibodies cannot reach them, so the response depends on cytotoxic T cells and NK cells, which expand and produce lymphocytosis. In infectious mononucleosis the large reactive atypical lymphocytes are mainly CD8 T cells responding to EBV-infected B cells. Pertussis is the classic bacterial exception: pertussis toxin blocks lymphocyte homing out of blood, producing a marked lymphocytosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-02-01-C07","obj":"HE-2.7","title":"ABO and Rh blood groups and transfusion compatibility","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"ABO antigens are carbohydrates on red cell membrane glycoproteins and glycolipids, also found on many other tissues and in secretions. All three start from the H antigen, an oligosaccharide ending in fucose. The A allele encodes a transferase that adds N-acetylgalactosamine to H, the B allele one that adds galactose, and the O allele encodes an inactive enzyme, so group O cells carry unmodified H. A and B are codominant and O is recessive."},{"type":"p","text":"People make antibodies against whichever of A and B they lack. These isohemagglutinins appear in the first months of life without exposure to foreign red cells, because gut bacteria carry carbohydrate antigens that resemble A and B. They are mainly IgM, a pentamer that agglutinates red cells and activates complement efficiently. Group O people also make IgG anti-A and anti-B, which can cross the placenta and cause usually mild ABO hemolytic disease of the newborn."},{"type":"p","text":"The Rh system is a set of membrane proteins, of which the D antigen is the most immunogenic; Rh positive means D positive, which is true of most people. Anti-D is not naturally occurring. An Rh-negative person forms it only after exposure to D-positive red cells by transfusion or pregnancy, and it is IgG, which crosses the placenta and causes hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn in a later D-positive pregnancy."},{"type":"table","title":"ABO groups and compatible donors","cols":["Recipient group","Antigens on red cells","Antibodies in plasma","Compatible red cells","Compatible plasma"],"rows":[["O","None (H only)","Anti-A and anti-B","O only","O, A, B, AB"],["A","A","Anti-B","A, O","A, AB"],["B","B","Anti-A","B, O","B, AB"],["AB","A and B","None","AB, A, B, O","AB only"]],"caption":"Red cells: the donor's antigens must not meet the recipient's antibodies. Plasma: the donor's antibodies must not meet the recipient's antigens. O negative is the universal red cell donor; AB is the universal plasma donor."},{"type":"p","text":"Compatibility follows from asking whose antibodies meet whose antigens. For red cells the danger is the recipient's antibodies attacking donor cells, so donor cells must lack any antigen the recipient has antibodies against; for Rh, a D-negative recipient should receive D-negative red cells to avoid sensitization. For plasma the logic reverses, because the plasma carries the donor's antibodies; Rh type does not matter for plasma since it contains no red cells and anti-D is not preformed."},{"type":"flow","title":"Mechanism of an ABO-incompatible red cell transfusion","steps":["Group A cells given to a group O recipient","Recipient IgM anti-A binds donor cells","Complement fixed through to the membrane attack complex","Intravascular hemolysis: hemoglobinemia, hemoglobinuria","Fever, hypotension, back pain; DIC and acute kidney injury may follow"],"caption":"IgM and complement lyse cells inside vessels within minutes, unlike IgG anti-D, which causes slower extravascular removal by splenic macrophages."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A group AB, Rh-negative patient needs red cells and plasma. Which groups can she receive?","explain":"Red cells: any ABO group (she has no anti-A or anti-B) but Rh negative, so AB, A, B, or O negative. Plasma: only AB, because any other plasma contains anti-A, anti-B, or both against her cells.","check":"Compare with the ABO table and the paragraph on whose antibodies meet whose antigens."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and biochemistry explain the parts. The liver builds albumin and most clotting and transport proteins, the kidney houses the oxygen sensor, and the red cell shows how losing organelles concentrates a cell on one job while leaving it dependent on glycolysis, the pentose phosphate pathway, and a flexible membrane skeleton. Respiratory and acid-base physiology meet in carbonic anhydrase and band 3, and immunology supplies the Th2, cytotoxic T cell, and IgM and IgG reasoning behind white cell changes and transfusion reactions."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology then follows each mechanism to its failure: hypoalbuminemia to edema, renal fibrosis to EPO-deficient anemia, a JAK2 mutation to EPO-independent polycythemia, and preformed IgM to intravascular hemolysis. The limits matter too. CBC indices are averages, so mixed deficiencies can give a normal MCV, reference ranges differ by laboratory, age, sex, and altitude, and a white cell differential suggests a category of cause rather than a diagnosis, so each pattern is read together with the history and the smear."}],"summary":["Plasma is unclotted blood's fluid with all clotting factors; serum is plasma minus fibrinogen and consumed factors, with slightly higher potassium.","Albumin provides about 75 to 80% of plasma oncotic pressure and carries unconjugated bilirubin, fatty acids, and about 40% of plasma calcium.","Hypoalbuminemia lowers total but not ionized calcium; alkalosis lowers ionized calcium by increasing binding to albumin.","Low albumin lowers capillary oncotic pressure, raises net filtration, and causes edema once lymphatic safety factors are exceeded.","The red cell lacks nucleus and mitochondria, relies on glycolysis, and uses carbonic anhydrase and band 3 to carry most CO2 as plasma bicarbonate.","Hypoxia stabilizes HIF-alpha in renal peritubular interstitial cells and raises EPO; EPO is low in CKD, high at altitude, and suppressed in polycythemia vera.","MCV identifies the failed step (hemoglobin synthesis versus DNA synthesis); the RPI separates hemolysis or blood loss from underproduction.","Neutrophilia with left shift suggests bacterial infection; eosinophilia suggests helminths or allergy; lymphocytosis suggests viral infection or pertussis.","ABO antibodies are naturally occurring IgM causing intravascular hemolysis; anti-D is acquired IgG causing hemolytic disease of the newborn.","O negative is the universal red cell donor; AB is the universal plasma donor."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia; Resistance of the Body to Infection: Leukocytes; Blood Types, Transfusion"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Blood; Transport of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide in the Blood"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders; Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Red cell metabolism and plasma proteins"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Transplantation Immunology (blood transfusion and ABO antigens)"}]},"HE-3":{"unit":"HE-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-03-01","title":"Blood Cell Breakdown: Red Cell Removal, Bilirubin, Hemolysis, and Iron","subject":"Hematologic System (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows blood cells from the end of their lives to the recycling of their parts. It explains why red cells survive about 120 days and how macrophages remove them, how heme becomes bilirubin and leaves the body, how laboratory findings reveal whether red cells are destroyed inside vessels or inside macrophages, how iron is absorbed and recycled under the control of hepcidin, and why blood counts fall in a fixed order when the marrow stops. Board questions usually give bilirubin fractions, urine findings, haptoglobin, iron studies, or a post-chemotherapy blood count and ask for the mechanism. Most answers come back to where a cell is destroyed, how water soluble a pigment is, or how long a cell lives.","prerequisites":["Red cell structure, hemoglobin, and erythropoiesis (HE-1, HE-2)","Heme synthesis and hemoglobin structure (HE-5)","Liver lobule, hepatocyte polarity, and biliary tree (GI-3)","Splenic red pulp and macrophage function (IM-2, IM-8)","Glomerular filtration and proximal tubule reabsorption (UR-6)"],"connections":[{"to":"Diseases of blood cell lysis (HE-9-B)","how":"Hereditary spherocytosis, G6PD deficiency, paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, and autoimmune hemolysis are recognized by the intravascular and extravascular patterns taught here."},{"to":"Diseases of blood cell production (HE-9-A)","how":"Iron deficiency, anemia of chronic disease, and aplastic anemia are explained by iron handling, hepcidin, and circulating life span."},{"to":"Hepatic and gallbladder disease (GI-13-C, GI-13-D)","how":"Bilirubin fractions and urine findings locate jaundice before, within, or after the hepatocyte, and chronic hemolysis forms black pigment gallstones."},{"to":"Complement and hypersensitivity (IM-7, IM-15-C)","how":"Whether antibody and complement complete the membrane attack complex decides whether red cells lyse in vessels or are eaten by macrophages."},{"to":"Cell and cytokine signaling (IM-6)","how":"Interleukin 6 induction of hepcidin links inflammation to low serum iron and iron-restricted red cell production."}],"terms":[{"term":"Culling","def":"Removal of whole senescent or damaged red cells by splenic macrophages."},{"term":"Pitting","def":"Removal of rigid inclusions from red cells by splenic macrophages, after which the cell returns to the circulation."},{"term":"Heme oxygenase","def":"The microsomal enzyme that opens the heme ring, releasing iron, carbon monoxide, and biliverdin."},{"term":"Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin","def":"Water-insoluble bilirubin that travels bound to albumin and cannot enter urine."},{"term":"Conjugated (direct) bilirubin","def":"Bilirubin linked to glucuronic acid by UGT1A1, water soluble and excretable in bile and, when raised in plasma, in urine."},{"term":"Urobilinogen","def":"Colorless products of bacterial reduction of bilirubin in the gut, partly reabsorbed and excreted in urine as urobilin."},{"term":"Haptoglobin","def":"A liver-made plasma protein that binds free hemoglobin dimers so macrophages can clear them."},{"term":"Hemosiderin","def":"Insoluble aggregates of partly degraded ferritin within lysosomes, stained blue by Prussian blue."},{"term":"Ferroportin","def":"The only known cellular iron exporter, present on enterocytes, macrophages, and hepatocytes."},{"term":"Hepcidin","def":"A hepatocyte peptide hormone that binds ferroportin and causes its degradation, lowering iron entry into plasma."},{"term":"Transferrin saturation","def":"Serum iron divided by total iron-binding capacity, the fraction of transferrin binding sites occupied by iron."},{"term":"Absolute neutrophil count (ANC)","def":"Total white cell count multiplied by the fraction of neutrophils and bands."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-03-01-C01","obj":"HE-3.1","title":"Red cell life span and removal of senescent cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mature red cells have no nucleus, ribosomes, or mitochondria. They make ATP only by glycolysis and NADPH only through the pentose phosphate pathway, and they cannot synthesize new protein. Every enzyme and membrane protein the cell will ever have is present when it leaves the marrow, so function declines steadily as these proteins are oxidized and lost. In healthy adults this gives a fairly uniform life span of about 120 days."},{"type":"p","text":"Aging makes the cell both easier to recognize and harder to squeeze through narrow spaces. Enzyme activity, including glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, falls, so oxidant defense weakens. The cell sheds membrane in small vesicles, losing surface area relative to volume, and becomes denser and less deformable. Oxidized band 3 protein clusters and binds naturally occurring IgG and complement C3 fragments, and phosphatidylserine moves from the inner to the outer membrane leaflet. Each of these changes is a signal for phagocytosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The spleen is the main quality-control site. In the open circulation of the red pulp, blood percolates through the splenic cords and must re-enter the venous sinuses through narrow slits between endothelial cells, only about 1 to 2 µm wide, while a red cell is about 7 to 8 µm across. A young, deformable cell folds through. A stiff old cell is held in the cords, where slow flow, low glucose, and low pH prolong its contact with cord macrophages, which engulf it (culling). Splenic macrophages can also remove rigid inclusions such as Howell-Jolly bodies and Heinz bodies and return the cell to the circulation (pitting). Kupffer cells in the liver and marrow macrophages remove the remaining old cells, and the liver becomes the dominant site when cells are heavily coated with complement or the spleen is absent."},{"type":"flow","title":"Removal of a senescent red cell","steps":["No nucleus or ribosomes: damaged proteins are not replaced","Oxidation, membrane loss, and stiffening over about 120 days","Clustered band 3 binds IgG and C3; phosphatidylserine exposed","Stiff cell trapped in splenic cords at the sinus slits","Cord macrophage engulfs the cell","Globin to amino acids, iron to transferrin, heme to bilirubin"],"caption":"Loss of deformability traps the cell, and surface changes mark it for phagocytosis; liver and marrow macrophages handle the remainder."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the phagolysosome, globin is hydrolyzed to amino acids that return to the plasma pool, iron is released from heme and exported to transferrin, and the porphyrin ring is converted to bilirubin (C02). About 1% of red cells, roughly 200 billion cells carrying about 6 g of hemoglobin, are removed each day and matched by marrow output, reflected in a reticulocyte count of about 0.5% to 2%. This recycling returns about 20 to 25 mg of iron a day, far more than the 1 to 2 mg absorbed from food (C04)."},{"type":"p","text":"Removing the spleen changes red cell survival little, because the liver takes over culling, but pitting is lost, so Howell-Jolly bodies, Pappenheimer bodies, and target cells appear on the smear. An enlarged, congested spleen does the opposite: it traps and destroys more cells, including normal ones, and pools platelets (hypersplenism)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-03-01-C02","obj":"HE-3.2","title":"Heme catabolism and the bilirubin pathway","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Adults produce about 250 to 350 mg of bilirubin a day, about 80% from hemoglobin of senescent red cells and the rest from ineffective erythropoiesis and other heme proteins such as myoglobin and cytochromes. Each step changes water solubility, which decides where the pigment can travel and which body fluid it can enter."},{"type":"h","text":"Formation in macrophages"},{"type":"p","text":"Microsomal heme oxygenase, using O2 and NADPH, opens the porphyrin ring at the alpha-methene bridge. The products are green biliverdin, ferrous iron, and carbon monoxide; heme breakdown is the main endogenous source of CO, which is why carboxyhemoglobin rises slightly in brisk hemolysis. Cytosolic biliverdin reductase, also using NADPH, then reduces biliverdin to yellow-orange unconjugated bilirubin."},{"type":"h","text":"Transport and the danger of free bilirubin"},{"type":"p","text":"Internal hydrogen bonds fold the bilirubin molecule and hide its polar groups, so unconjugated bilirubin is nearly insoluble in water and soluble in lipid. It travels in plasma tightly bound to albumin, which keeps it out of the glomerular filtrate, so it never appears in urine. The small unbound fraction crosses membranes, including the blood-brain barrier; at high levels in newborns it deposits in the basal ganglia and brainstem nuclei (kernicterus). Low albumin, or competitors for its binding site such as sulfonamides, salicylates, and free fatty acids, raise the free fraction."},{"type":"h","text":"Hepatic uptake, conjugation, and secretion"},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes take up bilirubin across the sinusoidal membrane by carrier-mediated transport and hold it with cytosolic binding proteins (ligandin, a glutathione S-transferase). In the endoplasmic reticulum, UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1 (UGT1A1) transfers glucuronic acid from UDP-glucuronic acid, forming mainly bilirubin diglucuronide. The ATP-dependent canalicular pump MRP2 secretes conjugated bilirubin into bile against a steep gradient. This secretion step is rate limiting, so when hepatocytes are injured, conjugated bilirubin refluxes into blood. Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and only loosely bound to albumin, so it is filtered and appears in urine whenever its plasma level rises."},{"type":"h","text":"Intestinal conversion and excretion"},{"type":"p","text":"Conjugated bilirubin is not absorbed in the small intestine. In the terminal ileum and colon, bacterial beta-glucuronidases remove the glucuronide and bacteria reduce the pigment to colorless urobilinogen. Most is converted through stercobilinogen to stercobilin, which colors stool brown. About 10% to 20% of urobilinogen is reabsorbed into portal blood; the liver takes up and re-excretes most of it, and a small amount is filtered by the kidney and oxidized to yellow urobilin in urine. The laboratory reports conjugated bilirubin as direct, because it reacts directly with the diazo reagent, and unconjugated as indirect. Total serum bilirubin is normally about 0.3 to 1.2 mg/dL (about 5 to 21 µmol/L), and scleral jaundice becomes visible at about 2 to 3 mg/dL."},{"type":"flow","title":"From heme to excreted pigments","steps":["Macrophage heme oxygenase opens the heme ring","Biliverdin, Fe2+, and CO are released","Biliverdin reductase forms unconjugated bilirubin","Albumin carries it to the liver; not filtered","Hepatocyte UGT1A1 adds glucuronic acid","MRP2 secretes conjugated bilirubin into bile","Colonic bacteria form urobilinogen","Stercobilin in stool; urobilin in urine"],"caption":"Bilirubin is water insoluble until conjugated and water soluble afterward."},{"type":"table","title":"Bilirubin pattern by site of the problem","cols":["Category","Main serum fraction","Urine bilirubin","Urine urobilinogen","Stool"],"rows":[["Increased production (hemolysis)","Unconjugated","Absent","Increased","Normal or dark"],["Reduced conjugation (Gilbert syndrome, Crigler-Najjar syndrome, newborn)","Unconjugated","Absent","Normal or low","Normal"],["Hepatocellular injury","Both, often mostly conjugated","Present","Often increased","Normal or pale"],["Biliary obstruction","Conjugated","Present (dark urine)","Low or absent","Pale (acholic)"]],"caption":"Urine bilirubin means conjugated hyperbilirubinemia; urine urobilinogen requires bilirubin to reach the gut."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A stone completely blocks the common bile duct. Predict urine bilirubin, urine urobilinogen, and stool color.","explain":"Conjugated bilirubin backs up into blood and, being water soluble, is filtered, so urine bilirubin is positive and urine is dark. No bilirubin reaches the gut, so bacteria form no urobilinogen or stercobilin: urine urobilinogen is absent and stools are pale.","check":"The intestinal conversion paragraph and the bilirubin pattern table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-03-01-C03","obj":"HE-3.3","title":"Intravascular versus extravascular hemolysis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemolysis means red cell survival shorter than about 120 days. Whether cells are destroyed inside macrophages (extravascular hemolysis) or burst within the circulation (intravascular hemolysis) decides which laboratory markers change. Most hemolysis is extravascular, and many disorders combine both, so findings identify the dominant site."},{"type":"h","text":"Findings shared by both patterns"},{"type":"p","text":"Any increase in destruction raises the heme load, so unconjugated bilirubin rises, usually modestly (rarely above about 4 to 5 mg/dL without liver disease), because a healthy liver can conjugate several times its usual load. Urine urobilinogen increases while urine bilirubin stays negative, and long-standing hemolysis concentrates bilirubin in bile, where calcium bilirubinate precipitates as black pigment gallstones. Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), abundant in red cells as LDH-1 and LDH-2, leaks out with destruction and is generally highest in intravascular hemolysis. A healthy marrow responds with reticulocytosis."},{"type":"h","text":"Intravascular hemolysis"},{"type":"p","text":"When a red cell ruptures in the circulation, hemoglobin tetramers split into alpha-beta dimers, which haptoglobin, a liver-made plasma glycoprotein, binds with very high affinity. Macrophages, mainly in the liver, clear the complex through the CD163 receptor, so haptoglobin is consumed faster than it is made and falls to very low or undetectable levels. Once haptoglobin is saturated, free hemoglobin turns plasma pink to red (hemoglobinemia), and released heme is bound by hemopexin. Free dimers (about 32 kDa) pass the glomerular filter and are reabsorbed by proximal tubule cells, which store the iron as ferritin and hemosiderin. When reabsorption is exceeded, hemoglobinuria appears: red to brown urine that is dipstick positive for blood with few or no red cells on microscopy. Tubule cells loaded with hemosiderin are shed days later, so hemosiderinuria, shown by Prussian blue staining of urine sediment, marks recurrent or chronic intravascular hemolysis even between episodes. Free hemoglobin also scavenges nitric oxide, causing smooth muscle spasm, and large loads injure tubules."},{"type":"h","text":"Extravascular hemolysis"},{"type":"p","text":"In extravascular hemolysis, macrophages of the spleen and liver ingest red cells and degrade the hemoglobin inside, so little reaches plasma. Haptoglobin is normal or moderately reduced, and the urine contains no hemoglobin or hemosiderin. Cells coated with warm IgG lose membrane when splenic macrophages remove pieces through Fc receptors, and cells with defective cytoskeletal proteins in hereditary spherocytosis shed membrane vesicles. Either way, surface area falls relative to volume, the cell becomes a spherocyte, and it is trapped in the splenic cords; the extra work enlarges the spleen. Complement adds a distinction: a completed membrane attack complex (ABO antibodies) lyses cells in vessels, whereas cells carrying only C3b (cold agglutinin disease) are cleared mainly by liver macrophages."},{"type":"table","title":"Intravascular versus extravascular hemolysis","cols":["Finding","Intravascular","Extravascular"],"rows":[["Site of destruction","Within blood vessels","Macrophages of spleen and liver"],["Haptoglobin","Very low or absent","Normal or moderately low"],["LDH","High, often markedly","High"],["Unconjugated bilirubin","High","High"],["Plasma","Pink to red (hemoglobinemia)","Clear"],["Hemoglobinuria","Present when haptoglobin is saturated","Absent"],["Hemosiderinuria","Present, appearing days later","Absent"],["Smear","Schistocytes when mechanical shear is the cause","Spherocytes"],["Examples","ABO-incompatible transfusion, paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, microangiopathic hemolysis (TTP, HUS, DIC), mechanical heart valve","Hereditary spherocytosis, warm autoimmune hemolysis, thalassemia, sickle cell disease (mostly)"]],"caption":"Shared markers confirm hemolysis; haptoglobin, plasma, and urine locate it."},{"type":"example","title":"Locating hemolysis from the laboratory","text":"A 62-year-old with a mechanical aortic valve has hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL, reticulocytes 6%, LDH three times the upper limit, indirect bilirubin 2.2 mg/dL, and undetectable haptoglobin. Urine is dipstick positive for blood without red cells, and the sediment stains with Prussian blue. Step 1: reticulocytosis with high LDH and indirect bilirubin means hemolysis with a responsive marrow. Step 2: absent haptoglobin with hemoglobinuria means free hemoglobin in plasma, so destruction is intravascular. Step 3: hemosiderinuria means it has continued for days or longer. Step 4: schistocytes on the smear would confirm mechanical shear by the valve. Warm autoimmune hemolysis would instead show spherocytes, a positive direct antiglobulin test, splenomegaly, and urine free of hemoglobin."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-03-01-C04","obj":"HE-3.4","title":"Iron absorption, transport, storage, and hepcidin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The body contains about 3 to 4 g of iron, roughly two thirds in hemoglobin and most of the rest stored as ferritin and hemosiderin in liver, spleen, and marrow. Free iron catalyzes hydroxyl radical formation (the Fenton reaction), yet there is no regulated way to excrete it; only about 1 to 2 mg a day is lost in shed cells and minor bleeding. Balance is therefore controlled where iron enters plasma."},{"type":"h","text":"Absorption"},{"type":"p","text":"A typical diet supplies about 10 to 20 mg a day; about 10% is absorbed, mainly in the duodenum. Nonheme iron is mostly ferric (Fe3+), poorly soluble above the acidic pH of the stomach. Duodenal cytochrome b on the brush border reduces it to ferrous (Fe2+) iron, aided by acid and ascorbate, and divalent metal transporter 1 (DMT1) carries Fe2+ into the enterocyte coupled to H+. Heme iron is absorbed more efficiently by a separate, incompletely characterized route and freed inside the cell by heme oxygenase. Inside the enterocyte, iron is either stored in ferritin, and lost when the cell is shed a few days later, or exported across the basolateral membrane by ferroportin. The copper-containing ferroxidase hephaestin oxidizes it back to Fe3+ for binding to transferrin; plasma ceruloplasmin does the same for iron leaving macrophages and hepatocytes."},{"type":"h","text":"Transport and storage"},{"type":"p","text":"Transferrin carries two Fe3+ ions per molecule. Erythroid precursors take up iron-loaded transferrin through transferrin receptor 1 by endocytosis, release the iron in the acidified endosome, and send it to mitochondria, where ferrochelatase inserts Fe2+ into protoporphyrin IX. Ferritin, a hollow 24-subunit shell, stores Fe3+ in soluble, nontoxic form; hemosiderin is aggregated, partly degraded ferritin."},{"type":"h","text":"Hepcidin"},{"type":"p","text":"Hepcidin, a 25-amino-acid hepatocyte peptide, binds ferroportin and causes its internalization and degradation, trapping iron in enterocytes, macrophages, and hepatocytes. Hepatocytes raise hepcidin when transferrin saturation and liver iron rise (sensed through HFE, transferrin receptor 2, and BMP6 signaling) and when interleukin 6 activates JAK-STAT3 in inflammation. Hepcidin falls with iron deficiency, hypoxia, and active erythropoiesis, when erythropoietin-stimulated erythroblasts secrete erythroferrone."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Hepcidin feedback on plasma iron","steps":["Transferrin saturation and liver iron rise","Hepatocytes sense iron (HFE, TfR2, BMP6)","Hepcidin secretion increases","Ferroportin internalized and degraded","Less gut absorption and macrophage release","Plasma iron falls, so hepcidin declines"],"caption":"Negative feedback on iron entry into plasma."},{"type":"p","text":"Inflammation raises hepcidin regardless of stores, locking iron in macrophages, so serum iron falls while ferritin is normal or high (anemia of chronic disease). In hereditary hemochromatosis, HFE mutations (most often homozygous C282Y) blunt hepcidin, so absorption continues despite overload and iron deposits in liver, pancreas, heart, and joints. In ineffective erythropoiesis, as in beta-thalassemia, erythroferrone suppresses hepcidin and iron overload develops even without transfusion."},{"type":"table","title":"Iron studies and what they reflect","cols":["Measure","What it reflects","Approximate adult reference","Iron deficiency","Anemia of chronic disease","Hereditary hemochromatosis"],"rows":[["Serum iron","Iron on transferrin","About 60 to 170 µg/dL","Low","Low","High"],["TIBC","Transferrin concentration","About 250 to 400 µg/dL","High","Low or normal","Low or normal"],["Transferrin saturation","Serum iron divided by TIBC","About 20% to 50%","Low","Low or normal","High"],["Ferritin","Stores; acute phase reactant","About 20 to 250 ng/mL (men), 10 to 120 (women)","Low","Normal or high","High"],["Hepcidin","Iron hormone (not routinely measured)","Not standardized","Low","High","Inappropriately low"]],"caption":"Hepatocytes make more transferrin when iron is scarce, so TIBC rises in deficiency and falls in inflammation."},{"type":"example","title":"Is iron absent or locked away?","text":"In active rheumatoid arthritis: hemoglobin 10.4 g/dL, serum iron 35 µg/dL, TIBC 220 µg/dL, saturation 16%, ferritin 180 ng/mL. Low serum iron does not separate the causes; TIBC does. Deficiency would raise TIBC, but here it is low, and ferritin shows stores are present, so IL-6-driven hepcidin is trapping iron in macrophages. Because ferritin rises with inflammation, a value below about 100 ng/mL here would suggest coexisting deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-03-01-C05","obj":"HE-3.5","title":"Circulating life span and the order of cytopenias after marrow suppression","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each blood count is a steady state: the number of cells in the blood equals the rate of production multiplied by the time each cell spends there. When production stops abruptly, as after cytotoxic chemotherapy, high-dose radiation, or drug-induced marrow aplasia, cells already in the blood keep aging out at their normal rate with no replacements. Each count then falls on a timescale set by the cell's circulating life span and by the marrow's reserve of finished cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Neutrophils circulate with a half-life of only about 6 to 8 hours before migrating into tissues, where they survive a day or two more. The marrow holds a storage pool of mature and band neutrophils many times larger than the circulating pool, which buffers the count for several days and is released quickly by stress, glucocorticoids, or granulocyte colony-stimulating factor. Once that pool is spent, the absolute neutrophil count (normally about 1,500 to 7,000 per µL) falls steeply, reaching its lowest point about 7 to 14 days after typical cytotoxic regimens. Below about 500 per µL the risk of bacterial and fungal infection rises sharply, and signs of inflammation may be muted because few cells are present to form pus."},{"type":"p","text":"Platelets survive about 7 to 10 days, and about one third are held in the spleen at any time. Thrombocytopenia therefore follows the neutropenia over about 1 to 2 weeks. Thrombopoietin is made by the liver at a fairly constant rate and is removed by binding to platelets and megakaryocytes, so its plasma level rises as the platelet mass falls. Low platelets cause petechiae and mucosal bleeding, with spontaneous bleeding more likely below about 10,000 to 20,000 per µL (normal about 150,000 to 400,000 per µL)."},{"type":"p","text":"Red cells last about 120 days, so with production halted the red cell mass falls by only about 1% a day and hemoglobin drifts down over weeks. The earliest red cell sign is a fall in the reticulocyte count, because reticulocytes mature within about a day in the blood. A patient after chemotherapy therefore presents with fever from neutropenia, or with bleeding, well before significant anemia. Anemia appears early only when blood is also lost or destroyed."},{"type":"table","title":"Circulating life span and the order of fall","cols":["Cell","Time in circulation","Buffer or regulator","After abrupt marrow shutdown"],"rows":[["Neutrophil","Half-life about 6 to 8 hours","Large marrow storage pool covers several days","Falls first; lowest about 7 to 14 days"],["Platelet","About 7 to 10 days","Splenic pool; thrombopoietin rises as mass falls","Falls second, over about 1 to 2 weeks"],["Red cell","About 120 days","Reticulocytes mature in about 1 day in blood","Reticulocytes vanish in days; hemoglobin falls about 1% a day"],["Lymphocyte","Weeks to years (memory cells)","Not tied to daily marrow output","Exception: radiation kills them directly within 1 to 2 days"]],"caption":"The shorter the circulating life, the sooner the count falls once production stops."},{"type":"p","text":"Lymphocytes are the exception: although long lived, they fall within about 24 to 48 hours of significant radiation because radiation kills them directly by apoptosis, not because production stops. The same life span logic also explains why a short pause in red cell production is dangerous only when red cell survival is already short."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Parvovirus B19 infects erythroid progenitors and halts red cell production for about a week. Predict the hemoglobin change in a healthy adult and in a patient with sickle cell disease, whose red cells survive about 10 to 20 days.","explain":"The healthy adult loses about 1% of red cell mass a day, roughly 7% over the week, which goes unnoticed. The sickle cell patient loses about 5% to 10% a day with no replacement, so hemoglobin falls steeply within days (transient aplastic crisis), with a reticulocyte count near zero.","check":"The steady-state paragraph and the red cell row of the life span table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology set the stage: the open circulation of the splenic red pulp tests red cell deformability, Kupffer cells line the hepatic sinusoids, the polarized hepatocyte takes up bilirubin at one face and secretes it at the canaliculus, and the proximal tubule reabsorbs filtered hemoglobin. Biochemistry supplies the chemistry of heme oxygenase, UGT1A1, and MRP2, whose inherited defects (Gilbert, Crigler-Najjar, and Dubin-Johnson syndromes) are distinguished by which bilirubin fraction rises. Immunology decides where antibody-coated cells die, depending on whether IgG engages splenic Fc receptors or complement completes the membrane attack complex, and IL-6 connects inflammation to hepcidin and iron sequestration."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology applies these mechanisms but rarely in pure form. Sickle cell disease, G6PD deficiency, and malaria mix intravascular and extravascular destruction; haptoglobin is also an acute phase reactant and falls in liver disease; and ferritin rises with inflammation, liver injury, and malignancy. Laboratory patterns are therefore read together with the smear rather than from a single value. Life span reasoning carries into microbiology and oncology, explaining why parvovirus B19 is dangerous in hemolytic disease and why infection risk after chemotherapy peaks before anemia appears."}],"summary":["Red cells lack a nucleus and ribosomes, so they cannot replace damaged proteins; they stiffen and are culled by splenic macrophages after about 120 days.","Heme oxygenase converts heme to biliverdin, Fe2+, and CO; biliverdin reductase forms water-insoluble unconjugated bilirubin carried on albumin.","UGT1A1 conjugates bilirubin with glucuronic acid; MRP2 secretion into bile is the rate-limiting step, and only conjugated bilirubin can enter urine.","Gut bacteria form urobilinogen, which becomes stercobilin in stool and urobilin in urine; complete biliary obstruction removes both.","LDH, unconjugated bilirubin, urine urobilinogen, and reticulocytes rise in all hemolysis; very low haptoglobin, hemoglobinemia, hemoglobinuria, and hemosiderinuria mark intravascular destruction.","Schistocytes point to mechanical intravascular hemolysis; spherocytes and splenomegaly point to extravascular hemolysis.","Duodenal cytochrome b reduces Fe3+ to Fe2+ for DMT1 uptake; ferroportin exports iron, hephaestin and ceruloplasmin reoxidize it, and transferrin carries it.","Hepcidin degrades ferroportin; inflammation raises it (anemia of chronic disease), while iron deficiency, hypoxia, and erythropoiesis lower it, and HFE mutations keep it inappropriately low.","Iron deficiency raises TIBC and lowers ferritin; anemia of chronic disease lowers TIBC with normal or high ferritin.","After abrupt marrow failure, neutrophils (hours) fall first, platelets (7 to 10 days) next, and hemoglobin (120 days) last."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Blood; hepatobiliary function and bilirubin metabolism"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Heme metabolism: degradation of heme and bilirubin"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders; Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Spleen: red pulp, splenic cords, and venous sinuses"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood Disorders"}]},"HE-4":{"unit":"HE-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-04-01","title":"Hematopoietic Growth Factors and Hemostasis","subject":"Hematology (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how growth factors steer marrow progenitors toward each blood cell line, then how the body stops bleeding and keeps the resulting clot local. It covers the platelet plug, the coagulation cascade, the natural anticoagulants and fibrinolysis, the laboratory tests that localize a bleeding disorder, and the Virchow triad for venous thrombosis. Board questions typically give a platelet count, PT, PTT, and mixing study result and ask you to name the defective step, or describe a patient with a clot and ask which arm of the triad is responsible. Knowing which molecule binds which receptor, and which factors each test measures, lets you reason through these questions rather than memorize patterns.","prerequisites":["Hematopoiesis from stem cell to each mature lineage (HE-1)","Platelet structure and alpha and dense granule contents (HE-1)","Erythropoietin regulation by renal oxygen sensing (HE-2)","Cytokine receptor signaling through JAK-STAT and G protein coupled receptors","Arachidonic acid metabolism by cyclooxygenase"],"connections":[{"to":"Hemoglobin and hematologic biochemistry (HE-5)","how":"Vitamin K dependent gamma-carboxylation and hepatic protein synthesis connect clotting to liver and nutritional biochemistry."},{"to":"Clotting abnormalities (HE-9-C)","how":"Hemophilia, von Willebrand disease, immune thrombocytopenia, DIC, and inherited thrombophilias are failures of the steps taught here."},{"to":"Pulmonary embolism (respiratory pathology)","how":"Most pulmonary emboli arise from deep vein thrombi in the legs that form through the Virchow triad."},{"to":"Ischemic heart disease and stroke (cardiovascular pathology)","how":"Arterial thrombosis on a ruptured plaque is driven mainly by platelet adhesion and aggregation under high shear."},{"to":"Chronic kidney disease and myeloproliferative neoplasms","how":"Erythropoietin deficiency explains renal anemia, and constitutive JAK2 signaling explains growth factor independent overproduction of blood cells."}],"terms":[{"term":"Colony-stimulating factor","def":"A glycoprotein cytokine that promotes survival, proliferation, and maturation of a hematopoietic progenitor line, such as G-CSF for neutrophils."},{"term":"Thrombopoietin","def":"The main growth factor for megakaryocytes, made mostly by the liver and cleared by binding to its receptor (MPL) on platelets and megakaryocytes."},{"term":"Primary hemostasis","def":"Formation of the initial platelet plug through adhesion, activation, and aggregation of platelets."},{"term":"Secondary hemostasis","def":"Generation of thrombin by the coagulation factor cascade and conversion of fibrinogen to a cross-linked fibrin mesh that stabilizes the platelet plug."},{"term":"Von Willebrand factor","def":"A large multimeric glycoprotein from endothelium and megakaryocytes that tethers platelets to exposed collagen through GpIb and carries and protects factor VIII in plasma."},{"term":"Tenase and prothrombinase","def":"Membrane-bound enzyme complexes on activated platelets: IXa with VIIIa activates factor X (tenase), and Xa with Va converts prothrombin to thrombin (prothrombinase)."},{"term":"Prothrombin time (PT)","def":"The clotting time of plasma after addition of tissue factor, phospholipid, and calcium; it measures factor VII and the common pathway."},{"term":"Activated partial thromboplastin time (PTT)","def":"The clotting time of plasma after addition of a contact activator, phospholipid, and calcium; it measures the contact (intrinsic) and common pathways."},{"term":"Antithrombin","def":"A hepatic serine protease inhibitor (serpin) that inactivates thrombin, factor Xa, and other activated factors, greatly accelerated by heparin or heparan sulfate."},{"term":"Plasmin","def":"The serine protease formed from plasminogen by tPA or urokinase that digests fibrin and fibrinogen."},{"term":"D-dimer","def":"A fibrin degradation fragment containing two D domains joined by a factor XIIIa cross-link, so it appears only when cross-linked fibrin is broken down."},{"term":"Mixing study","def":"Repeating a prolonged PT or PTT on a 1:1 mix of patient and normal plasma to separate a factor deficiency (corrects) from an inhibitor (does not correct)."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-04-01-C01","obj":"HE-4.1","title":"Growth factors that drive each blood cell lineage","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Marrow output can change many times over within days, and it can change for one lineage without the others. This selectivity comes from hematopoietic growth factors, glycoprotein cytokines that act on progenitors carrying their receptors. Early multipotent progenitors respond to broad factors, stem cell factor (kit ligand, acting on the c-KIT receptor) and IL-3, while committed progenitors depend on one dominant factor. So a single factor can raise one cell count alone."},{"type":"p","text":"Most of these receptors lack their own kinase activity and signal through associated Janus kinases (JAK) and STAT transcription factors. The main effect at physiologic levels is survival: without the factor, committed progenitors undergo apoptosis, and with it they proliferate and mature. The erythropoietin and thrombopoietin receptors both use JAK2, so the acquired JAK2 V617F mutation, which switches JAK2 on without ligand, causes growth factor independent overproduction in polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia, and primary myelofibrosis. In polycythemia vera the expanded red cell mass suppresses the kidney, so erythropoietin is low."},{"type":"table","title":"Growth factors by lineage","cols":["Factor","Main source","Target lineage","Regulation and clinical link"],"rows":[["Erythropoietin (EPO)","Peritubular interstitial cells of the renal cortex; a little from liver","Erythroid progenitors (CFU-E, proerythroblasts)","Rises with tissue hypoxia through HIF; low in chronic kidney disease, giving normocytic anemia"],["Thrombopoietin (TPO)","Liver, at a fairly constant rate","Megakaryocytes; also supports stem cells","Free level set by uptake on platelet MPL receptors; rises when platelets fall"],["G-CSF","Macrophages, endothelium, fibroblasts","Neutrophil progenitors","Rises in bacterial infection; also releases neutrophils from marrow"],["GM-CSF","T cells, macrophages, endothelium","Granulocyte and monocyte progenitors","Broader than G-CSF; also activates mature neutrophils and macrophages"],["M-CSF","Macrophages, endothelium, stromal cells","Monocytes and macrophages; osteoclast precursors","Needed for osteoclast formation with RANKL"],["IL-5","Th2 cells, type 2 innate lymphoid cells, mast cells","Eosinophils","Raised in parasitic and allergic disease, giving eosinophilia"],["IL-3 and stem cell factor","T cells (IL-3); marrow stroma (SCF)","Early multipotent progenitors; mast cells (SCF)","Act early and on several lines at once"]],"caption":"Committed progenitors depend on one dominant factor; early progenitors respond to broad factors."},{"type":"p","text":"Erythropoietin and thrombopoietin are regulated in opposite ways. Erythropoietin output is set at the source: when renal oxygen delivery falls, hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF) escapes oxygen-dependent degradation and turns on the EPO gene, and the reticulocyte count rises within several days. Thrombopoietin output is fairly constant, and the free level is set by removal. Platelets and megakaryocytes bind and internalize TPO through MPL, so a low platelet mass leaves more free TPO to stimulate megakaryocytes, and a high mass clears it. Inflammation adds a second input, because IL-6 increases hepatic TPO synthesis, which helps explain reactive thrombocytosis. Severe liver disease lowers TPO and contributes to thrombocytopenia."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Platelet mass regulates free thrombopoietin","steps":["Liver releases TPO at a steady rate","Platelets and megakaryocytes bind and remove TPO","Platelet count falls, so less TPO is removed","Free TPO rises and stimulates megakaryocytes","Platelet production increases","Larger platelet mass clears more TPO again"],"caption":"Removal by the target cells, not changes in synthesis, is the main control of free thrombopoietin."},{"type":"p","text":"Neutrophil production follows a similar demand signal. Bacterial products and IL-17 from T cells raise G-CSF, which speeds neutrophil production, shortens marrow transit, and releases stored neutrophils, producing neutrophilia with a left shift. GM-CSF supports both granulocyte and monocyte lines, so its effect is broader. IL-5, mainly from Th2 cells, is the dominant eosinophil factor, which links eosinophilia to helminth infection, asthma, and other type 2 responses."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-04-01-C02","obj":"HE-4.2","title":"Primary hemostasis: forming the platelet plug","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Intact endothelium keeps platelets quiet by releasing prostacyclin (PGI2) and nitric oxide, which raise platelet cAMP and cGMP, and by hiding the procoagulant subendothelium. When a vessel is cut, three events follow within seconds to minutes: vasoconstriction, platelet adhesion and activation, and platelet aggregation. The soft plug that results seals small breaks and becomes the surface for coagulation."},{"type":"p","text":"Vasoconstriction is brief. It comes from a local neural reflex, from endothelin released by injured endothelium, and from thromboxane A2 and serotonin released by platelets. It slows flow but cannot stop bleeding alone."},{"type":"p","text":"Adhesion depends on von Willebrand factor (vWF), a large multimer secreted by endothelium (from Weibel-Palade bodies) and stored in platelet alpha granules. Under the high shear stress of arterioles and small arteries, plasma vWF binds exposed subendothelial collagen and unfolds, exposing a site that binds platelet glycoprotein Ib (GpIb, part of the GpIb-IX-V complex). This tether slows platelets enough for direct collagen receptors (GpVI and the integrin alpha2beta1) to bind firmly and signal. Large vWF multimers are the most effective, which is why their loss impairs adhesion most."},{"type":"p","text":"Activation changes the platelet from a disc to a spiky sphere and triggers degranulation. Dense granules release ADP, calcium, and serotonin, and alpha granules release vWF, fibrinogen, and factor V. Phospholipase A2 frees arachidonic acid, which cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) converts to thromboxane A2, a vasoconstrictor and platelet agonist. ADP acting on P2Y12, thromboxane A2, and thrombin acting on protease-activated receptors recruit nearby platelets. Activated platelets also expose phosphatidylserine on their outer membrane, which later anchors the clotting factor complexes."},{"type":"p","text":"Aggregation requires GpIIb/IIIa (integrin alphaIIb beta3). On resting platelets it has low affinity, and agonist signaling switches it into a high-affinity form (inside-out signaling). Fibrinogen is a symmetric dimer, so one molecule can bind GpIIb/IIIa on two platelets at once and link them; under high shear, vWF can also bridge GpIIb/IIIa receptors. Aspirin acetylates COX-1 irreversibly, and because platelets have no nucleus to make new enzyme, thromboxane A2 production stays low for the platelet life span of about 7 to 10 days."},{"type":"flow","title":"Steps of primary hemostasis","steps":["Injury exposes collagen; reflex and endothelin cause vasoconstriction","vWF binds collagen and platelet GpIb (adhesion)","Platelets change shape and release ADP, calcium, serotonin","COX-1 makes thromboxane A2 from arachidonic acid","ADP (P2Y12) and thromboxane A2 activate GpIIb/IIIa","Fibrinogen links GpIIb/IIIa on neighboring platelets (aggregation)"],"caption":"Each step depends on the one before it; a defect at any step causes a platelet-type bleeding pattern."},{"type":"table","title":"Defects of primary hemostasis by step","cols":["Step lost","Disorder","Key laboratory clue"],"rows":[["Adhesion: vWF","Von Willebrand disease (most common inherited bleeding disorder)","Low vWF activity; ristocetin aggregation reduced and corrected by adding normal plasma; PTT may be prolonged"],["Adhesion: GpIb","Bernard-Soulier syndrome (autosomal recessive)","Giant platelets, mild thrombocytopenia; ristocetin aggregation absent and not corrected by normal plasma"],["Activation: COX-1","Aspirin effect","Arachidonic acid induced aggregation absent; count normal"],["Aggregation: GpIIb/IIIa","Glanzmann thrombasthenia (autosomal recessive)","No aggregation to ADP, collagen, or thrombin; ristocetin agglutination normal; count and size normal"],["Many steps","Uremia","Prolonged bleeding time with a normal count"]],"caption":"Ristocetin makes vWF bind GpIb in vitro, so it tests the adhesion step specifically."},{"type":"p","text":"Because platelets act in the first minutes and in small vessels, defects of primary hemostasis cause immediate bleeding from skin and mucosa: petechiae, easy bruising, gum bleeding, nosebleeds, and heavy menstrual bleeding. The PT is normal because the clotting factors are intact."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has mucosal bleeding, a normal platelet count, and platelets that fail to aggregate with ristocetin. How would you tell von Willebrand disease from Bernard-Soulier syndrome?","explain":"Add normal plasma. In von Willebrand disease the platelets are normal and the plasma lacks vWF, so supplying vWF restores agglutination. In Bernard-Soulier syndrome the platelet lacks GpIb, so added vWF has nothing to bind. Giant platelets also point to Bernard-Soulier.","check":"The adhesion rows of the defects table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-04-01-C03","obj":"HE-4.3","title":"Secondary hemostasis: the coagulation cascade and what PT and PTT measure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Secondary hemostasis reinforces the fragile platelet plug with fibrin through a series of proteolytic activations. Most factors circulate as inactive protease precursors (zymogens), and each active enzyme cleaves the next, amplifying a small trigger. Factors II, VII, IX, and X (and the anticoagulant proteins C and S) are vitamin K dependent: gamma-glutamyl carboxylase adds carboxyl groups to their glutamate residues, and these gamma-carboxyglutamate residues bind calcium, which lets the factors attach to phosphatidylserine on activated platelets. Without vitamin K, or when warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, these factors cannot bind membranes and do not work."},{"type":"h","text":"How clotting starts in the body"},{"type":"p","text":"In the body, clotting begins with tissue factor (TF) on fibroblasts and other cells outside the vessel, which normally never meet blood. At injury, TF binds factor VIIa, and the TF-VIIa complex activates factors X and IX. The small amount of Xa formed, with factor Va, converts a little prothrombin (II) to thrombin. Tissue factor pathway inhibitor soon shuts down TF-VIIa, so sustained clotting depends on the IXa-VIIIa route. That trace of thrombin is the key amplifier: it activates platelets, factor V, factor VIII (releasing it from vWF), and factor XI. Then IXa with VIIIa (tenase) and Xa with Va (prothrombinase) assemble on the activated platelet surface with calcium, and thrombin generation rises sharply. This is why hemophilia A and B bleed despite an intact TF-VIIa step."},{"type":"flow","title":"From tissue factor to a cross-linked clot","steps":["Injury exposes tissue factor, which binds VIIa","TF-VIIa activates X and IX","Xa with Va makes a little thrombin","Thrombin activates platelets, V, VIII, XI, and XIII","IXa-VIIIa and Xa-Va on platelets produce a thrombin burst","Thrombin cleaves fibrinogen; fibrin monomers polymerize","XIIIa cross-links fibrin into a stable clot"],"caption":"Initiation by TF-VIIa, amplification by thrombin, propagation on platelets."},{"type":"p","text":"Thrombin removes fibrinopeptides A and B from fibrinogen, and the fibrin monomers polymerize into a loose mesh. Factor XIIIa, a transglutaminase activated by thrombin, covalently cross-links fibrin, making the clot stable and resistant to plasmin. Factor XIII deficiency leaves PT and PTT normal, because those tests end when fibrin first forms, but causes delayed and umbilical stump bleeding."},{"type":"h","text":"The laboratory pathways"},{"type":"p","text":"The extrinsic and intrinsic pathways describe the two laboratory tests, not two separate systems in the body. The PT adds tissue factor and calcium to plasma, so it depends on factor VII and the common pathway (X, V, II, fibrinogen). The INR standardizes the PT between laboratories. The PTT adds a negatively charged surface, which activates factor XII with prekallikrein and high molecular weight kininogen, so it depends on XII, XI, IX, VIII, and the common pathway. Deficiency of factor XII, prekallikrein, or high molecular weight kininogen prolongs the PTT but does not cause bleeding, because the contact step is not needed for hemostasis. Factor XI deficiency causes only mild bleeding after injury."},{"type":"table","title":"What each screening test measures","cols":["Test","Factors measured","Isolated prolongation suggests","Typical reference range"],"rows":[["PT and INR","VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen","Factor VII deficiency, early vitamin K deficiency, early liver disease","PT about 11 to 13.5 s; INR about 0.8 to 1.2"],["PTT","XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, fibrinogen (plus prekallikrein and HMWK)","Hemophilia A (VIII) or B (IX), XI or XII deficiency, heparin, lupus anticoagulant","About 25 to 35 s"],["Neither prolonged","Not tested: XIII, platelets, vWF (usually)","Factor XIII deficiency or a platelet disorder","Not applicable"]],"caption":"Both prolonged points to the common pathway or several factors. Ranges vary with reagent and laboratory."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient on a poor diet with fat malabsorption has a prolonged PT but a normal PTT. Why does the PT become abnormal first?","explain":"All four vitamin K dependent factors fall, but factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 4 to 6 hours) and is measured only by the PT, so the PT lengthens before the PTT.","check":"The vitamin K paragraph and the test table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-04-01-C04","obj":"HE-4.4","title":"Natural anticoagulants and fibrinolysis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thrombin generation is explosive, so the system needs equally strong brakes to keep a clot at the injury and stop it spreading along healthy vessel. The brakes act at three levels: inhibiting the active enzymes, removing the cofactors that drive amplification, and shutting down initiation. Fibrinolysis then removes fibrin once the vessel has healed. Normal endothelium carries most of these brakes, which is why clotting stays confined to the site where endothelium is lost."},{"type":"table","title":"The brakes on clotting","cols":["Regulator","Source","Target","Effect of deficiency"],"rows":[["Antithrombin","Liver","Thrombin, Xa, and also IXa, XIa, XIIa","Venous thrombosis; heparin works poorly"],["Thrombomodulin","Endothelial surface","Binds thrombin and redirects it to activate protein C","Loss with endothelial damage favors thrombosis"],["Protein C","Liver (vitamin K dependent)","Activated form cleaves Va and VIIIa","Venous thrombosis; severe neonatal form causes purpura fulminans"],["Protein S","Liver (vitamin K dependent); endothelium","Cofactor for activated protein C","Venous thrombosis"],["Tissue factor pathway inhibitor","Endothelium","Xa, then the TF-VIIa-Xa complex","No common inherited deficiency; loss would leave initiation unchecked"],["Plasmin (from plasminogen)","Liver makes plasminogen; activated on fibrin","Fibrin, fibrinogen, factors V and VIII","Impaired clot removal"]],"caption":"Enzyme inhibitors, cofactor destruction, and initiation blockade act together with fibrinolysis."},{"type":"p","text":"Antithrombin is a serine protease inhibitor (serpin) that forms a stable complex with thrombin and factor Xa. On its own it acts slowly. Heparan sulfate on endothelium, or heparin given as a drug, binds antithrombin and changes its shape, speeding inhibition about a thousandfold, so clotting factors that drift onto healthy endothelium are inactivated quickly. Inherited antithrombin deficiency causes venous thrombosis, and nephrotic syndrome can produce acquired deficiency because antithrombin is lost in the urine."},{"type":"p","text":"The protein C pathway turns thrombin from a procoagulant into an anticoagulant. When thrombin reaches intact endothelium and binds thrombomodulin, it can no longer cleave fibrinogen or activate platelets well; instead the complex activates protein C, a step enhanced by the endothelial protein C receptor. Activated protein C, with protein S as its cofactor, cleaves factors Va and VIIIa, dismantling the prothrombinase and tenase complexes. Factor V Leiden is a point mutation (Arg506Gln) at the cleavage site in factor V, so factor Va resists inactivation and thrombin generation continues. Protein C has a short half-life, so when warfarin is started, protein C falls before most procoagulant factors, briefly favoring clotting; this underlies warfarin-induced skin necrosis in protein C deficiency."},{"type":"flow","title":"Protein C pathway","steps":["Thrombin binds thrombomodulin on intact endothelium","Thrombin-thrombomodulin activates protein C","Activated protein C binds protein S","The complex cleaves Va and VIIIa","Tenase and prothrombinase lose their cofactors","Thrombin generation slows; clotting stays local"],"caption":"Factor V Leiden blocks the cleavage step, so factor Va keeps working."},{"type":"p","text":"Tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI) works by feedback. It first binds factor Xa, and the TFPI-Xa pair then inhibits the TF-VIIa complex. Initiation therefore shuts itself down once some Xa has formed, which is why continued clotting depends on factors VIII and IX."},{"type":"h","text":"Fibrinolysis, fibrin degradation products, and D-dimer"},{"type":"p","text":"Endothelial cells release tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) slowly over days. Fibrin binds both tPA and plasminogen, which raises tPA activity many times, so plasmin forms on the clot rather than free in plasma. Free plasmin is quickly inactivated by alpha2-antiplasmin, and plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 (PAI-1) restrains tPA. Plasmin cleaves fibrin and fibrinogen into fibrin degradation products (FDPs). Only cross-linked fibrin, joined by factor XIIIa between D domains, yields D-dimer, so a raised D-dimer shows that a clot formed and is being broken down. D-dimer rises in deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, and DIC, but also after surgery, in pregnancy, cancer, and older age, so it is sensitive but not specific. A normal value helps exclude venous thrombosis in a low-probability patient."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-04-01-C05","obj":"HE-4.5","title":"Using laboratory tests to localize a bleeding disorder","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each screening test examines one part of hemostasis, so together they localize a bleeding disorder. The platelet count asks whether there are enough platelets (normal about 150,000 to 400,000/microL). The bleeding time, or its modern replacement the platelet function analyzer, asks whether platelets and vWF can form a plug. The PT and PTT test the clotting factors, fibrinogen (normal about 200 to 400 mg/dL) shows whether the substrate is present or being consumed, and the mixing study separates a missing factor from an inhibitor."},{"type":"p","text":"The bleeding pattern points the same way. Platelet and vWF disorders cause immediate mucocutaneous bleeding with petechiae. Clotting factor disorders cause delayed, deep bleeding into joints (hemarthrosis) and muscles, or renewed bleeding hours after surgery, because the plug is never reinforced by fibrin. Spontaneous bleeding from thrombocytopenia is uncommon until the count falls below about 20,000/microL."},{"type":"table","title":"Localizing a bleeding disorder","cols":["Disorder","Platelets","Bleeding time","PT","PTT","Other clue"],"rows":[["Immune thrombocytopenia","Low","Prolonged","Normal","Normal","Large platelets; otherwise normal smear"],["Platelet function defect (aspirin, uremia)","Normal","Prolonged","Normal","Normal","Abnormal aggregation studies"],["Von Willebrand disease","Normal","Prolonged","Normal","Normal or prolonged","Low vWF activity; factor VIII may be low"],["Hemophilia A or B","Normal","Normal","Normal","Prolonged","Corrects with mixing; low VIII or IX"],["Acquired factor VIII inhibitor","Normal","Normal","Normal","Prolonged","Does not correct, especially after incubation"],["Vitamin K deficiency or warfarin","Normal","Normal","Prolonged first","Normal or prolonged","Factor VII falls first"],["Liver disease","Normal or low","Normal or prolonged","Prolonged","Normal or prolonged","Factor VIII normal or high"],["Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Low","Prolonged","Prolonged","Prolonged","Low fibrinogen, high D-dimer, schistocytes"]],"caption":"Platelet disorders change the count or bleeding time; factor disorders change the PT or PTT."},{"type":"p","text":"In von Willebrand disease the PTT may be prolonged because vWF carries factor VIII and protects it from clearance, so low vWF lowers factor VIII. In liver disease, factor VIII is normal or high because it is made largely by endothelial cells, including liver sinusoidal endothelium, whereas the other factors are made by hepatocytes. Normal or high factor VIII therefore helps separate liver disease from DIC, where factor VIII is consumed."},{"type":"p","text":"In the mixing study, patient plasma is mixed 1:1 with normal plasma and the prolonged test is repeated. Any deficient factor rises to at least about 50% of normal, enough to normalize the time, so correction means deficiency. An inhibitor also acts on the factors in the added plasma, so the time stays long. Acquired factor VIII antibodies act slowly, so the mix may correct at first and prolong again after incubation at 37 °C (98.6 °F) for 1 to 2 hours. Lupus anticoagulant, an antiphospholipid antibody, prolongs the PTT in the tube by interfering with phospholipid-dependent reactions, yet causes thrombosis rather than bleeding in the body. Heparin also prevents correction."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a mixing study","text":"A 30-year-old woman with two miscarriages has an isolated PTT of 52 s (reference about 25 to 35 s), normal PT, and normal platelets. The 1:1 mix gives 50 s immediately. Step 1: the PT is normal, so VII and the common pathway are intact; the problem lies in XII, XI, IX, or VIII. Step 2: if a factor were missing, normal plasma would restore it to about 50% and the PTT would correct. It did not, so an inhibitor is present. Step 3: no bleeding, pregnancy loss, and immediate failure to correct favor lupus anticoagulant over a factor VIII antibody, which bleeds and often prolongs only after incubation. Heparin must be excluded."},{"type":"flow","title":"How DIC produces both clotting and bleeding","steps":["Sepsis, obstetric emergency, cancer, or trauma releases tissue factor","Widespread thrombin generation forms microthrombi","Platelets, fibrinogen, V, and VIII are consumed","Plasmin digests the fibrin, raising D-dimer","Low platelets and factors prolong PT and PTT","Bleeding from venipuncture sites and mucosa; organ ischemia"],"caption":"Fibrin strands in small vessels shear red cells into schistocytes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-04-01-C06","obj":"HE-4.6","title":"Virchow triad and venous thrombosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A thrombus is a clot that forms inside an intact vessel during life. Virchow triad names the three conditions that favor it: stasis or turbulence of blood flow, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability. Any one can be enough, but most thromboses involve two or three. Arterial thrombi form under high shear, usually on a ruptured atherosclerotic plaque, and are rich in platelets (pale). Venous thrombi form in slow flow, are rich in red cells and fibrin (red), and are driven mainly by stasis and hypercoagulability."},{"type":"p","text":"Stasis matters because flowing blood dilutes activated factors and brings fresh inhibitors, and because slow flow lets platelets contact the endothelium. Deep vein thrombi usually begin in the valve pockets of the calf veins, where flow is slowest, and the endothelium there becomes hypoxic and expresses adhesion molecules and procoagulant activity. Immobility, long travel, and paralysis remove the calf muscle pump; heart failure and atrial fibrillation slow flow in the venous system and atria. Endothelial injury exposes subendothelial collagen and tissue factor and removes thrombomodulin, heparan sulfate, prostacyclin, and nitric oxide."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for venous thrombosis by arm of the triad","cols":["Risk factor","Stasis","Endothelial injury","Hypercoagulability"],"rows":[["Immobility, long travel, paralysis","Loss of calf muscle pump","No","No"],["Major surgery, especially hip and knee","Bed rest","Vessel damage","Tissue factor release"],["Pregnancy and postpartum","Uterus compresses pelvic veins; venodilation","Delivery","More fibrinogen, VII, VIII, vWF; less protein S"],["Estrogen therapy or combined oral contraceptives","No","No","More hepatic clotting factors; less protein S and antithrombin"],["Malignancy","Immobility, tumor compression","Tumor invasion, catheters","Tumor tissue factor and mucins"],["Heart failure","Low cardiac output, venous congestion","No","No"]],"caption":"Surgery, pregnancy, and malignancy act on more than one arm at once, which is why their risk is high."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypercoagulability means a shift in the balance between procoagulant and anticoagulant proteins. Estrogen raises hepatic synthesis of fibrinogen and factors VII, VIII, and X, and lowers protein S, producing an acquired resistance to activated protein C. Pregnancy adds these changes to stasis from the enlarged uterus and progesterone-related venodilation, and the risk peaks in the weeks after delivery. Tumors, especially pancreatic, lung, and gastrointestinal adenocarcinomas, express tissue factor and release mucins that activate clotting, which can cause migratory superficial thrombophlebitis (Trousseau syndrome)."},{"type":"table","title":"Common inherited and acquired thrombophilias","cols":["Condition","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Factor V Leiden","Factor Va resists cleavage by activated protein C; most common inherited thrombophilia in people of European descent"],["Prothrombin G20210A","Gene variant raises plasma prothrombin levels"],["Antithrombin, protein C, or protein S deficiency","Loss of a natural anticoagulant"],["Antiphospholipid syndrome (acquired)","Antibodies to phospholipid-binding proteins promote arterial and venous thrombosis and pregnancy loss"]],"caption":"Inherited thrombophilia mostly adds hypercoagulability; clots usually appear when a second trigger, such as surgery or estrogen, is added."},{"type":"example","title":"Counting the arms of the triad","text":"A 34-year-old woman with factor V Leiden takes a combined oral contraceptive and develops a swollen left calf two weeks after a cesarean delivery. Hypercoagulability comes from three sources: factor Va that resists activated protein C, estrogen raising clotting factors and lowering protein S, and the postpartum state. Stasis comes from post-operative bed rest and pelvic venous compression late in pregnancy. Endothelial injury comes from surgery on pelvic vessels. All three arms are active, and her inherited defect has met two acquired triggers at once. The likely thrombus began in a deep calf vein valve pocket and can extend proximally, which is the usual source of pulmonary emboli."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemostasis draws on every Part I discipline. Biochemistry supplies vitamin K dependent gamma-carboxylation, serine protease and serpin chemistry, and the COX-1 pathway that makes thromboxane A2. Anatomy and histology explain why the endothelium is anticoagulant while the subendothelium is procoagulant, and why venous valve pockets in the calf are where deep vein thrombi start. Physiology adds shear-dependent platelet adhesion and growth factor feedback, such as thrombopoietin cleared by the platelet mass and erythropoietin set by renal oxygen sensing. Pathology then reads each disease as a failure of one step: too little (hemophilia, von Willebrand disease), too much (factor V Leiden), or both at once (DIC)."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and microbiology enter through inflammation. Bacterial endotoxin induces tissue factor on monocytes and endothelium and suppresses thrombomodulin, which links sepsis to DIC, and IL-6 raises fibrinogen and thrombopoietin as acute phase responses. Antibodies can attack platelets (immune thrombocytopenia), factor VIII (acquired inhibitors), or phospholipid-binding proteins (antiphospholipid syndrome). The laboratory tests have limits: PT and PTT measure plasma clotting time in a tube, not bleeding risk in the body, so factor XII deficiency and lupus anticoagulant prolong the PTT without bleeding, and factor XIII deficiency bleeds with normal tests."}],"summary":["Committed progenitors depend on one dominant growth factor: EPO for red cells, TPO for megakaryocytes, G-CSF for neutrophils, GM-CSF for granulocytes and monocytes, and IL-5 for eosinophils; most signal through JAK-STAT.","EPO is regulated at its source by renal hypoxia and HIF, while free TPO is regulated by uptake on the platelet mass.","Primary hemostasis is vasoconstriction, adhesion (vWF bridging collagen to GpIb), activation (ADP, thromboxane A2 by COX-1), and aggregation (fibrinogen bridging GpIIb/IIIa).","In the body, TF-VIIa starts clotting, a trace of thrombin amplifies it, and tenase (IXa-VIIIa) and prothrombinase (Xa-Va) on platelets propagate it; XIIIa cross-links fibrin.","PT measures VII and the common pathway; PTT measures XII, XI, IX, VIII, and the common pathway; factor VII has the shortest half-life, so vitamin K deficiency prolongs the PT first.","Antithrombin inhibits thrombin and Xa (heparin accelerates it), activated protein C with protein S cleaves Va and VIIIa, and TFPI shuts down TF-VIIa once Xa forms.","Plasmin forms on fibrin from tPA and plasminogen; D-dimer comes only from cross-linked fibrin and is sensitive but not specific.","Platelet defects cause immediate mucocutaneous bleeding; factor defects cause delayed deep bleeding into joints and muscles.","A prolonged time that corrects with mixing means a deficiency; one that does not correct means an inhibitor such as lupus anticoagulant, a factor VIII antibody, or heparin.","Virchow triad is stasis, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability; surgery, pregnancy, and malignancy act on more than one arm at once."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Blood, section on hemostasis and fibrinolysis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins, section on vitamin K"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood Disorders"}]},"HE-5":{"unit":"HE-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-05-01","title":"Hemoglobin and Hematologic Biochemistry","subject":"Hematologic System (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the biochemistry that keeps red cells working and blood clotting where it should: heme synthesis and the porphyrias, hemoglobin structure and oxygen binding, methemoglobin and carbon monoxide, red cell energy and antioxidant pathways, vitamin B12 and folate, and vitamin K. Board questions usually describe a patient and a lab pattern and ask you to locate the block in a pathway, predict which way the oxygen dissociation curve moves, or name the missing vitamin or enzyme from a marker such as urine porphobilinogen or methylmalonic acid. If you can follow each pathway from substrate to product and say what accumulates when a step fails, you can reason through most of these questions.","prerequisites":["Glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, and the role of NAD+, NADH, and NADPH","Enzyme kinetics, allosteric regulation, and feedback repression","Erythropoiesis and red cell structure (HE-1, HE-3)","Partial pressure, PaO2, and gas exchange (PU-7, PU-8)","Overview of the coagulation cascade and platelet activation (HE-4)"],"connections":[{"to":"Blood gas transport (PU-8)","how":"The oxygen dissociation curve and its shifts determine how much of the oxygen loaded in the lungs is actually delivered to tissue."},{"to":"Diseases of blood cell lysis (HE-9-B)","how":"Pyruvate kinase and G6PD deficiency are the classic enzyme causes of hemolytic anemia, and their smear findings follow from the pathway defects taught here."},{"to":"Diseases of blood cell production (HE-9-A)","how":"Sideroblastic, lead-induced, and megaloblastic anemias are failures of heme synthesis or DNA synthesis in erythroid precursors."},{"to":"Clotting abnormalities (HE-9-C)","how":"Vitamin K deficiency and antagonism lower factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S, which explains the PT pattern and early hypercoagulability."},{"to":"Metabolic and nutritional neurological diseases (NE-20-D)","how":"Acute porphyria and subacute combined degeneration from B12 deficiency are biochemical causes of neuropathy and spinal cord disease."}],"terms":[{"term":"Porphyrin","def":"A ring of four pyrrole units joined by methene bridges; with iron in its center it forms heme."},{"term":"Porphyria","def":"A disorder caused by partial deficiency of a heme synthetic enzyme, in which intermediates before the block accumulate and cause neurovisceral or skin disease."},{"term":"Rate-limiting enzyme","def":"The slowest, most regulated step of a pathway, whose activity sets overall flux; in heme synthesis this is ALA synthase."},{"term":"Cooperativity","def":"The property by which O2 binding at one heme of hemoglobin raises the O2 affinity of the remaining hemes, producing a sigmoid binding curve."},{"term":"P50","def":"The PO2 at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated, about 26 to 27 mmHg for adult blood; a higher P50 means lower affinity."},{"term":"Bohr effect","def":"The decrease in hemoglobin O2 affinity caused by H+ and CO2, which favors O2 unloading in metabolically active tissue."},{"term":"Methemoglobin","def":"Hemoglobin whose heme iron has been oxidized from ferrous (Fe2+) to ferric (Fe3+), which cannot bind O2."},{"term":"Reduced glutathione (GSH)","def":"A thiol-containing tripeptide that donates electrons to glutathione peroxidase to detoxify hydrogen peroxide; it is kept reduced by NADPH."},{"term":"Folate trap","def":"Accumulation of folate as N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate when B12-dependent methionine synthase fails, starving thymidylate synthesis of usable folate."},{"term":"Gamma-carboxyglutamate (Gla)","def":"A glutamate residue carrying a second carboxyl group, added by vitamin K dependent carboxylation, which binds Ca2+ and lets clotting proteins attach to phospholipid membranes."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-05-01-C01","obj":"HE-5.1","title":"Heme synthesis and its regulation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Heme is iron held in the center of a porphyrin ring. It is the O2-binding group of hemoglobin and myoglobin and the redox center of cytochromes, including the cytochrome P450 enzymes. Nearly all nucleated cells make heme, but about 85% is made by erythroid precursors in the marrow and most of the rest by the liver. Mature red cells cannot make heme because they have lost their mitochondria, where the pathway begins and ends."},{"type":"p","text":"The first step, in the mitochondrial matrix, condenses glycine with succinyl-CoA to form delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA), releasing CO2. Its enzyme, ALA synthase, is rate limiting and requires pyridoxal phosphate, the active form of vitamin B6, which binds glycine as a Schiff base. Succinyl-CoA comes from the citric acid cycle, which is why the pathway starts inside the mitochondrion; each heme uses eight glycines and eight succinyl-CoA."},{"type":"p","text":"ALA moves to the cytosol, where ALA dehydratase, a zinc enzyme, joins two ALA into the single pyrrole porphobilinogen (PBG). Four PBG are linked into a linear chain, the chain is closed into a ring, and side chains are trimmed to form coproporphyrinogen III. This returns to the mitochondrion to be oxidized to protoporphyrin IX, and ferrochelatase, on the matrix side of the inner membrane, inserts ferrous iron (Fe2+). Uroporphyrinogen, coproporphyrinogen, and protoporphyrinogen are colorless porphyrinogens; when they oxidize to porphyrins, the rings absorb light, which matters in the porphyrias."},{"type":"table","title":"Steps of heme synthesis","cols":["Step","Enzyme","Site","Disorder when the step fails"],"rows":[["Glycine + succinyl-CoA to ALA","ALA synthase (B6 dependent, rate limiting)","Mitochondrion","X-linked sideroblastic anemia (ALAS2); B6 deficiency"],["2 ALA to porphobilinogen","ALA dehydratase (zinc enzyme)","Cytosol","Lead inhibition; ALA rises"],["4 PBG to hydroxymethylbilane","PBG deaminase","Cytosol","Acute intermittent porphyria"],["Hydroxymethylbilane to uroporphyrinogen III","Uroporphyrinogen III synthase","Cytosol","Congenital erythropoietic porphyria"],["Uroporphyrinogen III to coproporphyrinogen III","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Cytosol","Porphyria cutanea tarda"],["Coproporphyrinogen III to protoporphyrin IX","Coproporphyrinogen and protoporphyrinogen oxidases","Mitochondrion","Hereditary coproporphyria; variegate porphyria"],["Protoporphyrin IX + Fe2+ to heme","Ferrochelatase","Mitochondrion","Lead inhibition; erythropoietic protoporphyria"]],"caption":"The first enzyme and the last three are mitochondrial; the middle four are cytosolic. Intermediates before a deficient enzyme accumulate."},{"type":"p","text":"Regulation differs by tissue. In the liver, ALA synthase 1 (ALAS1) follows demand: free heme represses its synthesis and blocks its import into mitochondria, so production matches use, and glucose and insulin also suppress it, so fasting raises it. Drugs that induce cytochrome P450 enzymes create new apoproteins that take up heme, which lifts the repression, as shown below. In erythroid cells, ALA synthase 2 (ALAS2) is encoded on the X chromosome and is controlled mainly by iron supply through an iron-responsive element in its mRNA, so marrow heme output follows iron delivery."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Heme feedback on hepatic ALA synthase","steps":["Inducing drug raises cytochrome P450 apoprotein synthesis","New apoproteins take up free heme","Free heme pool in the hepatocyte falls","Repression of ALAS1 is lifted","ALA synthesis and pathway flux rise","Heme pool recovers and represses ALAS1 again"],"caption":"Negative feedback keeps hepatic heme supply matched to demand; the same loop drives attacks of acute porphyria when a downstream enzyme is deficient."},{"type":"p","text":"When erythroid heme synthesis fails, iron delivered to mitochondria cannot be used and accumulates in mitochondria that encircle the nucleus, seen as ringed sideroblasts on Prussian blue stain, and the anemia is usually microcytic. Causes include ALAS2 mutations, B6 deficiency (for example from isoniazid, which depletes pyridoxal phosphate), alcohol, copper deficiency, and lead. Lead displaces zinc from ALA dehydratase and inhibits ferrochelatase, so urine ALA rises and zinc is inserted into protoporphyrin in place of iron, raising zinc protoporphyrin. Lead also inhibits pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase, leaving undegraded ribosomal RNA that appears as basophilic stippling."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-05-01-C02","obj":"HE-5.2","title":"Acute intermittent porphyria and porphyria cutanea tarda","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In a porphyria, partial loss of one enzyme makes the intermediates before it accumulate, while heme output stays near normal at baseline. The intermediate decides the symptoms. ALA and PBG, formed before the ring closes, are neurotoxic but do not absorb light, so blocks at or before PBG deaminase cause neurovisceral attacks without skin disease. Porphyrinogens formed after ring closure oxidize to porphyrins, which absorb visible light strongly near 400 nm and are phototoxic, so later blocks cause skin disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Acute intermittent porphyria"},{"type":"p","text":"Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP) is an autosomal dominant deficiency of PBG deaminase (hydroxymethylbilane synthase) with about 50% residual activity, and most carriers never have symptoms. An attack occurs when hepatic ALAS1 is induced, so that increased flux meets the bottleneck and ALA and PBG pour out of the liver. Triggers include P450-inducing drugs such as barbiturates, alcohol, fasting or low carbohydrate intake, and progesterone, which explains attacks in the luteal phase and their rarity before puberty. How ALA and PBG injure nerves is not settled; direct ALA toxicity (ALA resembles GABA) and heme deficiency in neurons are both proposed."},{"type":"p","text":"Autonomic neuropathy causes severe, poorly localized abdominal pain with little tenderness, vomiting, constipation, tachycardia, and hypertension. Motor neuropathy can progress to weakness and respiratory failure, and anxiety, confusion, or psychosis are common. Hyponatremia is frequent, partly from inappropriate ADH secretion. Urine PBG is markedly raised during an attack, and urine darkens to red-brown on standing as PBG polymerizes and oxidizes."},{"type":"h","text":"Porphyria cutanea tarda"},{"type":"p","text":"Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT), the most common porphyria, results from reduced hepatic uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD) activity. Most cases are sporadic and the rest carry a heterozygous UROD mutation, but in both, disease appears only when hepatic activity falls below about 20% to 25% of normal. Iron drives that fall, because iron-dependent oxidation in hepatocytes turns uroporphyrinogen into a UROD inhibitor. Hepatitis C, alcohol, hemochromatosis (HFE) mutations, estrogens, HIV, and smoking all promote this."},{"type":"p","text":"Uroporphyrin and related porphyrins accumulate in liver, plasma, and skin. Light excites them in the dermis, and the reactive oxygen species formed damage the dermal-epidermal junction, causing fragile skin and subepidermal blisters on the backs of the hands and other sun-exposed areas that heal with scars and milia, along with facial hypertrichosis and hyperpigmentation. ALA and PBG are normal, so there are no neurovisceral attacks, and urine is red-brown and fluoresces pink-orange under ultraviolet (Wood) light. Variegate porphyria and hereditary coproporphyria block later steps but also raise ALA and PBG during attacks, so they can cause both blistering and acute attacks."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute intermittent porphyria versus porphyria cutanea tarda","cols":["Feature","Acute intermittent porphyria","Porphyria cutanea tarda"],"rows":[["Deficient enzyme","PBG deaminase (about 50% activity)","Hepatic uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (under about 25%)"],["Inheritance","Autosomal dominant, low penetrance","Mostly acquired; some heterozygous UROD mutations"],["What accumulates","ALA and PBG (not light absorbing)","Uroporphyrin and heptacarboxyl porphyrin"],["Nervous system","Abdominal pain, neuropathy, psychiatric symptoms, hyponatremia","Not affected"],["Skin","No photosensitivity","Blistering, fragility, milia, hypertrichosis"],["Key urine finding","High PBG; darkens on standing","High uroporphyrin; pink-orange fluorescence"],["Precipitants","P450-inducing drugs, alcohol, fasting, progesterone","Iron, hepatitis C, alcohol, estrogens, HIV, smoking"]],"caption":"Neurovisceral disease follows accumulation of ALA and PBG; skin disease follows accumulation of photoreactive porphyrins."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient in an attack of acute intermittent porphyria spends the afternoon in bright sun. Will blisters form?","explain":"No. The block is before ring closure, so the accumulating compounds are ALA and PBG, which do not absorb visible light. Phototoxicity requires porphyrins formed after uroporphyrinogen.","check":"The opening paragraph on which intermediates absorb light."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-05-01-C03","obj":"HE-5.3","title":"Hemoglobin structure, cooperative binding, and curve shifts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemoglobin is a tetramer of two alpha chains and two non-alpha chains. Each chain holds one heme whose ferrous iron is bound to a histidine of the globin (the proximal histidine) and can bind one O2, so a tetramer carries up to four O2, and each gram of hemoglobin binds about 1.34 mL O2 when fully saturated. The alpha genes lie on chromosome 16 and the beta-like genes (epsilon, gamma, delta, beta) on chromosome 11, switched on in developmental order."},{"type":"table","title":"Normal hemoglobins","cols":["Hemoglobin","Chains","Share of adult hemoglobin","Notes"],"rows":[["HbA","Two alpha, two beta","About 95% to 97%","Main adult form"],["HbA2","Two alpha, two delta","About 2% to 3.5%","Raised in beta thalassemia trait"],["HbF","Two alpha, two gamma","Under about 1%","About 60% to 80% at birth; replaced by HbA over the first 6 to 12 months; higher O2 affinity"]],"caption":"Every normal postnatal hemoglobin contains alpha chains, so alpha chain defects affect all three."},{"type":"p","text":"Deoxyhemoglobin is held in the tense (T) state by salt bridges between subunits and has low O2 affinity. When O2 binds, the iron moves into the plane of the heme ring and pulls the proximal histidine and its helix, breaking salt bridges and shifting the whole tetramer toward the relaxed (R) state, so each O2 bound raises the affinity of the remaining sites. This positive cooperativity gives a sigmoid curve. Hemoglobin is about 97% saturated at an arterial PO2 near 100 mmHg, about 75% at the mixed venous PO2 of 40 mmHg, and unloads steeply at the lower PO2 of active muscle. Myoglobin, a single chain, has a hyperbolic, high-affinity curve suited to storing O2 in muscle."},{"type":"p","text":"The P50, the PO2 at 50% saturation, is about 26 to 27 mmHg in adult blood. A right shift raises P50, lowering affinity and favoring unloading; a left shift lowers P50, favoring loading but holding O2 back from tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"2,3-Bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG), made in red cells from the glycolytic intermediate 1,3-BPG, binds positively charged residues in the central cavity between the two beta chains of deoxyhemoglobin and stabilizes the T state. It rises over hours to days in chronic hypoxia, such as at altitude, in chronic anemia, and in lung disease. H+ protonates histidine residues that form T-state salt bridges, and CO2 binds terminal amino groups as carbamino compounds, so acidic, CO2-rich tissue lowers affinity (the Bohr effect); heat also weakens binding. All of these rise in working muscle, so unloading increases where demand is greatest."},{"type":"p","text":"In the gamma chain, a serine replaces a positively charged histidine at the 2,3-BPG binding site, so HbF binds 2,3-BPG poorly, stays closer to the R state, and has a P50 of about 19 mmHg. Fetal blood can therefore take O2 from maternal HbA across the placenta. Carbon monoxide and methemoglobin shift the curve left by holding the remaining sites of a tetramer in the R state (next section)."},{"type":"table","title":"Shifts of the oxygen dissociation curve","cols":["Change","Direction","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Higher 2,3-BPG (altitude, chronic anemia)","Right","Stabilizes the T state"],["Higher H+ (lower pH)","Right","Stabilizes the T state (Bohr effect)"],["Higher CO2","Right","Carbamino groups stabilize the T state"],["Higher temperature (fever, exercise)","Right","O2 binding releases heat, so heat lowers affinity"],["HbF","Left","Gamma chains bind 2,3-BPG poorly"],["Carbon monoxide","Left","Holds remaining sites in the R state"],["Methemoglobin","Left","Holds remaining sites in the R state"],["Alkalosis, hypothermia, low 2,3-BPG (stored blood)","Left","Favor the R state"]],"caption":"Right shifts favor unloading in tissue; left shifts favor loading but impair delivery."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A climber spends two weeks at high altitude. Which way does 2,3-BPG move, which way does the curve shift, and why does this help?","explain":"2,3-BPG rises, shifting the curve to the right, so more O2 is released at a given tissue PO2. Respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation partly offsets the shift.","check":"The paragraph on 2,3-BPG, the Bohr effect, and the shift table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-05-01-C04","obj":"HE-5.4","title":"Methemoglobin and carbon monoxide: low O2 delivery with a normal PaO2","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Arterial O2 content is the O2 bound to hemoglobin (1.34 x Hb x SaO2) plus the O2 dissolved in plasma (0.003 x PaO2), in mL O2 per dL. With hemoglobin 15 g/dL and SaO2 98%, content is about 20 mL/dL, of which dissolved O2 is only 0.3 mL/dL. PaO2 measures only the dissolved fraction, so it reflects alveolar gas and lung exchange, not how much hemoglobin can carry. When hemoglobin is disabled and the lungs are normal, PaO2 stays normal while content and delivery fall."},{"type":"h","text":"Methemoglobin"},{"type":"p","text":"Methemoglobin contains ferric (Fe3+) heme, which cannot bind O2, and the oxidized subunits push the remaining ferrous subunits of the same tetramer toward the R state, so the curve shifts left and the O2 still carried is released less readily. Heme iron oxidizes spontaneously at a low rate each day, and NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase, using NADH from glycolysis, reduces it back, keeping methemoglobin at about 1% or less of total hemoglobin. An NADPH-dependent reductase normally contributes little."},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital methemoglobinemia is usually autosomal recessive cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency; when limited to red cells (type I) it causes lifelong cyanosis with few symptoms, and when generalized (type II) it also causes severe neurologic impairment. Acquired disease is far more common and occurs when an oxidant outpaces the reductase: nitrites (contaminated well water in infants, amyl nitrite), dapsone, benzocaine and other topical anesthetics, primaquine, and aniline dyes. Young infants are vulnerable because their reductase activity is low."},{"type":"p","text":"Cyanosis appears at about 1.5 g/dL methemoglobin (roughly 10%), far less than the 5 g/dL of deoxyhemoglobin that produces cyanosis in hypoxemia, because methemoglobin is dark. Blood is chocolate brown and does not redden in air, and headache and dyspnea appear above about 20% to 30%. Supplemental O2 raises PaO2 but does not correct the cyanosis, because the defect is in hemoglobin, not the lungs."},{"type":"h","text":"Carbon monoxide"},{"type":"p","text":"Carbon monoxide (CO), produced by incomplete combustion, binds heme about 200 to 250 times more avidly than O2, so a small inspired fraction forms large amounts of carboxyhemoglobin (COHb). This lowers content and, by holding the other sites of each tetramer in the R state, shifts the curve left and makes it less sigmoid. CO also binds cytochrome c oxidase, impairing mitochondrial O2 use. Patients are not cyanotic because COHb is red. COHb is normally under about 3% in nonsmokers and up to about 10% in heavy smokers."},{"type":"p","text":"Pulse oximeters compare light absorbance at two wavelengths (about 660 and 940 nm). COHb absorbs like oxyhemoglobin at 660 nm, so the reading is falsely normal. Methemoglobin absorbs both wavelengths about equally, which drives the reading toward about 85% whatever the true saturation. Blood gas analyzers that calculate saturation from PaO2 are also falsely normal; co-oximetry, which measures absorbance at several wavelengths, reports the true fractions."},{"type":"table","title":"Telling the causes of low O2 delivery apart","cols":["Condition","PaO2","True saturation (co-oximetry)","Pulse oximetry","O2 content","Curve"],"rows":[["Hypoxemia from lung disease","Low","Low","Low (accurate)","Low","Normal position"],["Anemia","Normal","Normal","Normal","Low","Normal or right (higher 2,3-BPG)"],["Carbon monoxide poisoning","Normal","Low","Falsely normal","Low","Left"],["Methemoglobinemia","Normal","Low","About 85%","Low","Left"]],"caption":"A normal PaO2 rules out a gas exchange problem, not a hemoglobin problem."},{"type":"example","title":"Why 50% COHb is worse than halving hemoglobin","text":"Normal content is about 20 mL/dL. Patient A has anemia with hemoglobin 7.5 g/dL: 1.34 x 7.5 x 0.98 + 0.3, about 10 mL/dL. Patient B has hemoglobin 15 g/dL but 50% COHb, leaving about half the sites for O2: 1.34 x 15 x 0.49 + 0.3, also about 10 mL/dL. The contents match, yet B is far sicker. In A, the remaining hemoglobin has a normal or right-shifted curve and unloads freely. In B, the left shift means tissue PO2 must fall much lower before the remaining O2 is released, and CO also inhibits cytochrome c oxidase."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-05-01-C05","obj":"HE-5.5","title":"Red cell energy and antioxidant metabolism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mature red cells have no nucleus, ribosomes, or mitochondria. Without mitochondria they cannot run the citric acid cycle, oxidative phosphorylation, or fatty acid oxidation, so they depend entirely on glucose, which enters through insulin-independent GLUT1 transporters. About 90% of that glucose passes through anaerobic glycolysis to lactate, and the remainder enters the pentose phosphate pathway. Reducing pyruvate to lactate regenerates the NAD+ that glycolysis needs to continue. Without ribosomes the cell cannot replace an unstable enzyme, so activity declines with cell age."},{"type":"table","title":"What red cell glucose metabolism provides","cols":["Product","Source","Used for"],"rows":[["ATP","Glycolysis","Ion pumps, membrane upkeep, glutathione synthesis"],["NADH","Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase","Cytochrome b5 reductase (methemoglobin reduction)"],["2,3-BPG","Bisphosphoglycerate mutase shunt from 1,3-BPG","Lowers O2 affinity; bypasses an ATP-producing step"],["NADPH","Pentose phosphate pathway (G6PD and 6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase)","Glutathione reductase"]],"caption":"Glycolysis supplies energy and NADH; the pentose phosphate pathway is the only red cell source of NADPH."},{"type":"p","text":"ATP keeps the cell deformable enough to pass through capillaries and splenic cords narrower than itself. The Na+/K+-ATPase holds volume, a Ca2+ pump keeps cytosolic Ca2+ low, and an ATP-dependent flippase keeps phosphatidylserine on the inner leaflet, where its exposure would mark the cell for removal by macrophages."},{"type":"p","text":"Pyruvate kinase deficiency, usually autosomal recessive, is the most common glycolytic enzyme defect causing hemolysis. ATP falls, the pumps fail, and cells lose K+ and water, becoming dehydrated, rigid, and spiculated; splenic macrophages remove them, so hemolysis is chronic and extravascular, with jaundice, splenomegaly, and pigment gallstones. Intermediates above the block accumulate, including 2,3-BPG, which shifts the curve right, so patients often tolerate their anemia better than the hemoglobin level suggests."},{"type":"p","text":"Red cells face constant oxidant stress because they carry O2 at high concentration and hemoglobin autoxidation releases superoxide, which becomes hydrogen peroxide. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), the rate-limiting enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway, and the next dehydrogenase each reduce NADP+ to NADPH. Glutathione reductase uses NADPH to keep glutathione reduced (GSH), and glutathione peroxidase, a selenium enzyme, uses GSH to reduce hydrogen peroxide to water."},{"type":"p","text":"G6PD deficiency is X-linked, so it is expressed mainly in males, and it is the most common human enzyme deficiency, frequent where malaria was endemic because it gives partial protection against Plasmodium falciparum. In the common African A- variant the enzyme is unstable, so only older cells are deficient and hemolysis tends to be self-limited; Mediterranean variants have very low activity in cells of all ages and cause more severe hemolysis, including favism. Between exposures most patients are not anemic."},{"type":"flow","title":"Oxidant hemolysis in G6PD deficiency","steps":["Oxidant stress: infection, fava beans, dapsone, primaquine, sulfonamides","Too little NADPH; reduced glutathione is depleted","Hydrogen peroxide oxidizes hemoglobin and membrane proteins","Denatured hemoglobin precipitates as Heinz bodies on the membrane","Damaged membranes rupture in vessels: intravascular hemolysis","Splenic macrophages remove Heinz bodies, leaving bite cells","Rigid damaged cells are destroyed: extravascular hemolysis"],"caption":"Hemolysis typically begins 1 to 3 days after exposure, with hemoglobinuria, back pain, and jaundice."},{"type":"p","text":"Heinz bodies need a supravital stain, while bite cells appear on a routine smear. Because the most deficient old cells lyse first, G6PD activity measured just after an episode can be falsely normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Pyruvate kinase versus G6PD deficiency","cols":["Feature","Pyruvate kinase deficiency","G6PD deficiency"],"rows":[["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","X-linked"],["Pathway","Glycolysis","Pentose phosphate pathway"],["Product lost","ATP","NADPH, then reduced glutathione"],["Site of hemolysis","Extravascular","Intravascular and extravascular"],["Smear","Spiculated, dehydrated cells","Heinz bodies, bite cells, blister cells"],["2,3-BPG and curve","Raised; right shift","Not characteristically changed"]],"caption":"The lost product predicts the pattern of hemolysis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does G6PD deficiency damage red cells but rarely harm hepatocytes, which carry the same mutant enzyme?","explain":"Hepatocytes have other NADPH sources, such as malic enzyme, and can make new G6PD. Red cells have neither, so an unstable enzyme leaves them without NADPH.","check":"The opening paragraph and the products table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-05-01-C06","obj":"HE-5.6","title":"Vitamin B12 and folate in DNA synthesis and methylation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Folate works in cells as tetrahydrofolate (THF), which accepts one-carbon units, mainly from serine, and donates them for purine synthesis, thymidylate synthesis, and methionine regeneration. Thymidylate synthase transfers a carbon from N5,N10-methylene-THF to deoxyuridine monophosphate (dUMP), forming deoxythymidine monophosphate (dTMP); the folate leaves as dihydrofolate (DHF), which dihydrofolate reductase must reduce back to THF. Methotrexate blocks dihydrofolate reductase and 5-fluorouracil blocks thymidylate synthase, each stopping DNA synthesis."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Folate in thymidylate synthesis","steps":["THF accepts a carbon from serine, forming methylene-THF","Thymidylate synthase converts dUMP to dTMP","Folate is released as dihydrofolate (DHF)","Dihydrofolate reductase uses NADPH to regenerate THF"],"caption":"Each dTMP made consumes one methylene-THF, so DNA synthesis needs continuous folate recycling."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is a cofactor for only two human enzymes. Methionine synthase, using methylcobalamin, transfers the methyl group of N5-methyl-THF to homocysteine, forming methionine and freeing THF; methionine then forms S-adenosylmethionine (SAM), the main methyl donor. Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, using adenosylcobalamin in mitochondria, converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, the route by which propionyl-CoA from odd-chain fatty acids and from valine, isoleucine, methionine, and threonine enters the citric acid cycle."},{"type":"p","text":"Methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR) converts methylene-THF to methyl-THF in an effectively irreversible reaction, and methionine synthase is the only enzyme that removes that methyl group. In B12 deficiency, folate therefore accumulates as unusable methyl-THF, as shown below. This explains why the two deficiencies produce identical blood findings, and why large amounts of folic acid, which dihydrofolate reductase converts to THF without passing through methyl-THF, can correct the anemia of B12 deficiency while neurologic damage continues."},{"type":"flow","title":"The folate trap in B12 deficiency","steps":["B12 deficiency halts methionine synthase","Methyl-THF cannot give up its methyl group","Folate is trapped as methyl-THF; homocysteine rises","Methylene-THF for thymidylate synthase runs short","dTMP falls; uracil enters DNA and DNA synthesis stalls","Megaloblastic, ineffective erythropoiesis"],"caption":"Total folate may be normal or high, yet the cell cannot use it for DNA synthesis."},{"type":"p","text":"When dTMP is scarce, DNA polymerase inserts uracil in place of thymine, and repeated repair fragments the DNA. Replication stalls while RNA and protein synthesis continue, so nuclei stay large and immature while the cytoplasm matures, the asynchrony that defines megaloblasts. Many precursors die in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis), raising LDH and indirect bilirubin. Blood shows macrocytes (MCV over 100 fL), hypersegmented neutrophils, and often low white cell and platelet counts, because every dividing marrow line is affected."},{"type":"p","text":"Only B12 deficiency damages myelin, causing subacute combined degeneration: loss of the dorsal columns (vibration and position sense, positive Romberg sign) and lateral corticospinal tracts (spastic weakness, extensor plantar responses), with peripheral neuropathy and cognitive change, sometimes without anemia. The mechanism is not settled; reduced SAM for myelin methylation and abnormal fatty acids built from excess methylmalonyl-CoA and propionyl-CoA are both proposed. Liver B12 stores last about 3 to 5 years, while folate stores last about 3 to 4 months, so folate deficiency appears much sooner after intake falls."},{"type":"table","title":"Laboratory distinction of B12 and folate deficiency","cols":["Finding","B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency"],"rows":[["Macrocytosis and hypersegmented neutrophils","Yes","Yes"],["Homocysteine (normal about 5 to 15 µmol/L)","High","High"],["Methylmalonic acid (normal under about 0.4 µmol/L)","High","Normal"],["Neurologic disease","Yes (dorsal columns, corticospinal tracts, nerves)","No"],["Time to deficiency after intake stops","Years","Months"]],"caption":"Homocysteine tracks methionine synthase (both vitamins); methylmalonic acid tracks the mutase (B12 only)."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading homocysteine and methylmalonic acid","text":"A patient has MCV 112 fL, hypersegmented neutrophils, and a borderline serum B12. Homocysteine is 28 µmol/L and methylmalonic acid is 1.1 µmol/L. Step 1: a raised homocysteine shows methionine synthase is failing, which fits either deficiency. Step 2: a raised methylmalonic acid shows the mutase is also failing, and only B12 serves the mutase, so this is B12 deficiency. Step 3: had methylmalonic acid been normal with high homocysteine, folate deficiency would be likely. Kidney failure raises both markers, so renal function must be known first."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-05-01-C07","obj":"HE-5.7","title":"Vitamin K dependent gamma-carboxylation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin K occurs as phylloquinone (K1) from green plants and menaquinones (K2) made by intestinal bacteria. It is fat soluble, so its absorption needs bile and pancreatic lipase, and body stores are small. In the endoplasmic reticulum of hepatocytes, gamma-glutamyl carboxylase adds a second carboxyl group to the gamma carbon of specific glutamate residues near the amino terminus of factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S, forming gamma-carboxyglutamate (Gla). The reaction uses reduced vitamin K (the hydroquinone), O2, and CO2, and leaves vitamin K as its 2,3-epoxide."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The vitamin K cycle","steps":["Reduced vitamin K (hydroquinone) binds gamma-glutamyl carboxylase","Glutamate residues become Gla; vitamin K becomes epoxide","VKORC1 reduces the epoxide to vitamin K quinone","The quinone is reduced back to the hydroquinone"],"caption":"Vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1) lets each vitamin K molecule be reused many times; warfarin inhibits VKORC1."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the vitamin is recycled, inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1), as warfarin does, depletes reduced vitamin K just as dietary lack does. The liver then secretes undercarboxylated factors (proteins induced by vitamin K absence, or PIVKAs) that circulate but are nearly inactive."},{"type":"p","text":"Calcium is the reason Gla matters. The two adjacent carboxyl groups of each Gla residue chelate Ca2+, and a Gla domain holds about 9 to 12 of them. Bound Ca2+ folds the domain so that hydrophobic residues emerge and Ca2+ bridges the negative carboxylates to the negatively charged head groups of phosphatidylserine. Resting platelets keep phosphatidylserine on the inner leaflet with an ATP-dependent flippase; activation by thrombin and collagen opens a Ca2+-activated scramblase that exposes it on the outer surface. The tenase (IXa with VIIIa) and prothrombinase (Xa with Va) complexes therefore assemble on activated platelets at the injury, concentrating enzyme and substrate and speeding thrombin generation by several orders of magnitude while keeping clotting local."},{"type":"p","text":"For the same reason, citrate and EDTA in blood tubes chelate Ca2+ and prevent clotting, and the PT and aPTT add back Ca2+ and phospholipid."},{"type":"table","title":"Vitamin K dependent clotting proteins","cols":["Protein","Role","Approximate plasma half-life"],"rows":[["Factor VII","With tissue factor, starts the extrinsic pathway","4 to 6 hours"],["Protein C","Activated protein C inactivates Va and VIIIa","About 8 hours"],["Factor IX","Forms tenase with VIIIa","About 24 hours"],["Factor X","Forms prothrombinase with Va","About 40 hours"],["Protein S","Cofactor for activated protein C","About 40 hours"],["Factor II (prothrombin)","Precursor of thrombin","About 60 to 72 hours"]],"caption":"Short half-lives fall first when carboxylation stops, so factor VII and protein C drop within a day."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the anticoagulant proteins C and S are also Gla proteins, loss of carboxylation lowers both sides of the balance, and the half-lives decide the net effect. The PT, which depends on factor VII, prolongs first, and the aPTT follows as IX, X, and II fall; platelet count and platelet function are normal. Early loss of protein C before factors II and X decline explains the brief hypercoagulable state, and occasional skin necrosis, when warfarin is started."},{"type":"p","text":"Deficiency occurs in newborns (poor placental transfer, a sterile gut, little vitamin K in breast milk, and an immature liver), with fat malabsorption (biliary obstruction, cystic fibrosis, celiac disease), and with prolonged broad-spectrum antibiotics combined with poor intake. Liver failure also prolongs the PT, but it lowers factor V, which is not vitamin K dependent; a low factor V points to hepatocellular disease rather than vitamin K deficiency."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"One theme runs through this chapter: the red cell is a cell without mitochondria or ribosomes, so its survival depends on a few pathways it cannot repair. Glycolysis supplies ATP for deformability and NADH for methemoglobin reduction, the pentose phosphate pathway supplies NADPH for glutathione, and the precursors that built the cell needed mitochondria for heme and folate and B12 for DNA. A single enzyme loss in any of these produces a recognizable pattern: microcytosis with ringed sideroblasts, a porphyria, chronic or episodic hemolysis, cyanosis with a normal PaO2, or megaloblastic change. Pathology links each pattern to its cause: lead in sideroblastic anemia, iron and hepatitis C in PCT, oxidant drugs in G6PD deficiency, and autoimmune gastritis in B12 deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"The same biochemistry reaches beyond blood. Hemoglobin affinity links to respiratory physiology and acid-base balance, B12 to myelin and spinal cord disease, and vitamin K to liver function, fat absorption, and gut flora. The limits matter as much as the rules: PaO2 and pulse oximetry can both look normal in serious poisoning, enzyme assays can be falsely normal after hemolysis, homocysteine and methylmalonic acid rise in renal failure, and reference values vary between laboratories, so each finding is read with the rest of the picture."}],"summary":["Heme synthesis starts and ends in mitochondria; ALA synthase (B6 dependent) is rate limiting, and free heme represses the hepatic form.","Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, raising ALA and zinc protoporphyrin; failed erythroid heme synthesis leaves ringed sideroblasts.","Blocks before ring closure (AIP, PBG deaminase) cause neurovisceral attacks with high urine PBG; blocks after it (PCT, UROD) cause photosensitive blistering.","AIP attacks follow ALAS1 induction by drugs, alcohol, fasting, or progesterone; PCT expression depends on iron, hepatitis C, and alcohol suppressing hepatic UROD.","Cooperative T to R transition gives the sigmoid curve; 2,3-BPG, H+, CO2, and heat shift it right, while HbF, CO, methemoglobin, and alkalosis shift it left.","Methemoglobin and carbon monoxide lower O2 content and left-shift the curve with a normal PaO2; pulse oximetry is falsely normal in CO and fixed near 85% in methemoglobinemia.","Red cells rely on glycolysis for ATP and NADH and on the pentose phosphate pathway for NADPH; pyruvate kinase deficiency causes chronic extravascular hemolysis with high 2,3-BPG.","G6PD deficiency leaves glutathione unreduced, so oxidant stress produces Heinz bodies, bite cells, and episodic hemolysis.","Folate supplies methylene-THF for thymidylate; B12 deficiency traps folate as methyl-THF, so both cause megaloblastic anemia with high homocysteine, but only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid and damages myelin.","Vitamin K dependent carboxylation creates Gla residues that bind Ca2+ to phosphatidylserine on activated platelets; factor VII and protein C fall first, so the PT prolongs first."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Heme metabolism and porphyrias; pentose phosphate pathway and NADPH; folic acid, vitamin B12, and vitamin K"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide in Blood and Tissue Fluids; Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia; Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Oxygen and carbon dioxide transport in blood"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red blood cell and bleeding disorders"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood disorders"}]},"HE-6":{"unit":"HE-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-06-01","title":"Hematologic Genetics","subject":"Hematologic System (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how single gene changes produce inherited blood disease and how acquired mutations and translocations drive blood cancers. It covers sickle hemoglobin, the alpha and beta thalassemias, inheritance patterns and offspring risk, the BCR-ABL1 and JAK2 V617F driver mutations, the classic lymphoma and leukemia translocations, Fanconi anemia, and inherited thrombophilia. Board questions give a mutation, a pedigree, a karyotype, or a set of findings and ask you to predict the protein defect, the disease severity, or a child's risk. If you can trace each lesion from gene to protein to cell behavior, you can answer most of them.","prerequisites":["Hemoglobin structure, the oxygen dissociation curve, the Bohr effect, and 2,3-BPG","Gene structure: promoter, exons, introns, splice sites, and untranslated regions","Mendelian inheritance and X inactivation (lyonization)","Cell cycle control by cyclins, CDKs, and RB, and apoptosis by the BCL2 family","The coagulation cascade and the protein C anticoagulant pathway"],"connections":[{"to":"Red cell disorders and hemolytic anemias","how":"Sickle cell disease, thalassemia, G6PD deficiency, and hereditary spherocytosis are the genetic causes of hemolysis and microcytosis seen in anemia questions."},{"to":"Hemostasis and coagulation","how":"Hemophilia, von Willebrand disease, factor V Leiden, and prothrombin G20210A are the main inherited causes of bleeding and clotting disorders."},{"to":"Myeloid and lymphoid neoplasms","how":"Driver mutations and translocations define CML, polycythemia vera, Burkitt, follicular, and mantle cell lymphoma, and acute promyelocytic leukemia."},{"to":"Immunology: B cell development","how":"RAG-mediated recombination and class switching at the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus create the double-strand breaks that lymphoma translocations exploit."},{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"The hypoxic, hypertonic, acidic renal medulla is where sickle trait shows itself, causing hematuria and loss of concentrating ability."}],"terms":[{"term":"Point mutation","def":"A change in a single nucleotide, such as the A to T change in codon 6 of beta globin that produces HbS."},{"term":"Polymerization delay time","def":"The lag between deoxygenation of HbS and fiber formation, which shortens very steeply as HbS concentration rises."},{"term":"Chain imbalance","def":"Unequal production of alpha and non-alpha globin chains, leaving unpaired chains that form abnormal tetramers or precipitate."},{"term":"Ineffective erythropoiesis","def":"Death of erythroid precursors inside the marrow before they mature, despite marrow expansion."},{"term":"Lyonization","def":"Random inactivation of one X chromosome in each female cell, which can leave a heterozygous carrier symptomatic when skewed."},{"term":"Philadelphia chromosome","def":"The shortened chromosome 22 produced by t(9;22), carrying the BCR-ABL1 fusion gene."},{"term":"Constitutive kinase","def":"A kinase that signals continuously without its normal activating ligand."},{"term":"Pseudokinase domain","def":"A kinase-like region without catalytic activity; in JAK2 (JH2) it holds the active kinase domain in check."},{"term":"Enhancer hijacking","def":"Overexpression of an intact gene after a translocation places it next to a strong foreign enhancer, such as the immunoglobulin heavy chain enhancer."},{"term":"Interstrand crosslink","def":"A covalent bond joining the two strands of DNA, which blocks strand separation for replication and transcription."},{"term":"Activated protein C resistance","def":"Slowed inactivation of factor Va by activated protein C, most often caused by factor V Leiden."},{"term":"Thrombophilia","def":"An inherited or acquired tendency to form venous or arterial thrombi."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-06-01-C01","obj":"HE-6.1","title":"Sickle hemoglobin: from one amino acid to vaso-occlusion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Adult hemoglobin A (HbA) is a tetramer of two alpha and two beta chains. The beta globin gene lies on chromosome 11. In sickle hemoglobin (HbS), a single A to T substitution changes codon 6 from GAG to GTG, replacing glutamate with valine. Glutamate is charged and faces the solvent; valine is hydrophobic. HbS therefore carries less negative charge and migrates more slowly than HbA toward the anode on alkaline electrophoresis."},{"type":"p","text":"The valine creates a sticky patch on the outside of the beta chain. In the deoxygenated (T) conformation, this patch fits a hydrophobic pocket, formed by phenylalanine 85 and leucine 88, on the beta chain of a neighboring tetramer. Deoxy HbS molecules then stack into long, 14-strand fibers that bend the cell into a rigid sickle. In the oxygenated (R) conformation the pocket is not exposed, so oxy HbS does not polymerize, and early reoxygenation can melt the polymer. Repeated cycles damage the membrane, cause K+ and water loss, and leave irreversibly sickled cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Whether a cell sickles is a race between two times. Polymer forms only after a delay, and that delay shortens very steeply as the concentration of deoxy HbS inside the cell rises. If the delay is longer than the time a cell spends in the capillaries, it reaches the lungs and reoxygenates before fibers form. Anything that raises the fraction of HbS that is deoxygenated, raises the HbS concentration, or slows transit tips the race toward sickling."},{"type":"table","title":"What changes sickling, and why","cols":["Factor","Effect","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Hypoxia (altitude, lung disease, exertion)","More","More HbS is in the deoxy, polymerizing form"],["Acidosis","More","Bohr effect lowers O2 affinity, so more HbS is deoxygenated at a given PO2"],["High 2,3-BPG","More","Stabilizes the deoxy (T) state"],["Dehydration, high MCHC","More","Higher intracellular HbS concentration shortens the delay time"],["Cold, infection, inflammation","More","Vasoconstriction, stasis, and endothelial adhesion slow transit"],["High HbF","Less","Gamma chains lack the contact sites, so HbF and alpha2-beta-S-gamma hybrids are excluded from the polymer and dilute HbS"],["Coinherited alpha thalassemia","Less","Lower MCHC reduces HbS concentration in each cell"]],"caption":"Every factor acts by changing how much deoxy HbS is present, how concentrated it is, or how long the cell stays in low-oxygen tissue."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The sickling loop","steps":["Red cell enters a low-oxygen capillary","Deoxy HbS polymerizes; cell sickles and stiffens","Sickled and adherent cells obstruct small vessels","Downstream tissue becomes hypoxic and acidotic","More HbS deoxygenates in nearby cells","Sickling spreads to more cells"],"caption":"Vaso-occlusion produces the hypoxia and acidosis that drive further sickling, which explains painful crises and infarcts."},{"type":"p","text":"Newborns with sickle cell disease (HbSS) are protected because about 60 to 80% of their hemoglobin at birth is HbF; symptoms such as dactylitis appear after about 6 months as gamma chain production falls. Sickled cells are removed early by the spleen and also rupture in the circulation, so patients have chronic hemolytic anemia with a high reticulocyte count, and released hemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, favoring vasoconstriction. Repeated splenic infarction ends in autosplenectomy."},{"type":"p","text":"In sickle trait (HbAS), each cell holds only about 40% HbS, so the delay time is long and sickling needs an extreme setting. The renal medulla provides one: low PO2, high osmolality that dehydrates red cells, and low pH. Sickling in the vasa recta causes hematuria, papillary necrosis, and loss of concentrating ability (isosthenuria)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child with HbSS becomes dehydrated during a febrile illness. Predict the effect on sickling, and explain why a high HbF level would have the opposite effect.","explain":"Dehydration raises MCHC, and fever and infection add acidosis and stasis, so the delay time shortens and sickling rises. HbF and HbS-HbF hybrid tetramers are excluded from the polymer and dilute HbS in each cell, so the delay time lengthens and fewer cells sickle.","check":"The table of factors and the delay time paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-06-01-C02","obj":"HE-6.2","title":"Alpha and beta thalassemia: gene dosage and chain imbalance","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thalassemias are quantitative defects: the globin made is normal, but too little of one chain is produced. Disease comes from two problems at once, too little hemoglobin (microcytic, hypochromic cells) and an excess of the partner chain, which forms abnormal tetramers or precipitates. Which chain is in excess determines whether the cells die in the marrow or in the circulation."},{"type":"h","text":"Alpha thalassemia: counting deleted genes"},{"type":"p","text":"Each chromosome 16 carries two alpha globin genes (HBA1 and HBA2), so a normal person has four. Alpha thalassemia is usually caused by large deletions removing whole genes, and severity steps up with each gene lost. Excess gamma chains in the fetus form Hb Barts (gamma4), and excess beta chains after birth form HbH (beta4). Both tetramers bind O2 with very high affinity and show no cooperativity or Bohr effect, so they deliver almost no oxygen to tissue."},{"type":"table","title":"Alpha globin genes deleted and the resulting disorder","cols":["Genes deleted","Genotype","Disorder","Findings"],"rows":[["1 of 4","-α/αα","Silent carrier","Normal blood count; detected only by DNA testing"],["2 of 4","--/αα (cis) or -α/-α (trans)","Alpha thalassemia trait","Mild microcytosis, little or no anemia, normal HbA2"],["3 of 4","--/-α","HbH disease","Moderate hemolytic anemia, splenomegaly, HbH inclusions on supravital stain"],["4 of 4","--/--","Hb Barts hydrops fetalis","Severe fetal hypoxia, heart failure, and edema; usually fatal in utero or at birth"]],"caption":"Severity rises with each gene lost. The cis pattern is common in Southeast Asian ancestry and can pass a chromosome with no alpha genes to a child; the trans pattern, common in African ancestry, cannot."},{"type":"p","text":"Because beta4 tetramers are soluble, HbH disease is mainly a hemolytic disorder: HbH oxidizes and precipitates in older red cells, which the spleen removes. Hydropic fetuses survive into the third trimester only because embryonic Hb Portland (zeta2-gamma2) needs no alpha chains."},{"type":"h","text":"Beta thalassemia: point mutations and precipitating alpha chains"},{"type":"p","text":"There is one beta globin gene per chromosome 11, and beta thalassemia is usually caused by point mutations rather than deletions. Promoter mutations reduce transcription, splice site mutations prevent correct removal of introns or create aberrant splice sites, and nonsense or frameshift mutations produce unstable mRNA or truncated chain. An allele that makes some beta chain is beta+; one that makes none is beta0."},{"type":"p","text":"Beta thalassemia minor (one abnormal allele) causes microcytosis with mild or no anemia, a normal or high red cell count, and HbA2 (alpha2-delta2) above about 3.5% (normal about 2 to 3.5%), because the delta chains pair with the surplus alpha chains. Beta thalassemia major (both alleles severely affected) appears at about 6 to 9 months, when the switch from gamma to beta chains removes the partner that kept alpha chains paired before birth; HbF stays high because gamma chains still pair with alpha chains."},{"type":"flow","title":"How beta thalassemia major damages the marrow and beyond","steps":["Little or no beta chain is made","Free alpha chains precipitate in erythroid precursors","Membrane damage kills precursors in the marrow","Severe anemia drives erythropoietin and marrow expansion","Skull and facial bone widening; extramedullary hematopoiesis","Suppressed hepcidin increases iron absorption and overload"],"caption":"Free alpha chains are insoluble, so beta thalassemia major is dominated by ineffective erythropoiesis, whereas soluble HbH in alpha thalassemia mainly causes hemolysis."},{"type":"example","title":"Can two parents with alpha thalassemia trait have a hydropic fetus?","text":"Both parents have mild microcytosis, normal iron studies, and normal HbA2, so each has two deleted alpha genes. If both carry the cis pattern (--/αα), each passes a --/ chromosome half the time, so each pregnancy has a 1 in 4 chance of --/-- (Hb Barts hydrops fetalis). If either parent has the trans pattern (-α/-α), that parent always passes a chromosome with one gene, so no child can lack all four genes; the worst outcome is HbH disease (--/-α), possible only if the other parent is cis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-06-01-C03","obj":"HE-6.3","title":"Inheritance patterns and offspring risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The inheritance pattern of a blood disorder follows from where the gene lies and how much normal protein a person needs. Genes on the X chromosome give X-linked patterns. Autosomal disorders are recessive when one normal copy makes enough functional product, and dominant when half the normal amount is not enough or a mutant protein interferes with the normal one (dominant negative effect)."},{"type":"table","title":"Inheritance of the major hereditary blood disorders","cols":["Disorder","Gene and product","Inheritance","Why this pattern"],"rows":[["Hemophilia A","F8, factor VIII (Xq28)","X-linked recessive","Males are hemizygous; about one third of cases are new mutations"],["Hemophilia B","F9, factor IX (Xq27)","X-linked recessive","Same as hemophilia A; the two are clinically indistinguishable"],["G6PD deficiency","G6PD (Xq28)","X-linked recessive","Heterozygous females are red cell mosaics from lyonization"],["Sickle cell disease","HBB, beta globin (chromosome 11)","Autosomal recessive","One normal allele gives enough HbA to prevent disease"],["Alpha and beta thalassemia","HBA1 and HBA2 (16); HBB (11)","Autosomal recessive","Severity depends on the number of affected genes"],["Hereditary spherocytosis (most)","Ankyrin, spectrin, band 3, protein 4.2","Autosomal dominant (about 75%)","Half the normal membrane protein cannot hold the skeleton together"],["von Willebrand disease (types 1 and 2)","VWF (chromosome 12)","Autosomal dominant","Partial deficiency or mutant subunits disrupt multimers; type 3 is autosomal recessive"]],"caption":"Exceptions: some hereditary spherocytosis is autosomal recessive or de novo, and type 3 and type 2N von Willebrand disease are recessive."},{"type":"p","text":"For an X-linked recessive disorder, a carrier mother passes the mutant X to half her children, so each son has a 50% chance of being affected and each daughter a 50% chance of being a carrier. An affected father passes his X to every daughter and his Y to every son, so all daughters are obligate carriers and no son is affected; male to male transmission excludes X linkage. Carrier females usually have enough normal protein, but skewed X inactivation can leave a carrier with low factor VIII or a G6PD-deficient red cell population."},{"type":"p","text":"For an autosomal recessive disorder, two carrier parents have a 25% chance with each pregnancy of an affected child, 50% of a carrier, and 25% of a noncarrier. An unaffected sibling of an affected child cannot be the affected genotype, so the chance that the sibling is a carrier is 2 in 3, not 1 in 2. For an autosomal dominant disorder, each child of a heterozygous parent has a 50% risk regardless of sex, although variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance, marked in type 1 von Willebrand disease, mean an affected relative may have few symptoms."},{"type":"example","title":"Offspring risk for sickle cell disease","text":"A healthy woman's brother has sickle cell disease, so both her parents are carriers. She is unaffected, which removes the HbSS possibility, leaving AA, AS, and SA in equal proportion: her chance of being a carrier is 2/3. Her partner has documented sickle trait (carrier probability 1). An affected child requires both parents to be carriers and both to pass S: 2/3 x 1 x 1/4 = 1/6. If her own testing shows trait, the risk becomes 1 x 1 x 1/4 = 1/4."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with hemophilia A and a woman who is not a carrier have a son and a daughter. What is each child's risk of hemophilia, and what is the risk for the daughter's future sons?","explain":"Neither child is affected: the son receives his father's Y, and the daughter receives his mutant X and is an obligate carrier. Each of the daughter's sons has a 50% risk.","check":"The X-linked paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-06-01-C04","obj":"HE-6.4","title":"Driver kinases in myeloproliferative neoplasms: BCR-ABL1 and JAK2 V617F","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal blood cell production is matched to demand because growth factor receptors signal through tyrosine kinases only when their ligand binds. Myeloproliferative neoplasms arise when an acquired mutation in a hematopoietic stem cell leaves one of these kinases switched on. Because the mutation is in a stem cell, all myeloid lines can expand, and the cells still mature, unlike acute leukemia."},{"type":"h","text":"CML and the Philadelphia chromosome"},{"type":"p","text":"In chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), a reciprocal translocation, t(9;22)(q34;q11), moves the ABL1 gene from chromosome 9 into the BCR gene on chromosome 22. The shortened chromosome 22 is the Philadelphia chromosome, and it carries a BCR-ABL1 fusion gene that in CML usually encodes a 210 kDa protein. ABL1 is a normally tightly regulated tyrosine kinase. The BCR portion contributes a coiled-coil domain that makes the fusion protein oligomerize, so the kinase domains phosphorylate one another and stay active without any signal."},{"type":"flow","title":"From t(9;22) to CML","steps":["t(9;22) joins BCR to ABL1 in a stem cell","BCR coiled-coil oligomerizes the fusion protein","ABL1 kinase autophosphorylates and stays active","RAS-MAPK, PI3K-AKT, and STAT5 signal continuously","Growth factor independent proliferation and less apoptosis","Granulocytes at all stages expand; marrow and spleen enlarge"],"caption":"Additional mutations later block differentiation and cause blast crisis."},{"type":"p","text":"The blood shows marked leukocytosis with granulocytes at every stage of maturation, prominent basophils and eosinophils, often thrombocytosis, and splenomegaly from extramedullary hematopoiesis. Low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase and the BCR-ABL1 fusion separate CML from a reactive leukemoid reaction. The disease depends on this single kinase, which is why an ATP-site inhibitor such as imatinib suppresses the clone; the same fusion, usually as a smaller 190 kDa protein, occurs in some acute lymphoblastic leukemias."},{"type":"h","text":"Polycythemia vera and JAK2 V617F"},{"type":"p","text":"JAK2 is the cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase attached to the erythropoietin, thrombopoietin (MPL), and G-CSF receptors. It has an active kinase domain (JH1) and an inactive pseudokinase domain (JH2) that restrains it. The V617F mutation, a valine to phenylalanine change in JH2, removes this restraint, so JAK2 phosphorylates STAT5 and activates downstream pathways with little or no erythropoietin. Erythroid progenitors become independent of, or hypersensitive to, the hormone."},{"type":"p","text":"JAK2 V617F is present in about 95% of polycythemia vera, and most of the rest carry JAK2 exon 12 mutations; it is also found in about half of essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis. Red cell mass rises, often with granulocytes and platelets, while normal oxygen sensing in the kidney suppresses erythropoietin. A low serum erythropoietin therefore separates polycythemia vera from secondary polycythemia caused by hypoxia or an erythropoietin-secreting tumor. Increased blood viscosity and platelet activation cause thrombosis, headache, and erythromelalgia; basophil and mast cell histamine causes pruritus after warm bathing; and high cell turnover raises uric acid."},{"type":"table","title":"CML and polycythemia vera compared","cols":["Feature","CML","Polycythemia vera"],"rows":[["Lesion","t(9;22) BCR-ABL1 fusion","JAK2 V617F point mutation"],["Kinase","ABL1, activated by BCR oligomerization","JAK2, released from JH2 autoinhibition"],["Dominant line","Granulocytes at all stages, basophilia","Red cells, often platelets and granulocytes"],["Hormone level","Not a defining feature","Erythropoietin low"],["Typical complications","Blast crisis","Thrombosis; late myelofibrosis or AML"]],"caption":"Both are clonal stem cell disorders in which a constitutive kinase replaces growth factor control."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-06-01-C05","obj":"HE-6.5","title":"Translocations that deregulate MYC, BCL2, cyclin D1, and RARA","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"B cells deliberately break their own DNA. RAG enzymes cut and rejoin V, D, and J segments, and activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) starts the DNA lesions of class switching and somatic hypermutation. When these breaks are joined to a break on another chromosome, a translocation results. Many lymphoma translocations therefore involve the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus (IGH) on chromosome 14q32."},{"type":"p","text":"Translocations act in two ways. In enhancer hijacking, an intact gene moves next to the IGH enhancer, which is highly active in B cells, and is overexpressed; the protein itself is normal. In a gene fusion, the breaks fall within two genes and create a new chimeric protein, as with BCR-ABL1 and PML-RARA."},{"type":"table","title":"Classic translocations and their consequences","cols":["Translocation","Tumor","Gene and mechanism","Normal role of the gene","Behavior"],"rows":[["t(8;14)(q24;q32)","Burkitt lymphoma","MYC driven by IGH enhancer","Transcription factor for growth, ribosome synthesis, and glycolysis","Very fast proliferation, nearly 100% of cells cycling; starry sky from macrophages clearing apoptotic cells"],["t(14;18)(q32;q21)","Follicular lymphoma","BCL2 driven by IGH enhancer","Blocks mitochondrial apoptosis","Cells accumulate slowly; indolent; BCL2 protein in germinal centers, unlike reactive follicles"],["t(11;14)(q13;q32)","Mantle cell lymphoma","CCND1 (cyclin D1) driven by IGH enhancer","Activates CDK4 and CDK6 to phosphorylate RB","Cells pass the G1 checkpoint; usually CD5 positive"],["t(15;17)(q24;q21)","Acute promyelocytic leukemia","PML-RARA fusion protein","RARA is the retinoic acid receptor that drives myeloid differentiation genes","Maturation arrest at promyelocytes; Auer rods; DIC"]],"caption":"Three are enhancer hijacking in B cells; the fourth is a fusion protein in a myeloid cell. In Burkitt variants, MYC joins the kappa (chromosome 2) or lambda (chromosome 22) light chain locus instead."},{"type":"p","text":"The three B cell lymphomas show how gene function sets pace. MYC drives cells into continuous growth and division, so Burkitt lymphoma doubles rapidly. BCL2 does not increase division at all; it prevents the apoptosis that normally removes germinal center B cells, so follicular lymphoma grows slowly by accumulation, and further mutations, often in MYC, can transform it to an aggressive lymphoma. Cyclin D1 sits between the two, pushing cells from G1 into S phase by inactivating RB and releasing E2F."},{"type":"p","text":"In acute promyelocytic leukemia, PML-RARA binds retinoic acid response elements and recruits corepressor and histone deacetylase complexes that physiologic retinoic acid cannot release, so promyelocytes cannot mature. Pharmacologic all-trans retinoic acid releases the corepressors and degrades the fusion protein, and the cells differentiate, which confirms the mechanism. The abnormal promyelocytes release tissue factor and express annexin II, which promotes fibrinolysis, explaining the disseminated intravascular coagulation that makes this leukemia dangerous at presentation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-06-01-C06","obj":"HE-6.6","title":"Fanconi anemia: failed crosslink repair and marrow failure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An interstrand crosslink covalently ties the two DNA strands together, so the helix cannot be unwound for replication or transcription. Crosslinks come from chemotherapy agents such as mitomycin C, but also from normal metabolism, mainly reactive aldehydes such as formaldehyde and acetaldehyde. The Fanconi anemia pathway repairs them."},{"type":"p","text":"More than 20 FANC genes are known. Most are inherited as autosomal recessive traits; FANCB is X-linked, and a rare form (RAD51) is dominant. When a replication fork stalls at a crosslink, the Fanconi core complex monoubiquitinates the FANCD2 and FANCI pair. This recruits nucleases that cut the crosslink out of one strand, translesion polymerases that copy past the lesion, and homologous recombination proteins that rebuild the broken fork. Several of these downstream proteins are breast cancer genes: FANCD1 is BRCA2 and FANCN is PALB2."},{"type":"flow","title":"From an unrepaired crosslink to marrow failure and cancer","steps":["Aldehydes create DNA interstrand crosslinks","Replication forks stall at the crosslink","No FANCD2 monoubiquitination, so repair fails","Breaks persist; p53 drives arrest or apoptosis","Hematopoietic stem cells are progressively lost","Surviving cells accumulate mutations: MDS and AML"],"caption":"The same genomic instability produces marrow failure, chromosome breakage, and cancer."},{"type":"p","text":"Hematopoietic stem cells divide throughout life and are especially sensitive to aldehyde damage, so the marrow fails progressively, usually in childhood. Macrocytosis and raised HbF often appear before the counts fall, reflecting stressed erythropoiesis, and pancytopenia follows. Defective repair during embryonic growth explains the developmental anomalies: short stature, absent or hypoplastic thumbs and radii (radial ray defects), cafe-au-lait spots and other pigment changes, microcephaly, and kidney malformations. A substantial minority of patients have no physical anomalies, so normal appearance does not exclude the disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis rests on the defect itself: cultured lymphocytes exposed to diepoxybutane or mitomycin C show many more chromosome breaks and radial figures than normal cells. Persistent DNA damage also selects for cells that escape control, so patients have a very high risk of myelodysplastic syndrome and AML, and of squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck and anogenital region in young adulthood."},{"type":"table","title":"Fanconi anemia and related congenital conditions","cols":["Condition","Defect","Blood findings","Limb findings"],"rows":[["Fanconi anemia","Interstrand crosslink repair","Progressive pancytopenia; MDS and AML","Absent or hypoplastic thumbs and radii"],["Thrombocytopenia absent radius (TAR)","RBM8A","Thrombocytopenia only, present at birth and improving with age","Absent radii with thumbs present"],["Diamond-Blackfan anemia","Ribosomal protein genes (often RPS19)","Pure red cell aplasia, macrocytic","Thumb anomalies, including triphalangeal thumbs"]],"caption":"Thumbs present with absent radii suggests TAR; a red cell-only failure suggests Diamond-Blackfan; failure of all lines with chromosome fragility indicates Fanconi anemia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-06-01-C07","obj":"HE-6.7","title":"Inherited thrombophilia: factor V Leiden and prothrombin G20210A","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thrombin generation is normally limited by its own feedback. Thrombin binds thrombomodulin on intact endothelium and activates protein C. Activated protein C (APC), with protein S as cofactor, cleaves and inactivates factor Va and factor VIIIa, shutting down the prothrombinase and tenase complexes. Inherited thrombophilia results either from a procoagulant that resists this control or is overproduced (gain of function) or from loss of an anticoagulant."},{"type":"p","text":"Factor V Leiden is a single base change (G1691A) that replaces arginine 506 with glutamine. Arg506 is the first site APC cleaves in factor Va, so Leiden Va is inactivated about ten times more slowly, and prothrombinase keeps making thrombin. Intact factor V cleaved by APC also helps APC inactivate factor VIIIa, a function the Leiden protein performs poorly. Factor V Leiden is the most common inherited thrombophilia in people of European ancestry, carried by about 5%. Heterozygotes have a several-fold increase in venous thrombosis risk, and homozygotes a much higher one."},{"type":"flow","title":"How factor V Leiden prolongs thrombin generation","steps":["Thrombin and thrombomodulin activate protein C","APC and protein S attack factor Va","Arg506 is replaced, so the first cleavage fails","Factor Va persists in the prothrombinase complex","More thrombin and fibrin form at each stimulus","Venous thrombosis risk rises several-fold"],"caption":"The protein clots normally; it simply resists being turned off."},{"type":"p","text":"Prothrombin G20210A is a G to A change in the 3' untranslated region of the prothrombin gene. It does not alter the protein. It makes processing of the mRNA 3' end more efficient, so more mRNA accumulates and plasma prothrombin is about 30% higher, giving more substrate for thrombin generation. It is found in about 2% of people of European ancestry and roughly doubles to triples venous thrombosis risk."},{"type":"table","title":"Inherited thrombophilias by mechanism","cols":["Condition","Mechanism","Laboratory clue"],"rows":[["Factor V Leiden","Gain of function: Va resists APC","Abnormal APC resistance assay; genetic test"],["Prothrombin G20210A","Gain of function: more prothrombin","Genetic test; PT normal"],["Protein C or S deficiency","Loss of anticoagulant: Va and VIIIa not inactivated","Low protein C or S activity"],["Antithrombin deficiency","Loss of anticoagulant: thrombin and Xa not neutralized","Low antithrombin activity"]],"caption":"All increase venous more than arterial thrombosis; the two gain-of-function variants are far more common than the deficiencies."},{"type":"p","text":"Both variants add procoagulant activity rather than remove a factor, so the PT and aPTT, which detect factor deficiencies, are normal. Thrombosis is mainly venous: deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, and less common sites such as cerebral veins. Because these variants add a constant risk, a clot usually needs a second hit from Virchow triad. Estrogen and pregnancy raise clotting factor levels and cause acquired APC resistance, and surgery and immobility add stasis and endothelial injury, so the risks multiply rather than add."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 24-year-old woman develops a deep vein thrombosis three months after starting an estrogen-containing contraceptive. Her PT and aPTT are normal, and adding APC to her plasma prolongs the aPTT much less than in control plasma. What is the likely defect, and why are routine clotting times normal?","explain":"Factor V Leiden: her factor Va resists APC, so APC cannot prolong clotting normally. The PT and aPTT measure how fast clotting proceeds when factors are present, and none is missing, so they are normal; estrogen added a second hit.","check":"The factor V Leiden paragraph and the final paragraph on second hits."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Molecular biology links every disorder in this chapter. A missense change alters protein shape (HbS, factor V Leiden, JAK2 V617F), deletions and splice or promoter mutations change gene dosage (alpha and beta thalassemia), a noncoding change raises expression (prothrombin G20210A), and translocations either hijack an enhancer or create a fusion kinase or transcription factor. Immunology explains why IGH is so often involved, since B cells break their own DNA during V(D)J recombination and class switching, and DNA repair biology connects Fanconi anemia to BRCA2 and to cancer predisposition."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology then explain the presentation. Sickling follows oxygen transport rules (Bohr effect, 2,3-BPG, transit time), thalassemia severity follows chain balance, and thrombophilia follows the protein C feedback loop and Virchow triad. Population genetics adds context: sickle trait, thalassemia, and G6PD deficiency are common where falciparum malaria was endemic because carriers resist severe malaria. Genotype does not fully predict phenotype, since HbF level, coinherited alpha thalassemia, X inactivation, penetrance, and environmental triggers all modify severity."}],"summary":["HbS has valine for glutamate at beta 6; the hydrophobic patch binds a neighboring deoxy tetramer, so deoxy HbS polymerizes and cells sickle.","Hypoxia, acidosis, high 2,3-BPG, dehydration, and stasis increase sickling; HbF and coinherited alpha thalassemia decrease it.","Sickle trait cells hold about 40% HbS and sickle mainly in the hypoxic, hypertonic, acidic renal medulla.","Alpha thalassemia is usually deletional: one gene lost is silent, two trait, three HbH disease (beta4), four Hb Barts hydrops (gamma4); cis deletions allow hydrops in offspring.","Beta thalassemia is usually caused by point mutations (promoter, splice site, nonsense); minor has HbA2 above about 3.5%, and major causes ineffective erythropoiesis from precipitated alpha chains after about 6 months.","Hemophilia A and B and G6PD deficiency are X-linked recessive; sickle cell disease and thalassemias autosomal recessive; most hereditary spherocytosis and von Willebrand disease autosomal dominant.","An unaffected sibling of a child with an autosomal recessive disease has a 2/3 chance of being a carrier.","BCR-ABL1 from t(9;22) is an oligomerized, constitutive tyrosine kinase in CML; JAK2 V617F releases JAK2 from its pseudokinase brake in polycythemia vera, with low erythropoietin.","IGH translocations overexpress MYC (Burkitt, rapid), BCL2 (follicular, blocked apoptosis), or cyclin D1 (mantle cell); PML-RARA blocks differentiation in APL.","Fanconi anemia is failed crosslink repair causing marrow failure, radial ray defects, chromosome fragility, and AML; factor V Leiden resists APC and prothrombin G20210A raises prothrombin, both with normal PT and aPTT."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Globular proteins: hemoglobinopathies"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia; Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood Disorders; Neoplasia"}]},"HE-7":{"unit":"HE-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-07-01","title":"Immune Responses in Hematologic Disease","subject":"Hematology (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains what happens when the immune system turns on blood cells, on transfused blood, or on the clotting system, and what happens when the spleen is lost. It covers autoimmune hemolysis and the Coombs test, hypersensitivity classification, transfusion reactions, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, antiphospholipid syndrome, Epstein-Barr virus in B cells, and infection after asplenia. Board questions give a smear, a laboratory pattern, or a time course and ask for the antibody, effector, or organ responsible. The recurring idea is that antibody class, temperature, antigen location, and Fc receptor engagement decide whether the result is hemolysis, thrombosis, or infection.","prerequisites":["Antibody classes, Fc receptors, and the classical complement pathway","Gell and Coombs hypersensitivity types I to IV","Red cell destruction: intravascular versus extravascular hemolysis and their laboratory markers","Platelet activation and the coagulation cascade, including the PT and PTT","B and T cell activation, T cell help, and class switching"],"connections":[{"to":"Hemolytic anemias (HE chapters on red cell disorders)","how":"Immune hemolysis must be separated from membrane, enzyme, and hemoglobin defects, and the direct Coombs test is the dividing line."},{"to":"Hemostasis and thrombosis","how":"HIT and antiphospholipid syndrome show that antibodies can drive clotting, not bleeding, by activating platelets, endothelium, and monocytes."},{"to":"Immunology (IM)","how":"Hypersensitivity types, complement, Fc receptors, and T-independent versus T-dependent antigens explain every disorder in this chapter."},{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae, EBV, and encapsulated bacteria link infection to cold agglutinins, lymphoma, and post-splenectomy sepsis."},{"to":"Renal and rheumatologic pathology","how":"SLE causes warm hemolysis and antiphospholipid antibodies, and cryoglobulinemia causes immune complex glomerulonephritis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Extravascular hemolysis","def":"Destruction of antibody- or complement-coated red cells by macrophages in the spleen and liver rather than in the circulation."},{"term":"Intravascular hemolysis","def":"Lysis of red cells inside vessels, usually by the complement membrane attack complex, releasing free hemoglobin into plasma."},{"term":"Direct Coombs test (direct antiglobulin test)","def":"A test that adds anti-human globulin to the patient's washed red cells to detect IgG or C3 already bound to them in the body."},{"term":"Indirect Coombs test (indirect antiglobulin test)","def":"A test that detects free antibody in the patient's serum by incubating it with reagent red cells before adding anti-human globulin."},{"term":"Cold agglutinin","def":"An IgM autoantibody that binds red cells at temperatures below body temperature and fixes complement."},{"term":"Alloantibody","def":"An antibody against an antigen from another individual of the same species, such as a transfused or fetal red cell antigen."},{"term":"Platelet factor 4 (PF4)","def":"A positively charged chemokine stored in platelet alpha granules that binds heparin and forms the antigen in HIT."},{"term":"Lupus anticoagulant","def":"An antiphospholipid antibody detected functionally by prolonging phospholipid-dependent clotting tests, despite causing thrombosis in vivo."},{"term":"Atypical lymphocyte","def":"A large activated CD8 T cell with abundant cytoplasm seen in the blood in infectious mononucleosis."},{"term":"T-independent antigen","def":"An antigen, such as a repetitive capsular polysaccharide, that activates B cells without T cell help and gives mainly IgM with little memory."},{"term":"Conjugate vaccine","def":"A vaccine that links a capsular polysaccharide to a carrier protein so that helper T cells drive class switching and memory."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-07-01-C01","obj":"HE-7.1","title":"Warm autoimmune hemolysis, cold agglutinin disease, and the Coombs test","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) the patient makes antibodies against their own red cell antigens. Both forms produce the general signs of hemolysis: anemia, reticulocytosis, raised lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) and unconjugated bilirubin, and low haptoglobin. They differ in antibody class, which sets the temperature at which the antibody binds."},{"type":"h","text":"Warm AIHA"},{"type":"p","text":"Warm autoantibodies are IgG and bind best at 37 °C (98.6 °F), often to Rh-related membrane proteins. IgG activates complement poorly, so the main effector is the splenic macrophage, whose Fc gamma receptors grip the IgG-coated cell and remove a piece of membrane. The cell reseals with less surface area for its volume and becomes a spherocyte, which is rigid and is trapped in the splenic cords on later passes. Hemolysis is therefore extravascular, and splenomegaly is common. About half of cases are idiopathic; the rest are secondary to autoimmune disease (SLE), lymphoid neoplasms (CLL), or drugs, which may act as haptens or induce true autoantibodies."},{"type":"h","text":"Cold agglutinin disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Cold agglutinins are IgM, usually against the I antigen, and bind in cooler blood of the fingers, toes, ears, and nose, generally below about 30 °C (86 °F). Because IgM is a pentamer it bridges neighboring cells, causing agglutination, and a single bound molecule activates C1. On rewarming the IgM detaches but C3b remains, so Kupffer cells in the liver clear C3b-coated cells (mostly extravascular), while some cells complete the cascade and lyse intravascularly. Acrocyanosis and hemolysis worsen in the cold. Transient polyclonal cold agglutinins follow Mycoplasma pneumoniae (anti-I) and EBV mononucleosis (anti-i); chronic disease is usually driven by a clonal B cell population making monoclonal IgM."},{"type":"table","title":"Warm versus cold autoimmune hemolysis","cols":["Feature","Warm AIHA","Cold agglutinin disease"],"rows":[["Antibody","Polyclonal IgG","IgM, usually anti-I; monoclonal in chronic disease"],["Binding temperature","37 °C (98.6 °F)","Below about 30 °C (86 °F), strongest near 4 °C (39 °F)"],["Main effector","Splenic macrophage Fc gamma receptors","Complement: C3b clearance in liver, some membrane attack complex lysis"],["Site of hemolysis","Extravascular, spleen","Mostly extravascular, liver; partly intravascular"],["Smear","Spherocytes, polychromasia","Red cell agglutinates"],["Direct Coombs","IgG positive, with or without C3","C3 positive, IgG negative"],["Associations","Idiopathic, SLE, CLL, drugs","Mycoplasma pneumoniae, EBV, clonal B cell disorders"]],"caption":"Antibody class determines temperature of binding, effector mechanism, and the Coombs pattern."},{"type":"h","text":"Reading the Coombs test"},{"type":"p","text":"The direct Coombs test asks whether antibody or complement is already on the patient's red cells. Washed cells are mixed with anti-human globulin, an antibody against human IgG or C3; if the red cells are coated, the reagent bridges them and they agglutinate. The indirect test asks whether free antibody is in the serum: the serum is first incubated with reagent red cells and then tested the same way, which is how antibody screens and crossmatches work. A positive direct test means immune coating, whether autoimmune (AIHA), alloimmune (transfusion reaction, hemolytic disease of the newborn), or drug related; it does not prove hemolysis, because a small fraction of healthy people test positive."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: interpreting a positive direct Coombs test","text":"A 25-year-old with a week of cough and patchy infiltrates develops anemia, raised LDH, and low haptoglobin. The analyzer reports a falsely high MCV because clumps are counted as single large cells. The direct Coombs is C3 positive and IgG negative. Step one: the markers confirm hemolysis, and the positive direct test shows an immune cause. Step two: C3 without IgG means the antibody detached after fixing complement, which fits IgM. Step three: agglutination and a respiratory infection point to Mycoplasma-induced cold agglutinins (anti-I). IgG with spherocytes in SLE would instead indicate warm AIHA."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why is the direct Coombs test in cold agglutinin disease positive for C3 but not IgM?","explain":"IgM binds weakly and detaches on warming and washing, but the C3 it fixed stays covalently bound to the membrane.","check":"The cold agglutinin mechanism and the Coombs table row."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-07-01-C02","obj":"HE-7.2","title":"Hypersensitivity types in hematologic disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypersensitivity type is decided by where the antibody meets its antigen and which effector it recruits. In type II reactions the antigen is fixed on a cell or in matrix, so the antibody marks that cell for destruction by complement, by Fc receptor phagocytosis, or by antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity. In type III reactions the antigen is soluble; antibody and antigen form immune complexes that circulate and deposit in vessel walls and glomeruli, where complement and neutrophils damage tissue that was never the target. Blood disorders fall mainly into these two groups."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypersensitivity types with hematologic examples","cols":["Type","Mechanism","Hematologic examples"],"rows":[["I","Preformed IgE on mast cells, immediate mediator release","Allergic (urticarial) transfusion reactions"],["II","IgG or IgM against a cell-bound antigen; complement, phagocytosis, ADCC","Acute and delayed hemolytic transfusion reactions, AIHA, hemolytic disease of the newborn, immune thrombocytopenia"],["III","Soluble antigen and antibody form complexes that deposit and fix complement","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, serum sickness"],["IV","T cell mediated, delayed","Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease"]],"caption":"Hemolytic disorders are type II; cryoglobulinemia is the main hematologic type III disorder."},{"type":"h","text":"Type II: three red cell examples"},{"type":"p","text":"In the acute hemolytic transfusion reaction, the recipient's preformed IgM anti-A or anti-B (isohemagglutinins, made in infancy after exposure to similar carbohydrate antigens on gut bacteria) bind incompatible donor cells and activate complement to the membrane attack complex, so lysis is intravascular. In AIHA the target is self. In hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn (HDFN), maternal IgG crosses the placenta, carried by the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), and coats fetal red cells, which are removed in the fetal spleen. IgM cannot cross the placenta, which is why naturally occurring anti-A and anti-B usually spare the fetus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rh hemolytic disease of the newborn","steps":["RhD-negative mother carries RhD-positive fetus","Fetal cells enter maternal blood, usually at delivery","Mother makes IgM, then memory B cells","Next RhD-positive pregnancy: anamnestic IgG anti-D","IgG crosses placenta via FcRn and coats fetal cells","Fetal anemia, hydrops; neonatal jaundice and kernicterus risk"],"caption":"Sensitization usually spares the first pregnancy; later pregnancies are affected by IgG anti-D."},{"type":"p","text":"Severe fetal anemia causes high-output heart failure, and extramedullary hematopoiesis in the liver reduces albumin synthesis, together producing hydrops fetalis. Before birth the placenta clears bilirubin; after birth the immature liver cannot conjugate it fast enough, and unconjugated bilirubin can cross the blood-brain barrier (kernicterus). ABO incompatibility is more common but milder: mothers who are group O can make IgG anti-A and anti-B, it can affect a first pregnancy, and fetal A and B antigens are weakly expressed and also present on other tissues that absorb antibody."},{"type":"h","text":"Type III: cryoglobulinemia"},{"type":"p","text":"Cryoglobulins are immunoglobulins that precipitate below 37 °C (98.6 °F) and redissolve on warming. Type I is a single monoclonal immunoglobulin (myeloma, Waldenström macroglobulinemia) and causes mainly hyperviscosity and vessel occlusion. Mixed types II and III contain IgM rheumatoid factor (monoclonal in type II, polyclonal in type III) bound to polyclonal IgG; these complexes deposit in small vessels, consume complement (C4 especially low), and cause leukocytoclastic vasculitis with palpable purpura, arthralgia, membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, and neuropathy. Chronic hepatitis C is the leading cause, because persistent antigen stimulates B cell clonal expansion; Sjögren syndrome and SLE are others. The blood must be kept at 37 °C until the serum is separated, or the cryoglobulin precipitates with the clot and the test is falsely negative."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-07-01-C03","obj":"HE-7.3","title":"Transfusion reactions by mechanism, timing, and findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Transfusion reactions are sorted by three questions: what in the unit or the recipient caused it, how soon it began, and whether red cells were destroyed. Only hemolytic reactions show hemolysis and a positive direct Coombs test."},{"type":"table","title":"Transfusion reactions","cols":["Reaction","Mechanism","Timing","Key findings"],"rows":[["Acute hemolytic","Recipient IgM anti-A or anti-B fix complement on ABO-incompatible donor cells; intravascular lysis","Minutes, within 24 hours","Fever, chills, flank pain, hypotension, red urine (hemoglobinuria), positive direct Coombs, DIC, acute kidney injury"],["Delayed hemolytic","Anamnestic IgG against minor antigens (Kidd, Rh, Kell, Duffy) from prior transfusion or pregnancy; extravascular","About 3 to 10 days","Unexplained fall in hemoglobin, mild jaundice, spherocytes, new positive direct Coombs and antibody screen"],["Febrile nonhemolytic","Cytokines from donor white cells in storage, or recipient anti-HLA against donor leukocytes","During or within about 4 hours","Rise of at least 1 °C (1.8 °F) to 38 °C (100.4 °F) or more, chills; no hemolysis; diagnosis of exclusion"],["Anaphylactic","IgA-deficient recipient with anti-IgA antibodies reacts with donor plasma IgA","Seconds to minutes","Hypotension, bronchospasm, angioedema, urticaria; no fever"],["Septic","Bacteria in the unit; endotoxin and bacteremia","Minutes to hours","High fever, rigors, hypotension, shock; same organism in unit and recipient"],["TRALI","Donor anti-HLA or anti-neutrophil antibodies activate primed recipient neutrophils in lung capillaries","Within 6 hours","Acute hypoxemia, bilateral infiltrates, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, often fever and hypotension"]],"caption":"Timing and the presence or absence of hemolysis separate most reactions."},{"type":"p","text":"The acute hemolytic reaction is the most dangerous and is usually caused by a clerical or identification error. Complement activation releases the anaphylatoxins C3a and C5a and inflammatory cytokines, producing fever and hypotension. Free hemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, adding vasoconstriction, while hypotension and pigment injure the tubules. Exposure of tissue factor and phospholipid on damaged cells can trigger disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). Plasma turns pink and haptoglobin falls."},{"type":"p","text":"The delayed hemolytic reaction occurs in a patient previously sensitized whose antibody had fallen below detection, so the pretransfusion screen was negative; Kidd antibodies are notorious for this. Re-exposure triggers a memory IgG response, and coated cells are cleared by splenic macrophages. Febrile nonhemolytic reactions are the most common and are reduced by removing white cells before storage (leukoreduction), which explains their cytokine mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Anaphylaxis follows exposure to a plasma protein the recipient lacks. Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency, and a minority of affected people have anti-IgA antibodies; the reaction appears within seconds and lacks fever, which distinguishes it from hemolysis and sepsis. Septic reactions are most often from platelets, stored at about 20 to 24 °C (68 to 75 °F), which allows skin flora such as staphylococci to grow; refrigerated red cells can carry Yersinia enterocolitica, which grows at cold temperatures and thrives on iron. Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) follows a two-hit model: illness primes neutrophils that sequester in the lung, and donor antibodies, often from plasma of women with many pregnancies, activate them to release proteases and oxidants that make capillaries leak. It must be separated from circulatory overload, which is hydrostatic and comes with hypertension and raised natriuretic peptides."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: sorting a reaction by timing","text":"A woman with three prior pregnancies receives 2 units of red cells after surgery. Her antibody screen was negative. Seven days later her hemoglobin has fallen from 10 to 8 g/dL, bilirubin is mildly raised, the smear shows spherocytes, and the direct Coombs is newly IgG positive. Step one: hemolysis began days later, which excludes the acute reactions. Step two: IgG with spherocytes means extravascular clearance. Step three: a delay of days in a previously pregnant patient fits an anamnestic response to a minor antigen that had dropped below detection. This is a delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction, and a repeat screen will often now identify the antibody, for example anti-Jka (Kidd)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-07-01-C04","obj":"HE-7.4","title":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is an immune reaction in which antibodies cause clotting rather than bleeding. The antigen is created when heparin meets platelet factor 4 (PF4), a positively charged chemokine released from platelet alpha granules. Heparin is a long, negatively charged polysaccharide, and when it binds several PF4 molecules at suitable ratios they form large complexes in which PF4 changes shape and exposes new epitopes. Some patients make IgG against these neoepitopes."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The HIT amplification loop","steps":["Heparin binds PF4, exposing a new epitope","IgG binds heparin and PF4 complexes on platelets","Fc portions cross-link platelet Fc gamma RIIa","Platelets activate, aggregate, and shed procoagulant microparticles","Alpha granules release more PF4","More PF4 forms more antigen with heparin"],"caption":"Each round of activation releases more PF4, so the antigen and the platelet activation reinforce each other."},{"type":"p","text":"The key step is the Fc gamma RIIa receptor on platelets. The IgG binds the complex on the platelet surface, and its Fc portion engages Fc receptors on the same and neighboring platelets, which delivers an activating signal. Activated platelets aggregate, release more PF4, and shed microparticles that provide phospholipid for clotting factor complexes. The same immune complexes activate monocytes, which express tissue factor, and bind PF4 attached to endothelial heparan sulfate, injuring the endothelium. The combined result is a burst of thrombin generation. Antibody-coated platelets are also removed by macrophages, so the platelet count falls while clots form."},{"type":"h","text":"Timing and findings"},{"type":"p","text":"Because a new IgG response must develop, the count typically falls 5 to 10 days after heparin is started. If the patient received heparin within about the previous 100 days, circulating antibody may already be present and the fall can begin within 24 hours. The drop is usually more than 50% from baseline, with a median nadir of about 50,000 to 60,000/µL (normal 150,000 to 400,000/µL); counts below 20,000/µL are unusual, which is one reason bleeding is uncommon. Venous thrombosis (deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism) is more frequent than arterial thrombosis (limb ischemia, stroke, myocardial infarction), and skin necrosis at injection sites and adrenal hemorrhage from adrenal vein thrombosis can occur."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk is higher with unfractionated heparin than with low molecular weight heparin, because longer chains bridge more PF4 molecules and form the large complexes that are most antigenic, and higher after cardiac and orthopedic surgery than in medical patients. HIT must be distinguished from nonimmune heparin-associated thrombocytopenia, a mild fall in the first 1 to 2 days caused by a direct effect of heparin on platelets, which recovers despite continued heparin and carries no thrombotic risk. Laboratory testing reflects the mechanism: an immunoassay for anti-PF4 and heparin antibodies is sensitive but detects many antibodies that do not activate platelets, while a functional assay that measures platelet activation (serotonin release) is more specific."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why would a patient with a platelet count of 60,000/µL from HIT have a thrombosis rather than bleeding?","explain":"The count is low because platelets are being activated and consumed in clots and cleared after antibody coating. Activated platelets, microparticles, monocyte tissue factor, and endothelial injury generate thrombin, and 60,000/µL is still enough for hemostasis.","check":"The amplification loop and the paragraph on Fc gamma RIIa."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-07-01-C05","obj":"HE-7.5","title":"Antiphospholipid syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) is an autoimmune disorder defined by persistent antiphospholipid antibodies together with thrombosis or pregnancy morbidity. Despite the name, the main targets are proteins that bind anionic phospholipid, chiefly beta2 glycoprotein I and prothrombin. Three laboratory measures are used: lupus anticoagulant (a functional clotting test), anticardiolipin antibodies, and anti-beta2 glycoprotein I antibodies (both immunoassays). APS can be primary or secondary, most often to SLE, and the antibodies must be present on two occasions at least 12 weeks apart, because transient antibodies follow many infections."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the PTT is prolonged in the tube"},{"type":"p","text":"The tenase and prothrombinase complexes assemble on a phospholipid surface. The PTT reagent supplies only a limited amount of phospholipid, and antibodies that bind beta2 glycoprotein I or prothrombin on that surface occupy it and block the complexes. The PTT, and especially the more sensitive dilute Russell viper venom time, is prolonged. A mixing study with normal plasma does not correct it, which shows an inhibitor rather than a factor deficiency, and adding excess phospholipid does correct it, which confirms that the inhibitor is phospholipid dependent. The PT is usually normal because its thromboplastin reagent contains abundant phospholipid."},{"type":"flow","title":"Same antibody, opposite effects","steps":["Antibody binds beta2 glycoprotein I on phospholipid","In the tube: scarce reagent phospholipid is blocked","Clotting complexes cannot assemble; PTT prolonged","In the body: phospholipid surfaces are abundant","Antibody cross-links beta2 glycoprotein I on cells","Endothelium, monocytes, platelets, complement activated; thrombosis"],"caption":"A laboratory artifact of limited phospholipid hides a strongly procoagulant effect in vivo."},{"type":"p","text":"In the body, membranes provide abundant phospholipid, so the anticoagulant effect is irrelevant. Antibodies instead cross-link beta2 glycoprotein I bound to cell surfaces and signal through cell receptors. Endothelial cells express tissue factor and adhesion molecules, monocytes express tissue factor, platelets become activated, and complement is activated, with C5a amplifying inflammation. The antibodies also impair natural anticoagulant defenses, including the protein C pathway and the annexin A5 shield that normally covers phospholipid on placental surfaces, and they reduce fibrinolysis. Because this acts on any vessel, APS causes both venous thrombosis (deep vein thrombosis is most common) and arterial thrombosis (stroke and transient ischemic attack), an unusual combination that points to the diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Pregnancy loss reflects placental thrombosis and infarction, impaired trophoblast invasion, and complement-mediated inflammation at the maternal and fetal interface. Recognized pregnancy morbidity includes unexplained fetal death at or after 10 weeks, premature birth before 34 weeks from severe preeclampsia or placental insufficiency, and three or more unexplained consecutive losses before 10 weeks. Other findings explained by the antibodies are mild thrombocytopenia, livedo reticularis, and a false-positive nontreponemal syphilis test, because the RPR and VDRL antigen contains cardiolipin."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient's PTT is prolonged and does not correct with a 1:1 mix of normal plasma. Should you expect bleeding?","explain":"Not necessarily. Failure to correct means an inhibitor. If it is a lupus anticoagulant, the risk is thrombosis, because the antibody blocks only the scarce phospholipid in the reagent and activates cells in vivo. A factor VIII inhibitor would instead cause bleeding, which is why the clinical picture and phospholipid-dependent tests are needed.","check":"Why the PTT is prolonged in the tube."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-07-01-C06","obj":"HE-7.6","title":"Epstein-Barr virus, mononucleosis, and lymphoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV, human herpesvirus 4) is an enveloped double-stranded DNA herpesvirus spread by saliva. It replicates in oropharyngeal epithelium and infects B cells in the tonsils. Its envelope glycoprotein gp350 binds CD21, also called complement receptor 2 (CR2), the B cell receptor for the complement fragment C3d, and glycoprotein gp42 binds HLA class II to complete entry. The virus then establishes lifelong latency in memory B cells as a circular episome."},{"type":"p","text":"Latent EBV proteins make infected B cells proliferate. EBNA-1 maintains the viral genome in dividing cells, EBNA-2 drives B cell growth genes, latent membrane protein 1 (LMP1) mimics a constitutively active CD40 receptor and activates NF-kappa B, and LMP2A mimics B cell receptor signaling. These are the same signals a B cell normally receives from T cell help and antigen, delivered without either."},{"type":"flow","title":"From EBV infection to mononucleosis","steps":["Saliva exposure; virus replicates in pharyngeal epithelium","gp350 binds CD21 on tonsillar B cells","Latent proteins drive polyclonal B cell proliferation","CD8 T cells expand to kill infected B cells","Atypical lymphocytes, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, pharyngitis","Infection controlled; lifelong latency in memory B cells"],"caption":"Most symptoms of mononucleosis come from the T cell response, not from direct viral damage."},{"type":"p","text":"Infectious mononucleosis occurs mainly in adolescents and young adults, whose primary infection provokes a vigorous response. CD8 cytotoxic T cells expand massively against the infected B cells; these activated T cells are the atypical lymphocytes (large cells with abundant cytoplasm that indents around neighboring red cells), and the infected B cells are a small minority. Lymphoid hyperplasia causes fever, exudative pharyngitis, posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, and splenomegaly, with a risk of splenic rupture, and mild hepatitis is common. Polyclonal B cell activation produces heterophile antibodies (IgM that agglutinates sheep or horse red cells, the basis of the Monospot test), which may be absent early and in young children, and cold agglutinins with anti-i specificity."},{"type":"table","title":"EBV-associated lymphoid neoplasms","cols":["Neoplasm","Setting","Role of EBV"],"rows":[["Endemic Burkitt lymphoma","Children in equatorial Africa; jaw and facial masses","Nearly all EBV positive; MYC translocation, usually t(8;14), is the essential event; Plasmodium falciparum malaria drives B cell expansion and weakens T cell control"],["Sporadic and HIV-associated Burkitt lymphoma","Abdominal masses in Western countries; HIV","A minority of sporadic cases EBV positive; MYC translocation is still required"],["Classical Hodgkin lymphoma","Especially mixed cellularity type","EBV in Reed-Sternberg cells in a large fraction; LMP1 supplies NF-kappa B survival signals"],["Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder","Organ or stem cell transplant with T cell immunosuppression","Unrestrained EBV-driven B cell growth, often with full latent protein expression"],["AIDS-related lymphomas","Advanced HIV, low CD4 count","Primary CNS lymphoma is almost always EBV positive; also diffuse large B cell lymphoma"]],"caption":"EBV contributes to lymphoma when cofactors add genetic hits or when T cell surveillance fails."},{"type":"p","text":"EBV is necessary for transformation in some settings but not sufficient. In Burkitt lymphoma the virus and chronic malaria expand the pool of proliferating B cells, raising the chance of an error during class switching or somatic hypermutation that moves MYC next to an immunoglobulin gene. In immunosuppressed patients the problem is loss of CD8 T cell surveillance, so infected B cells expressing the growth program are no longer removed. EBV is also linked to nasopharyngeal carcinoma, an epithelial tumor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-07-01-C07","obj":"HE-7.7","title":"Asplenia and infection by encapsulated bacteria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib), and Neisseria meningitidis are covered by a polysaccharide capsule. The capsule is hydrophilic and negatively charged, hides surface ligands from phagocyte receptors, and limits deposition of C3b; most meningococcal capsules, for example, contain sialic acid, which recruits the complement regulator factor H. These bacteria are therefore cleared efficiently only after they are opsonized by antibody and complement."},{"type":"p","text":"The spleen is built for this task. In the red pulp, blood flows slowly through the cords past macrophages before squeezing back into the sinusoids, so poorly opsonized bacteria are captured that the liver, with its faster flow, would miss. The marginal zone between red and white pulp contains B cells that respond to polysaccharides without T cell help and rapidly secrete IgM, which activates complement and opsonizes with C3b. The spleen is also an important site of early antibody production after bloodstream exposure."},{"type":"table","title":"What the spleen contributes and what loss of it shows","cols":["Function","Normal role","After asplenia"],"rows":[["Filtration by red pulp macrophages","Removes poorly opsonized bacteria and parasitized red cells","Fulminant bacteremia; severe babesiosis and malaria"],["Marginal zone B cells","Fast IgM against capsular polysaccharide","Weak early antibody to encapsulated bacteria"],["Pitting and culling of red cells","Removes nuclear remnants and inclusions; destroys old cells","Howell-Jolly bodies, target cells, Pappenheimer bodies"],["Platelet pool","Holds about one third of platelets","Thrombocytosis"]],"caption":"Blood smear changes reveal asplenia even when the history does not."},{"type":"p","text":"Without a spleen, bacteria that are poorly opsonized multiply in the blood, and overwhelming post-splenectomy infection can progress within hours from fever to septic shock and DIC. S. pneumoniae causes most cases. Risk is greatest in young children and in the first years after splenectomy but is lifelong. Asplenia may be surgical (trauma, hereditary spherocytosis) or functional. In sickle cell anemia, sickled cells repeatedly occlude the slow splenic circulation, causing infarction and fibrosis; function is lost in early childhood, and the scarred, shrunken organ is called autosplenectomy. Howell-Jolly bodies on the smear of a child with sickle cell disease signal this loss."},{"type":"h","text":"Why vaccination protects"},{"type":"p","text":"A plain capsular polysaccharide is a T-independent antigen: its repeating units cross-link B cell receptors and produce mainly IgM, with little class switching, affinity maturation, or memory, and children under about 2 years respond poorly because their marginal zone is immature. Conjugate vaccines attach the polysaccharide to a carrier protein. The resulting IgG opsonizes the capsule and activates complement, so the bacteria can be cleared by macrophages in the liver and elsewhere without the spleen, and memory B cells allow a rapid response on exposure."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a conjugate vaccine recruits T cell help","steps":["B cell receptor binds capsular polysaccharide","B cell internalizes the attached carrier protein","Carrier peptides presented on MHC class II","Carrier-specific CD4 T cell gives CD40 ligand and cytokines","Germinal center: class switch to IgG, affinity maturation","Memory B cells and long-lived plasma cells against the capsule"],"caption":"The T cell recognizes the protein, but the antibody is directed at the polysaccharide (linked recognition)."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Immunology supplies the rules, and the blood disorders apply them. Antibody class decides location and effector: IgG engages Fc receptors on splenic macrophages (warm AIHA, delayed hemolytic reaction) or on platelets (HIT), while pentameric IgM fixes complement and lyses cells in the circulation (acute hemolytic reaction, cold agglutinins). The same Fc receptor that clears coated red cells activates coated platelets, which is why one disease destroys cells and the other builds clots. Antigen location separates type II from type III, so cryoglobulins damage vessels and kidneys rather than the cells they began on."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and pathology meet in the spleen and in B cells. Mycoplasma and EBV trigger cold agglutinins through molecular mimicry and polyclonal B cell activation; EBV hijacks B cell growth signals and, when T cell control fails or malaria adds proliferative pressure, contributes to lymphoma; and splenic loss in sickle cell disease removes the main defense against encapsulated bacteria. Laboratory tests must be read with their limits in mind: a positive direct Coombs test shows coating, not necessarily hemolysis, PF4 antibody immunoassays detect antibodies that may not activate platelets, and a prolonged PTT from lupus anticoagulant signals thrombosis, not bleeding."}],"summary":["Warm AIHA is IgG at 37 °C (98.6 °F): splenic Fc receptor phagocytosis, spherocytes, extravascular hemolysis, direct Coombs IgG positive; associated with SLE, CLL, and drugs.","Cold agglutinin disease is IgM binding below about 30 °C (86 °F): agglutination and complement, C3 only on direct Coombs; associated with Mycoplasma (anti-I), EBV (anti-i), and clonal B cell disease.","The direct Coombs test detects IgG or C3 already on red cells; the indirect test detects free antibody in serum, as in screens and crossmatches.","Acute hemolytic transfusion reactions, AIHA, and hemolytic disease of the newborn are type II; mixed cryoglobulinemia with low C4, purpura, and glomerulonephritis, usually from hepatitis C, is type III.","Transfusion reactions sort by timing and hemolysis: acute hemolytic in minutes, anaphylactic (anti-IgA) in seconds without fever, TRALI within 6 hours, febrile nonhemolytic within hours, delayed hemolytic in about 3 to 10 days.","In HIT, IgG against heparin and PF4 cross-links platelet Fc gamma RIIa, activating platelets; counts fall more than 50% at 5 to 10 days and thrombosis, mostly venous, results.","Antiphospholipid antibodies prolong the PTT in vitro by blocking scarce reagent phospholipid but activate endothelium, monocytes, platelets, and complement in vivo, causing venous and arterial thrombosis and pregnancy loss.","EBV enters B cells through CD21; atypical lymphocytes in mononucleosis are CD8 T cells; EBV contributes to endemic Burkitt, Hodgkin, and immunosuppression-related lymphomas.","Asplenia, surgical or from sickle cell autosplenectomy, allows sepsis from S. pneumoniae, Hib, and N. meningitidis; conjugate vaccines convert a T-independent polysaccharide response into T-dependent IgG and memory."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders (immunohemolytic anemias, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, antiphospholipid antibody syndrome)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus (infectious mononucleosis, Burkitt and Hodgkin lymphoma, spleen)"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders; B cell activation and T-independent antigens"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses (Epstein-Barr virus); Streptococci, Haemophilus, and Neisseriae"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood Disorders"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Blood Types; Transfusion; Tissue and Organ Transplantation"}]},"HE-8":{"unit":"HE-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-08-01","title":"Blood-Borne Pathogens and the Red Cell","subject":"Hematology (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the organisms that use blood, and especially the red cell, as habitat: Plasmodium species, Babesia microti, the schistosomes that live in venous plexuses, and parvovirus B19, which attacks the erythroid progenitor rather than the mature cell. The teaching logic runs from life cycle to host receptor to tissue injury, because board questions are usually built that way: a travel or exposure history, a blood smear or serology, and a mechanism to name. Red cell genetics belongs here as well, since sickle trait, G6PD deficiency, thalassemia trait, and the Duffy-negative phenotype are the clearest examples of pathogen pressure shaping the human genome.","prerequisites":["Normal erythropoiesis, from burst-forming unit through reticulocyte, and the role of erythropoietin","Red cell membrane proteins, glycolysis, and the hexose monophosphate shunt with glutathione reduction","Hemoglobin structure, the globin gene clusters, and the switch from fetal to adult hemoglobin","Mechanisms of intravascular versus extravascular hemolysis and the laboratory pattern of each","Basic virology of nonenveloped DNA viruses and the elements of innate and adaptive antiparasitic immunity"],"connections":[{"to":"Hemoglobin biochemistry and hematologic genetics (HE-5, HE-6)","how":"The same mutations that cause sickle cell disease, G6PD deficiency, and thalassemia are the ones selected by falciparum malaria, so the genetics and the microbiology explain each other."},{"to":"Immune responses in hematologic disease (HE-7)","how":"Cytokine release, splenic macrophage activity, and antibody-dependent clearance determine how much of malarial and babesial anemia is parasite lysis and how much is host response."},{"to":"Spleen and reticuloendothelial anatomy","how":"Splenic cords and sinusoids are the filter that removes rigid and antibody-coated red cells, which is why splenectomy worsens babesiosis and why the spleen enlarges in chronic malaria."},{"to":"Hepatic and portal pathology (GI chapters)","how":"Schistosome eggs embolized to portal radicles produce granulomatous periportal fibrosis and portal hypertension with preserved hepatocyte function."},{"to":"Renal and urinary tract pathology (UR chapters)","how":"Schistosoma haematobium eggs in the bladder wall cause chronic inflammation, metaplasia, and squamous cell carcinoma, and malarial hemoglobinuria injures tubules."}],"terms":[{"term":"Sporozoite","def":"The slender infective stage of Plasmodium injected in mosquito saliva that travels in blood to hepatocytes."},{"term":"Schizogony","def":"Asexual multiple fission in which a parasite nucleus divides repeatedly inside a host cell and the cell then ruptures to release merozoites."},{"term":"Hypnozoite","def":"A dormant intrahepatic form of Plasmodium vivax and Plasmodium ovale that can reactivate weeks to months later and cause relapse."},{"term":"Recrudescence","def":"Return of symptoms from persistent low-level blood-stage parasites, as opposed to relapse from liver forms or reinfection from a new bite."},{"term":"Gametocyte","def":"The sexual blood stage of Plasmodium that is infective to the mosquito but does not multiply in the human host."},{"term":"Parasitized red cell sequestration","def":"Adherence of infected red cells to microvascular endothelium, removing them from the circulating smear and obstructing capillary flow."},{"term":"PfEMP1","def":"Plasmodium falciparum erythrocyte membrane protein 1, a variable adhesive protein exported to knobs on the infected red cell surface."},{"term":"Antigenic variation","def":"Sequential expression of different versions of a surface protein from a multigene family, so antibody against one version does not clear the next."},{"term":"Duffy antigen (Fy)","def":"A chemokine receptor on the red cell membrane, coded by ACKR1, that serves as the invasion receptor for Plasmodium vivax."},{"term":"Maltese cross","def":"A tetrad of Babesia merozoites inside one red cell, arranged as four joined bodies, seen on Giemsa-stained smear."},{"term":"Cercaria","def":"The free-swimming, fork-tailed larval schistosome released from a snail that penetrates intact human skin."},{"term":"Transient aplastic crisis","def":"An abrupt fall in hemoglobin with reticulocytopenia when erythropoiesis halts in a patient whose red cell survival is already short."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-08-01-C01","obj":"HE-8.1","title":"The Plasmodium life cycle and the origin of relapse","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Plasmodium is an apicomplexan protozoan with an obligate two-host cycle. The female Anopheles mosquito is the definitive host, where sexual reproduction occurs, and the human is the intermediate host, where the parasite multiplies asexually. Only the female takes blood, for the protein her eggs require, so only the female transmits infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Sporozoites enter the dermis with saliva, reach the bloodstream, and are cleared from plasma within about an hour, a fraction of them entering hepatocytes while the rest are destroyed. There the parasite undergoes exoerythrocytic schizogony, producing thousands of merozoites from a single sporozoite. This liver phase is clinically silent and sets the incubation period, roughly one to two weeks for Plasmodium falciparum and somewhat longer for the others. Rupture of the hepatic schizont releases merozoites into sinusoidal blood."},{"type":"flow","title":"From mosquito bite to the first fever","steps":["Infected female Anopheles injects sporozoites with saliva","Sporozoites travel in blood to hepatocytes","Exoerythrocytic schizogony: thousands of merozoites per sporozoite","Hepatic schizont ruptures; merozoites enter the blood","Merozoites invade red cells; trophozoite then blood schizont","Red cell ruptures; merozoites invade new red cells","Synchronized rupture and cytokine release produce paroxysmal fever"],"caption":"The liver phase sets the incubation period; the erythrocytic phase explains fever and anemia."},{"type":"p","text":"In the red cell the merozoite becomes a ring-form trophozoite, digests hemoglobin in a food vacuole, and leaves hemozoin pigment behind. It then divides into a blood schizont holding merozoites, and the cell ruptures, releasing them to invade again. Each erythrocytic cycle takes about 48 hours in Plasmodium vivax, Plasmodium ovale, and Plasmodium falciparum and about 72 hours in Plasmodium malariae, giving tertian and quartan fever periodicity when broods are synchronized. Falciparum is often asynchronous, so its fever may be continuous or irregular."},{"type":"p","text":"A minority of merozoites commit instead to the sexual line and become male and female gametocytes, which do not divide in humans and cause no symptoms but are the stage the mosquito must ingest. In the midgut the gametes fuse, the zygote becomes a motile ookinete that crosses the gut wall, and the resulting oocyst produces sporozoites that migrate to the salivary glands."},{"type":"table","title":"Species differences that drive the clinical picture","cols":["Feature","P. falciparum","P. vivax and P. ovale","P. malariae"],"rows":[["Erythrocytic cycle","About 48 h, often asynchronous","About 48 h (tertian)","About 72 h (quartan)"],["Red cells invaded","All ages, no limit","Reticulocytes and young cells","Senescent cells"],["Usual parasitemia","May exceed 10%","Usually under 2%","Low"],["Liver dormancy","None","Hypnozoites present","None"],["Late disease without new bites","Recrudescence over weeks","Relapse months to years later","Recrudescence for years; nephrotic syndrome"]],"caption":"Cell-age preference caps parasitemia in every species except falciparum, the lethal one."},{"type":"p","text":"Plasmodium vivax and Plasmodium ovale differ from the rest in forming hypnozoites, dormant uninucleate forms that persist in hepatocytes after the primary attack resolves. Weeks to months later a hypnozoite resumes schizogony and seeds the blood again, producing true relapse in a patient who has left the endemic area and whose blood had cleared. Relapse is distinct from recrudescence, where blood-stage parasites were never fully eliminated and expand again, and from reinfection by a new bite. A febrile illness months after travel is therefore still malaria until proven otherwise."},{"type":"p","text":"Blood-stage parasites also transmit without a mosquito, by transfusion, shared needles, or transplacental passage, because the infective unit is a parasitized red cell. Such infections skip the liver, so transfusion-transmitted vivax does not relapse."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A traveler treated for vivax malaria in Thailand is well for four months, then has the same fevers with no further travel. Where did the parasites come from, and why was the smear negative in between?","explain":"From hypnozoites in hepatocytes. Dormant liver forms carry no red cell parasites, so smear and symptoms both clear until a hypnozoite resumes schizogony and reseeds the blood.","check":"The relapse paragraph and the hypnozoite row of the species table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-08-01-C02","obj":"HE-8.2","title":"Why Plasmodium falciparum is the lethal species","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Falciparum virulence comes from three linked properties: it invades red cells of any age, it hides mature stages in the microvasculature, and it changes the surface protein that holds them there faster than antibody can follow."},{"type":"p","text":"Invasion depends on receptor use. Falciparum merozoites bind sialic acid residues on glycophorins A, B, and C and can also use sialic acid independent pathways, and none of these receptors is restricted to young cells. Because the whole red cell mass is available, parasitemia can rise through several percent to above ten percent in a few cycles. Vivax, by contrast, needs reticulocytes, which are roughly one percent of circulating cells, so its parasitemia is self-limited even when fever is severe."},{"type":"p","text":"The resulting anemia outruns the marrow and exceeds the number of cells actually parasitized. Schizont rupture destroys infected cells directly, complement fragments and parasite material deposited on uninfected cells mark those for splenic clearance, and inflammatory cytokines, chiefly tumor necrosis factor, suppress erythropoiesis. Hemoglobin digestion within the parasite releases free heme, and massive intravascular lysis can give hemoglobinuria dark enough to be called blackwater fever, with pigment nephropathy and acute tubular injury."},{"type":"p","text":"The second property is sequestration. From about 12 to 16 hours into its 48 hour cycle, as the ring matures into a trophozoite, the falciparum-infected cell exports PfEMP1, Plasmodium falciparum erythrocyte membrane protein 1, to electron-dense knobs on its own membrane. PfEMP1 binds endothelial receptors, including CD36, intercellular adhesion molecule 1 on brain endothelium, and endothelial protein C receptor, and in placental infection chondroitin sulfate A. Infected cells also adhere to uninfected cells (rosetting) and to each other. Mature forms therefore leave the circulating blood, which is why the falciparum smear shows mainly delicate rings, sometimes more than one per cell, with banana-shaped gametocytes and scarce schizonts, and why peripheral parasitemia understates the true burden."},{"type":"flow","title":"How adherence becomes organ failure","steps":["Infected cell exports PfEMP1 to surface knobs","PfEMP1 binds CD36, ICAM-1, or EPCR on endothelium","Infected and uninfected cells aggregate; cells stiffen","Capillary flow slows and obstructs","Local hypoxia and anaerobic glycolysis: lactic acidosis","Endothelial activation, cytokine release, loss of protein C signaling","Cerebral malaria, respiratory distress, or placental insufficiency"],"caption":"Sequestration explains coma with no meningitis, lactic acidosis out of proportion to anemia, and low birth weight in placental malaria."},{"type":"p","text":"Sequestration also explains why the clinical syndrome depends on where the cells stick. Binding to ICAM-1 and endothelial protein C receptor in cerebral vessels gives impaired consciousness and seizures with hypoxic and hemorrhagic injury but little inflammatory infiltrate, while chondroitin sulfate A binding in placental vessels gives intervillositis, fetal growth restriction, and maternal anemia. Hypoglycemia is common, driven by parasite glucose consumption and host anaerobic metabolism."},{"type":"p","text":"The third property is immune evasion. PfEMP1 is encoded by a family of about 60 var genes per parasite genome, of which only one is transcribed at a time. Switching, which is epigenetic rather than a change in DNA sequence, presents a new adhesive variant that existing antibody does not recognize, prolonging infection and allowing repeat infections with different variants. Protective immunity in endemic areas therefore accumulates slowly across many infections and reduces severe disease long before it reduces parasitemia. Adherence also evades the spleen, since a cell held on endothelium never passes through the splenic cords."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a severe falciparum case","text":"A traveler has a temperature of 39.5 C (103.1 F), is confused, and has a lactate of 6 mmol/L, glucose of 45 mg/dL, hemoglobin of 7 g/dL, and only 3% rings on the smear. The low parasitemia does not reassure: mature sequestered forms are absent from the smear, so the circulating count understates the burden. Confusion with a normal cerebrospinal fluid fits ICAM-1 and EPCR adhesion in cerebral microvessels, lactate reflects obstructed capillary flow rather than anemia alone, and hypoglycemia reflects parasite consumption plus anaerobic host metabolism."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-08-01-C03","obj":"HE-8.3","title":"Red cell polymorphisms that blunt malaria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Falciparum malaria has been the strongest recent selective pressure on the human genome, and the evidence is a set of red cell variants that are common exactly where transmission is or was intense. Each is a balanced polymorphism: the heterozygote or carrier gains protection, while the homozygote pays a hematologic price. The protection is against severe and fatal disease more than against infection itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Sickle cell trait (HbAS) is the clearest case. Carriers are clinically well, because polymerization needs a high proportion of hemoglobin S, yet their risk of severe or cerebral malaria falls by roughly 90%. Several mechanisms act together. Parasitized AS cells sickle preferentially in the low oxygen tension of the microvasculature and are removed by the spleen; parasite growth is impaired in the acidic, potassium-poor, dehydrated interior of a sickled cell; knob formation and PfEMP1 display are abnormal, so adherence is weaker; and heme oxygenase 1 induction with mild chronic hemolysis dampens the inflammatory response that produces cerebral disease. Hemoglobin C and hemoglobin E protect by related effects on parasite growth and on knob-mediated adherence, with the largest reported reduction in homozygous hemoglobin C."},{"type":"p","text":"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency acts through redox chemistry. G6PD is the rate-limiting enzyme of the hexose monophosphate shunt and the red cell's only source of NADPH, which keeps glutathione reduced. The intraerythrocytic parasite generates oxidant stress as it digests hemoglobin, and in a deficient cell that stress is poorly buffered, so the cell is damaged and cleared early, cutting parasite multiplication. The X-linked inheritance matters: heterozygous females are mosaics after X inactivation, carrying both deficient and normal cells, which appears to give protection with less hemolytic risk than in deficient males."},{"type":"p","text":"Thalassemia trait reduces globin chain output, giving small, hypochromic cells with less hemoglobin to digest, altered surface antigen display that favors antibody binding and splenic removal, and reduced rosetting. Protection is modest and is best documented against severe disease. Hereditary ovalocytosis and certain glycophorin C variants work differently again, by disturbing the membrane skeleton or the invasion receptor itself."},{"type":"table","title":"Protective variants and their mechanisms","cols":["Variant","Protective mechanism","Stage affected","Cost to the homozygote or deficient state"],"rows":[["Sickle trait (HbAS)","Sickling of infected cells, poor parasite growth, weak knob adherence, HO-1 induction","Intraerythrocytic growth and sequestration","Sickle cell disease in HbSS"],["G6PD deficiency","Unbuffered parasite-generated oxidant stress; early clearance of infected cells","Intraerythrocytic growth","Oxidant-triggered hemolysis in deficient males"],["Alpha or beta thalassemia trait","Less hemoglobin per cell, altered antigen display, reduced rosetting","Growth and adherence","Severe anemia in homozygous states"],["Duffy negative (FyFy)","Absent invasion receptor for P. vivax","Red cell invasion, blocked at entry","None; benign phenotype"]],"caption":"Only the Duffy-negative phenotype blocks invasion outright, and it is species-specific to vivax."},{"type":"p","text":"Duffy negativity is a different kind of protection. Plasmodium vivax merozoites use the Duffy binding protein to engage the Duffy antigen receptor for chemokines, encoded by ACKR1, and that interaction forms the tight junction required for entry. A promoter mutation that abolishes Duffy expression on erythrocytes, while sparing other tissues, is near fixation in much of sub-Saharan West and Central Africa, and those red cells resist vivax invasion almost completely. Vivax malaria is correspondingly rare in Duffy-negative populations, although occasional cases through alternative receptors are now described, which is why the resistance is best taught as near complete rather than absolute. The phenotype has no hematologic cost, and it does not protect against falciparum, which uses glycophorins instead."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does the Duffy-negative phenotype eliminate vivax from a population while sickle trait only reduces falciparum deaths?","explain":"Duffy negativity removes the receptor the parasite must bind to enter the cell, so invasion fails at the first step. Sickle trait allows invasion and only makes the intracellular environment and the adhesive surface less favorable, so infection still occurs and only severity falls.","check":"The Duffy paragraph against the sickle trait mechanisms in the same section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-08-01-C04","obj":"HE-8.4","title":"Babesia microti: tick transmission, ring forms, and coinfection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Babesia microti is a piroplasm, an apicomplexan related to Plasmodium but with an important simplification: it has no liver phase. Sporozoites inoculated by the tick invade red cells directly, so there is no exoerythrocytic schizogony, no hypnozoite, and no true relapse from a tissue reservoir."},{"type":"p","text":"The vector is the nymphal stage of Ixodes scapularis, the same deer tick that transmits Lyme disease in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States. White-footed mice are the reservoir and deer maintain the adult ticks. Transmission requires prolonged attachment, usually more than a day, and the nymph is poppy-seed sized, so most patients never recall the bite. Incubation after a tick bite runs about one to four weeks. Because the infective form is an intraerythrocytic parasite, Babesia is also transmitted by transfusion of red cells, and rarely by platelet units carrying residual red cells, where incubation is longer, up to several months, and transplacentally. Transfusion-transmitted babesiosis is a recognized blood supply concern precisely because carriers are often asymptomatic and the organism survives refrigerated storage."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the red cell the parasite grows and divides by binary fission rather than schizogony, producing two or four merozoites that escape and invade new cells. Lysis is therefore unsynchronized, which is why babesiosis gives sustained or irregular fever with drenching sweats rather than the periodic paroxysms of malaria. Hemolysis is largely intravascular with some splenic clearance, so the laboratory picture combines anemia, elevated lactate dehydrogenase and indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin, reticulocytosis, and often thrombocytopenia and elevated transaminases."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing Babesia from Plasmodium falciparum on a smear","cols":["Feature","Babesia microti","P. falciparum"],"rows":[["Pigment","No hemozoin pigment","Hemozoin present in older forms"],["Characteristic forms","Pleomorphic rings, tetrads (Maltese cross)","Delicate rings, banana-shaped gametocytes"],["Extracellular forms","Merozoites may lie outside cells","Rare outside cells"],["Multiplication","Binary fission, asynchronous","Schizogony, often synchronized broods"],["Exposure history","Ixodes bite or transfusion; no travel needed","Travel to an endemic region"]],"caption":"The absence of pigment and the presence of tetrads are the most reliable morphologic clues on Giemsa stain."},{"type":"p","text":"The tetrad of four merozoites joined within one red cell, described as a Maltese cross, is the pathognomonic finding, although it is uncommon at low parasitemia. Rings may be multiple per cell and vary in size and shape, and because merozoites can lie free in plasma, extracellular organisms do not exclude babesiosis. Low parasitemia is easily missed, so molecular and serologic testing supplements the smear when exposure is likely."},{"type":"p","text":"Severity is governed by the host rather than by parasite variation. The spleen is the main defense, since it removes rigid and antibody-coated infected cells, so asplenic patients, people over about 50, and those with impaired cell-mediated immunity can reach parasitemias above 10% with severe hemolysis, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and acute respiratory distress or renal failure. In healthy younger adults infection is often mild or silent."},{"type":"p","text":"Coinfection with Borrelia burgdorferi, the Lyme spirochete, follows from the shared vector: a single nymph may carry both, and patients may acquire both at once. Coinfection presents as Lyme disease with unusually prominent hemolytic features, so the erythema migrans rash and arthralgia appear alongside anemia, thrombocytopenia, and elevated lactate dehydrogenase, a combination that Lyme disease alone does not explain. Anaplasma phagocytophilum shares the same vector. When one tick delivers several organisms the clinical picture is additive, so unexplained hemolysis in a patient with Lyme disease points back to the red cells themselves."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-08-01-C05","obj":"HE-8.5","title":"The Schistosoma life cycle and where the adults live","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Schistosomes are blood flukes, trematodes that live as adults inside human veins. Unlike other flukes they are not hermaphroditic: the sexes are separate, and the slender female lies permanently in the ventral gynecophoral canal of the thicker male. They feed on blood and can survive for years, so the disease is driven by continuous egg output rather than by the worms themselves."},{"type":"flow","title":"Schistosoma life cycle","steps":["Eggs reach fresh water in human stool or urine","Egg hatches; ciliated miracidium swims","Miracidium penetrates the specific snail intermediate host","Asexual multiplication in snail sporocysts","Fork-tailed cercariae emerge into the water","Cercaria penetrates intact human skin; becomes a schistosomulum","Migration via venous blood, lungs, and liver to adulthood","Paired adults in mesenteric or vesical venules shed eggs"],"caption":"Humans acquire infection through skin contact with fresh water, not by ingestion, and the snail is obligatory."},{"type":"p","text":"The intermediate host is a specific freshwater snail, which anchors the geography of each species: Biomphalaria for Schistosoma mansoni in Africa, the Middle East, and South America, Oncomelania for Schistosoma japonicum in East Asia, and Bulinus for Schistosoma haematobium in Africa and the Middle East. Inside the snail a single miracidium multiplies asexually, so one egg yields many cercariae, which is the amplification step in the cycle. Cercariae penetrate intact skin using proteolytic secretions, shed their forked tails, and become schistosomula. They travel in venous blood to the right heart and lungs, then reach the hepatic portal system, mature, pair, and migrate against portal flow to their final venules."},{"type":"p","text":"Location determines the disease. Schistosoma mansoni and Schistosoma japonicum occupy the inferior and superior mesenteric venules respectively, so eggs enter the gut wall and the portal circulation. Schistosoma haematobium occupies the vesical and pelvic venous plexus, so eggs enter the bladder wall and the urine. Japonicum lays the largest number of eggs per day, which accounts for its heavier hepatic and occasional central nervous system involvement."},{"type":"table","title":"Species, vein, and target organ","cols":["Species","Snail host","Adult location","Eggs appear in","Egg spine","Principal pathology"],"rows":[["S. mansoni","Biomphalaria","Inferior mesenteric venules","Stool","Large lateral spine","Colonic polyps, periportal fibrosis, portal hypertension"],["S. japonicum","Oncomelania","Superior mesenteric venules","Stool","Small lateral knob","Heavy hepatic fibrosis; eggs may embolize to brain"],["S. haematobium","Bulinus","Vesical and pelvic plexus","Urine","Terminal spine","Hematuria, bladder fibrosis, squamous cell carcinoma"]],"caption":"Egg morphology and the specimen in which eggs appear identify the species and predict the organ injured."},{"type":"p","text":"Injury is immunologic and comes from eggs, not worms. Adults coat themselves with host antigens and remain largely invisible to immune attack, but eggs trapped in tissue release soluble antigens that drive a T helper 2 response, with eosinophils, interleukin 4, interleukin 5, and interleukin 13, and a granuloma forms around each egg. Repeated granulomas heal by fibrosis. In the liver, eggs embolized through portal radicles produce the characteristic pipestem periportal fibrosis, which obstructs presinusoidal flow and causes portal hypertension with esophageal varices and splenomegaly while hepatocellular function stays relatively preserved. In the bladder, chronic granulomatous inflammation causes hematuria, calcification, obstructive uropathy, and squamous metaplasia that predisposes to squamous cell carcinoma. Acute reactions mark earlier stages: cercarial penetration can give a pruritic papular rash, and the onset of egg laying can give an acute serum sickness like illness with fever, eosinophilia, and hepatosplenomegaly."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-08-01-C06","obj":"HE-8.6","title":"Parvovirus B19 and the erythroid progenitor","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parvovirus B19 is a small nonenveloped virus with a single-stranded linear DNA genome. Having no envelope makes it resistant to lipid solvents, detergents, and drying, so it survives on surfaces and in solvent-detergent treated plasma derivatives. Transmission is mainly respiratory, with vertical and transfusion routes as well."},{"type":"p","text":"Tissue targeting follows from one receptor. The virus binds the P antigen, a glycosphingolipid also called globoside, which is expressed on erythroid progenitors as well as on megakaryocytes, placental cells, and endothelium. Its dependence on host DNA polymerase means it can replicate only in cells in S phase, and the rapidly dividing erythroid colony-forming and burst-forming units are ideal. Infection lyses these progenitors, producing giant pronormoblasts with inclusion bearing nuclei in the marrow. The rare individuals who lack P antigen are naturally resistant."},{"type":"flow","title":"From infection to the hematologic consequence","steps":["Respiratory inoculation; viremia in about 1 week","Virus binds P antigen on erythroid progenitors","Replication requires S phase; progenitors are lysed","Erythropoiesis halts for about 7 to 10 days","Reticulocytes disappear from the blood","Normal red cell lifespan buffers the pause: mild drop only","Antibody clears viremia; rash and arthralgia follow"],"caption":"The hematologic result is set by how long the patient's red cells live, not by how severe the viral infection is."},{"type":"p","text":"In a healthy person the pause in production is harmless. Red cells survive about 120 days, so losing ten days of output drops hemoglobin by only about one gram per deciliter. The illness is then dominated by immune complex phenomena that begin as antibody appears: erythema infectiosum, or fifth disease, with the slapped cheek rash and a lacy reticular rash on the limbs in children, and symmetric polyarthralgia, more often in adults. Because those features are immune mediated and viremia has already fallen, patients are most contagious before the rash and no longer infectious once it appears."},{"type":"p","text":"In a patient with a chronic hemolytic anemia the same ten day pause is dangerous. When red cells survive only 10 to 20 days, as in sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis, thalassemia, or an autoimmune hemolytic anemia, survival depends on continuous compensatory erythropoiesis at several times the normal rate. Stopping production removes that compensation, and hemoglobin falls steeply over days. This is transient aplastic crisis, and its signature is severe anemia with an inappropriately low reticulocyte count, which separates it from splenic sequestration or an accelerated hemolytic episode, where reticulocytes are high. It resolves once antibody clears the viremia and the marrow recovers with a brisk reticulocytosis. Patients who cannot make neutralizing antibody develop chronic pure red cell aplasia instead."},{"type":"example","title":"Separating aplastic crisis from a hemolytic episode","text":"A child with hereditary spherocytosis has a hemoglobin of 5 g/dL, down from a baseline of 11, with a reticulocyte count of 0.2% and a bilirubin no higher than his usual value. Accelerated hemolysis would raise both the reticulocyte count and the bilirubin, so the low reticulocyte count points to failed production rather than accelerated destruction. In a patient whose spherocytes survive about two weeks, an arrest of erythropoiesis for about ten days accounts for the whole fall, and parvovirus B19 infection of erythroid progenitors is the mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal infection combines the same marrow effect with two aggravating factors: a shorter fetal red cell lifespan with a far higher erythropoietic rate, and viral injury to myocardium, which also bears P antigen. Severe anemia causes high output cardiac failure, and the result is generalized edema, ascites, and pleural effusions, that is, nonimmune hydrops fetalis, with fetal loss the other possible outcome. Risk is highest with infection in the first and second trimesters. Calling it nonimmune separates it mechanistically from hydrops of Rh alloimmunization, where maternal antibody destroys fetal red cells."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each organism in this chapter is understood through a receptor and a host cell. Plasmodium vivax needs Duffy, falciparum uses glycophorins and then displays PfEMP1 for endothelial adherence, Schistosoma needs a specific snail and then a particular venous bed, and parvovirus B19 needs P antigen on a dividing erythroid progenitor. Change the receptor and the disease disappears, which is the clearest demonstration in microbiology that tropism and pathology are the same question. The same logic connects to genetics: malaria selected sickle trait, G6PD deficiency, thalassemia trait, and Duffy negativity, so the hemoglobinopathy and enzymopathy chapters describe the price a population paid for that protection."},{"type":"p","text":"The injury mechanisms also divide cleanly along immunologic lines. Falciparum kills by mechanical obstruction and cytokine-driven inflammation, so the lesion is vascular and the smear may understate it. Schistosomiasis injures through a T helper 2 granulomatous response to eggs, so the lesion is fibrotic and progressive long after exposure ends. Babesia depends on splenic function rather than on antigenic variation, so the host history predicts severity. Parvovirus B19 produces no injury of its own that matters in a normal host and becomes lethal only when it interacts with an existing shortened red cell lifespan. Interpreting a laboratory result requires knowing which of these mechanisms is at work, and the limit worth stating is that smears, serology, and egg counts each miss early or low-burden infection, so exposure history remains part of the reasoning."}],"summary":["Plasmodium sporozoites undergo silent liver schizogony that sets the incubation period, then an erythrocytic cycle of about 48 hours (72 in P. malariae) whose synchronized rupture causes paroxysmal fever; gametocytes infect the mosquito but do not multiply in humans.","Relapse comes from hypnozoites of P. vivax and P. ovale in hepatocytes, recrudescence from persistent blood-stage parasites, and reinfection from a new bite.","P. falciparum invades red cells of all ages, so parasitemia can exceed 10%, while vivax is limited to reticulocytes and malariae to senescent cells.","PfEMP1 on knobs binds CD36, ICAM-1, EPCR, and placental chondroitin sulfate A, sequestering mature forms, obstructing capillaries, and producing cerebral malaria, lactic acidosis, and placental insufficiency; epigenetic switching among about 60 var genes evades antibody.","Sickle trait, G6PD deficiency, and thalassemia trait reduce severe malaria by impairing intracellular parasite growth, adherence, and survival of infected cells, while Duffy negativity blocks vivax invasion at the receptor itself.","Babesia microti has no liver stage, is transmitted by Ixodes scapularis nymphs and by transfusion, divides by binary fission into pleomorphic rings and Maltese cross tetrads without pigment, and is severe in asplenic and older patients.","Shared vector means Babesia and Borrelia burgdorferi coinfection, presenting as Lyme disease plus unexplained hemolytic anemia and thrombocytopenia.","Schistosome cercariae from freshwater snails penetrate skin; adults live in mesenteric venules (S. mansoni, S. japonicum) or the vesical plexus (S. haematobium), and the eggs, not the worms, drive granulomatous fibrosis.","Schistosomal periportal pipestem fibrosis causes presinusoidal portal hypertension with preserved hepatocyte function, and S. haematobium bladder injury predisposes to squamous cell carcinoma.","Parvovirus B19 uses P antigen to infect S-phase erythroid progenitors, pausing erythropoiesis for about 7 to 10 days: trivial in a normal host, transient aplastic crisis with reticulocytopenia in chronic hemolysis, chronic red cell aplasia without antibody, and nonimmune hydrops fetalis in utero."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology: blood and tissue protozoa; Trematodes; Parvoviruses"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases: parasitic infections; Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to Microbes, immune evasion and immunity to parasites"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases; Blood Disorders"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia"}]},"HE-9-A":{"unit":"HE-9-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01","title":"Diseases of Blood Cell Production","subject":"Hematopoietic System (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers disorders in which the marrow makes too few or too many blood cells: the microcytic anemias (iron deficiency, anemia of chronic disease, thalassemia trait, sideroblastic anemia), the megaloblastic anemias of vitamin B12 and folate deficiency, aplastic anemia, the polycythemias, blood loss, neutropenia, and the leukemoid reaction. Board questions usually give iron studies, a smear, a cell count, or an erythropoietin level and ask for the cause or the next consequence. Each answer follows from one question: which raw material, signal, or stem cell has failed, and how does the marrow respond?","prerequisites":["Hematopoiesis, the stem cell hierarchy, and growth factors such as erythropoietin and G-CSF (HE-1, HE-4)","Iron absorption, transport, and storage (HE-3)","Heme synthesis and hemoglobin structure (HE-5)","Red cell indices: MCV, MCH, MCHC, RDW, and the reticulocyte count (HE-2)","Folate and cobalamin coenzyme chemistry and thymidylate synthesis"],"connections":[{"to":"Diseases of blood cell lysis (HE-9-B)","how":"Hemolysis and blood loss both raise the reticulocyte count, while the production disorders here keep it inappropriately low."},{"to":"Blood and lymph neoplasms (HE-9-D)","how":"Polycythemia vera, myelofibrosis, chronic myeloid leukemia, and myelodysplasia are clonal stem cell disorders that mimic or follow the conditions in this chapter."},{"to":"Deficiency and malabsorption conditions (GI-13-E)","how":"Celiac disease, atrophic gastritis, ileal disease, and gastrectomy cause iron, B12, and folate deficiency by removing the absorptive sites."},{"to":"Kidney in acid-base and blood pressure control (UR-7)","how":"Renal peritubular cells sense oxygen delivery and release erythropoietin, so kidney disease and kidney tumors change red cell production."},{"to":"Immune responses in hematologic disease (HE-7)","how":"Autoimmunity causes pernicious anemia, most aplastic anemia, and immune neutropenia, and inflammatory cytokines drive anemia of chronic disease."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hepcidin","def":"A liver peptide hormone that binds the iron exporter ferroportin and causes its degradation, lowering iron absorption and release from macrophages."},{"term":"Ferritin","def":"The intracellular iron storage protein; the small amount in serum is proportional to body iron stores and also rises with inflammation."},{"term":"Total iron-binding capacity (TIBC)","def":"The amount of iron that serum transferrin could bind, used as an indirect measure of the transferrin concentration."},{"term":"Red cell distribution width (RDW)","def":"A measure of variation in red cell size (anisocytosis); it rises when a new population of cells differs in size from the old."},{"term":"Ringed sideroblast","def":"An erythroblast whose iron-loaded mitochondria form a ring of Prussian blue positive granules around the nucleus."},{"term":"Ineffective erythropoiesis","def":"Destruction of developing red cells inside the marrow, so the marrow is busy but few cells reach the blood."},{"term":"Megaloblast","def":"A large erythroid precursor whose nucleus matures more slowly than its cytoplasm because DNA synthesis is impaired."},{"term":"Intrinsic factor","def":"A glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells that binds vitamin B12 and allows its uptake in the terminal ileum."},{"term":"Pancytopenia","def":"Reduction of red cells, white cells, and platelets at the same time."},{"term":"Erythropoietin (EPO)","def":"A glycoprotein hormone made mainly by renal peritubular interstitial cells in response to low oxygen delivery that keeps erythroid progenitors alive and dividing."},{"term":"Absolute neutrophil count (ANC)","def":"Total white cell count multiplied by the fraction of segmented neutrophils plus bands."},{"term":"Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP)","def":"An enzyme in mature neutrophils whose stained score is high in reactive neutrophilia and low in chronic myeloid leukemia."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C01","obj":"HE-9-A.1","title":"Iron deficiency anemia: causes and laboratory profile","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The body has no regulated way to excrete iron, so iron balance is controlled at absorption. An adult absorbs about 1 to 2 mg per day in the duodenum and upper jejunum, which matches obligatory losses from shed skin and gut cells; menstruation adds an average of about 0.5 to 1 mg per day. Nonheme ferric iron (Fe3+) is reduced at the brush border by duodenal cytochrome b, enters through divalent metal transporter 1 (DMT1), and leaves the enterocyte through ferroportin, after which hephaestin reoxidizes it for binding to transferrin. Gastric acid keeps iron soluble and favors reduction, so achlorhydria and gastrectomy impair uptake, and celiac disease destroys the duodenal surface that absorbs it."},{"type":"p","text":"Iron deficiency anemia, the most common anemia worldwide, appears when losses or needs exceed this small intake. In adults the usual cause is chronic blood loss, and in a man or a postmenopausal woman a gastrointestinal source such as colorectal cancer must be considered. Pregnancy needs about 1 g of extra iron in total for the expanded red cell mass, placenta, and fetus."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of iron deficiency","cols":["Mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["Chronic blood loss","Menorrhagia; peptic ulcer, colorectal cancer, angiodysplasia; hookworm infection worldwide"],["Poor intake","Infants fed mostly cow's milk, restrictive diets, poor diet in older adults"],["Poor absorption","Celiac disease, gastrectomy or bypass of the duodenum, achlorhydria from atrophic gastritis"],["Increased need","Pregnancy and lactation, infancy and adolescent growth"]],"caption":"Blood loss is the leading cause in adults; poor intake matters most when needs are high."},{"type":"p","text":"Deficiency develops in stages. Stores are used first, so ferritin falls while hemoglobin is still normal; ferritin is the earliest and most specific single test. Next, transport iron falls. Hepatocytes respond to low iron by making more transferrin, so TIBC rises while serum iron falls and transferrin saturation (serum iron divided by TIBC) drops. Ferrochelatase then inserts zinc in place of the missing iron, so zinc protoporphyrin rises, and erythroblasts display more transferrin receptors, raising soluble transferrin receptor. Hepcidin is suppressed, which maximizes absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"Only then does anemia appear. Erythroid precursors are thought to keep dividing until they reach a set hemoglobin concentration, so when hemoglobin synthesis lags they divide an extra time and the cells come out small (microcytic, MCV below about 80 fL) and pale (hypochromic, central pallor wider than one third of the cell). Because these small cells mix with older normal cells, the RDW rises early, which helps separate iron deficiency from thalassemia trait. The smear may show elongated pencil cells, and a reactive thrombocytosis is common. Iron-dependent enzymes in rapidly renewing epithelia also fail, producing glossitis, angular cheilitis, and spoon-shaped nails (koilonychia); pica, especially for ice, is a classic but poorly explained feature."},{"type":"table","title":"Iron studies: what each test measures","cols":["Test","Approximate adult reference range","Iron deficiency"],"rows":[["Serum ferritin","About 15 to 300 ng/mL (lower in women)","Low; below about 15 ng/mL is diagnostic"],["Serum iron","About 60 to 170 µg/dL","Low"],["TIBC (transferrin)","About 250 to 400 µg/dL","High"],["Transferrin saturation","About 20% to 50%","Low, often below 15%"],["RDW","About 11.5% to 14.5%","High"],["MCV","About 80 to 100 fL","Low (late)"]],"caption":"Ferritin reflects stores, TIBC reflects transferrin, and saturation reflects how much transport iron is available to the marrow. Ranges vary by laboratory."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C02","obj":"HE-9-A.2","title":"Separating the microcytic anemias with iron studies and the smear","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemoglobin needs iron, protoporphyrin, and globin, so a microcytic anemia means one of four steps has failed: iron supply (iron deficiency), iron release from stores (anemia of chronic disease), globin synthesis (thalassemia), or heme synthesis (sideroblastic anemia). Iron studies and the smear identify the step."},{"type":"flow","title":"How inflammation restricts iron","steps":["Infection, autoimmunity, or cancer raises IL-6","IL-6 induces hepcidin synthesis in hepatocytes","Hepcidin binds ferroportin, which is internalized and degraded","Macrophages and enterocytes cannot export iron","Serum iron falls; stored iron and ferritin stay normal or high","Iron-restricted erythropoiesis produces anemia"],"caption":"Hepcidin withholds iron from the marrow, which likely evolved to deprive microbes of iron."},{"type":"p","text":"In anemia of chronic disease, iron is present but locked away. Serum iron falls within hours of inflammation. Ferritin is normal or high because iron accumulates in macrophages and because ferritin is itself an acute phase reactant, while transferrin is a negative acute phase reactant, so TIBC is low. Inflammatory cytokines such as interleukin-1, tumor necrosis factor, and interferon-gamma also blunt erythropoietin release and the marrow response to it, and red cell survival is mildly shortened. The anemia is usually mild and normocytic, becoming mildly microcytic when it lasts."},{"type":"p","text":"Thalassemia trait leaves iron handling normal. Reduced globin synthesis makes many small cells, so the red cell count is normal or high and the microcytosis is out of proportion to a mild anemia; a Mentzer index (MCV divided by red cell count in millions per µL) below about 13 favors thalassemia. Cells are uniformly small, so the RDW is usually normal, and target cells form because the membrane is large relative to the hemoglobin it holds. Beta thalassemia trait raises hemoglobin A2 above about 3.5%, while alpha trait gives a normal adult electrophoresis."},{"type":"p","text":"In sideroblastic anemia, iron reaches the erythroblast but cannot enter protoporphyrin, so it collects in mitochondria. Serum iron, ferritin, and transferrin saturation are high, TIBC is low or normal, and Prussian blue staining of marrow shows ringed sideroblasts. The smear is often dimorphic, with a mixed population of hypochromic and normal cells, and may show Pappenheimer bodies (iron granules)."},{"type":"table","title":"Iron studies in microcytic anemia","cols":["Disorder","Serum iron","Ferritin","TIBC","Saturation","Smear and other clues"],"rows":[["Iron deficiency","Low","Low","High","Low","High RDW, pencil cells, high platelets"],["Anemia of chronic disease","Low","Normal or high","Low","Low or normal","Normal RDW, inflammatory disease present"],["Thalassemia trait","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal","Target cells, high red cell count, normal RDW"],["Sideroblastic anemia","High","High","Low or normal","High","Dimorphic cells, ringed sideroblasts in marrow"]],"caption":"TIBC moves opposite to ferritin in iron deficiency and in the same direction in anemia of chronic disease."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: inflammation plus blood loss","text":"A woman with active rheumatoid arthritis has hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL, MCV 76 fL, serum iron 30 µg/dL, TIBC 260 µg/dL, and ferritin 70 ng/mL. Ferritin of 70 would exclude iron deficiency in a healthy person, but inflammation raises ferritin independent of stores, so a normal value no longer proves adequate iron. Clues to added iron deficiency are a TIBC that is not low, a high RDW, and microcytosis more marked than inflammation alone usually causes. A high soluble transferrin receptor would confirm it, because erythroblasts starved of iron increase their receptors, whereas hepcidin-restricted marrow in pure anemia of chronic disease does not. Many references accept ferritin up to about 100 ng/mL as compatible with iron deficiency when inflammation is present."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict the hepcidin level and the rate of duodenal iron absorption in iron deficiency compared with anemia of chronic disease.","explain":"Iron deficiency suppresses hepcidin, so ferroportin stays on enterocytes and absorption rises; inflammation raises hepcidin, so ferroportin is degraded and absorption falls even though the patient is anemic.","check":"Compare with the flow on how inflammation restricts iron and the staged changes in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C03","obj":"HE-9-A.3","title":"Causes of sideroblastic anemia and the heme effects of lead","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Heme synthesis begins and ends in the mitochondrion. Aminolevulinic acid synthase (ALAS) condenses glycine and succinyl-CoA into aminolevulinic acid (ALA), using pyridoxal phosphate (the active form of vitamin B6) as cofactor; this is the rate-limiting step. The middle steps occur in the cytosol, and the last enzyme, ferrochelatase, inserts ferrous iron into protoporphyrin IX back in the mitochondrion. Erythroblasts use their own isoform, ALAS2, whose translation increases when iron is plentiful. When heme synthesis fails, iron keeps arriving in mitochondria that cannot use it, and these iron-loaded mitochondria, clustered around the nucleus, form the ringed sideroblast."},{"type":"flow","title":"Heme synthesis and the sites of lead inhibition","steps":["Glycine plus succinyl-CoA (mitochondrion)","ALA synthase, needs vitamin B6, forms ALA","ALA dehydratase forms porphobilinogen (cytosol); lead inhibits","Porphobilinogen converted through porphyrinogens","Protoporphyrin IX formed (mitochondrion)","Ferrochelatase inserts Fe2+ to make heme; lead inhibits"],"caption":"Blocks after iron delivery trap iron in mitochondria; lead also leaves ALA and protoporphyrin to accumulate."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of sideroblastic anemia","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Distinguishing points"],"rows":[["X-linked ALAS2 mutation","Reduced erythroid ALA synthase; many mutations lower affinity for pyridoxal phosphate","Hereditary, mostly males, microcytic; some respond to high-dose pyridoxine, reflecting the cofactor defect"],["Alcohol","Mitochondrial toxin; impairs pyridoxal phosphate formation and heme enzymes","Common acquired cause; reverses with abstinence"],["Pyridoxine deficiency","Too little cofactor for ALA synthase","Often drug-induced, classically by isoniazid, which binds and depletes B6"],["Lead","Inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase","Basophilic stippling, high zinc protoporphyrin and urinary ALA"],["Other acquired","Copper deficiency (including zinc excess); clonal defect in myelodysplastic syndrome","Myelodysplasia gives a macrocytic or dimorphic picture in older adults"]],"caption":"The main hereditary form affects the first step; most acquired forms are toxic or nutritional."},{"type":"p","text":"Lead binds sulfhydryl groups and displaces zinc from ALA dehydratase, so ALA accumulates in blood and urine. It also inhibits ferrochelatase, so protoporphyrin accumulates and zinc is inserted in place of iron, giving a high zinc protoporphyrin (free erythrocyte protoporphyrin). Zinc protoporphyrin also rises in iron deficiency, because ferrochelatase lacking iron uses zinc, so a high value supports lead exposure only with a normal ferritin or a raised blood lead level, which is the diagnostic test."},{"type":"p","text":"Lead also inhibits pyrimidine 5' nucleotidase, the enzyme that clears pyrimidine nucleotides from degraded ribosomal RNA as reticulocytes mature. Undegraded ribosomes aggregate into coarse blue dots on Wright stain, called basophilic stippling. The anemia of lead poisoning is mild, hypochromic, and microcytic or normocytic, with a hemolytic component from damaged membranes and enzymes; ringed sideroblasts are present in some patients but not all. Lead's neurologic and renal effects are taught with those systems."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C04","obj":"HE-9-A.4","title":"Vitamin B12 deficiency, megaloblastic anemia, and subacute combined degeneration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) comes only from animal foods. Acid and pepsin release it from food protein, and it binds haptocorrin (R-binder) from saliva. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases digest haptocorrin and B12 binds intrinsic factor from parietal cells. The complex is taken up by the cubilin receptor in the terminal ileum, and transcobalamin carries B12 in plasma. Body stores of about 2 to 5 mg, mostly in the liver, far exceed a daily need of a few micrograms, so deficiency takes years to appear."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of B12 deficiency by the step that fails","cols":["Step","Cause"],"rows":[["Intake","Strict vegan diet without fortified foods"],["Release from food","Achlorhydria, atrophic gastritis, long-term acid suppression"],["Intrinsic factor","Pernicious anemia; total or proximal gastrectomy"],["Release from haptocorrin","Pancreatic insufficiency"],["Ileal uptake","Ileal resection, Crohn disease of the terminal ileum"],["Competition in the gut","Fish tapeworm Dibothriocephalus (formerly Diphyllobothrium) latus; small bowel bacterial overgrowth"]],"caption":"Every step of the absorption pathway is a possible point of failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Pernicious anemia is autoimmune atrophic gastritis of the body and fundus. CD4 T cells and antibodies against the parietal cell proton pump (H+/K+ ATPase) destroy parietal cells, and antibodies against intrinsic factor block B12 binding; anti-intrinsic factor antibodies are less sensitive but highly specific. Loss of acid causes antral G cell hyperplasia with high gastrin and raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and neuroendocrine tumors, and the disease clusters with other autoimmune disorders such as Hashimoto thyroiditis. The fish tapeworm, acquired from raw or undercooked freshwater fish, absorbs B12 in the small intestine before the host can."},{"type":"flow","title":"From B12 deficiency to megaloblastic anemia","steps":["Methionine synthase lacks methylcobalamin","Folate is trapped as methyl-THF","Less methylene-THF for thymidylate synthase","dTMP falls; DNA synthesis slows","Nucleus matures late while cytoplasm grows","Megaloblasts die in marrow; macrocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils"],"caption":"The methyl-folate trap explains why B12 deficiency produces the same blood picture as folate deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"B12 serves two enzymes. Methionine synthase transfers a methyl group from methyl-tetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, forming methionine and regenerating tetrahydrofolate (THF); without it, folate is trapped and thymidine synthesis fails, so DNA replication lags behind RNA and protein synthesis in every dividing cell. Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA; without it, methylmalonic acid rises. Homocysteine and methylmalonic acid are therefore both high. Many megaloblasts die in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis), raising LDH and indirect bilirubin and lowering haptoglobin, while the reticulocyte count stays low. The blood shows oval macrocytes (MCV above 100 fL), hypersegmented neutrophils (commonly defined as at least 5% with five lobes or any with six), and often pancytopenia; the marrow is hypercellular. Glossitis reflects the same failure in the oral epithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"Subacute combined degeneration is symmetric demyelination of the dorsal columns (loss of vibration and position sense, positive Romberg sign) and lateral corticospinal tracts (spastic weakness, hyperreflexia, extensor plantar responses), often with peripheral neuropathy and cognitive change. Low S-adenosylmethionine impairs methylation of myelin proteins and lipids, and accumulated methylmalonyl-CoA may produce abnormal fatty acids in myelin. Neurologic damage can appear before or without anemia and may be permanent."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with unrecognized B12 deficiency takes high-dose folic acid. What happens to the anemia and to the spinal cord lesions?","explain":"Folic acid is reduced to THF through dihydrofolate reductase, bypassing the methyl-folate trap, so thymidine synthesis and the anemia improve. Methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase still lack B12, so demyelination continues while the blood count hides it.","check":"Compare with the two B12-dependent reactions and the flow above."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C05","obj":"HE-9-A.5","title":"Folate deficiency and nonmegaloblastic macrocytosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Folate is found in leafy vegetables, legumes, and liver, is destroyed by prolonged cooking, and is absorbed in the jejunum after brush border enzymes cleave its polyglutamate tail. Body stores last only about 3 to 4 months, so deficiency develops far faster than B12 deficiency. Its blood and marrow findings are the same megaloblastic picture, because both deficiencies cut the supply of methylene-THF to thymidylate synthase."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of folate deficiency","cols":["Mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["Poor intake","Alcohol use disorder, poor diet in older or low-income adults"],["Increased need","Pregnancy, chronic hemolytic anemia (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis), exfoliative skin disease"],["Malabsorption","Celiac disease, tropical sprue, extensive jejunal disease"],["Blocked regeneration of THF","Methotrexate (and weaker human effects of trimethoprim and pyrimethamine) inhibit dihydrofolate reductase"]],"caption":"Alcohol lowers folate by several routes at once: intake, absorption, hepatic storage, and enterohepatic cycling."},{"type":"p","text":"Methotrexate shows the mechanism clearly. Thymidylate synthase oxidizes methylene-THF to dihydrofolate (DHF) as it makes dTMP, and dihydrofolate reductase must reduce DHF back to THF. Blocking that enzyme depletes active folate in any rapidly dividing cell, producing megaloblastic marrow and mucositis."},{"type":"table","title":"B12 versus folate deficiency","cols":["Feature","B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency"],"rows":[["Megaloblastic anemia, hypersegmented neutrophils","Yes","Yes"],["Homocysteine","High","High"],["Methylmalonic acid","High","Normal"],["Neurologic damage","Yes, subacute combined degeneration","No"],["Time for stores to run out","Years","Months"]],"caption":"Methylmalonic acid is the biochemical test that separates the two deficiencies."},{"type":"p","text":"Not every macrocytosis is megaloblastic. In nonmegaloblastic macrocytosis DNA synthesis is normal, the cells are round rather than oval, neutrophils are not hypersegmented, and the marrow is not megaloblastic. Liver disease raises cholesterol and phospholipid in the red cell membrane, enlarging the cell and producing target cells. Alcohol is directly toxic to erythroid precursors and raises the MCV even with normal folate and a normal liver, although all three problems often coexist. Reticulocytes are larger than mature red cells, so a brisk reticulocytosis after hemorrhage or in hemolysis raises the MCV; the smear shows polychromasia and the reticulocyte count is high. Hypothyroidism is another common cause."},{"type":"table","title":"Megaloblastic versus nonmegaloblastic macrocytosis","cols":["Feature","Megaloblastic (B12, folate, DNA synthesis drugs)","Nonmegaloblastic (alcohol, liver disease, reticulocytosis, hypothyroidism)"],"rows":[["Red cell shape","Oval macrocytes","Round macrocytes; target cells in liver disease"],["Neutrophils","Hypersegmented","Normal"],["Other cell lines","Often low (pancytopenia)","Usually normal"],["Reticulocytes","Low","Normal or low; high in reticulocytosis"],["LDH and indirect bilirubin","High (ineffective erythropoiesis)","Normal unless hemolysis"],["Marrow","Hypercellular with megaloblasts","Normoblastic"]],"caption":"Look at the smear and the reticulocyte count before attributing a high MCV to a vitamin deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C06","obj":"HE-9-A.6","title":"Aplastic anemia: failure of the stem cell","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Aplastic anemia is failure of the multipotent hematopoietic stem cell, giving pancytopenia with a hypocellular marrow in which fat replaces hematopoietic tissue. Because the defect lies above every lineage, red cells, granulocytes, and platelets fall together, and the reticulocyte count is low. That combination separates it from peripheral destruction or sequestration, where the marrow is full and working."},{"type":"p","text":"Most acquired cases are immune. Activated cytotoxic T cells recognize stem and progenitor cells, and interferon-gamma and tumor necrosis factor drive them into apoptosis; the disease often responds to immunosuppression, which supports this mechanism. Other causes injure stem cells directly. Some agents act in a dose-dependent, predictable way (ionizing radiation, alkylating agents and other cytotoxic chemotherapy, benzene), while others cause rare idiosyncratic aplasia (chloramphenicol, carbamazepine, some sulfonamides). Hepatitis-associated aplasia appears weeks to months after an acute hepatitis, usually one not caused by hepatitis A, B, or C. A minority of patients carry inherited telomere maintenance defects."},{"type":"p","text":"Fanconi anemia is an inherited, mainly autosomal recessive defect in repair of DNA interstrand crosslinks (FANC gene pathway). Stem cells accumulate DNA damage and die, so marrow failure usually appears in childhood. Associated findings include short stature, absent or abnormal thumbs and radii, cafe au lait spots, and renal anomalies, and chromosome breakage increases when cells are exposed to crosslinking agents such as diepoxybutane or mitomycin C, which is the diagnostic test. Survivors have a high risk of acute myeloid leukemia and squamous cell carcinomas."},{"type":"p","text":"Manifestations follow the missing cells: anemia causes fatigue and pallor, thrombocytopenia causes petechiae and mucosal bleeding, and neutropenia causes bacterial and fungal infection. The MCV is normal or mildly high. The spleen, liver, and lymph nodes are not enlarged, because the marrow is empty rather than infiltrated and there is no extramedullary hematopoiesis; splenomegaly points to another diagnosis. Aplastic anemia may later evolve into paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria or myelodysplasia, reflecting clonal escape from immune attack. Parvovirus B19 infects only erythroid progenitors, so it causes pure red cell aplasia rather than pancytopenia."},{"type":"table","title":"Telling aplastic anemia from other causes of pancytopenia","cols":["Condition","Marrow","Spleen","Distinguishing clue"],"rows":[["Aplastic anemia","Hypocellular, fatty","Normal","Low reticulocytes, no abnormal cells"],["Megaloblastic anemia","Hypercellular, megaloblasts","Normal","Oval macrocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils"],["Myelophthisis or myelofibrosis","Infiltrated or fibrotic, often a dry tap","Often enlarged","Teardrop cells, leukoerythroblastic smear"],["Hypersplenism","Normal or hypercellular","Enlarged","Cells pooled in the spleen, marrow production normal"],["Hypoplastic myelodysplasia or leukemia","Hypocellular with dysplasia or blasts","Variable","Clonal cytogenetic abnormalities"]],"caption":"A marrow biopsy is required: the peripheral count alone cannot show whether production is absent or cells are being lost."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C07","obj":"HE-9-A.7","title":"Polycythemia vera versus secondary and relative polycythemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Polycythemia means a high hemoglobin or hematocrit, above about 16.5 g/dL or 49% in men and 16 g/dL or 48% in women. The first question is whether the red cell mass is truly increased or only concentrated by lost plasma. The second is what erythropoietin is doing, because erythropoietin is the normal controller of red cell mass."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Oxygen sensing and erythropoietin feedback","steps":["Oxygen delivery to the kidney falls","HIF-2 alpha escapes degradation in peritubular interstitial cells","Erythropoietin is released into blood","Erythroid progenitors survive and divide","Red cell mass and oxygen delivery rise","HIF is degraded and erythropoietin falls"],"caption":"The loop senses oxygen delivery, not hemoglobin; the liver adds a smaller share of erythropoietin in adults."},{"type":"p","text":"Polycythemia vera (PV) is a myeloproliferative neoplasm in which the loop is bypassed at the progenitor. About 95% of patients carry JAK2 V617F, which disables the autoinhibitory pseudokinase domain of JAK2, the kinase bound to the erythropoietin receptor; most of the rest have JAK2 exon 12 mutations. Signaling through STAT5 continues without hormone, so the clone expands independently and normal feedback drives erythropoietin low or low normal. JAK2 also serves the thrombopoietin and G-CSF receptors, so platelets and neutrophils often rise too, and the marrow shows proliferation of all three lineages. Clinical clues are facial plethora, splenomegaly from extramedullary hematopoiesis, a high LAP score, and aquagenic pruritus, itching after a warm bath from histamine released by the expanded basophil and mast cell population."},{"type":"p","text":"In secondary polycythemia the clone is normal and erythropoietin is high. It is appropriate when tissue oxygen delivery is truly low: chronic lung disease, sleep apnea, high altitude, right-to-left shunts, and high oxygen affinity hemoglobins. Smoking belongs here because carbon monoxide occupies hemoglobin and shifts the dissociation curve left; standard pulse oximetry misreads carboxyhemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin and may look normal, while co-oximetry shows the true saturation. Erythropoietin is inappropriate when it is secreted without hypoxia, as by renal cell carcinoma, hepatocellular carcinoma, cerebellar hemangioblastoma, or uterine leiomyoma, or by renal cysts and hydronephrosis that compress local tissue; oxygen saturation is normal. Exogenous erythropoietin and androgens also raise red cell mass."},{"type":"p","text":"Relative polycythemia has a normal red cell mass and a reduced plasma volume, from dehydration, vomiting and diarrhea, diuretics, or burns. Erythropoietin is normal and the hematocrit corrects when plasma volume is restored."},{"type":"table","title":"Types of polycythemia","cols":["Type","Red cell mass","Erythropoietin","Arterial oxygen saturation","Examples"],"rows":[["Polycythemia vera","High","Low","Normal","JAK2 V617F neoplasm; high white cells and platelets, splenomegaly"],["Secondary, appropriate","High","High","Low (carbon monoxide: low by co-oximetry, pulse oximetry may read normal)","Lung disease, sleep apnea, altitude, cyanotic heart disease, smoking"],["Secondary, inappropriate","High","High","Normal","Renal cell or hepatocellular carcinoma, hemangioblastoma, leiomyoma, renal cysts"],["Relative","Normal","Normal","Normal","Dehydration, diuretics, burns"]],"caption":"Erythropoietin is the branch point: low in PV, high in secondary forms, normal in relative polycythemia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with a hematocrit of 58% has normal arterial oxygen saturation by co-oximetry and a high erythropoietin. What should be searched for, and why is PV unlikely?","explain":"High erythropoietin without hypoxia means a source is secreting it autonomously, most often a renal cell or hepatocellular carcinoma. In PV the clone grows without erythropoietin and feedback suppresses it.","check":"Compare with the feedback cycle and the types of polycythemia table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C08","obj":"HE-9-A.8","title":"Complications of polycythemia vera","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blood viscosity rises steeply, not linearly, as hematocrit increases. By Poiseuille's law, flow varies inversely with viscosity, so above a certain hematocrit the extra oxygen carried per milliliter is outweighed by slower flow, and delivery to the brain, heart, and digits falls. Hyperviscosity explains headache, dizziness, tinnitus, blurred vision, and angina. Thrombosis is the leading cause of illness and death in PV, and it reflects more than viscosity: clonal platelets and neutrophils are activated and adhere to endothelium, and high platelet counts add to the risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Thrombosis affects arteries (stroke, transient ischemic attack, myocardial infarction) and veins (deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism). PV and other JAK2 neoplasms are a leading identified cause of thrombosis in unusual abdominal sites: hepatic vein thrombosis (Budd-Chiari syndrome, with painful hepatomegaly and ascites) and portal or mesenteric vein thrombosis. Such a clot may be the first sign of PV, and blood counts can look only mildly abnormal because the expanded plasma volume and sequestration in a large spleen dilute them."},{"type":"p","text":"Erythromelalgia is burning pain, warmth, and redness of the hands and feet, made worse by heat, caused by platelet-rich microthrombi and inflammation in small arterioles; it can progress to digital ischemia. High cell turnover increases purine breakdown, so uric acid rises, causing gout and urate stones. Histamine from basophils and mast cells contributes to pruritus and peptic ulcer. Paradoxically, bleeding can occur when platelet counts are extremely high, because platelets adsorb and clear large von Willebrand factor multimers (acquired von Willebrand syndrome)."},{"type":"p","text":"PV is a clonal stem cell disease and evolves over years. In post-PV myelofibrosis, the spent phase, clonal megakaryocytes release fibrogenic cytokines such as transforming growth factor beta and platelet-derived growth factor, and fibroblasts lay down collagen. The marrow fails, the hematocrit falls, and hematopoiesis moves to the spleen and liver, producing massive splenomegaly, teardrop cells, and a leukoerythroblastic smear (nucleated red cells and immature granulocytes). A smaller fraction of patients transform to acute myeloid leukemia, with a poor outlook."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of polycythemia vera by mechanism","cols":["Mechanism","Complication","Key features"],"rows":[["Hyperviscosity, low flow","Neurologic and visual symptoms, angina","Headache, dizziness, tinnitus, blurred vision"],["Arterial thrombosis","Stroke, transient ischemic attack, myocardial infarction","Activated clonal platelets and leukocytes"],["Venous thrombosis","Budd-Chiari syndrome, portal or mesenteric vein thrombosis, DVT","May be the presenting event"],["Microvascular platelet occlusion","Erythromelalgia","Burning, red, warm hands and feet worsened by heat"],["High cell turnover","Hyperuricemia, gout, urate stones","Purine catabolism from expanded cell mass"],["Histamine release","Aquagenic pruritus, peptic ulcer","Basophil and mast cell expansion"],["Clonal evolution","Post-PV myelofibrosis, acute myeloid leukemia","Falling counts, massive spleen, teardrop cells, blasts"]],"caption":"Thrombosis is the main early danger; fibrosis and leukemia are late."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C09","obj":"HE-9-A.9","title":"Acute and chronic blood loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemoglobin and hematocrit are concentrations. In acute hemorrhage, red cells and plasma are lost together, so the concentration right after bleeding is normal or near normal even when the loss is large; the early clues are tachycardia, hypotension, and low urine output, not the blood count. The count falls only as plasma volume is restored. Lower capillary hydrostatic pressure draws interstitial fluid into the vessels (transcapillary refill), and the kidney retains sodium and water through the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and antidiuretic hormone. This takes about 1 to 3 days."},{"type":"flow","title":"Time course after acute hemorrhage","steps":["Whole blood lost; hemoglobin concentration unchanged","Hours: neutrophilia and thrombocytosis from stress and demargination","Hours to 3 days: fluid refill dilutes red cells; hemoglobin falls","Erythropoietin rises within hours of reduced oxygen delivery","About 3 to 5 days: reticulocytosis appears","About 7 to 10 days: reticulocyte count peaks","About 3 to 6 weeks: red cell mass restored if iron is adequate"],"caption":"The marrow response lags because progenitors need several days to mature into reticulocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"The anemia is normocytic and normochromic, and iron studies are normal unless stores were already low. Once the marrow responds, polychromasia and a high reticulocyte count appear, and because reticulocytes are large the MCV may rise slightly. A brisk reticulocyte response shows that the marrow, iron supply, and erythropoietin axis are intact, which separates blood loss and hemolysis from the production disorders in this chapter."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why the first hematocrit underestimates the loss","text":"A man with a blood volume of 5.0 L and a hematocrit of 45% has 2.25 L of red cells. He rapidly loses 1.0 L of blood, which takes 0.45 L of red cells and 0.55 L of plasma. The remaining 4.0 L has 1.8 L of red cells, and 1.8 divided by 4.0 is still 45%. Over the next 1 to 3 days, refill and renal retention return his blood volume to about 5.0 L without adding red cells. His hematocrit becomes 1.8 divided by 5.0, or 36%. The 20% loss of red cell mass was present from the first minute but became visible only after plasma volume was restored."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic blood loss behaves differently. Each milliliter of blood at a normal hemoglobin carries about 0.5 mg of iron, so losing even a few milliliters a day from the gut or through heavy menstruation exceeds the 1 to 2 mg absorbed. The hemoglobin stays normal while stores drain, then an iron deficiency anemia develops with the profile in C01, and the reticulocyte count is inappropriately low because iron limits the response. Blood that escapes into tissues or body cavities, as in a large hematoma, is broken down by macrophages and its iron is reused, so internal bleeding does not cause iron deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C10","obj":"HE-9-A.10","title":"Neutropenia: mechanisms and infection risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neutrophils are made in the marrow over about two weeks, circulate for only hours, and are split between a circulating pool and a marginated pool adherent to vessel walls. The absolute neutrophil count (ANC) equals the white cell count multiplied by the fraction of segmented neutrophils plus bands. Neutropenia is an ANC below about 1500/µL in adults, graded as mild (1000 to 1500), moderate (500 to 1000), or severe (below 500/µL). Agranulocytosis is a severe, usually abrupt, loss of neutrophils, often defined as an ANC below 500/µL."},{"type":"p","text":"Infection risk climbs as the ANC falls and becomes high below 500/µL, especially when neutropenia is prolonged. Infections come mainly from the patient's own flora: gram-negative enteric bacilli and Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Staphylococcus aureus, coagulase-negative staphylococci, and streptococci, with Candida and, after prolonged neutropenia, Aspergillus. Because neutrophils make pus and much of the local inflammatory reaction, signs are muted, and fever may be the only finding; oral ulcers and mucositis are common. Defense against viruses depends mainly on lymphocytes and is relatively preserved."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of neutropenia","cols":["Mechanism","Examples","Notes"],"rows":[["Marrow failure or replacement","Aplastic anemia, leukemia, metastatic infiltration, megaloblastic anemia, radiation","Other cell lines usually low too"],["Drug-induced, dose-dependent","Cytotoxic chemotherapy","Predictable suppression of dividing precursors"],["Drug-induced, idiosyncratic agranulocytosis","Propylthiouracil, methimazole, clozapine, sulfonamides","Immune destruction or direct toxicity to precursors; abrupt fever and sore throat"],["Immune destruction","Autoimmune neutropenia, systemic lupus erythematosus, Felty syndrome (rheumatoid arthritis with splenomegaly), neonatal alloimmune neutropenia","Antibodies or T cells remove neutrophils; Felty syndrome adds splenic sequestration"],["Sequestration","Hypersplenism","Cells pooled in an enlarged spleen"],["Inherited production defect","Cyclic neutropenia (ELANE), severe congenital neutropenia (ELANE, HAX1)","Promyelocyte apoptosis; continuous maturation arrest in the congenital form"]],"caption":"Neutropenia arises from low production, destruction, or redistribution."},{"type":"p","text":"Cyclic neutropenia is usually autosomal dominant and caused by mutations in ELANE, the gene for neutrophil elastase. Mutant elastase misfolds and triggers the unfolded protein response and apoptosis in promyelocytes, and, for reasons not fully explained, the count oscillates with a period of about 21 days, with neutropenia lasting several days in each cycle, typically bringing fever, mouth ulcers, and infections. Other ELANE mutations cause the more severe, continuous severe congenital neutropenia. An important exception is the lower ANC seen in many healthy people of African or Middle Eastern ancestry who have the Duffy-null red cell phenotype; neutrophil production and function are normal, and infection risk is not increased."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-01-C11","obj":"HE-9-A.11","title":"Leukemoid reaction versus chronic myeloid leukemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A leukemoid reaction is an exaggerated, normal response of the marrow, usually a white count above about 50,000/µL, to severe infection (for example Clostridioides difficile colitis or tuberculosis), sepsis, hemorrhage, acute hemolysis, or a tumor that secretes G-CSF. G-CSF and other cytokines speed release of marrow neutrophils and shorten maturation, so less mature forms enter the blood. This is the left shift: more bands, with metamyelocytes and occasional myelocytes. Cells released early show toxic granulation (prominent primary granules), Döhle bodies (patches of residual rough endoplasmic reticulum), and cytoplasmic vacuoles. Activated, polyclonal neutrophils have a high LAP score, and the count falls once the cause resolves."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is a myeloproliferative neoplasm defined by BCR-ABL1, the fusion gene formed by the reciprocal translocation t(9;22) that creates the Philadelphia chromosome. The fusion protein is a constitutively active tyrosine kinase that drives proliferation and resists apoptosis. The clone matures, so the blood shows granulocytes at every stage, with peaks of myelocytes and segmented neutrophils and few blasts in chronic phase. Basophilia and often eosinophilia and thrombocytosis are characteristic, and the spleen is enlarged. The clonal neutrophils have a low LAP score. Without control, CML progresses to an accelerated phase and then a blast crisis resembling acute leukemia."},{"type":"table","title":"Leukemoid reaction versus chronic myeloid leukemia","cols":["Feature","Leukemoid reaction","Chronic myeloid leukemia"],"rows":[["Cause","Reactive, polyclonal response to infection, inflammation, or tumor G-CSF","Clonal stem cell neoplasm with BCR-ABL1"],["Left shift","Mainly bands and metamyelocytes","Full spectrum, myelocyte peak"],["Toxic granulation, Döhle bodies","Common","Usually absent"],["Basophils and eosinophils","Not increased","Increased; basophilia is a key clue"],["LAP score","High","Low"],["Spleen","Usually normal","Often markedly enlarged"],["Course","Resolves with the cause","Persists and progresses"]],"caption":"Basophilia, low LAP, and splenomegaly point to CML; demonstrating BCR-ABL1 confirms it."},{"type":"p","text":"LAP is not specific to one disease. It is high in PV and in pregnancy, and low in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, so it supports rather than proves a diagnosis, and molecular testing for BCR-ABL1 has largely replaced it. A leukemoid reaction should also be distinguished from a leukoerythroblastic picture, in which nucleated red cells and immature granulocytes appear because the marrow is infiltrated or fibrotic rather than stimulated."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each disorder in this chapter is a failure of supply, signal, or stem cell. Supply failures come from gut anatomy and biochemistry: iron is absorbed in the duodenum, folate in the jejunum, and B12 in the terminal ileum after binding a gastric protein, so celiac disease, gastrectomy, atrophic gastritis, Crohn disease, and a tapeworm each predict a specific deficiency. Heme and thymidine pathways explain the cell size, with too little hemoglobin making small cells and too little DNA synthesis making large ones. Signal failures involve hepcidin, erythropoietin, and G-CSF, which link inflammation, renal oxygen sensing, and infection to blood counts. Stem cell failures range from immune destruction in aplastic anemia to clonal growth in PV and CML."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and microbiology recur throughout: autoantibodies against parietal cells and neutrophils, cytotoxic T cells against stem cells, and IL-6 against iron availability, while neutropenia sets up infection with endogenous bacteria and fungi. Ferritin, LAP, MCV, and the first hemoglobin after bleeding can all mislead when inflammation, coexisting deficiencies, or time course are ignored, so values are read together with the smear and the clinical setting."}],"summary":["Iron deficiency lowers ferritin first, then raises TIBC and lowers saturation, and finally produces a microcytic hypochromic anemia with a high RDW.","In anemia of chronic disease, IL-6 induces hepcidin, which degrades ferroportin and traps iron in macrophages, so ferritin is normal or high and TIBC is low.","Thalassemia trait has normal iron studies, a high red cell count, and target cells; sideroblastic anemia has high iron and ringed sideroblasts.","Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, raising ALA and zinc protoporphyrin, and inhibits pyrimidine 5' nucleotidase, causing basophilic stippling.","B12 deficiency traps folate and blocks methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, so megaloblastic anemia comes with high methylmalonic acid and subacute combined degeneration.","Folate deficiency gives the same megaloblastic blood picture within months, with high homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid, and no neurologic damage.","Aplastic anemia is stem cell failure, usually T cell mediated, with pancytopenia, low reticulocytes, a fatty marrow, and no splenomegaly.","PV has JAK2 V617F and low erythropoietin; secondary polycythemia has high erythropoietin, appropriate with hypoxia and inappropriate with tumors; relative polycythemia has a normal red cell mass.","After acute hemorrhage the hemoglobin falls only as plasma volume is restored, and reticulocytes peak at about 7 to 10 days; chronic loss causes iron deficiency.","An ANC below 500/µL carries a high risk of bacterial and fungal infection; a leukemoid reaction has high LAP and toxic granulation, while CML has basophilia, low LAP, and BCR-ABL1."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders (anemias of diminished erythropoiesis)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus (leukopenia, reactive leukocytosis, myeloproliferative neoplasms)"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins (folic acid and cobalamin) and heme synthesis"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood Disorders"}]},"HE-9-B":{"unit":"HE-9-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02","title":"Diseases of Blood Cell Lysis","subject":"Hematopoietic System (HEME)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers anemias caused by red cells being destroyed before the end of their normal 120-day lifespan, along with the spleen disorders that decide where much of that destruction happens. It moves from hemoglobin defects (sickle cell disease, beta thalassemia) through enzyme defects (G6PD and pyruvate kinase deficiency), membrane defects (hereditary spherocytosis and elliptocytosis), antibody and complement injury (hemolytic disease of the newborn, PNH), and mechanical injury (TTP, HUS, valve hemolysis). Board questions usually give a smear finding, a lab pattern, or a complication and ask you to name the defect and the site of destruction. Once you can explain why a cell is lysed and where, most findings follow.","prerequisites":["Hemoglobin structure, oxygen binding, and the switch from fetal (gamma) to adult (beta) globin chains (HE-5, HE-6)","Red cell metabolism: glycolysis for ATP and the pentose phosphate pathway for NADPH (HE-5)","Heme breakdown to unconjugated bilirubin and hepatic conjugation by UGT1A1 (HE-3)","Red cell membrane and cytoskeleton: lipid bilayer, spectrin, ankyrin, band 3","Complement pathways, IgG and IgM antibodies, and splenic anatomy (HE-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Hemoglobin biochemistry and hematologic genetics (HE-5, HE-6)","how":"The molecular lesions of HbS, thalassemia, G6PD, and pyruvate kinase deficiency taught there produce the diseases described here."},{"to":"Immune responses in hematologic disease (HE-7)","how":"Autoimmune hemolysis, transfusion reactions, and asplenic infection share the antibody, complement, and splenic mechanisms used in this chapter."},{"to":"Diseases of blood cell production (HE-9-A)","how":"Reticulocyte count and marrow cellularity separate destruction from underproduction, and chronic urinary iron loss links hemolysis to iron deficiency."},{"to":"Clotting abnormalities (HE-9-C)","how":"TTP, HUS, and DIC all consume platelets and fragment red cells, but only DIC prolongs the PT and PTT."},{"to":"Hepatobiliary and renal pathology","how":"Unconjugated bilirubin from hemolysis forms pigment gallstones and causes kernicterus, while free hemoglobin and Shiga toxin injure the kidney."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hemolysis","def":"Premature destruction of red cells, either by macrophages (extravascular) or within the circulation (intravascular)."},{"term":"Reticulocyte","def":"A young red cell released from the marrow within the last day or so; its count rises when the marrow responds to red cell loss."},{"term":"Haptoglobin","def":"A plasma protein that binds free hemoglobin and is cleared with it by the liver, so its level falls in hemolysis."},{"term":"Ineffective erythropoiesis","def":"Death of red cell precursors inside the marrow before they mature, so marrow activity is high but reticulocyte output is low."},{"term":"Spherocyte","def":"A small, round red cell without central pallor that has lost membrane surface area relative to its volume."},{"term":"Schistocyte","def":"A fragmented red cell (helmet cell, triangle) produced by shear or by passage through fibrin and platelet strands."},{"term":"Heinz body","def":"A clump of oxidized, denatured hemoglobin attached to the inner membrane, seen only with a supravital stain."},{"term":"Direct antiglobulin test (DAT)","def":"The direct Coombs test, which detects IgG or complement already bound to a person's red cells."},{"term":"Indirect antiglobulin test (IAT)","def":"The indirect Coombs test, which detects free red cell antibodies in a person's serum."},{"term":"Hydrops fetalis","def":"Abnormal fluid accumulation in two or more fetal compartments, such as skin edema, ascites, and pleural or pericardial effusion."},{"term":"Thrombotic microangiopathy","def":"Platelet-rich thrombi in small vessels causing thrombocytopenia, fragmentation hemolysis, and organ ischemia."},{"term":"Hypersplenism","def":"Cytopenias caused by pooling and destruction of blood cells in an enlarged spleen while the marrow stays active."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C01","obj":"HE-9-B.1","title":"Sickle cell disease: vaso-occlusion and hemolysis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every hemolytic anemia shares a core pattern: the marrow responds with reticulocytes, broken cells release lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), heme is converted to unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin, and haptoglobin falls as it binds free hemoglobin. Where the cell dies adds a second layer, summarized below and used throughout this chapter."},{"type":"table","title":"Extravascular versus intravascular hemolysis","cols":["Feature","Extravascular","Intravascular"],"rows":[["Site","Macrophages of spleen (and liver)","Inside blood vessels"],["Typical causes","Sickle cell, thalassemia, spherocytosis, warm (IgG) autoimmune hemolysis","G6PD crisis, PNH, TTP, valve shear"],["Haptoglobin","Low","Very low or absent"],["Unconjugated bilirubin and gallstones","High; pigment stones common with chronic disease","High"],["Free plasma hemoglobin, hemoglobinuria, hemosiderinuria","Absent","Present"],["Spleen","Often enlarged (work hypertrophy)","Usually normal size"]],"caption":"Both raise reticulocytes and LDH; many diseases mix the two sites."},{"type":"p","text":"Sickle cell disease is autosomal recessive. A point mutation in the beta globin gene (HBB) replaces glutamate with valine at position 6, producing HbS. When HbS gives up oxygen, the valine fits a hydrophobic pocket on a neighboring molecule and the molecules stack into long polymers that bend the cell into a rigid sickle. Polymerization takes a short delay, so most cells clear the capillaries first; anything that raises the deoxygenated fraction or HbS concentration, or slows transit, tips the balance. Hypoxia, acidosis (which favors the deoxy state), and dehydration (which concentrates HbS) promote sickling. HbF does not enter the polymer, so infants are protected until gamma chain production falls at about 6 months."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The sickling cycle","steps":["Hypoxia, acidosis, or dehydration in a vascular bed","Deoxygenated HbS polymerizes; cells sickle and stiffen","Rigid, adherent cells obstruct small vessels","Downstream ischemia and infarction","Local hypoxia and acidosis increase"],"caption":"Occlusion worsens the conditions that caused it, which is why crises are self-amplifying."},{"type":"p","text":"Repeated sickling damages the membrane, so cells are removed early in the spleen (extravascular hemolysis, with an intravascular share). Red cell survival falls to roughly 10 to 20 days. Free hemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, adding vasoconstriction and endothelial activation."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of sickle cell disease","cols":["Complication","Mechanism","Typical setting"],"rows":[["Dactylitis","Infarction of marrow in small bones of hands and feet","Painful swollen hands and feet in infants"],["Acute chest syndrome","Pulmonary sickling after infection, marrow fat embolism, or hypoventilation","Chest pain, fever, hypoxemia, new infiltrate; leading adult cause of death"],["Stroke","Intimal hyperplasia of distal internal carotid and middle cerebral arteries","Ischemic stroke, mainly in children"],["Splenic sequestration, then autoinfarction","Pooling of sickled cells, then repeated infarcts and fibrosis","Functional asplenia early; Howell-Jolly bodies; encapsulated organism sepsis"],["Salmonella osteomyelitis","Infarcted bone, gut microinfarcts admitting bacteria, poor splenic clearance","Classic association; Staphylococcus aureus also common"],["Renal papillary necrosis","Hypoxic, acidic, hypertonic medulla favors sickling","Hematuria, poor urine concentration; also in trait"],["Priapism","Sickling in corpora cavernosa blocks venous outflow","Painful ischemic erection"],["Pigment gallstones","Chronic hemolysis raises bilirubin in bile","Black stones, even in childhood"],["Aplastic crisis","Parvovirus B19 infects erythroid progenitors (P antigen)","Sudden anemia, reticulocytes near zero"]],"caption":"Occlusion explains the infarcts; hemolysis explains gallstones and marrow-arrest vulnerability."},{"type":"p","text":"Aplastic crisis shows why red cell survival matters. Parvovirus stops red cell production for about a week, which a normal person with 120-day cells barely notices, but a patient whose cells live 10 to 20 days loses a large share of red cell mass. Sickle cell trait (about 40% HbS) causes no anemia, yet the renal medulla is harsh enough to cause hematuria, impaired concentration, and papillary necrosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 6-year-old with sickle cell disease has a fever and his hemoglobin falls from 8 to 4 g/dL over 3 days. The reticulocyte count is 0.2% and the spleen is not palpable. What is happening?","explain":"Parvovirus B19 has halted erythropoiesis, and short red cell survival turns a brief arrest into severe anemia. Sequestration would give a high reticulocyte count and an enlarging spleen.","check":"Compare with the aplastic crisis and splenic sequestration rows of the complications table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C02","obj":"HE-9-B.2","title":"Beta thalassemia: ineffective erythropoiesis and iron overload","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal adult hemoglobin is about 95 to 98% HbA (alpha2 beta2), about 2 to 3.5% HbA2 (alpha2 delta2), and under about 1% HbF (alpha2 gamma2). Beta thalassemia is usually caused by point mutations in HBB on chromosome 11, affecting the promoter, splice sites, or creating stop codons. These either abolish beta chain synthesis (beta0) or reduce it (beta+). Alpha thalassemia, by contrast, is mostly due to gene deletions (HE-6.2)."},{"type":"p","text":"In beta thalassemia major (beta0 or severe beta+ on both alleles), the harm comes from the unpaired alpha chains rather than from the missing beta chains alone. Free alpha chains are insoluble, precipitate in developing erythroblasts, damage the membrane, and trigger apoptosis inside the marrow. This is ineffective erythropoiesis: the marrow is crowded with precursors, yet few reticulocytes leave it. The cells that do escape are microcytic, hypochromic, and carry inclusions, so the spleen removes them early as well. Symptoms begin at about 6 months, when HbF production has largely switched off."},{"type":"flow","title":"From missing beta chains to iron overload","steps":["Little or no beta globin synthesis","Excess alpha chains precipitate in erythroblasts","Precursors die in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis)","Severe anemia drives high erythropoietin","Marrow expands; extramedullary hematopoiesis in liver and spleen","Erythroferrone from erythroblasts suppresses hepcidin","Gut iron absorption rises; transfusion iron adds more","Iron deposits in heart, liver, and endocrine glands"],"caption":"Anemia, bone changes, organomegaly, and iron overload all follow from precursor death in the marrow."},{"type":"p","text":"Marrow expansion widens the diploic space and thins the cortex, giving the crew-cut (hair-on-end) skull on radiographs, maxillary overgrowth with dental malocclusion, and pathologic fractures. Extramedullary hematopoiesis in the liver and spleen, together with splenic work hypertrophy from clearing abnormal cells, produces hepatosplenomegaly. The body has no regulated route to excrete iron, so iron from increased absorption and transfusions accumulates; cardiac iron (cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia) is a major cause of death, and liver fibrosis, diabetes, hypogonadism, and growth failure follow. The smear shows marked microcytosis, target cells, and nucleated red cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading hemoglobin electrophoresis in microcytosis","cols":["Condition","HbA","HbA2","HbF","Other clues"],"rows":[["Normal adult","About 95 to 98%","About 2 to 3.5%","Under about 1%","Normal indices"],["Beta thalassemia minor","Slightly reduced","Above about 3.5%","Normal or mildly raised","Mild anemia, very low MCV, normal or high RBC count, normal ferritin"],["Beta thalassemia major (beta0)","Absent","Variable, often raised","Most of the hemoglobin","Severe anemia from about 6 months, nucleated red cells"],["Iron deficiency","Normal proportion","Normal or low","Normal","Low ferritin, high RDW, RBC count reduced"]],"caption":"A raised HbA2 with normal iron studies marks beta thalassemia minor."},{"type":"p","text":"In beta thalassemia minor, one gene still works, so beta chain output is about halved. Some spare alpha chains pair with delta chains, which raises HbA2 above about 3.5%, the diagnostic marker. Coexisting iron deficiency lowers HbA2 and can hide the diagnosis until iron is restored."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: microcytosis without iron deficiency","text":"A 24-year-old woman of Mediterranean descent has hemoglobin 11.0 g/dL, MCV 63 fL, and RBC count 5.8 million/µL; ferritin is normal. Step 1: the MCV is far lower than the mild anemia would predict, and the RBC count is high, so the marrow is making many small cells rather than too few cells. Step 2: normal ferritin excludes iron deficiency. Step 3: electrophoresis shows HbA2 5.2% and HbF 2%. Raised HbA2 indicates reduced beta chain output with spare alpha chains pairing with delta. Diagnosis: beta thalassemia minor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C03","obj":"HE-9-B.3","title":"G6PD deficiency: oxidant hemolysis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Red cells have no mitochondria, so the pentose phosphate pathway is their only source of NADPH. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) catalyzes its first, rate-limiting step. Glutathione reductase uses NADPH to keep glutathione in its reduced form (GSH), and glutathione peroxidase uses GSH to convert hydrogen peroxide to water. Without enough G6PD, an oxidant load overwhelms this defense."},{"type":"p","text":"Unopposed hydrogen peroxide then oxidizes hemoglobin and membrane proteins. Oxidized hemoglobin denatures and precipitates as Heinz bodies attached to the inner membrane, and the damaged, rigid cells lyse in the circulation (mainly intravascular hemolysis). Splenic macrophages pluck Heinz bodies from surviving cells, leaving bite cells (an extravascular share)."},{"type":"p","text":"G6PD deficiency is X-linked recessive, so hemizygous males are affected; heterozygous females vary because of random X inactivation. It is most common in people of African, Mediterranean, Middle Eastern, and Asian ancestry, reflecting partial protection against Plasmodium falciparum malaria. The common African variant (G6PD A minus) is an unstable enzyme that is adequate in young cells but runs out in older ones, so hemolysis is moderate and self-limited. The Mediterranean variant has very low activity in cells of all ages, so episodes are severe and favism occurs."},{"type":"table","title":"Common triggers and why they cause oxidant injury","cols":["Trigger","Source of oxidant stress"],"rows":[["Infection (most common trigger)","Reactive oxygen species released by activated phagocytes"],["Fava beans","Divicine and isouramil, derived from vicine and convicine, generate oxidants (favism, mainly Mediterranean variant)"],["Sulfonamides, dapsone, primaquine, nitrofurantoin","Drugs or metabolites that produce hydrogen peroxide or redox cycle"]],"caption":"Drugs appear here only as oxidant triggers that explain the mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Between episodes, blood counts and the smear are usually normal. One to three days after exposure, the patient develops sudden anemia, back or abdominal pain, dark urine from hemoglobinuria, and jaundice. Heinz bodies are invisible on a routine Wright stain and need a supravital stain such as crystal violet or new methylene blue. Bite cells (cells missing a semicircular piece of their edge) and blister cells appear on the routine smear. The DAT is negative. An enzyme assay done during an episode can be falsely normal, because the oldest, most deficient cells have already lysed and reticulocytes carry more enzyme; repeating it weeks later avoids this error. G6PD deficiency is also an important cause of neonatal jaundice."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C04","obj":"HE-9-B.4","title":"Hereditary spherocytosis: membrane loss and splenic trapping","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The red cell membrane is a lipid bilayer held to an underlying spectrin cytoskeleton. Vertical linkages tie the bilayer to the skeleton: ankyrin binds beta spectrin to band 3, the anion exchanger, and protein 4.2 stabilizes this link. Horizontal linkages join spectrin dimers into tetramers and attach them to actin and protein 4.1, which lets the skeleton stretch and recoil. A normal cell has spare surface area, so it can fold through openings far narrower than its 7 to 8 µm diameter."},{"type":"p","text":"Hereditary spherocytosis is autosomal dominant in about 75% of cases and is the most common inherited hemolytic anemia in people of Northern European ancestry. Defects in ankyrin (the most common), band 3, spectrin, or protein 4.2 weaken the vertical links. Lipid that is not anchored buds off as microvesicles, so the cell loses surface area while keeping most of its volume and becomes a sphere, the shape with the least surface for its volume. To return from the splenic cords to the sinuses, a red cell must squeeze through slits in the sinus wall a few micrometers wide. Spherocytes cannot deform enough, so they are held in the cords, where low glucose and acid conditions strip more membrane, and macrophages engulf them."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in hereditary spherocytosis and their causes","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Spherocytes without central pallor","Reduced surface area for volume"],["MCHC high (above about 36 g/dL)","Cell dehydration and loss of membrane concentrate hemoglobin"],["MCV normal or slightly low","Volume is largely preserved"],["Reticulocytosis, jaundice","Marrow response; heme converted to unconjugated bilirubin"],["Splenomegaly","Work hypertrophy of red pulp clearing trapped cells"],["Pigment gallstones","Chronic high bilirubin excretion into bile"],["Increased osmotic fragility","No spare surface, so cells lyse at higher saline concentrations as water enters"],["Reduced eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) binding","Less band 3 and related proteins for the dye to bind on flow cytometry"],["Negative DAT","The defect is intrinsic, not antibody coating"],["Aplastic crisis with parvovirus B19","Short red cell survival, as in sickle cell disease"]],"caption":"The table links each lab result to membrane loss or splenic destruction."},{"type":"p","text":"The DAT is the key distinction from warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, which also produces spherocytes, because there macrophages nibble IgG-coated membrane (HE-7.1). A positive family history and a negative DAT point to hereditary spherocytosis. Spherocytes persist after the spleen is removed, yet red cell survival improves, which confirms that the spleen is the site of destruction rather than the cause of the shape change."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C05","obj":"HE-9-B.5","title":"Rh and ABO hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Rh D antigen is a membrane protein on red cells; people who are D negative usually lack the RHD gene entirely. Unlike anti-A and anti-B, anti-D does not form naturally, because no common environmental antigen mimics D. It forms only after exposure to D-positive red cells, through transfusion or pregnancy."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal red cells enter the maternal circulation in small amounts during pregnancy and in larger amounts at delivery, miscarriage, abortion, ectopic pregnancy, abdominal trauma, or invasive procedures. In a D-negative mother carrying a D-positive fetus, this first exposure produces a primary response that is slow and begins with IgM, which cannot cross the placenta, so the first child is usually spared. The response leaves memory B cells. In a later D-positive pregnancy, even a small bleed provokes a rapid secondary response of high-affinity IgG, mainly IgG1 and IgG3, which the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) on the syncytiotrophoblast actively carries into the fetus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rh sensitization and fetal hemolysis","steps":["D-negative mother carries a D-positive fetus","Fetal red cells enter her blood, mostly at delivery","Primary response forms memory B cells","Next D-positive fetus triggers a rapid IgG anti-D response","IgG crosses the placenta through FcRn","Fetal red cells coated with IgG are destroyed in the fetal spleen"],"caption":"Disease usually appears in the second or later D-positive pregnancy."},{"type":"p","text":"Destruction of antibody-coated cells in the fetal spleen causes anemia, and the anemia drives erythropoietin. The fetus responds with extramedullary hematopoiesis in the liver and spleen, giving hepatosplenomegaly and large numbers of circulating nucleated red cells, the erythroblastosis that gave the disease its name. In severe disease, two forces combine to cause hydrops fetalis. Severe anemia produces high-output cardiac failure and raised venous pressure, and a liver crowded with hematopoietic tissue makes less albumin, lowering plasma oncotic pressure. Fluid leaves the vessels into the skin, peritoneum, pleura, and pericardium, and the fetus may die in utero. Before birth the placenta clears bilirubin into the mother, so the fetus is anemic but not markedly jaundiced. Antibodies to other antigens, notably Kell, can cause similar disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Rh versus ABO hemolytic disease","cols":["Feature","Rh (anti-D)","ABO"],"rows":[["Typical mother and infant","D-negative mother, D-positive infant","Group O mother, group A or B infant"],["Antibody","Immune IgG anti-D after prior sensitization","Naturally occurring IgG anti-A or anti-B"],["First pregnancy affected","Rarely","Often"],["Antigen on fetal red cells","Fully expressed","Weakly expressed"],["Severity","Can be severe: anemia, hydrops, fetal death","Usually mild: early jaundice, mild anemia"],["DAT on infant","Strongly positive","Often weakly positive"],["Smear","Many nucleated red cells","Spherocytes"]],"caption":"ABO disease is more common; Rh disease is more dangerous."},{"type":"p","text":"ABO disease affects infants of group O mothers because group O people make a larger IgG component of anti-A and anti-B than group A or B people, and this IgG can cross the placenta in a first pregnancy. It stays mild because A and B antigens are weakly developed on fetal red cells, and A and B substances on other fetal tissues and in body fluids absorb much of the antibody. ABO incompatibility also partly protects against Rh sensitization, because the mother's anti-A or anti-B clears incompatible fetal cells before the D antigen can be recognized."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C06","obj":"HE-9-B.6","title":"Coombs testing and neonatal bilirubin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"IgG molecules attached to red cells are too small to bridge neighboring cells, so IgG-coated cells do not agglutinate on their own. Antihuman globulin (Coombs reagent) binds IgG or complement on one cell and links it to the next, producing visible clumping. The two antiglobulin tests ask different questions."},{"type":"table","title":"Direct and indirect antiglobulin tests","cols":["Feature","Direct (DAT)","Indirect (IAT)"],"rows":[["Question asked","Are this person's red cells already coated with antibody or complement?","Does this person's serum contain free antibody against red cell antigens?"],["Sample","Patient red cells, washed, plus antiglobulin","Patient serum incubated with reagent red cells of known antigens, then antiglobulin"],["Tested in hemolytic disease","Infant (cord blood)","Mother"],["Positive result means","Maternal IgG is bound to the infant's red cells","Mother is sensitized; titer reflects antibody level"]],"caption":"Direct tests the cells; indirect tests the serum."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: interpreting Coombs results","text":"A D-negative woman in her second pregnancy has a positive IAT with anti-D, and the titer rises across pregnancy. Her infant is D positive, the cord DAT is strongly positive, and cord hemoglobin is 10 g/dL. Step 1: the positive IAT shows free anti-D in the mother, so she was sensitized earlier, most likely by her first pregnancy. Step 2: the positive infant DAT shows that this IgG crossed the placenta and coated fetal cells. Step 3: low hemoglobin confirms active hemolysis. If the infant had been D negative, the DAT would be negative and the baby unaffected, despite the mother's antibody."},{"type":"p","text":"Before birth, unconjugated bilirubin from fetal hemolysis is lipid soluble, crosses the placenta, and is conjugated by the mother's liver, so cord bilirubin is only modestly raised. Clamping the cord removes this route. Maternal IgG persists in the infant for weeks (half-life about 3 weeks), so hemolysis continues, while the newborn liver has low UGT1A1 activity for the first days of life. Unconjugated bilirubin therefore climbs quickly, and jaundice within the first 24 hours is always pathologic. Continued hemolysis can also cause a late anemia several weeks after birth."},{"type":"flow","title":"From birth to kernicterus","steps":["Placental clearance of bilirubin ends at birth","Maternal IgG keeps destroying infant red cells","Heme load yields large amounts of unconjugated bilirubin","Immature UGT1A1 cannot conjugate enough","Albumin binding sites saturate; free bilirubin rises","Free bilirubin crosses the blood-brain barrier","Deposits in basal ganglia and brainstem nuclei: kernicterus"],"caption":"Only unbound unconjugated bilirubin enters the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"Kernicterus is staining and injury of the globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus, hippocampus, cerebellum, and brainstem nuclei, including oculomotor and auditory nuclei. Acute bilirubin encephalopathy causes lethargy, hypotonia, and a high-pitched cry, followed by hypertonia and arching (opisthotonus). Survivors may have athetoid cerebral palsy, sensorineural hearing loss, upward gaze palsy, and dental enamel dysplasia. Risk rises with prematurity, acidosis, low albumin, sepsis, and drugs that displace bilirubin from albumin, such as sulfonamides and ceftriaxone. Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and does not cause kernicterus."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A group O, D-positive mother delivers a group A infant who is jaundiced at 18 hours. Predict the infant DAT, the mother's IAT for anti-D, and the smear.","explain":"This is ABO disease: the infant DAT is weakly positive from IgG anti-A, the mother has no anti-D because she is D positive, and the smear shows spherocytes. Early jaundice reflects continuing hemolysis once placental clearance stops.","check":"Compare with the Rh versus ABO table and the antiglobulin test table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C07","obj":"HE-9-B.7","title":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Many surface proteins are attached to the outer membrane by a glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor built in the endoplasmic reticulum. PIGA, an X-linked gene, encodes a subunit of the enzyme for the first step of anchor synthesis. In paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH), an acquired somatic PIGA mutation arises in a hematopoietic stem cell. Because only one active X chromosome is present in both sexes, a single mutation removes all GPI-anchored proteins from every descendant: red cells, granulocytes, monocytes, platelets, and some lymphocytes. PNH is therefore an acquired clonal disorder, not an inherited one."},{"type":"p","text":"Two lost proteins explain the hemolysis. CD55 (decay-accelerating factor) speeds the breakdown of C3 and C5 convertases on the cell surface. CD59 (membrane inhibitor of reactive lysis) binds C8 and C9 and blocks assembly of the membrane attack complex (MAC); its loss matters most. The alternative pathway deposits C3b on cells continuously at a low rate, and without these regulators the MAC punches holes in red cells inside vessels. Hemolysis worsens with infection, surgery, or exertion. The name reflects dark urine on waking, classically attributed to mild respiratory acidosis during sleep enhancing complement activity, but many patients do not show a nocturnal pattern."},{"type":"table","title":"Manifestations of PNH and their mechanisms","cols":["Manifestation","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Intravascular hemolysis: high LDH, absent haptoglobin, hemoglobinuria, hemosiderinuria","MAC lysis of red cells lacking CD59; DAT negative because no antibody is involved"],["Iron deficiency","Chronic iron loss in urine as hemoglobin and hemosiderin"],["Venous thrombosis: hepatic vein (Budd-Chiari), portal, mesenteric, cerebral sinus","Complement-activated platelets, procoagulant microparticles, and nitric oxide depletion; the leading cause of death"],["Abdominal pain, esophageal spasm, erectile dysfunction","Nitric oxide depletion causes smooth muscle contraction"],["Cytopenias","Underlying marrow failure; PNH often arises with aplastic anemia because the GPI-deficient clone escapes immune attack on the marrow"],["Later evolution","Myelodysplastic syndrome or acute myeloid leukemia in a minority"]],"caption":"Thrombosis at unusual venous sites with hemolysis and cytopenias should raise suspicion of PNH."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis rests on flow cytometry showing absent CD55 and CD59 on red cells and granulocytes. Fluorescent aerolysin (FLAER), which binds the GPI anchor itself, is used on white cells. Granulocytes give a more reliable clone size than red cells, because PNH red cells are continually destroyed and transfused cells dilute them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C08","obj":"HE-9-B.8","title":"Pyruvate kinase deficiency and hereditary elliptocytosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Without mitochondria, red cells make all their ATP by glycolysis. Pyruvate kinase (PK) catalyzes the last step, converting phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate and generating ATP. PK deficiency is autosomal recessive (PKLR gene, red cell isozyme) and is the most common glycolytic enzyme defect that causes hemolysis. Low ATP starves the Na+/K+ ATPase and the Ca2+ pump, so the cell loses potassium and water, gains calcium, and becomes dehydrated and rigid. Spiculated cells (echinocytes) appear on the smear, and the spleen removes the stiff cells, so hemolysis is extravascular and chronic from birth, with neonatal jaundice, splenomegaly, and pigment gallstones."},{"type":"p","text":"The block also causes glycolytic intermediates upstream of PK to accumulate, including 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG). Higher 2,3-BPG shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to the right, so hemoglobin unloads more oxygen to tissue, and patients tolerate a given hemoglobin level better than expected. G6PD deficiency differs in timing: counts are normal between oxidant episodes rather than continuously hemolytic."},{"type":"p","text":"Hereditary elliptocytosis is usually autosomal dominant and results from defects in horizontal skeletal interactions, most often alpha or beta spectrin mutations that impair joining of spectrin dimers into tetramers, or protein 4.1 defects. The skeleton cannot recover its shape after the cell is stretched in the circulation, so cells become permanently oval or elongated. Most people with the trait have many elliptocytes but little or no hemolysis. Homozygous or compound heterozygous forms cause more severe hemolysis, and hereditary pyropoikilocytosis, the most severe form, shows fragments and microspherocytes. Elliptocytosis is common in regions with endemic malaria."},{"type":"table","title":"Inherited intrinsic red cell defects compared","cols":["Disorder","Inheritance","Defect","Smear","Hemolysis pattern"],"rows":[["Hereditary spherocytosis","Mostly autosomal dominant","Vertical links: ankyrin, band 3, spectrin, protein 4.2","Spherocytes","Chronic, extravascular; high MCHC"],["Hereditary elliptocytosis","Mostly autosomal dominant","Horizontal links: spectrin tetramer formation, protein 4.1","Elliptocytes","Usually mild or none"],["Pyruvate kinase deficiency","Autosomal recessive","Glycolysis: low ATP, pump failure","Echinocytes","Chronic, extravascular; high 2,3-BPG"],["G6PD deficiency","X-linked recessive","Pentose phosphate pathway: low NADPH","Bite cells; Heinz bodies with supravital stain","Episodic after oxidant stress, mainly intravascular"]],"caption":"All four have a negative DAT; inheritance, smear, and timing separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C09","obj":"HE-9-B.9","title":"Thrombotic microangiopathies: TTP and HUS","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) and hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) are thrombotic microangiopathies: platelet-rich thrombi form in arterioles and capillaries. Platelets are consumed, causing thrombocytopenia. Red cells forced through partly blocked vessels under high shear are torn into schistocytes, giving microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with high LDH, low haptoglobin, and reticulocytosis. Because the thrombi are built from platelets and the coagulation cascade is not broadly activated, the PT, PTT, and fibrinogen stay normal. That single point separates both conditions from disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)."},{"type":"p","text":"In TTP the defect is in von Willebrand factor (vWF) processing. Endothelial cells secrete vWF as ultralarge multimers, which bind platelets most avidly. The plasma metalloprotease ADAMTS13 normally cleaves these multimers into smaller forms as soon as shear stress unfolds them. Most adult TTP is acquired, caused by an IgG autoantibody that inhibits ADAMTS13; a rarer congenital form results from ADAMTS13 mutations. Persisting ultralarge multimers bind platelet glycoprotein Ib spontaneously, and microthrombi form widely, especially in the brain, heart, and kidney. Patients show hemolysis, severe thrombocytopenia, neurologic signs (confusion, headache, stroke, seizures), fever, and usually milder renal injury; only a minority show all five features. ADAMTS13 activity is typically below 10%."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of TTP","steps":["Autoantibody or mutation reduces ADAMTS13 activity","Ultralarge vWF multimers persist on endothelium and in plasma","Platelets bind spontaneously through glycoprotein Ib","Platelet-rich microthrombi form in small vessels","Platelet consumption causes thrombocytopenia","Shear fragments red cells into schistocytes","Ischemia of brain, heart, and kidney"],"caption":"The coagulation cascade is not the driver, so clotting times stay normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Typical HUS follows infection with Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli, classically O157:H7 from undercooked ground beef or contaminated produce, or Shigella dysenteriae type 1. It mainly affects young children about a week after bloody diarrhea. Shiga toxin enters the blood and binds globotriaosylceramide (Gb3), which is abundant on glomerular endothelium. After entry, its A subunit removes an adenine from 28S ribosomal RNA of the 60S subunit, halting protein synthesis. Injured endothelium swells and detaches, exposes a prothrombotic surface, and seeds glomerular thrombi, producing acute kidney injury. The triad is microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury, with normal ADAMTS13 activity. Atypical HUS arises from inherited defects in complement regulators such as factor H."},{"type":"table","title":"TTP, HUS, and DIC compared","cols":["Feature","TTP","Typical HUS","DIC"],"rows":[["Primary defect","ADAMTS13 deficiency, ultralarge vWF","Shiga toxin injury to renal endothelium","Systemic activation of coagulation"],["Typical patient","Adult, often a woman","Child after bloody diarrhea","Sepsis, trauma, obstetric or malignant disease"],["Dominant organ","Brain","Kidney","Multiple; bleeding and thrombosis"],["Schistocytes and thrombocytopenia","Yes","Yes","Yes"],["PT and PTT","Normal","Normal","Prolonged"],["Fibrinogen","Normal","Normal","Low"],["D-dimer","Normal or mildly raised","Normal or mildly raised","Markedly raised"],["ADAMTS13 activity","Below about 10%","Normal or modestly reduced","Not diagnostic"]],"caption":"Normal clotting times and fibrinogen separate thrombotic microangiopathy from DIC."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A septic patient has schistocytes, platelets of 25,000/µL, a prolonged PT and PTT, and low fibrinogen. Is this TTP?","explain":"No. Prolonged clotting times and low fibrinogen show consumption of clotting factors, which points to DIC. TTP and HUS consume platelets but spare the coagulation factors.","check":"Compare with the PT, PTT, and fibrinogen rows of the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C10","obj":"HE-9-B.10","title":"Mechanical hemolysis and secondary iron deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A normal red cell deforms easily, but it cannot survive repeated exposure to very high shear. Mechanical prosthetic heart valves, especially with a paravalvular leak or dysfunction, and severe aortic stenosis create high-velocity turbulent jets that tear the membrane. The fragments circulate as schistocytes (helmet cells and triangles), some reseal as small spherocytes, and many cells rupture outright inside the vessel. Mechanical valves cause more hemolysis than bioprosthetic (tissue) valves. Mild compensated hemolysis is common, and worsening hemolysis can be a sign of valve dysfunction."},{"type":"p","text":"The lab picture is intravascular hemolysis: high LDH, absent haptoglobin, raised unconjugated bilirubin, reticulocytosis, and a negative DAT. Unlike TTP, the platelet count is normal and there are no organ microthrombi, because the injury comes from flow at a single site, not from platelet aggregation."},{"type":"p","text":"In extravascular hemolysis, macrophages recover iron from heme and return it to the marrow. In intravascular hemolysis, once haptoglobin is saturated, hemoglobin splits into dimers small enough to cross the glomerular filter. Proximal tubule cells reabsorb and degrade them, storing the iron as ferritin and hemosiderin, and are later shed into the urine. Hemosiderinuria, shown by a Prussian blue stain of the urine sediment, appears several days after hemolysis begins and persists afterward. Over months, urinary iron loss exceeds dietary absorption. Ferritin falls, the MCV drifts downward, and the marrow loses the iron it needs to compensate, which deepens the anemia. The result is a mixed picture of schistocytes and high LDH together with microcytosis and low ferritin, a pattern also seen in PNH."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C11","obj":"HE-9-B.11","title":"Splenic rupture, infarct, abscess, and the post-splenectomy blood","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The red pulp filters blood slowly through cords lined by macrophages, which remove old and damaged red cells and pluck inclusions from cells without destroying them (pitting). The white pulp, especially marginal zone B cells, produces IgM against polysaccharide capsules. The spleen also holds about one third of the body's platelets. Branches of the splenic artery are end arteries, so blocking one infarcts the segment it supplies. The spleen lies under the left ninth to eleventh ribs and touches the diaphragm."},{"type":"table","title":"Structural disorders of the spleen","cols":["Disorder","Common causes","Mechanism","Clinical clues"],"rows":[["Rupture","Blunt abdominal trauma (most common); infectious mononucleosis; malaria","Enlarged spleen has a stretched, thin capsule and soft, infiltrated pulp","Left upper quadrant pain, left shoulder pain (Kehr sign), hemoperitoneum, hypovolemic shock"],["Infarct","Sickle cell disease; emboli from the left heart (atrial fibrillation, endocarditis, mural thrombus); massive splenomegaly","Occlusion of an end artery gives a pale, wedge-shaped, subcapsular infarct","Left upper quadrant pain, often pleuritic; may be silent"],["Abscess","Septic emboli from infective endocarditis (most common); infected infarct, as in sickle cell disease; immunosuppression","Bacteria seed devitalized or ischemic tissue","Fever, left upper quadrant pain, leukocytosis, left pleural effusion"]],"caption":"Diaphragmatic irritation refers pain to the left shoulder through the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5)."},{"type":"p","text":"In infectious mononucleosis caused by Epstein-Barr virus, reactive lymphocytes infiltrate the pulp, the capsule, and the trabeculae, so the spleen becomes large, soft, and fragile. Rupture can follow trivial trauma or occur spontaneously, mostly in the first 3 to 4 weeks of illness."},{"type":"p","text":"After splenectomy, or with functional asplenia from sickle cell autoinfarction, nothing pits inclusions or trims excess membrane, so cells the spleen normally cleans or removes stay in circulation. The loss of the splenic platelet pool raises the platelet count, and the risk of infection with encapsulated organisms rises (HE-7.7)."},{"type":"table","title":"Blood findings after splenectomy","cols":["Finding","Why it appears"],"rows":[["Howell-Jolly bodies","Nuclear (DNA) remnants are no longer pitted"],["Pappenheimer bodies","Iron-containing granules are no longer removed"],["Target cells","Excess membrane is not trimmed"],["Acanthocytes and nucleated red cells","Abnormal cells are no longer culled"],["Thrombocytosis","Platelets normally held in the spleen remain in circulation; often marked and early"],["Leukocytosis and lymphocytosis","Loss of splenic sequestration"]],"caption":"The same smear appears in functional asplenia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-02-C12","obj":"HE-9-B.12","title":"Hypersplenism versus marrow failure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypersplenism is defined by four features: splenomegaly, a reduction in one or more blood cell lines, a normal or hypercellular marrow, and correction of the cytopenias when the spleen is removed. The marrow is working; the cells are being held back or destroyed after release."},{"type":"p","text":"The most common setting is cirrhosis, where portal hypertension backs pressure up the splenic vein and causes congestive splenomegaly. Two mechanisms then produce the cytopenias. First, an enlarged red pulp with slow flow pools far more blood than usual; the normal spleen holds about one third of platelets, and a massively enlarged spleen can hold most of them. Second, macrophage hyperplasia increases destruction of cells that pass slowly through the cords. Expansion of plasma volume adds a dilutional effect. Cytopenias are usually moderate, with thrombocytopenia most common, followed by leukopenia and then anemia. Pooled platelets remain functional and can be mobilized, so bleeding is less than the count suggests."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of hypersplenism","cols":["Category","Examples"],"rows":[["Congestive","Cirrhosis with portal hypertension, portal or splenic vein thrombosis"],["Infiltrative","Gaucher disease, myelofibrosis, lymphoma, hairy cell leukemia, chronic myeloid leukemia, amyloidosis"],["Infectious and inflammatory","Malaria, visceral leishmaniasis, Felty syndrome (rheumatoid arthritis)"],["Work hypertrophy","Chronic hemolysis, such as hereditary spherocytosis and thalassemia"]],"caption":"Any process that enlarges the spleen can cause hypersplenism."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypersplenism versus marrow failure","cols":["Feature","Hypersplenism","Marrow failure (for example aplastic anemia)"],"rows":[["Spleen","Enlarged","Not enlarged; splenomegaly argues against aplastic anemia"],["Marrow cellularity","Normal or increased","Markedly reduced, replaced by fat"],["Reticulocyte response","Present","Low"],["Severity of cytopenias","Usually moderate","Often severe"],["Effect of splenectomy","Counts improve","No improvement"]],"caption":"Marrow cellularity and spleen size are the two decisive findings."},{"type":"p","text":"The distinction can blur when one disease both enlarges the spleen and damages the marrow. Myelofibrosis, hairy cell leukemia, and lymphoma infiltrate both organs, so cytopenias reflect splenic pooling and marrow replacement together. In those cases the marrow biopsy shows fibrosis or infiltration rather than simple hypocellularity or hyperplasia."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each disease in this chapter fits one question: what makes this red cell die early, and where? Biochemistry explains the enzyme defects, because red cells without mitochondria depend on glycolysis for ATP and the pentose phosphate pathway for NADPH. Genetics explains the hemoglobinopathies and membrane disorders, and their inheritance patterns are frequently tested (X-linked G6PD, autosomal dominant spherocytosis, autosomal recessive sickle cell and PK deficiency). Immunology explains hemolytic disease of the newborn through IgG transport by FcRn, and PNH through loss of complement regulators. Microbiology enters through parvovirus B19, Salmonella, Shiga toxin, and encapsulated organisms after asplenia."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology ties these together through the site of destruction. Extravascular hemolysis enlarges the spleen, forms pigment gallstones, and conserves iron; intravascular hemolysis empties haptoglobin, spills hemoglobin into urine, and can cause iron deficiency. The limits matter: many diseases mix both sites, a single smear can mislead (spherocytes occur in both spherocytosis and warm autoimmune hemolysis), and enzyme assays or electrophoresis can be distorted by reticulocytosis, transfusion, or coexisting iron deficiency, so results are read alongside the clinical setting."}],"summary":["All hemolysis raises reticulocytes, LDH, and unconjugated bilirubin and lowers haptoglobin; intravascular hemolysis adds hemoglobinuria and hemosiderinuria.","HbS (glutamate to valine at beta 6) polymerizes when deoxygenated; occlusion causes dactylitis, acute chest syndrome, stroke, priapism, papillary necrosis, and splenic autoinfarction, while short red cell survival allows parvovirus aplastic crisis.","In beta thalassemia major, precipitated alpha chains kill erythroblasts; marrow expansion, extramedullary hematopoiesis, and hepcidin suppression explain the skull changes, hepatosplenomegaly, and iron overload.","Beta thalassemia minor shows mild microcytic anemia, a high RBC count, normal ferritin, and HbA2 above about 3.5%.","G6PD deficiency (X-linked) lowers NADPH and reduced glutathione, so oxidant triggers cause episodic, mainly intravascular hemolysis with Heinz bodies and bite cells.","Hereditary spherocytosis (vertical link defects) causes membrane loss, splenic trapping, high MCHC, increased osmotic fragility, reduced EMA binding, and a negative DAT.","Rh disease needs prior sensitization and IgG anti-D crossing by FcRn, causing anemia, erythroblastosis, and hydrops; ABO disease in infants of group O mothers is common but mild.","A positive infant DAT and maternal IAT confirm antibody-mediated hemolysis; unconjugated bilirubin rises after birth and free bilirubin causes kernicterus.","PNH is an acquired PIGA mutation removing CD55 and CD59, causing complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis, venous thrombosis, and cytopenias diagnosed by flow cytometry.","TTP (ADAMTS13 deficiency) and HUS (Shiga toxin) cause schistocytes and thrombocytopenia with normal PT and PTT; hypersplenism causes cytopenias with a cellular marrow and splenomegaly."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus, section on the spleen"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Red Blood Cells, Anemia, and Polycythemia; Blood Types, Transfusion, Tissue and Organ Transplantation"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Pentose Phosphate Pathway and NADPH; Glycolysis; Globular Proteins (hemoglobinopathies)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood Disorders"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric Gram-negative rods (Enterobacteriaceae), Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli"}]},"HE-9-C":{"unit":"HE-9-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03","title":"Clotting Abnormalities: Bleeding and Thrombotic Disorders","subject":"Hematopoietic System (HEME)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter applies normal hemostasis to the disorders that make blood clot too little or too much: disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), the hemophilias, immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP), von Willebrand disease, vitamin K deficiency, liver failure, inherited platelet defects, inherited thrombophilias, and hematologic paraneoplastic syndromes. Board questions usually give a bleeding pattern with a platelet count, prothrombin time (PT), partial thromboplastin time (PTT), and perhaps a mixing study, and ask which step of hemostasis has failed. If you can place each defect in primary hemostasis, secondary hemostasis, or natural anticoagulation, the lab pattern and the clinical picture follow from the mechanism.","prerequisites":["Primary hemostasis: platelet adhesion through GpIb and von Willebrand factor, activation, and aggregation through GpIIb/IIIa and fibrinogen","Secondary hemostasis: tissue factor initiation, the tenase and prothrombinase complexes, thrombin, fibrin, and factor XIII crosslinking","Natural anticoagulants (antithrombin, protein C and protein S, tissue factor pathway inhibitor) and fibrinolysis by plasmin","What the PT, PTT, platelet count, and bleeding time measure","X-linked recessive and autosomal inheritance patterns"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology","how":"ITP, acquired factor VIII inhibitors, and thymoma-associated red cell aplasia are autoimmune diseases explained by antibody-mediated (type II) or T-cell-mediated injury."},{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Gram-negative sepsis and meningococcemia are classic triggers of DIC, and gut bacteria supply part of the body's vitamin K."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal and hepatic disease","how":"Fat malabsorption causes vitamin K deficiency, and cirrhosis lowers clotting factor synthesis and thrombopoietin while portal hypertension sequesters platelets in the spleen."},{"to":"Renal disease","how":"Uremia impairs platelet function, nephrotic syndrome wastes antithrombin in urine, and renal cell carcinoma can secrete erythropoietin."},{"to":"Obstetrics and oncology","how":"Placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, acute promyelocytic leukemia, and mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas release procoagulants that start DIC or migratory thrombosis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Primary hemostasis","def":"Formation of the platelet plug through platelet adhesion, activation, and aggregation within seconds of vessel injury."},{"term":"Secondary hemostasis","def":"Generation of thrombin and a crosslinked fibrin mesh by the coagulation factors, which stabilizes the platelet plug."},{"term":"Prothrombin time (PT)","def":"Clotting time of plasma after adding tissue factor and phospholipid, sensitive to factors VII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen."},{"term":"Partial thromboplastin time (PTT)","def":"Clotting time of plasma after a contact activator and phospholipid, sensitive to factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, and fibrinogen."},{"term":"Mixing study","def":"Repeat of a prolonged PT or PTT after mixing patient plasma 1:1 with normal plasma, to separate a factor deficiency (corrects) from an inhibitor (does not correct)."},{"term":"D-dimer","def":"A degradation product of fibrin that has been crosslinked by factor XIIIa and then cleaved by plasmin, so it shows that clot formed and was broken down."},{"term":"Schistocyte","def":"A fragmented red cell sheared by fibrin strands or abnormal surfaces in small vessels, the marker of microangiopathic hemolysis."},{"term":"Ristocetin cofactor activity","def":"A functional assay of von Willebrand factor that measures how well patient plasma makes normal platelets agglutinate through GpIb in the presence of ristocetin."},{"term":"Gamma-carboxylation","def":"Vitamin K-dependent addition of a second carboxyl group to glutamate residues, which lets factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S bind calcium and phospholipid."},{"term":"Thrombophilia","def":"An inherited or acquired tendency to thrombosis, usually from loss of a natural anticoagulant or gain of procoagulant function."},{"term":"Paraneoplastic syndrome","def":"A disorder caused by a tumor's secreted products or by the immune response to it, rather than by local growth or metastasis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C01","obj":"HE-9-C.1","title":"DIC: triggers and the laboratory pattern of consumption","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal coagulation is local. Tissue factor (TF) is exposed only where a vessel is injured, thrombin forms on activated platelets at that site, and antithrombin, the protein C system, and tissue factor pathway inhibitor stop thrombin from spreading. Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is what happens when procoagulant stimulus enters or arises throughout the circulation and overwhelms these controls. DIC is never a primary disease; it is always secondary to an underlying trigger."},{"type":"p","text":"Most triggers work by adding tissue factor or a TF-like activator to blood, or by injuring endothelium so that it loses its anticoagulant surface. In sepsis, endotoxin and cytokines (TNF and IL-1) make monocytes and endothelial cells express TF, lower endothelial thrombomodulin so less protein C is activated, and raise plasminogen activator inhibitor 1. Obstetric tissue is rich in TF, which is why placental abruption and amniotic fluid embolism start DIC abruptly. Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) cells carry procoagulant and fibrinolysis-promoting material in their granules, so bleeding often dominates. Mucin from adenocarcinomas of the pancreas, lung, stomach, and prostate can activate factor X directly and tends to cause a slower, chronic, more thrombotic form."},{"type":"table","title":"Major triggers of DIC and how they start it","cols":["Trigger","Main procoagulant mechanism","Typical course"],"rows":[["Sepsis (most common, especially gram-negative and meningococcal)","Endotoxin and cytokines induce TF; thrombomodulin falls; fibrinolysis is suppressed","Acute"],["Major trauma, burns, crush injury, head injury","Release of TF from damaged tissue (brain is very rich in TF); endothelial injury","Acute"],["Placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, retained dead fetus","TF-rich placental tissue or amniotic fluid enters maternal blood","Acute (retained fetus can be chronic)"],["Acute promyelocytic leukemia","Granule procoagulants plus enhanced fibrinolysis","Acute, bleeding predominant"],["Mucin-secreting adenocarcinoma","Mucin and tumor TF activate factor X and coagulation","Chronic, thrombosis predominant"]],"caption":"Every trigger adds procoagulant activity to the whole circulation, either by supplying tissue factor or by stripping endothelium of its anticoagulant function."},{"type":"flow","title":"From trigger to the DIC laboratory pattern","steps":["Trigger exposes tissue factor or injures endothelium","Thrombin forms throughout the circulation","Fibrin microthrombi deposit in small vessels","Platelets, fibrinogen, and factors V and VIII are consumed","Plasmin lyses crosslinked fibrin, releasing D-dimer","Red cells shear on fibrin strands into schistocytes"],"caption":"Each laboratory abnormality in DIC is a footprint of one step: consumption lowers platelets and factors, fibrinolysis raises D-dimer, and microthrombi fragment red cells."},{"type":"p","text":"The laboratory pattern follows directly. Platelets fall below the reference range of about 150,000 to 400,000/µL because they are incorporated into microthrombi. PT and PTT prolong because factors, especially V and VIII and fibrinogen, are used up faster than the liver can replace them. Fibrinogen falls below about 200 mg/dL (2 g/L), although because fibrinogen is an acute phase reactant, a value in the normal range in a septic patient can still represent a large drop. D-dimer is high because it can be produced only when thrombin has formed crosslinked fibrin and plasmin has then cleaved it. A peripheral smear shows schistocytes, the evidence of microangiopathic hemolysis. Serial values matter more than any single value: falling platelets and fibrinogen with rising D-dimer confirm ongoing consumption."},{"type":"p","text":"In chronic DIC from cancer, synthesis can keep pace with consumption. Platelets and PT may be near normal while D-dimer is high, and the patient presents with venous thrombosis rather than bleeding (see C10)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C02","obj":"HE-9-C.2","title":"DIC: why patients clot and bleed at the same time","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"DIC has two clinical faces that come from one process. Fibrin microthrombi block arterioles and capillaries and cause ischemic organ injury, while the same clotting uses up platelets and factors and activates plasmin, which leaves too little hemostatic capacity to seal everyday vascular breaks. The balance between the two depends on the trigger and its speed: acute obstetric and APL-related DIC tend to bleed, and chronic cancer-related DIC tends to clot."},{"type":"table","title":"Organ injury from microthrombi","cols":["Organ","Mechanism","Manifestation"],"rows":[["Kidney","Glomerular and cortical capillary thrombi","Oliguria, acute kidney injury, rarely bilateral cortical necrosis"],["Lung","Alveolar capillary thrombi and endothelial injury","Hypoxemia, acute respiratory distress syndrome"],["Brain","Microinfarcts and small hemorrhages","Confusion, delirium, seizures, coma"],["Adrenal glands","Thrombosis with hemorrhagic infarction","Adrenal hemorrhage and acute adrenal insufficiency (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome in meningococcemia)"],["Skin","Dermal vessel thrombosis","Purpura, acral cyanosis, and in severe cases purpura fulminans with skin necrosis"]],"caption":"Organs with dense microcirculation, especially kidney, lung, and brain, are the most vulnerable to fibrin microthrombi."},{"type":"p","text":"Bleeding has three causes that act together. Thrombocytopenia removes the platelet plug, so petechiae, purpura, and mucosal bleeding appear. Low fibrinogen and factors V and VIII mean that whatever plug forms cannot be stabilized by fibrin. Fibrin and fibrinogen degradation products also interfere with fibrin polymerization and platelet aggregation, so the remaining clots are weak. The characteristic sign is oozing from sites that should have sealed: venipuncture and intravenous catheter sites, surgical wounds, and mucosa of the gums, nose, gastrointestinal tract, and urinary tract."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The self-amplifying loop of acute DIC","steps":["Widespread thrombin generation forms microthrombi","Ischemia injures endothelium and organs","Injured tissue and endothelium release more tissue factor","Consumption and fibrinolysis deplete hemostatic capacity","Bleeding, shock, and hypoperfusion worsen tissue injury"],"caption":"Organ ischemia and shock feed further tissue factor exposure, which is why DIC can accelerate once it starts and why removing the trigger is central to stopping it."},{"type":"p","text":"The adrenal link is a classic board item. In fulminant meningococcemia, endotoxin-driven DIC causes bilateral adrenal hemorrhage, and loss of cortisol and aldosterone adds refractory hypotension to septic shock."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman develops a placental abruption, then oozes from her intravenous site, becomes oliguric, and has platelets of 45,000/µL with fibrinogen of 90 mg/dL. Would you expect D-dimer and factor VIII to be high or low?","explain":"D-dimer is high because fibrin is being formed and lysed throughout the circulation; factor VIII is low because it is consumed along with fibrinogen and factor V. Oliguria reflects renal microthrombi, while oozing reflects consumption.","check":"Compare with the DIC flow in C01 and the bleeding mechanisms in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C03","obj":"HE-9-C.3","title":"Hemophilia A and B and the acquired factor VIII inhibitor","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Factors VIIIa and IXa assemble on the activated platelet surface as the intrinsic tenase complex, which generates most of the factor Xa, and therefore most of the thrombin, during the amplification phase of coagulation. Tissue factor and factor VIIa start clotting but are quickly shut down by tissue factor pathway inhibitor, so without VIII or IX the burst of thrombin needed for a firm fibrin clot never happens. The platelet plug forms normally, then fails because it is not reinforced by fibrin."},{"type":"p","text":"Hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency, about 1 in 5,000 male births) and hemophilia B (factor IX deficiency, about one fifth as common) are both X-linked recessive, so nearly all patients are male, and each son of a carrier mother has a 50% chance of being affected. About one third of cases arise from new mutations, so a negative family history does not exclude the diagnosis. The two diseases are clinically identical and are separated only by specific factor assays."},{"type":"p","text":"Severity tracks residual factor activity. Severe disease (below 1% of normal) causes spontaneous bleeding into joints (hemarthroses, especially knees, elbows, and ankles) and deep muscles such as the iliopsoas, and repeated joint bleeding leads to synovial iron deposition, synovitis, and a destructive arthropathy. Bleeding after surgery or dental extraction is typically delayed, because the initial platelet plug holds for a while before it breaks down. Petechiae are not a feature, since platelet function is intact."},{"type":"table","title":"Separating isolated PTT prolongations","cols":["Finding","Hemophilia A or B","Acquired factor VIII inhibitor","Lupus anticoagulant"],"rows":[["Who","Males from childhood; X-linked","Older adults, postpartum women, autoimmune disease or malignancy; either sex","Antiphospholipid syndrome, systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)"],["PT, platelets, bleeding time","Normal","Normal","PT usually normal; platelets normal or mildly low"],["Mixing study","Corrects","Fails to correct, especially after incubation","Fails to correct"],["Clinical problem","Hemarthroses and deep muscle bleeds","Large soft tissue bruises and muscle bleeds; hemarthroses less common","Thrombosis, not bleeding"]],"caption":"An isolated prolonged PTT with a normal PT points to the intrinsic pathway; the mixing study decides between a missing factor and an antibody."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading a mixing study","text":"A 72-year-old woman with no prior bleeding history has large bruises on her arms and thigh. PT is normal and PTT is 68 s (reference about 25 to 35 s). Step 1: an isolated PTT means a problem in factors XII, XI, IX, or VIII, or an inhibitor. Step 2: her plasma is mixed 1:1 with normal plasma. If she lacked a factor, the normal plasma would supply at least 50% activity, which is enough to normalize the PTT. Step 3: her mix is nearly normal at first but prolongs again after 1 to 2 hours at 37 °C (98.6 °F). That time and temperature dependence is typical of an autoantibody against factor VIII, which slowly neutralizes the factor VIII in the added normal plasma. Her age, sex, and new onset also fit acquired hemophilia, not the inherited disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C04","obj":"HE-9-C.4","title":"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is antibody-mediated destruction of otherwise normal platelets. IgG autoantibodies, most often against the GpIIb/IIIa complex and less often GpIb/IX, coat platelets. Splenic macrophages bind the Fc portion of the bound IgG through Fc gamma receptors and phagocytose the platelets, a type II hypersensitivity reaction. The spleen is both the main site of destruction and an important site of autoantibody production, and the antibodies can also impair platelet production by megakaryocytes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of ITP","steps":["Loss of tolerance produces IgG against GpIIb/IIIa","Antibody coats circulating platelets","Splenic macrophages remove coated platelets via Fc receptors","Platelet count falls, survival shortens to hours or days","Marrow responds with more megakaryocytes and larger platelets","Petechiae and mucosal bleeding with normal PT and PTT"],"caption":"ITP is a destruction disorder: the marrow is normal and responds, which is why megakaryocytes increase rather than decrease."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the defect lies outside the marrow, the marrow shows normal or increased megakaryocytes, and the smear shows a low count with some large, young platelets and no other abnormal cells. Coagulation factors are untouched, so PT and PTT are normal, while the bleeding time is prolonged by the low count. The spleen is not enlarged, and palpable splenomegaly should suggest another diagnosis such as lymphoma or hypersplenism. ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion: drugs, HIV, hepatitis C, pseudothrombocytopenia from EDTA clumping, and thrombotic microangiopathies must be ruled out."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute childhood ITP versus chronic adult ITP","cols":["Feature","Acute (childhood)","Chronic (adult)"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Child, boys and girls equally","Woman of reproductive age (roughly 3:1 female)"],["Onset","Abrupt, about 1 to 3 weeks after a viral infection or vaccination","Insidious, often found on a routine count"],["Associations","Antibody probably arises in response to the virus","Primary, or secondary to SLE, HIV, hepatitis C, chronic lymphocytic leukemia, or H. pylori"],["Course","Self-limited; most remit within about 6 months","Persistent, with relapses"]],"caption":"Both forms share the same laboratory picture: isolated thrombocytopenia, normal PT and PTT, and normal or increased marrow megakaryocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Bleeding reflects the count. Petechiae, purpura, epistaxis, gum bleeding, and heavy menses appear first, and the risk of spontaneous bleeding rises sharply below about 20,000/µL, with intracranial hemorrhage the most serious complication. Because the autoantibody is IgG, it crosses the placenta, so a newborn of a mother with ITP can have transient thrombocytopenia that resolves as the maternal antibody is cleared."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C05","obj":"HE-9-C.5","title":"Von Willebrand disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Von Willebrand factor (vWF) is a large multimeric glycoprotein made by endothelial cells, which store it in Weibel-Palade bodies, and by megakaryocytes, which package it in platelet alpha granules. It has two jobs. First, at sites of injury it binds exposed subendothelial collagen and tethers platelets through their GpIb receptor, which is essential under the high shear stress of arterioles and capillaries; the largest multimers are the most effective. Second, in plasma it binds and carries factor VIII, protecting it from rapid clearance and proteolysis."},{"type":"p","text":"Von Willebrand disease (vWD) is the most common inherited bleeding disorder. Loss of the adhesion function gives a platelet-type bleeding pattern: easy bruising, epistaxis, gum bleeding, heavy menstrual bleeding, and prolonged bleeding after dental work or surgery. Platelets are present in normal numbers but cannot stick, so the count is normal and the bleeding time (or a platelet function analyzer closure time) is prolonged. Loss of the carrier function shortens factor VIII survival, so factor VIII falls and the PTT may be prolonged; the PT is normal because factor VIII is not in the extrinsic pathway."},{"type":"p","text":"Ristocetin is an antibiotic that makes vWF bind platelet GpIb in vitro. Ristocetin cofactor activity therefore measures how well the patient's vWF can agglutinate normal platelets, and it is low in nearly all forms of vWD (type 2N, a defect only in factor VIII binding, is the exception). This also separates vWD from Bernard-Soulier syndrome (C08): in both, ristocetin fails to agglutinate the patient's platelets, but in vWD adding normal plasma corrects the defect because the missing part is plasma vWF, whereas in Bernard-Soulier it does not because the platelets lack GpIb."},{"type":"table","title":"Main types of von Willebrand disease","cols":["Type","Defect","Inheritance","Laboratory clue"],"rows":[["Type 1 (most common, about 70% or more)","Partial quantitative deficiency of normal vWF","Autosomal dominant","vWF antigen and activity reduced in parallel; factor VIII normal or mildly low"],["Type 2","Qualitative defect (for example loss of large multimers)","Mostly autosomal dominant","Activity reduced more than antigen"],["Type 3","Near-total absence of vWF","Autosomal recessive","Very low factor VIII; prolonged PTT; hemophilia-like joint bleeds possible"]],"caption":"The more vWF is lost, the more factor VIII falls, so severe type 3 disease adds deep factor-type bleeding to the mucosal pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"vWF levels vary within the same person. They are about 25% lower in people with blood group O, and they rise with estrogen, pregnancy, stress, exercise, and inflammation, since vWF behaves as an acute phase reactant. A normal result during pregnancy or illness therefore does not exclude mild type 1 disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 16-year-old girl has had heavy periods since menarche and bled for a day after a tooth extraction. Platelets are 260,000/µL, PT is normal, and PTT is mildly prolonged. Which single defect explains both the mucosal bleeding and the PTT?","explain":"Reduced vWF impairs platelet adhesion through GpIb, causing mucosal bleeding with a normal count, and the loss of vWF as a carrier shortens factor VIII survival, prolonging the PTT. Ristocetin cofactor activity would be low.","check":"Compare with the two functions of vWF at the start of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C06","obj":"HE-9-C.6","title":"Vitamin K deficiency and why the PT prolongs first","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Factors II, VII, IX, and X and the anticoagulant proteins C and S are made in the liver with several glutamate residues near their amino terminus. The enzyme gamma-glutamyl carboxylase converts these to gamma-carboxyglutamate (Gla), and it needs reduced vitamin K (the hydroquinone) as a cofactor. Each reaction oxidizes vitamin K to its epoxide, which vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKOR) recycles back to the active form. The Gla residues bind calcium, which bridges the factors to the negatively charged phosphatidylserine on activated platelets, where the tenase and prothrombinase complexes assemble. Without vitamin K the liver still secretes the proteins, but they cannot bind calcium and are nearly inactive."},{"type":"flow","title":"Vitamin K cycle and factor activation","steps":["Reduced vitamin K activates gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in hepatocytes","Glutamates on II, VII, IX, X, C, S become Gla","Vitamin K is oxidized to vitamin K epoxide","VKOR regenerates reduced vitamin K for reuse","Gla domains bind calcium and platelet phospholipid","Functional factors assemble tenase and prothrombinase complexes"],"caption":"Deficiency of vitamin K, or blockade of VKOR, leaves the factors uncarboxylated and unable to bind calcium-phospholipid surfaces."},{"type":"p","text":"Body stores of vitamin K are small and turn over quickly, so deficiency appears within days to weeks when supply stops. Dietary vitamin K1 (phylloquinone) comes from green leafy vegetables, and vitamin K2 (menaquinones) is made by intestinal bacteria. Both are fat soluble and need bile salts and micelles for absorption."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of vitamin K deficiency","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Newborn not given vitamin K at birth","Poor placental transfer, sterile gut, low vitamin K in breast milk, immature liver; vitamin K deficiency bleeding of the newborn"],["Fat malabsorption","Biliary obstruction, cholestasis, cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, and short bowel impair micelle-dependent absorption"],["Prolonged broad-spectrum antibiotics","Loss of menaquinone-producing gut flora, mattering most when intake is also poor"],["Poor intake","Malnutrition, prolonged fasting, critically ill or parenterally fed patients, alcohol use disorder"]],"caption":"Hospitalized patients who are not eating and are on antibiotics are a classic adult setting; the newborn is the classic pediatric setting."},{"type":"p","text":"The PT prolongs first because of half-life. As carboxylated factors are cleared and replaced by inactive ones, each falls at the rate set by its plasma half-life: factor VII about 4 to 6 hours, IX about 24 hours, X about 40 hours, and prothrombin about 60 hours. Factor VII is the only vitamin K-dependent factor measured by the PT but not the PTT, so the PT rises within a day or two while the PTT is still normal. The PTT prolongs later as IX, X, and II fall. Platelets, fibrinogen, and factor VIII are not vitamin K dependent and stay normal. Protein C has a short half-life too (about 8 hours), close to that of factor VII."},{"type":"p","text":"Newborn vitamin K deficiency bleeding classically appears in the first week of life as bleeding from the umbilical stump, gastrointestinal tract, skin, or circumcision site, and a later form in breast-fed infants with unrecognized malabsorption can present weeks later with intracranial hemorrhage."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C07","obj":"HE-9-C.7","title":"Liver failure versus vitamin K deficiency (and DIC)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin K deficiency is a single, selective defect: the liver is healthy but cannot carboxylate four factors. Liver failure is a global synthetic failure, so it lowers almost every coagulation protein the hepatocyte makes, including fibrinogen and factor V, which are not vitamin K dependent. Both prolong the PT, so the question is always which additional findings are present."},{"type":"p","text":"Several features of liver failure have specific mechanisms. Fibrinogen falls once synthetic loss is advanced, and the fibrinogen made may be structurally abnormal (dysfibrinogenemia). Platelets fall because portal hypertension enlarges the spleen, which sequesters platelets, and because the liver makes most of the body's thrombopoietin. Factor VIII, by contrast, is normal or high. It is produced mainly by endothelial cells, including liver sinusoidal endothelium and endothelium outside the liver, its level rises with vWF, and its clearance is reduced in liver disease. Factor V, which is made by hepatocytes and is not vitamin K dependent, is low in liver failure and normal in vitamin K deficiency."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing the three common acquired coagulopathies","cols":["Test","Vitamin K deficiency","Liver failure","DIC"],"rows":[["PT","Prolonged (first)","Prolonged","Prolonged"],["PTT","Normal or prolonged","Prolonged","Prolonged"],["Platelets","Normal","Low (sequestration, low thrombopoietin)","Low (consumption)"],["Fibrinogen","Normal","Low or normal","Low"],["Factor V","Normal","Low","Low"],["Factor VIII","Normal","Normal or high","Low"],["D-dimer","Normal","Normal or mildly high","High"],["PT after parenteral vitamin K","Corrects within about a day","Little or no change","Little or no change"]],"caption":"Factor V separates vitamin K deficiency from liver failure, and factor VIII separates liver failure from DIC."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: one prolonged PT, three explanations","text":"Three patients each have a PT of 22 s (reference about 11 to 13.5 s). Patient A had obstructive jaundice for three weeks: platelets 240,000/µL, fibrinogen 310 mg/dL, factor V normal. Only vitamin K-dependent factors are low, so this is malabsorption of vitamin K, and the PT corrects with parenteral vitamin K. Patient B has cirrhosis with splenomegaly: platelets 70,000/µL, fibrinogen 130 mg/dL, factor V low, factor VIII high. Global synthetic failure plus sequestration explains it, and factor VIII made outside hepatocytes stays high. Patient C is septic: platelets 60,000/µL, fibrinogen 110 mg/dL, D-dimer very high, factor VIII low. Factor VIII is consumed, which points to DIC rather than liver failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Liver failure also lowers antithrombin, protein C, and protein S, so hemostasis is rebalanced rather than simply impaired. The prolonged PT does not mean these patients are protected from thrombosis; portal vein thrombosis and venous thromboembolism still occur."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C08","obj":"HE-9-C.8","title":"Qualitative platelet disorders: Bernard-Soulier, Glanzmann, and uremia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Primary hemostasis requires three platelet steps. In adhesion, the GpIb-IX-V complex binds vWF on exposed collagen. In activation, platelets change shape, release ADP and thromboxane A2 from their granules and membranes, and switch GpIIb/IIIa (integrin alpha IIb beta 3) into a high-affinity state. In aggregation, activated GpIIb/IIIa binds fibrinogen, which bridges neighboring platelets. Qualitative disorders block one of these steps, so they cause mucocutaneous bleeding and a prolonged bleeding time even when the platelet count is normal or near normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Bernard-Soulier syndrome is an autosomal recessive deficiency of GpIb-IX-V, so platelets cannot adhere to vWF. Because GpIb also links the platelet membrane to its cytoskeleton and is needed for normal platelet formation, the platelets are giant (often near the size of a red cell) and mildly reduced in number. Ristocetin cannot agglutinate them, and unlike vWD, adding normal plasma does not help."},{"type":"p","text":"Glanzmann thrombasthenia is an autosomal recessive deficiency or dysfunction of GpIIb/IIIa. Platelets adhere and activate normally but cannot bind fibrinogen, so they do not aggregate in response to ADP, collagen, epinephrine, or thrombin. Platelet count and size are normal, and on a fingerstick smear the platelets lie singly rather than in the usual small clumps. Ristocetin agglutination is normal because GpIb and vWF are intact. GpIIb/IIIa is also the usual target of the autoantibodies in ITP (C04), which illustrates the difference between losing the receptor and losing the platelets that carry it."},{"type":"table","title":"Qualitative platelet disorders compared","cols":["Feature","Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Glanzmann thrombasthenia","Uremic platelet dysfunction"],"rows":[["Defective step","Adhesion","Aggregation","Adhesion and aggregation"],["Molecular basis","GpIb-IX-V deficiency","GpIIb/IIIa deficiency","Retained uremic toxins, more nitric oxide, less thromboxane A2"],["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal recessive","Acquired (advanced kidney disease)"],["Platelet count and size","Mildly low, giant platelets","Normal count and size","Normal or mildly low, normal size"],["Ristocetin","Abnormal, not corrected by normal plasma","Normal","Variable"],["ADP, collagen, epinephrine","Normal aggregation","Absent aggregation","Reduced aggregation"]],"caption":"All three cause mucocutaneous bleeding with a prolonged bleeding time and normal PT and PTT."},{"type":"p","text":"In uremia, retained nitrogenous compounds such as guanidinosuccinic acid increase nitric oxide production and impair platelet granule release, thromboxane A2 generation, and GpIIb/IIIa activation, so both adhesion and aggregation weaken. Anemia adds to the defect, because fewer red cells in the center of the stream push fewer platelets toward the vessel wall. The result is bruising and mucosal and gastrointestinal bleeding with a normal count and normal PT and PTT. Aspirin is the classic acquired comparison: it irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase 1, blocking thromboxane A2 for the platelet's lifespan of about 7 to 10 days."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C09","obj":"HE-9-C.9","title":"Inherited thrombophilias: antithrombin, protein C, protein S, and homocysteine","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thrombin formation is normally limited by two main brakes. Antithrombin is a serine protease inhibitor that inactivates thrombin and factor Xa, and to a lesser degree IXa and XIa; heparan sulfate on endothelium, like heparin, accelerates it about a thousandfold. The protein C system works differently: thrombin bound to endothelial thrombomodulin activates protein C, and activated protein C, with protein S as its cofactor, cleaves and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa, removing the cofactors that drive prothrombinase and tenase. Losing either brake lets each burst of thrombin grow larger and last longer."},{"type":"flow","title":"The protein C anticoagulant pathway","steps":["Thrombin binds thrombomodulin on intact endothelium","Thrombin-thrombomodulin activates protein C","Activated protein C binds its cofactor protein S","The complex cleaves factors Va and VIIIa","Tenase and prothrombinase slow, thrombin generation falls"],"caption":"Thrombin switches from procoagulant to anticoagulant when it binds thrombomodulin, a negative feedback that fails in protein C or protein S deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"These deficiencies cause mainly venous thromboembolism (deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism) because in slow venous flow the brakes on thrombin matter more than platelet adhesion. Heterozygous deficiencies of antithrombin, protein C, or protein S are autosomal dominant; antithrombin deficiency carries the highest risk of the three. Acquired deficiency is common and must be excluded: antithrombin is lost in the urine in nephrotic syndrome and consumed in DIC, and all three fall in liver disease. Free protein S falls in pregnancy and with estrogen. Homozygous protein C deficiency causes neonatal purpura fulminans, and the short half-life of protein C explains warfarin-induced skin necrosis in heterozygotes, since protein C activity falls before factors II and X."},{"type":"p","text":"Hyperhomocysteinemia differs because it raises both venous and arterial risk. Homocysteine is remethylated to methionine by a folate- and B12-dependent enzyme or converted to cystathionine by B6-dependent cystathionine beta-synthase, so deficiency of folate, B12, or B6, chronic kidney disease, or inherited cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency (homocystinuria) raises it. Excess homocysteine injures endothelium through oxidative stress and impairs its anticoagulant functions."},{"type":"table","title":"Inherited thrombophilias in this chapter","cols":["Disorder","Lost function","Distinctive clue"],"rows":[["Antithrombin deficiency","Inactivation of thrombin and Xa","Reduced heparin effect; acquired in nephrotic syndrome"],["Protein C deficiency","Inactivation of Va and VIIIa","Warfarin skin necrosis; neonatal purpura fulminans if homozygous"],["Protein S deficiency","Cofactor for activated protein C","Free protein S also low in pregnancy and with estrogen"],["Hyperhomocysteinemia","Endothelial integrity","Arterial and venous clots; low folate, B12, or B6; homocystinuria"]],"caption":"Factor V Leiden and the prothrombin gene mutation, the most common inherited thrombophilias, are covered in HE-6."},{"type":"p","text":"Suspect an inherited thrombophilia when venous thrombosis occurs at a young age (commonly cited as before about 45 to 50 years), recurs, appears without a provoking factor, appears at an unusual site (cerebral venous sinuses, mesenteric, portal, or hepatic veins), or runs in first-degree relatives."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 28-year-old man with a mesenteric vein thrombosis has a strong family history of clots. Which part of the coagulation system is most likely defective, and why is the clot venous rather than arterial?","explain":"A natural anticoagulant, antithrombin, protein C, or protein S, is likely deficient, so thrombin generation is not shut off. In slow venous flow, unchecked thrombin and fibrin formation drives thrombosis, whereas arterial clots depend more on platelets and endothelial injury.","check":"Compare with the protein C flow and the clues for inherited thrombophilia in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-03-C10","obj":"HE-9-C.10","title":"Hematologic paraneoplastic syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Some cancers change the blood without invading it, through procoagulants, hormones, or an immune response. Three patterns are tested often, and each has a clear mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Migratory thrombophlebitis (Trousseau syndrome) is recurrent superficial or deep venous thrombosis that appears in one site, resolves, and reappears elsewhere. It is classically linked to pancreatic adenocarcinoma and also to lung, stomach, and other mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas. Tumor cells express tissue factor and release mucin and other procoagulants that can activate factor X, producing the chronic, low-grade DIC described in C01, in which thrombosis outweighs bleeding. The same hypercoagulable state can deposit sterile fibrin-platelet vegetations on heart valves (nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis), which can embolize. Unexplained or migratory venous thrombosis in an older adult should raise suspicion of an occult cancer."},{"type":"p","text":"Paraneoplastic erythrocytosis results from inappropriate erythropoietin (EPO) secretion by the tumor. Renal cell carcinoma is the classic example: loss of the VHL tumor suppressor stabilizes hypoxia-inducible factor, which turns on EPO transcription even with normal oxygen delivery. Hepatocellular carcinoma, cerebellar hemangioblastoma, and uterine leiomyoma can do the same. The pattern is a raised hematocrit with high EPO and normal arterial oxygen saturation, which separates it from hypoxic secondary polycythemia (low saturation) and from polycythemia vera (low EPO, JAK2 mutation)."},{"type":"p","text":"Pure red cell aplasia is selective failure of erythropoiesis associated with thymoma. Autoreactive T cells or IgG autoantibodies, probably reflecting disordered T cell development within the thymoma, suppress erythroid progenitors. The result is a normocytic anemia with very low reticulocytes and a marrow nearly devoid of erythroid precursors, while white cells, platelets, and megakaryocytes are normal. The same tumor can cause myasthenia gravis. Parvovirus B19, which infects erythroid progenitors, is the main alternative cause."},{"type":"table","title":"Hematologic paraneoplastic syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Classic tumors","Mechanism","Key findings"],"rows":[["Trousseau syndrome","Pancreatic and other mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas","Tumor tissue factor and mucin activate coagulation","Migratory venous thrombosis, high D-dimer"],["Erythrocytosis","Renal cell and hepatocellular carcinoma","Ectopic, hypoxia-independent EPO","High hematocrit, high EPO, normal oxygen saturation"],["Pure red cell aplasia","Thymoma","Autoimmune suppression of erythroid progenitors","Anemia, reticulocytopenia, absent marrow erythroid precursors"]],"caption":"Each syndrome is explained by a tumor product (procoagulant or hormone) or by tumor-driven autoimmunity."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every disorder in this chapter fits one map of hemostasis. Anatomy and cell biology supply the players: endothelium makes vWF, thrombomodulin, and much of factor VIII, hepatocytes make most factors and thrombopoietin, megakaryocytes make platelets, and the spleen stores and clears them. Biochemistry explains vitamin K-dependent gamma-carboxylation and the homocysteine pathways, and immunology explains ITP, acquired factor VIII inhibitors, and thymoma-associated red cell aplasia as autoimmune injury. Microbiology and obstetrics supply DIC triggers, and pathology links the resulting microthrombi to injury in kidney, lung, brain, and adrenal glands."},{"type":"p","text":"The reasoning for any case is the same. Platelet count and bleeding pattern place the defect in primary hemostasis; PT, PTT, and the mixing study place it in the factors; fibrinogen, D-dimer, factor V, and factor VIII separate consumption from failed synthesis; and a clot in a young patient points to a lost anticoagulant. The limits are real: reference ranges differ between laboratories, vWF and fibrinogen are acute phase reactants that can hide a deficiency, and a prolonged PT in liver disease measures procoagulant loss without measuring the parallel loss of anticoagulants."}],"summary":["Platelet and vWF defects cause mucocutaneous bleeding with a prolonged bleeding time; factor defects cause deep, delayed bleeding with normal bleeding time.","DIC is systemic thrombin generation from tissue factor or endothelial injury; it gives low platelets and fibrinogen, prolonged PT and PTT, high D-dimer, and schistocytes.","In DIC, microthrombi injure kidney, lung, brain, and adrenals while consumption and fibrinolysis cause oozing from venipuncture sites and mucosa.","Hemophilia A (VIII) and B (IX) are X-linked, cause hemarthroses and muscle bleeds, and show an isolated prolonged PTT that corrects on mixing; an acquired VIII inhibitor does not correct.","ITP is IgG against GpIIb/IIIa with splenic destruction: isolated thrombocytopenia, normal PT and PTT, increased megakaryocytes, no splenomegaly; acute in children after viral illness, chronic in adult women.","vWF mediates adhesion through GpIb and carries factor VIII, so vWD gives mucosal bleeding, normal count, prolonged bleeding time, normal or prolonged PTT, and low ristocetin cofactor activity.","Vitamin K is needed to carboxylate II, VII, IX, X, C, and S; factor VII has the shortest half-life, so the PT prolongs first.","Liver failure lowers fibrinogen, factor V, and platelets but keeps factor VIII normal or high; DIC lowers factor VIII.","Bernard-Soulier (GpIb, giant platelets) blocks adhesion, Glanzmann (GpIIb/IIIa) blocks aggregation, and uremic toxins impair both with a normal count.","Antithrombin, protein C, and protein S deficiency and hyperhomocysteinemia promote thrombosis; tumors cause Trousseau syndrome, EPO-driven erythrocytosis, and thymoma-linked red cell aplasia."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus; Red Blood Cell and Bleeding Disorders"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolic Disease, and Shock"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Hemostasis and Blood Coagulation"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Blood Clotting; Vitamins (vitamin K, folate, and B12)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood Disorders: disorders of hemostasis"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders: antibody-mediated diseases"}]},"HE-9-D":{"unit":"HE-9-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04","title":"Neoplasms of Blood and Lymphoid Tissue","subject":"Hematologic and Lymphoreticular System (HEME)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the clonal neoplasms of blood-forming and lymphoid cells: the acute and chronic leukemias, Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphomas, T cell lymphomas, plasma cell neoplasms, myelodysplastic syndromes, primary myelofibrosis, and Langerhans cell histiocytosis, ending with tumor lysis syndrome. Each disease is explained from its cell of origin and its driving mutation, because those two facts predict the smear, the immunophenotype, the organs involved, and the complications. Board questions usually give an age, a smear finding, a marker, or a translocation and ask you to name the neoplasm or predict what it will do next, so the emphasis here is on reasoning from mechanism to finding.","prerequisites":["Hematopoiesis: myeloid and lymphoid lineages and their maturation stages","B and T cell development, germinal center reaction, and immunoglobulin structure","Proto-oncogenes, tumor suppressor genes, and how translocations activate oncogenes","Bone remodeling: RANKL, osteoprotegerin, osteoclasts, and osteoblasts","Coagulation cascade, fibrinolysis, and disseminated intravascular coagulation (HE-9-C)"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology (cluster of differentiation markers and immunodeficiency)","how":"Surface markers identify the neoplastic cell's lineage and stage, and loss of normal immune cells explains the infections in CLL and myeloma."},{"to":"Microbiology (oncogenic viruses and Helicobacter pylori)","how":"EBV, HTLV-1, and H. pylori drive specific lymphomas by sustaining proliferation of infected or antigen-stimulated lymphocytes."},{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"Light chain casts, hypercalcemia, and tumor lysis crystals each injure the nephron by a distinct mechanism."},{"to":"Biochemistry (purine degradation)","how":"Xanthine oxidase converts released purines to uric acid, which crystallizes in acidic tubular fluid in tumor lysis syndrome."},{"to":"Endocrinology (posterior pituitary)","how":"Langerhans cell histiocytosis of the pituitary stalk interrupts ADH delivery and causes central diabetes insipidus."}],"terms":[{"term":"Blast","def":"An immature hematopoietic precursor; 20% or more blasts in marrow or blood defines acute leukemia in standard criteria."},{"term":"Immunophenotype","def":"The pattern of surface and cytoplasmic markers (CD antigens, TdT, myeloperoxidase) that identifies a cell's lineage and maturation stage."},{"term":"Terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT)","def":"A DNA polymerase that adds nucleotides at gene segment junctions during receptor rearrangement, normally expressed only in early lymphoid precursors."},{"term":"Auer rod","def":"A needle-shaped cytoplasmic inclusion formed from fused, myeloperoxidase-positive primary granules, found only in neoplastic myeloid cells."},{"term":"Philadelphia chromosome","def":"The shortened chromosome 22 produced by t(9;22), carrying the BCR-ABL1 fusion gene."},{"term":"Reed-Sternberg cell","def":"The large, often binucleate neoplastic cell of classic Hodgkin lymphoma, derived from a germinal center B cell and positive for CD15 and CD30."},{"term":"M protein (M spike)","def":"A monoclonal immunoglobulin or light chain that appears as a narrow band on serum protein electrophoresis."},{"term":"Ineffective hematopoiesis","def":"Production of precursors that die in the marrow before release, giving a cellular marrow with low blood counts."},{"term":"Extramedullary hematopoiesis","def":"Blood cell production outside the marrow, chiefly in spleen and liver, when the marrow cannot support it."},{"term":"Leukoerythroblastic smear","def":"Nucleated red cells and immature granulocytes in the blood, a sign of marrow infiltration or fibrosis."},{"term":"Dry tap","def":"Failure to aspirate marrow because fibrosis or packed cells hold it in place; a core biopsy is then needed."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C01","obj":"HE-9-D.1","title":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute leukemias arise when mutations both block differentiation and allow continued proliferation, so immature blasts accumulate in the marrow. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a neoplasm of lymphoid precursors (lymphoblasts). It is the most common cancer of childhood, peaking at about 2 to 5 years of age, and about 85% of cases are precursor B cell tumors; the rest are precursor T cell tumors."},{"type":"p","text":"The defining marker is terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT), the enzyme that adds random nucleotides at gene segment junctions during immunoglobulin and T cell receptor rearrangement. Because normally only early lymphoid precursors express it, nuclear TdT identifies a lymphoblast and separates ALL from mature lymphoid neoplasms such as CLL or Burkitt lymphoma. B-ALL blasts are usually CD19 and CD10 positive; T-ALL blasts express CD3 and other T cell markers and commonly present in adolescent males as a mediastinal (thymic) mass that can compress the airway or superior vena cava. Lymphoblasts are myeloperoxidase negative and lack Auer rods."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical picture follows from marrow replacement. Crowding of normal precursors causes anemia (fatigue, pallor), thrombocytopenia (petechiae, bleeding), and neutropenia (fever, infection). Expansion of the marrow cavity and periosteal infiltration cause bone pain and tenderness, which may make a child limp or refuse to walk. Blasts also infiltrate lymph nodes, liver, and spleen."},{"type":"p","text":"The central nervous system and testes are sanctuary sites: the blood-brain and blood-testis barriers exclude many drugs, so blasts there survive and cause relapse. CNS involvement presents with headache, vomiting, or cranial nerve palsies. Down syndrome raises ALL risk roughly 10 to 20 times. Cytogenetics carries prognosis: hyperdiploidy and t(12;21) ETV6-RUNX1 are favorable childhood findings, while t(9;22) BCR-ABL1, more common in adults, is unfavorable."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanism behind each feature of ALL","cols":["Feature","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Anemia, bleeding, infection","Blasts crowd out erythroid, megakaryocytic, and granulocytic precursors"],["Bone pain","Marrow expansion and subperiosteal infiltration"],["Lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly","Tissue infiltration by circulating blasts"],["CNS and testicular relapse","Barriers keep drugs out, so blasts persist"],["TdT positive, myeloperoxidase negative","Early lymphoid precursor, not a myeloid cell"]],"caption":"Every feature of ALL traces to blast accumulation in marrow or tissue, or to the identity of the precursor cell."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C02","obj":"HE-9-D.2","title":"Acute myeloid leukemia and acute promyelocytic leukemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a neoplasm of myeloid precursors that accumulate as myeloblasts. It is mainly a disease of adults, with a median age near 65 to 70 years. The traditional diagnostic threshold is at least 20% blasts in marrow or blood; certain defining genetic lesions, such as t(15;17), establish AML at lower blast counts. Cytopenias produce the same anemia, bleeding, and infection seen in ALL. Monocytic subtypes infiltrate gums and skin, and very high blast counts can cause leukostasis in lung and brain vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"Myeloid identity is shown by myeloperoxidase (MPO) positivity and by Auer rods, which are crystallized primary granules. Auer rods are never seen in normal cells or in ALL, so one rod in a blast establishes myeloid lineage."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk factors share one theme, damage to hematopoietic stem cell DNA: prior alkylating chemotherapy, topoisomerase II inhibitors, ionizing radiation, and benzene exposure. Myelodysplastic syndromes and myeloproliferative neoplasms progress to AML by acquiring further mutations. Down syndrome raises AML risk, most strikingly acute megakaryoblastic leukemia before about age 5, often with GATA1 mutations."},{"type":"h","text":"Acute promyelocytic leukemia and DIC"},{"type":"p","text":"Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) carries t(15;17), which fuses PML to the retinoic acid receptor alpha gene (RARA). The fusion protein represses genes that retinoic acid normally switches on, so cells arrest at the promyelocyte stage. The promyelocytes are packed with granules and often many Auer rods. Their tissue factor activates coagulation, and annexin II on their surface speeds plasmin generation, so APL produces DIC with prominent fibrinolysis. Patients can bleed catastrophically, and DIC can worsen as cells break down. Pharmacologic doses of all-trans retinoic acid overcome the block and force maturation, which confirms the mechanism. For these reasons APL is a hematologic emergency."},{"type":"flow","title":"From t(15;17) to DIC in APL","steps":["t(15;17) creates the PML-RARA fusion gene","Fusion protein blocks retinoic acid-driven maturation","Granule-rich promyelocytes accumulate in marrow and blood","Tissue factor triggers thrombin generation","Annexin II speeds plasmin formation and fibrinolysis","Clotting factors and platelets are consumed: DIC with bleeding"],"caption":"APL links a single translocation to a coagulation emergency through the contents of arrested promyelocytes."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 35-year-old has gum bleeding, a platelet count of 25,000/uL, a prolonged PT, low fibrinogen, and blasts with many Auer rods. What translocation is most likely, and what will happen to the coagulation tests as more of these cells break down?","explain":"Explain why promyelocyte granules cause both clotting and fibrinolysis.","check":"Compare with the APL paragraph and the flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C03","obj":"HE-9-D.3","title":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is a neoplasm of small, mature-appearing B cells and is the most common leukemia of adults in Western countries, with a median age near 70. When the same cells are mainly in lymph nodes rather than blood, the disease is called small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL). Many patients are asymptomatic and are found because a routine count shows an absolute lymphocytosis. Others present with painless lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, or infections."},{"type":"p","text":"The immunophenotype is distinctive. CLL cells express the B cell markers CD19 and CD20 (dimly) and surface immunoglobulin at low levels, and they coexpress CD5, normally a T cell marker, together with CD23. CD5 also appears on mantle cell lymphoma, but mantle cell lymphoma is CD23 negative, which separates the two. On the smear, CLL cells are fragile and are crushed by the spreading slide, leaving smudge cells."},{"type":"p","text":"The complications come from immune dysregulation rather than marrow crowding alone. Normal B cell function is suppressed, so hypogammaglobulinemia is common, with recurrent bacterial infections, often by encapsulated organisms. At the same time tolerance fails: nonneoplastic B cells produce IgG autoantibodies against red cells, causing warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia with a positive direct antiglobulin test and spherocytes, and less often immune thrombocytopenia."},{"type":"p","text":"CLL is indolent, but in a small minority of patients (roughly 2% to 10%) the clone acquires further mutations and undergoes Richter transformation, most often to diffuse large B cell lymphoma. Transformation is suggested by a rapidly enlarging node, fever, weight loss, and a rising LDH, and it carries a poor prognosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in CLL and their causes","cols":["Finding","Cause"],"rows":[["Lymphocytosis with smudge cells","Accumulation of fragile, long-lived mature B cells"],["CD5 and CD23 positive","Characteristic phenotype of the CLL clone"],["Hypogammaglobulinemia, infections","Suppression of normal B cell antibody production"],["Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia","IgG autoantibodies from dysregulated nonneoplastic B cells"],["Abrupt node growth, fever, high LDH","Richter transformation to an aggressive lymphoma"]],"caption":"CLL injures the patient mainly through immune failure and autoimmunity, not through blast crowding."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C04","obj":"HE-9-D.4","title":"Chronic myeloid leukemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is a myeloproliferative neoplasm of a pluripotent stem cell, usually presenting in adults aged about 40 to 60. Its defining lesion is t(9;22), which joins BCR on chromosome 22 to ABL1 on chromosome 9. The shortened chromosome 22 is the Philadelphia chromosome. The fusion protein is a constitutively active tyrosine kinase that drives growth factor-independent proliferation through RAS and other signaling pathways."},{"type":"p","text":"Unlike the acute leukemias, maturation is preserved in chronic phase, so the blood fills with granulocytes at every stage: myelocytes and metamyelocytes predominate over blasts, alongside band and segmented neutrophils. Basophilia and eosinophilia are typical, and platelets are often high. The spleen enlarges, sometimes massively, from extramedullary hematopoiesis and leukemic infiltration, causing left upper quadrant fullness or early satiety. Symptoms are often mild fatigue and weight loss."},{"type":"p","text":"The main differential is a leukemoid reaction, a reactive neutrophilia from severe infection or inflammation. Basophilia and splenomegaly favor CML, and a low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score has historically supported CML, but diagnosis is confirmed only by detecting t(9;22) by karyotype or FISH, or the BCR-ABL1 transcript by RT-PCR. Its presence also predicts response to drugs that inhibit the ABL1 kinase, which shows how completely the disease depends on this one enzyme."},{"type":"p","text":"Without control, CML progresses. Genomic instability adds mutations that block maturation, moving the disease into an accelerated phase (rising blasts and basophils, worsening counts and spleen size) and then blast crisis, in which 20% or more blasts make it behave as acute leukemia. About two thirds of blast crises are myeloid and one third lymphoid, reflecting the stem cell origin."},{"type":"flow","title":"Progression of chronic myeloid leukemia","steps":["t(9;22) forms BCR-ABL1 in a stem cell","Constitutive tyrosine kinase signaling drives proliferation","Chronic phase: all maturation stages, basophilia, splenomegaly","New mutations impair maturation: accelerated phase","Blast crisis: 20% or more blasts, myeloid or lymphoid"],"caption":"CML begins with intact maturation and ends as an acute leukemia once maturation is blocked."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C05","obj":"HE-9-D.5","title":"Hairy cell leukemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hairy cell leukemia is an uncommon, indolent neoplasm of mature B cells, typically in middle-aged men (median age about 55, men affected about four to five times as often as women). It is named for fine hairlike cytoplasmic projections seen on the smear and on electron microscopy. Nearly all classic cases carry the BRAF V600E mutation, which constantly activates the MAPK pathway and is a useful diagnostic marker."},{"type":"p","text":"The cells have a characteristic phenotype. They are B cells (CD19, CD20) that also express CD11c, CD25, and CD103, along with annexin A1. Historically they were identified by tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP) staining, which is still tested even though flow cytometry has largely replaced it."},{"type":"p","text":"The distribution of hairy cells explains the clinical picture. They home to the bone marrow, the splenic red pulp, and the liver, rather than to lymph nodes. In the marrow they induce a fine reticulin fibrosis that traps cells, so aspiration yields a dry tap and a core biopsy is needed. In the spleen they fill the red pulp, producing massive splenomegaly, while lymphadenopathy is characteristically absent. Marrow infiltration and splenic sequestration together cause pancytopenia, and monocytopenia is a typical clue. Infections, including atypical mycobacterial infections, follow from the loss of monocytes and neutrophils."},{"type":"table","title":"Chronic leukemias compared","cols":["Feature","CLL","CML","Hairy cell leukemia"],"rows":[["Cell","Mature B cell","Myeloid stem cell","Mature B cell"],["Key marker or lesion","CD5 and CD23","t(9;22) BCR-ABL1","TRAP, CD11c, CD103, BRAF V600E"],["Blood","Lymphocytosis, smudge cells","Neutrophils at all stages, basophilia","Pancytopenia, monocytopenia, hairy cells"],["Spleen and nodes","Nodes and spleen often enlarged","Splenomegaly","Massive splenomegaly, nodes spared"],["Marrow","Infiltrated by small lymphocytes","Hypercellular, granulocytic","Dry tap from reticulin fibrosis"]],"caption":"A chronic leukemia is identified by its cell of origin, its marker, and where the cells settle."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C06","obj":"HE-9-D.6","title":"Hodgkin lymphoma versus non-Hodgkin lymphoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lymphomas are neoplasms of lymphocytes that form masses, usually in lymph nodes. Classic Hodgkin lymphoma is unusual because the neoplastic cells, Reed-Sternberg cells and their mononuclear variants, make up only a small fraction of the tumor. A Reed-Sternberg cell is large, with two mirror-image nuclei or nuclear lobes, each with a prominent inclusion-like nucleolus. It derives from a germinal center B cell that has lost most of its B cell program, so it is typically CD15 and CD30 positive, CD45 negative, and usually CD20 negative. Its cytokines recruit reactive lymphocytes, eosinophils, histiocytes, and fibroblasts, which form most of the mass."},{"type":"p","text":"Hodgkin lymphoma usually begins in a single node group, often cervical or mediastinal, and spreads in an orderly way to adjacent groups through lymphatics. This contiguous spread means it is often localized at diagnosis and that anatomic stage predicts behavior well; extranodal and leukemic disease is uncommon. Age distribution is bimodal, with a peak in young adults and a second in older adults. EBV is present in the tumor cells of a substantial fraction of cases, most often the mixed cellularity and lymphocyte-depleted types. Cytokines from the tumor and its reactive background produce B symptoms (fever, drenching night sweats, and loss of more than 10% of body weight over 6 months)."},{"type":"table","title":"Subtypes of Hodgkin lymphoma","cols":["Subtype","Typical patient","Distinctive features"],"rows":[["Nodular sclerosis (most common)","Adolescents and young adults, both sexes","Collagen bands divide nodules; lacunar cell variant; mediastinal mass"],["Mixed cellularity","Older adults, men, people with HIV","Many eosinophils and plasma cells; often EBV positive"],["Lymphocyte-rich","Adults","Few RS cells among small lymphocytes; favorable"],["Lymphocyte-depleted","Older adults, HIV","Many RS cells, few lymphocytes; usually EBV positive; least favorable"],["Nodular lymphocyte predominant","Men, often 30 to 50 years","Popcorn cells, CD20 positive, CD15 and CD30 negative"]],"caption":"The first four are classic Hodgkin lymphoma; nodular lymphocyte predominant Hodgkin lymphoma has a different, B cell phenotype."},{"type":"p","text":"Non-Hodgkin lymphomas (NHL) are a varied group, most of B cell origin, in which the neoplastic cells form most of the mass. They often involve several noncontiguous node groups, including mesenteric nodes and Waldeyer ring, and frequently arise in or spread to extranodal sites such as the gastrointestinal tract, skin, and central nervous system. Many also enter the marrow and blood. For this reason anatomic stage carries less prognostic weight than histologic type."},{"type":"table","title":"Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma compared","cols":["Feature","Classic Hodgkin lymphoma","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma"],"rows":[["Neoplastic cells","Few Reed-Sternberg cells in a reactive background","Neoplastic cells form most of the mass"],["Markers","CD15 and CD30 positive","Mostly B cell markers (CD19, CD20); some T cell"],["Spread","Contiguous, node to adjacent node","Noncontiguous, often widespread"],["Extranodal disease","Uncommon","Common"],["Age","Bimodal","Rises with age (Burkitt and lymphoblastic types in children)"],["Infectious link","EBV in a subset","EBV, HTLV-1, H. pylori, HIV in specific types"]],"caption":"Contiguous spread and a sparse neoplastic population are the two features that set classic Hodgkin lymphoma apart."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 24-year-old woman has a mediastinal mass and night sweats. Biopsy shows bands of collagen and scattered large cells in clear spaces that are CD15 and CD30 positive. Predict the subtype and whether disease is likely to be confined to adjacent node groups.","explain":"Explain how the pattern of spread differs from a non-Hodgkin lymphoma.","check":"Compare with the subtype table and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C07","obj":"HE-9-D.7","title":"Major B cell non-Hodgkin lymphomas","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Many B cell lymphomas carry a translocation that moves an oncogene next to an immunoglobulin locus, most often the heavy chain locus (IGH) on chromosome 14. B cells drive their immunoglobulin genes very hard, so the moved oncogene is overexpressed. The function of that oncogene sets the tumor's behavior."},{"type":"p","text":"Diffuse large B cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is the most common NHL, about a quarter to a third of cases. Sheets of large B cells efface the node. It usually presents in older adults as a rapidly enlarging nodal or extranodal mass and is aggressive. It can arise de novo or by transformation of follicular lymphoma or CLL. Rearrangements of BCL6 are common."},{"type":"p","text":"Follicular lymphoma carries t(14;18), which places the anti-apoptotic gene BCL2 under IGH control. Cells survive when they should die, so the tumor grows slowly by accumulation, forming crowded back-to-back follicles. It is indolent, with painless lymphadenopathy that may wax and wane, but it is rarely cured and can transform to DLBCL. In normal germinal centers BCL2 is off, so BCL2 staining of follicles separates follicular lymphoma from reactive hyperplasia."},{"type":"p","text":"Burkitt lymphoma carries t(8;14), placing MYC under IGH control (variants pair MYC with light chain loci). MYC drives cell cycle entry and growth, so nearly every cell is dividing; it is among the fastest-growing human tumors. Many cells die, and macrophages that ingest them appear as pale spaces among dark tumor cells, the starry sky pattern. The endemic African form, almost always EBV positive, presents as a jaw or facial mass in children; the sporadic form presents as an abdominal mass, often ileocecal."},{"type":"p","text":"Mantle cell lymphoma carries t(11;14), overexpressing cyclin D1, which pushes cells from G1 into S phase. It is CD5 positive like CLL but CD23 negative, occurs in older men, and is aggressive, often with GI involvement (lymphomatous polyposis). MALT lymphoma, an extranodal marginal zone lymphoma, arises where chronic inflammation has created lymphoid tissue: the stomach with Helicobacter pylori, salivary glands in Sjogren syndrome, and thyroid in Hashimoto thyroiditis. Early gastric MALT lymphoma depends on H. pylori-driven T cell help and can regress after the infection is eradicated."},{"type":"table","title":"B cell non-Hodgkin lymphomas","cols":["Lymphoma","Lesion or driver","Effect","Key feature","Behavior"],"rows":[["Diffuse large B cell","Varied; BCL6 common","Varied","Sheets of large cells; most common","Aggressive"],["Follicular","t(14;18) BCL2","Blocks apoptosis","Back-to-back follicles","Indolent, may transform"],["Burkitt","t(8;14) MYC; EBV","Drives proliferation","Starry sky","Very fast growing"],["Mantle cell","t(11;14) cyclin D1","Drives G1 to S transition","CD5 positive, CD23 negative","Aggressive"],["MALT","H. pylori, autoimmunity","Antigen-driven growth","Extranodal mucosal sites","Indolent, may regress"]],"caption":"Whether the activated gene blocks death or drives division predicts whether the lymphoma is indolent or aggressive."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C08","obj":"HE-9-D.8","title":"T cell lymphomas: adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma and mycosis fungoides","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral T cell lymphomas are neoplasms of mature, post-thymic T cells and are far less common than B cell lymphomas in Western countries. Two are emphasized for boards because each is defined by a clear mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma (ATLL) is a neoplasm of CD4 T cells caused by human T cell leukemia virus type 1 (HTLV-1), a retrovirus endemic in southern Japan, the Caribbean, parts of South America, and West Africa. It spreads through breast milk, sexual contact, and blood. The viral Tax protein activates host transcription factors such as NF-kappaB and drives T cell proliferation; after decades of infection, a small fraction of carriers (a few percent) develop ATLL. The cells are CD4 and CD25 positive and often have multilobed flower-shaped nuclei. Typical features are skin lesions, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, lymphocytosis, hypercalcemia, and lytic bone lesions. Tumor cells secrete PTH-related protein and express RANKL, both of which activate osteoclasts. The acute form is aggressive."},{"type":"p","text":"Mycosis fungoides is a neoplasm of skin-homing CD4 T cells, the most common cutaneous T cell lymphoma. The cells carry homing receptors that direct them to the skin and infiltrate the epidermis (epidermotropism), where they cluster as Pautrier microabscesses, which are collections of atypical lymphocytes rather than true abscesses. Their nuclei are folded and cerebriform. Disease progresses slowly over years from eczema-like patches to plaques and then tumors. When the clone spreads to the blood, the circulating cells are called Sezary cells; Sezary syndrome is the combination of generalized erythroderma, lymphadenopathy, and Sezary cells in the blood."},{"type":"table","title":"Two T cell lymphomas compared","cols":["Feature","Adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma","Mycosis fungoides and Sezary syndrome"],"rows":[["Cell","CD4 T cell, CD25 positive","Skin-homing CD4 T cell"],["Cause","HTLV-1 (Tax protein)","None established"],["Hallmark findings","Hypercalcemia, lytic bone lesions, flower cells","Patches, plaques, Pautrier microabscesses, cerebriform nuclei"],["Blood","Leukemic phase common","Sezary cells in Sezary syndrome"],["Course","Usually aggressive","Usually indolent for years"]],"caption":"Both are CD4 T cell neoplasms; HTLV-1 and hypercalcemia identify ATLL, while skin-limited epidermotropic disease identifies mycosis fungoides."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C09","obj":"HE-9-D.9","title":"Multiple myeloma: from clonal plasma cells to end-organ damage","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Multiple myeloma is a neoplasm of plasma cells, the terminally differentiated B cells that secrete antibody. It occurs mainly in older adults and is about twice as common in Black as in White Americans. The clone grows in the marrow, depends on IL-6 from marrow stromal cells, and secretes a single immunoglobulin or light chain in enormous amounts. On serum protein electrophoresis this monoclonal protein forms a narrow M spike in the gamma region, most often IgG, next most often IgA, and in about a fifth of cases free light chains only. Normal polyclonal immunoglobulin falls."},{"type":"p","text":"Bone disease is the most characteristic damage. Myeloma cells raise RANKL and lower osteoprotegerin, the decoy receptor that normally neutralizes RANKL, so osteoclasts are activated. They also inhibit osteoblasts, partly through DKK1, a blocker of Wnt signaling. Resorption without new bone formation produces punched-out lytic lesions, most often in the skull, spine, ribs, and pelvis, with pathologic fractures and hypercalcemia. Because osteoblasts are inactive, serum alkaline phosphatase is usually normal and radionuclide bone scans often miss the lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Free light chains are small enough to be filtered. They overwhelm proximal tubular reabsorption, appear in urine as Bence Jones protein (which routine dipsticks, sensitive mainly to albumin, miss), and in the distal nephron bind uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) to form obstructing, inflammatory casts. This light chain cast nephropathy, hypercalcemia, and dehydration all contribute to renal failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Anemia, usually normocytic, results from marrow replacement, inflammatory cytokines, and reduced erythropoietin from injured kidneys. The high concentration of serum protein reduces the charge that normally keeps red cells apart, so they stack as rouleaux and the erythrocyte sedimentation rate rises. Infections, especially with encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, follow from the loss of functional antibody. Hypercalcemia, renal injury, anemia, and bone lesions (CRAB) are the end-organ features that define symptomatic myeloma."},{"type":"flow","title":"Myeloma bone disease","steps":["Clonal plasma cells expand in the marrow","RANKL rises and osteoprotegerin falls","Osteoclasts are activated","DKK1 suppresses osteoblast bone formation","Punched-out lytic lesions and pathologic fractures","Calcium released from bone causes hypercalcemia"],"caption":"Uncoupled resorption explains lytic lesions, hypercalcemia, normal alkaline phosphatase, and a falsely reassuring bone scan."},{"type":"table","title":"Myeloma manifestations and mechanisms","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["M spike, low normal immunoglobulins","One clone secretes one antibody; normal B cells are suppressed"],["Lytic lesions, hypercalcemia","RANKL-driven osteoclast activation with osteoblast inhibition"],["Renal failure","Light chain casts, hypercalcemia, dehydration, amyloid"],["Anemia","Marrow replacement, cytokines, low erythropoietin"],["Rouleaux, high ESR","Excess serum protein lets red cells stack"],["Bacterial infections","Lack of functional polyclonal antibody"]],"caption":"Each feature of myeloma follows from plasma cell expansion, its secreted protein, or its effect on bone."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 68-year-old with back pain has calcium 12.8 mg/dL, creatinine 2.4 mg/dL, normal alkaline phosphatase, and a negative urine dipstick for protein. Predict what a urine protein electrophoresis and a skull radiograph will show.","explain":"Explain why the dipstick and the alkaline phosphatase are misleading.","check":"Compare with the bone and renal paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C10","obj":"HE-9-D.10","title":"MGUS, Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia, and AL amyloidosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An M protein does not by itself mean myeloma. Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) is a small clonal plasma cell population that produces a modest M protein without damaging organs. It is common, present in about 3% of people over 50 and more with age. The defining limits are an M protein below 3 g/dL, fewer than 10% clonal plasma cells in the marrow, and no CRAB features or other myeloma-defining biomarkers (such as 60% or more marrow plasma cells). MGUS progresses to myeloma or a related disorder at about 1% per year, so it is a premalignant state. Smoldering myeloma sits between them: a higher M protein or 10% to 60% marrow plasma cells, still without end-organ damage."},{"type":"p","text":"Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia is a lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma, a B cell neoplasm with cells ranging from small lymphocytes to plasma cells, that secretes monoclonal IgM. Most cases carry the MYD88 L265P mutation. IgM is a pentamer of about 900 kDa that largely stays in the vessels, so a high level raises plasma viscosity. Hyperviscosity slows flow in small vessels, causing blurred vision with dilated, segmented retinal veins, headache, dizziness, confusion, and mucosal bleeding from impaired platelet function. Because the cell is lymphoplasmacytic, lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly are common, while lytic lesions and hypercalcemia are characteristically absent."},{"type":"p","text":"AL amyloidosis results when a plasma cell clone, often small, secretes light chains, usually lambda, that misfold into beta-pleated sheet fibrils. The fibrils deposit in the kidney (nephrotic syndrome), heart (restrictive cardiomyopathy with low voltage on ECG), tongue (macroglossia), nerves (peripheral and autonomic neuropathy, carpal tunnel syndrome), and vessel walls (periorbital purpura). Tissue stains with Congo red and shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light. AL amyloidosis can occur alone or with myeloma."},{"type":"table","title":"Plasma cell and related disorders","cols":["Feature","MGUS","Multiple myeloma","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia"],"rows":[["M protein","IgG or IgA most often; below 3 g/dL","IgG or IgA, or light chains only","IgM"],["Marrow","Under 10% clonal plasma cells","10% or more clonal plasma cells","Lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate"],["End-organ damage","None","CRAB features","Hyperviscosity, cytopenias"],["Bones","Normal","Lytic lesions","No lytic lesions"],["Nodes and spleen","Normal","Usually normal","Often enlarged"]],"caption":"The protein class, the plasma cell burden, and the presence or absence of end-organ damage separate the three disorders."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: classifying a monoclonal protein","text":"Patient A has an IgG M protein of 1.4 g/dL, 6% marrow plasma cells, normal calcium, creatinine, and hemoglobin, and no bone lesions. All three MGUS criteria are met, so this is MGUS. Patient B has IgG 4.2 g/dL, 30% plasma cells, hemoglobin 9.1 g/dL, and vertebral lytic lesions. The plasma cell burden exceeds 10% and there are CRAB features (anemia, bone lesions), so this is multiple myeloma. Patient C has an IgM spike of 3.5 g/dL, blurred vision, epistaxis, enlarged nodes, and a normal skeletal survey. IgM, hyperviscosity, nodal disease, and absent lytic lesions point to Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia. The class of protein and the type of organ damage decide the diagnosis, not the size of the spike alone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C11","obj":"HE-9-D.11","title":"Myelodysplastic syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS) are clonal stem cell neoplasms in which hematopoiesis is both abnormal in appearance (dysplastic) and ineffective. Most patients are older adults, with a median age near 70. Therapy-related MDS follows alkylating agents or radiation, usually after a latency of several years, and often shows loss of chromosome 5 or 7 material; benzene exposure is another recognized risk."},{"type":"p","text":"The central paradox is a marrow that is usually normocellular or hypercellular in a patient with low blood counts. The clone produces many precursors, but they are defective and undergo apoptosis in the marrow before release. This intramedullary death is what ineffective hematopoiesis means. Patients therefore present with cytopenias: macrocytic or normocytic anemia, neutropenia with infections, and thrombocytopenia with bleeding. Many are found because an unexplained macrocytic anemia does not respond to vitamin B12 or folate."},{"type":"p","text":"Dysplasia is visible in each lineage. Pseudo-Pelger-Huet neutrophils have bilobed nuclei (or nonsegmented nuclei) and often reduced granules, resembling the inherited Pelger-Huet anomaly but acquired. Ringed sideroblasts are erythroid precursors whose iron-laden mitochondria encircle the nucleus, seen on Prussian blue stain; they reflect defective heme synthesis and are strongly associated with SF3B1 splicing factor mutations. Megakaryocytes are small with hypolobated nuclei, and erythroid precursors may look megaloblastoid."},{"type":"p","text":"MDS is defined by fewer than 20% marrow blasts. Additional mutations raise the blast count, and about a third of patients progress to AML, which carries a poor prognosis. Many others die of the cytopenias themselves, through infection or bleeding, before transformation occurs. Isolated deletion of 5q is a distinct, more favorable subtype, typically in older women, with macrocytic anemia and normal or high platelets."},{"type":"table","title":"Dysplastic findings in MDS","cols":["Lineage","Finding","Meaning"],"rows":[["Granulocytic","Pseudo-Pelger-Huet neutrophils, hypogranular cells","Abnormal nuclear segmentation and granule formation"],["Erythroid","Ringed sideroblasts, megaloblastoid precursors","Defective heme synthesis and nuclear maturation"],["Megakaryocytic","Small hypolobated megakaryocytes","Abnormal megakaryocyte maturation"],["Blasts","Under 20%","Above this threshold the disease is AML"]],"caption":"Dysplasia in one or more lineages, cytopenias, and a cellular marrow together define MDS."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C12","obj":"HE-9-D.12","title":"Primary myelofibrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Primary myelofibrosis is a myeloproliferative neoplasm, a relative of polycythemia vera and essential thrombocythemia, in which the marrow becomes progressively scarred. Its driver mutations all keep JAK-STAT signaling switched on without growth factor: JAK2 V617F in about half to 60% of cases, CALR in about a quarter, and MPL (the thrombopoietin receptor) in about 5% to 10%. A small fraction has none of the three."},{"type":"p","text":"The fibrosis is reactive and is driven by neoplastic megakaryocytes. These cells proliferate in atypical clusters and release growth factors, including platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta), that stimulate normal fibroblasts to lay down reticulin and then collagen. The bone may become sclerotic. In an early prefibrotic phase the marrow is hypercellular and platelet counts may be high, but as scarring advances the marrow can no longer support hematopoiesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Hematopoiesis then shifts to the spleen and liver, where it took place in fetal life. This extramedullary hematopoiesis produces massive splenomegaly, with early satiety and left upper quadrant discomfort, and hepatomegaly; portal hypertension can follow. Marrow fibrosis makes aspiration fail, a dry tap, so diagnosis requires a core biopsy. Red cells squeezed through scarred marrow and released from extramedullary sites are deformed into teardrop cells (dacrocytes), and the loss of marrow architecture releases nucleated red cells and immature granulocytes, producing a leukoerythroblastic smear. Anemia is the typical count abnormality, with variable white cell and platelet counts. High cell turnover raises uric acid and LDH, and constitutional symptoms are common. A minority of patients transform to AML."},{"type":"flow","title":"From driver mutation to splenomegaly","steps":["JAK2, CALR, or MPL mutation activates JAK-STAT signaling","Atypical megakaryocytes proliferate in the marrow","PDGF and TGF-beta stimulate fibroblast collagen deposition","Fibrotic marrow cannot support hematopoiesis","Hematopoiesis moves to spleen and liver: massive splenomegaly","Teardrop cells and leukoerythroblastic smear appear"],"caption":"The fibroblasts are normal; the neoplastic megakaryocytes cause the fibrosis that displaces blood formation."},{"type":"p","text":"Teardrop cells and a leukoerythroblastic picture are not specific. Marrow infiltration by metastatic carcinoma, granulomas, or other neoplasms (myelophthisis) produces the same smear, and secondary fibrosis can follow polycythemia vera or essential thrombocythemia, so the diagnosis rests on marrow biopsy and mutation testing."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C13","obj":"HE-9-D.13","title":"Langerhans cell histiocytosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Langerhans cells are dendritic antigen-presenting cells of the epidermis and mucosa. Langerhans cell histiocytosis (LCH) is a clonal proliferation of cells with the Langerhans cell phenotype, now regarded as a myeloid neoplasm because many cases carry activating mutations of the MAPK pathway, most often BRAF V600E (in about half). The lesional cells have abundant pale cytoplasm and grooved, folded nuclei, and they are usually mixed with many eosinophils."},{"type":"p","text":"The cells are identified by immunostaining and ultrastructure. They express CD1a, S100, and langerin (CD207). Langerin is the protein that forms Birbeck granules, rod-shaped cytoplasmic structures with a dilated end that gives them a tennis racket shape on electron microscopy."},{"type":"p","text":"Presentation depends on how widely the cells spread, and it is mainly a disease of children. Unifocal disease, called eosinophilic granuloma, is a single lytic bone lesion, most often in the skull, femur, vertebra, or rib, in an older child or young adult. It causes local pain, swelling, or a pathologic fracture, and can regress spontaneously. Multifocal bone disease may involve the skull base and orbit and the pituitary stalk. Infiltration of the stalk or posterior pituitary interrupts ADH delivery, causing central diabetes insipidus with polyuria and polydipsia. Multisystem disease, usually in children under about 2 years, involves skin (a scaly, seborrheic-like eruption), liver, spleen, lymph nodes, lungs, and marrow; marrow involvement causes cytopenias, and the course can be aggressive."},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical patterns of LCH","cols":["Pattern","Typical age","Main findings"],"rows":[["Unifocal (eosinophilic granuloma)","Older children, young adults","Single lytic bone lesion, often skull; pain or fracture"],["Multifocal bone","Young children","Several lytic lesions, skull base and orbit; diabetes insipidus"],["Multisystem","Infants and toddlers","Skin rash, hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, lung disease, cytopenias"]],"caption":"The same CD1a and S100 positive cell causes a single bone lesion or widespread disease, depending on extent."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-04-C14","obj":"HE-9-D.14","title":"Tumor lysis syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tumor lysis syndrome (TLS) is the metabolic consequence of releasing the contents of a very large number of tumor cells into the blood faster than the kidneys can clear them. It usually begins within about 12 to 72 hours after cytotoxic therapy starts, and it can occur spontaneously in tumors with very high turnover. Risk is greatest with a large, rapidly dividing, treatment-sensitive tumor: Burkitt lymphoma, ALL and AML with high white counts, and other bulky aggressive lymphomas. Preexisting kidney disease, dehydration, and a high pretreatment uric acid or LDH increase risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Each abnormality follows from what cells contain. Potassium is the main intracellular cation, so hyperkalemia appears early and is the most immediately dangerous change, causing peaked T waves, conduction block, and ventricular arrhythmias. Phosphate is concentrated in cells (malignant lymphoblasts carry even more than normal lymphocytes), so hyperphosphatemia follows. Phosphate binds calcium and precipitates as calcium phosphate, lowering ionized calcium; the resulting hypocalcemia increases neuromuscular excitability (paresthesias, tetany, seizures) and prolongs the QT interval. Nucleic acids are broken down, and their purines are converted by xanthine oxidase, through hypoxanthine and xanthine, to uric acid, giving hyperuricemia."},{"type":"p","text":"The kidney is injured by crystals. Uric acid has a pKa of about 5.5, so in the acidic, concentrated fluid of the distal tubules and collecting ducts it exists largely as poorly soluble uric acid and crystallizes. Calcium phosphate also deposits in tubules and interstitium. The resulting acute kidney injury reduces excretion of potassium, phosphate, and uric acid, which raises them further, a self-reinforcing cycle."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The renal cycle in tumor lysis syndrome","steps":["Tumor cells lyse and release K+, phosphate, and purines","Uric acid and calcium phosphate crystallize in tubules","Acute kidney injury lowers excretion","Serum K+, phosphate, and uric acid rise further"],"caption":"Kidney injury both results from and amplifies the metabolic abnormalities."},{"type":"table","title":"Laboratory pattern in tumor lysis syndrome","cols":["Value","Direction","Source","Main danger"],"rows":[["Potassium","High","Intracellular K+ released","Arrhythmia, cardiac arrest"],["Phosphate","High","Intracellular phosphate released","Calcium phosphate deposition"],["Calcium","Low","Precipitates with phosphate","Tetany, seizures, long QT"],["Uric acid","High","Purine breakdown by xanthine oxidase","Crystal nephropathy"],["Creatinine","High","Crystal-induced kidney injury","Further retention of K+ and phosphate"]],"caption":"Four of the five values rise; calcium is the one that falls, because it is consumed by phosphate."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: predicting the labs","text":"A 9-year-old boy with a large abdominal Burkitt lymphoma starts chemotherapy. Thirty-six hours later he has muscle cramps and reduced urine output. Start from cell contents. Massive lysis releases potassium, so expect potassium above about 6 mEq/L with peaked T waves. Released phosphate rises and binds calcium, so phosphate is high and total and ionized calcium are low; the cramps are hypocalcemic tetany. Released purines become uric acid, which crystallizes in acidic tubular fluid, and calcium phosphate deposits as well, so creatinine rises and urine output falls. Reduced filtration then retains more potassium and phosphate. The only value that falls is calcium, and the most urgent threat is the potassium."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Across these neoplasms the same few principles predict almost everything. The cell of origin sets the markers and the sites involved: TdT marks precursor lymphoid cells, myeloperoxidase and Auer rods mark myeloid blasts, CD5 marks CLL and mantle cell lymphoma, CD15 and CD30 mark Reed-Sternberg cells, and plasma cells reveal themselves through the protein they secrete. The driver lesion sets the behavior: a block in maturation gives an acute leukemia, a constantly active tyrosine kinase with intact maturation gives a myeloproliferative neoplasm (CML, primary myelofibrosis), a block in apoptosis gives an indolent lymphoma, and forced cell division gives an aggressive one. Microbiology adds EBV, HTLV-1, and H. pylori as drivers of lymphocyte proliferation, and immunology explains why CLL and myeloma cause infections and autoimmunity."},{"type":"p","text":"Complications link these diseases to other systems. Marrow failure produces anemia, bleeding, and infection regardless of the cause, so cytopenias alone do not identify the neoplasm. Bone is attacked through RANKL in myeloma and ATLL and by direct infiltration in ALL and LCH; the kidney is injured by light chains, calcium, amyloid, and tumor lysis crystals; coagulation fails in APL; and the pituitary fails in LCH. Teardrop cells, a dry tap, or an M protein point toward a diagnosis but are shared by other conditions, so classification ultimately rests on morphology combined with immunophenotype and genetics, and diagnostic thresholds are revised as classification systems change."}],"summary":["Acute leukemias combine blocked maturation with proliferation; TdT identifies ALL, and myeloperoxidase with Auer rods identifies AML.","APL with t(15;17) PML-RARA arrests cells at the promyelocyte stage, and their granules trigger DIC with marked fibrinolysis.","CLL is a CD5 and CD23 positive mature B cell neoplasm whose main harms are hypogammaglobulinemia, warm autoimmune hemolysis, and Richter transformation.","CML is driven by the BCR-ABL1 tyrosine kinase from t(9;22), shows all maturation stages with basophilia, and ends in blast crisis when maturation fails.","Hairy cell leukemia (TRAP, CD11c, CD103, BRAF V600E) homes to marrow and splenic red pulp, causing a dry tap, pancytopenia, and massive splenomegaly without lymphadenopathy.","Classic Hodgkin lymphoma has sparse CD15 and CD30 positive Reed-Sternberg cells and spreads contiguously; non-Hodgkin lymphoma spreads noncontiguously and often extranodally.","B cell lymphoma behavior follows the translocated gene: BCL2 in follicular lymphoma is indolent, MYC in Burkitt lymphoma is very fast, and cyclin D1 marks mantle cell lymphoma.","Myeloma cells activate osteoclasts through RANKL and suppress osteoblasts, causing lytic lesions and hypercalcemia; light chains form tubular casts and can form AL amyloid.","MGUS has an M protein below 3 g/dL, under 10% marrow plasma cells, and no organ damage; Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia secretes IgM, causing hyperviscosity without lytic lesions.","Tumor lysis releases K+, phosphate, and purines, producing hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia, hyperuricemia, and crystal-induced acute kidney injury."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System, section on amyloidosis"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Blood Disorders; Neoplasia"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Lymphocyte development and antigen receptor gene rearrangement"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Human cancer viruses (EBV and HTLV-1)"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Nucleotide metabolism, purine degradation"}]},"HE-9-E":{"unit":"HE-9-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-05","title":"Infectious Diseases of the Blood and Lymphatics","subject":"Hematopoietic System (HEME)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers infections whose main damage is done in the blood, the vessels, and the lymphatic system: the red cell parasites Babesia and Plasmodium, the blood fluke Schistosoma, the Aedes-borne arboviruses, the filoviruses, plague, cat scratch disease, and streptococcal lymphangitis. Board questions usually give an exposure, a smear or biopsy finding, and a laboratory pattern, then ask for the organism or the next complication. Each disease becomes predictable once you know where the organism lives, which host cell or vessel it injures, and whether the damage comes from the organism itself or from the host response to it.","prerequisites":["Red cell structure, splenic filtration, and laboratory markers of hemolysis (HE-1 to HE-3)","Th1, Th2, and antibody effector responses, including Fc receptors and granuloma formation (IM units)","Portal venous anatomy and the causes of portal hypertension (GI units)","Coagulation cascade and disseminated intravascular coagulation (HE-6)","Lymph node architecture: follicles, paracortex, medulla, and sinuses"],"connections":[{"to":"Hemolytic anemias (HE-8)","how":"Babesia and Plasmodium produce the same intravascular and extravascular hemolysis pattern taught for intrinsic and extrinsic red cell disorders."},{"to":"Hepatic and portal disease (GI units)","how":"S. mansoni is the classic cause of presinusoidal portal hypertension with preserved liver synthetic function."},{"to":"Urinary bladder pathology (UR units)","how":"S. haematobium explains why squamous cell carcinoma, rather than urothelial carcinoma, arises in the bladder in endemic regions."},{"to":"Shock and sepsis (IM-6)","how":"Severe dengue, filovirus disease, and septicemic plague all end in vascular leak, DIC, and shock driven by cytokines."},{"to":"Lymphomas (HE-9 lymphoid neoplasms)","how":"Reading a reactive lymph node pattern correctly is the first step in separating benign enlargement from follicular and other lymphomas."}],"terms":[{"term":"Parasitemia","def":"The percentage of red cells that contain parasites on a blood smear, a direct measure of parasite burden."},{"term":"Merozoite","def":"The form of Babesia or Plasmodium released from a ruptured cell that invades a new red cell."},{"term":"Hypnozoite","def":"A dormant liver stage of P. vivax and P. ovale that can reactivate weeks to months later and cause relapse."},{"term":"Sequestration","def":"Adhesion of red cells containing mature P. falciparum parasites to small vessel endothelium, removing them from the circulating blood."},{"term":"Hemozoin","def":"The insoluble brown pigment that Plasmodium forms from heme after digesting hemoglobin; Babesia does not make it."},{"term":"Granuloma","def":"A focal collection of activated macrophages (epithelioid histiocytes), often with giant cells and lymphocytes, that walls off material the immune system cannot clear."},{"term":"Presinusoidal portal hypertension","def":"Raised portal pressure caused by obstruction in the portal venules before blood reaches the hepatic sinusoids."},{"term":"Antibody-dependent enhancement","def":"Increased viral entry into Fc receptor-bearing cells when the virus is coated by antibodies that bind it but do not neutralize it."},{"term":"Plasma leakage","def":"Loss of plasma from the vascular space through permeable endothelium, producing hemoconcentration, effusions, and hypovolemia."},{"term":"Bubo","def":"A greatly enlarged, tender, inflamed lymph node, classically the necrotic, bacteria-filled node of bubonic plague."},{"term":"Lymphangitis","def":"Inflammation of lymphatic vessels, seen on the skin as tender red streaks running toward the draining lymph nodes."},{"term":"Tingible body macrophage","def":"A germinal center macrophage containing engulfed apoptotic B cell fragments, a marker of a reactive follicle."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-05-C01","obj":"HE-9-E.1","title":"Babesiosis: an intraerythrocytic parasite controlled by the spleen","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Babesiosis is caused by Babesia, an apicomplexan protozoan that lives only inside red cells. In the United States the usual species is Babesia microti, carried by the black-legged (deer) tick Ixodes scapularis, the same tick that transmits Lyme disease and anaplasmosis, so coinfection is possible. Cases cluster in the Northeast and upper Midwest in late spring and summer, when nymphal ticks feed. Because the parasite lives in red cells, it also spreads by transfusion, in any season or region."},{"type":"p","text":"Unlike Plasmodium, Babesia has no liver stage. It enters red cells directly, divides by budding, and leaves by rupturing the cell. Multiplication is not synchronized, so fever is continuous or irregular rather than periodic, and there is no relapse from a dormant liver form. Rupture of parasitized cells causes intravascular hemolysis, and the spleen removes infected and damaged cells, adding extravascular hemolysis. The laboratory picture follows: low hemoglobin, raised lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) and indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin, reticulocytosis, and often hemoglobinuria and mildly raised transaminases. Thrombocytopenia is common and probably reflects splenic sequestration and immune platelet destruction."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the spleen decides severity"},{"type":"p","text":"Splenic macrophages remove infected red cells, and the spleen also hosts the antibody and T cell responses that control the parasite. After splenectomy, parasitemia can rise from the usual 1% to 10% to very high levels, with massive hemolysis. Older age (over about 50 years) and immunosuppression (HIV infection, cancer, organ transplantation, B cell depleting therapy such as rituximab) weaken the same defenses. In these patients the parasite burden drives cytokine release and microvascular injury, which explains acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), DIC, acute kidney injury, and heart failure. In healthy hosts, infection is often asymptomatic or a mild flulike illness, with fever up to about 40 °C (104 °F)."},{"type":"table","title":"Babesia compared with Plasmodium on a thin smear","cols":["Feature","Babesia","Plasmodium"],"rows":[["Ring forms","Small, often several per cell, pleomorphic","Present in all species; multiple rings typical of P. falciparum"],["Tetrad (Maltese cross)","Diagnostic when seen, but uncommon","Never"],["Hemozoin pigment","Absent","Present in mature stages"],["Schizonts and gametocytes","Absent","Present (falciparum schizonts rarely circulate)"],["Extracellular forms","Sometimes seen","Rare"],["Liver stage and relapse","None","Liver stage in all; hypnozoite relapse in P. vivax and P. ovale"]],"caption":"Small rings with no pigment and no schizonts in a patient without travel to a malaria area point to Babesia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-05-C02","obj":"HE-9-E.2","title":"Malaria: synchronized red cell rupture and the fever cycle","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Malaria is caused by Plasmodium species transmitted by the female Anopheles mosquito. P. falciparum, P. vivax, P. ovale, and P. malariae are the classic human species; P. knowlesi, a monkey parasite in Southeast Asia, also infects people. The mosquito injects sporozoites, which travel quickly to the liver, multiply in hepatocytes, and release thousands of merozoites. The liver stage causes no symptoms. Disease begins only when merozoites enter red cells."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The Plasmodium cycle in the human host","steps":["Mosquito injects sporozoites into the skin and blood","Sporozoites invade hepatocytes and form liver schizonts","Liver schizonts release merozoites into the blood","Merozoites invade red cells and form ring trophozoites","Trophozoites digest hemoglobin and mature into schizonts","Schizonts rupture, releasing merozoites, pigment, and toxins","New merozoites invade fresh red cells"],"caption":"Some merozoites become gametocytes, which infect the next mosquito. P. vivax and P. ovale also leave dormant hypnozoites in the liver."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the red cell, each parasite passes through ring, trophozoite, and schizont stages and then ruptures the cell. Once the infection becomes synchronized, a whole cohort of schizonts ruptures together. Parasite products released at rupture, including glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchors and hemozoin, stimulate macrophages to release TNF and IL-1. These pyrogens raise prostaglandin E2 in the hypothalamus and reset the temperature set point. The result is a paroxysm of shaking chills, high fever up to about 40 to 41 °C (104 to 105.8 °F), then drenching sweats as the set point falls. Fever therefore recurs at the length of the red cell cycle: about 48 hours for P. vivax and P. ovale (tertian fever) and about 72 hours for P. malariae (quartan fever). Early infections and P. falciparum infections are often unsynchronized, so fever may be daily or irregular."},{"type":"table","title":"The four classic Plasmodium species","cols":["Species","Red cell cycle","Cells invaded","Key features"],"rows":[["P. vivax","About 48 h","Young cells (reticulocytes); entry uses the Duffy antigen","Enlarged cells with Schuffner dots; hypnozoites cause relapse"],["P. ovale","About 48 h","Young cells","Oval, enlarged cells with fringed edges; hypnozoites cause relapse"],["P. malariae","About 72 h","Older cells","Band-form trophozoites; low parasitemia; can persist for years; immune complex nephrotic syndrome"],["P. falciparum","About 48 h, often irregular","Red cells of all ages","High parasitemia; sequestration; most deaths (C03)"]],"caption":"The age of red cell a species can invade limits its maximum parasitemia, which is why P. falciparum reaches the highest burden."},{"type":"p","text":"Anemia is worse than the parasitemia predicts. Infected cells rupture, but the spleen also removes many uninfected cells whose membranes are altered or coated with antibody and complement, and TNF suppresses erythropoiesis in the marrow. The spleen enlarges because its macrophages and lymphocytes proliferate and its red pulp fills with parasitized cells; an acutely enlarged spleen, especially in P. vivax infection, can rupture. Thrombocytopenia is very common and results from splenic sequestration, immune destruction, and consumption. Lack of the Duffy antigen blocks P. vivax entry, and sickle cell trait and G6PD deficiency reduce severe falciparum disease, which explains why these traits are common in endemic regions."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has fevers that peak every 72 hours and later develops proteinuria and edema. Which species, and why is the parasitemia low?","explain":"P. malariae has a 72-hour cycle and invades only older red cells, which limits parasitemia; chronic antigen exposure forms immune complexes that injure glomeruli.","check":"Compare with the species table and the paragraph on fever timing."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-05-C03","obj":"HE-9-E.3","title":"Plasmodium falciparum: sequestration and severe malaria","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"P. falciparum causes most malaria deaths because it combines two properties. It invades red cells of any age, so parasitemia can exceed 10%, and it makes infected cells sticky. As the parasite matures it exports P. falciparum erythrocyte membrane protein 1 (PfEMP1) to knobs on the red cell surface. PfEMP1 binds endothelial receptors such as CD36, ICAM-1, and endothelial protein C receptor, and also binds uninfected red cells (rosetting). Mature trophozoites and schizonts therefore stick in small venules and capillaries of the brain, kidney, lung, gut, and placenta. Sequestration helps the parasite escape splenic clearance, but it obstructs microvascular flow and activates endothelium."},{"type":"flow","title":"From sequestration to organ failure","steps":["Mature parasites express PfEMP1 on red cell knobs","Infected cells adhere to venular endothelium and form rosettes","Microvascular flow falls and endothelium is activated","Local hypoxia, cytokine release, and vascular leak follow","Brain, kidney, and lung dysfunction develop","Lactic acidosis and shock in the most severe cases"],"caption":"Damage in severe falciparum malaria comes from blocked, inflamed small vessels in the organs where infected cells sequester."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of falciparum malaria explained by mechanism","cols":["Complication","Main mechanism","Findings"],"rows":[["Cerebral malaria","Sequestration in cerebral microvessels, endothelial activation, brain swelling","Impaired consciousness, seizures, coma; retinal changes"],["Severe anemia","Rupture of many cells, splenic removal of uninfected cells, marrow suppression","Hemoglobin below about 5 g/dL in children or 7 g/dL in adults"],["Hypoglycemia","Parasite glucose use, raised demand in fever, impaired hepatic gluconeogenesis","Glucose below about 40 mg/dL (2.2 mmol/L); worse in children and pregnancy"],["Acute kidney injury","Hypovolemia, renal sequestration, and heme pigment causing acute tubular necrosis","Oliguria, rising creatinine"],["Hemoglobinuria","Massive intravascular hemolysis exceeding haptoglobin binding","Dark (blackwater) urine, low haptoglobin, high LDH"],["Acute respiratory distress","Increased pulmonary capillary permeability; acidotic breathing","Hypoxemia, bilateral infiltrates"]],"caption":"Lactic acidosis, shock, jaundice, and bleeding also define severe malaria. Thresholds follow WHO severe malaria criteria and are approximate."},{"type":"p","text":"The thin smear reflects sequestration. Because mature forms are stuck in vessels, peripheral blood usually shows only young ring forms and gametocytes. The rings are small and delicate, often two or more in one red cell, sometimes with two chromatin dots or flattened against the cell edge (accole forms). Infected cells are not enlarged, unlike in P. vivax and P. ovale infection. The gametocytes are crescent or banana shaped, which is unique to P. falciparum. Seeing schizonts in the peripheral blood is unusual and suggests a very heavy burden. The thick smear is more sensitive for detecting parasites, and the thin smear identifies the species and allows parasitemia to be counted."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading a falciparum smear","text":"A traveler returns from West Africa with fever and confusion. The thin smear shows normal-sized red cells, 12% parasitemia, many cells with two or three small rings, and a few crescent-shaped gametocytes. Step 1: normal-sized cells exclude P. vivax and P. ovale, which enlarge cells. Step 2: very high parasitemia means the parasite invades cells of all ages, which only P. falciparum does at this level. Step 3: only rings and gametocytes are visible because trophozoites and schizonts are sequestered in vessels. Step 4: sequestration in cerebral microvessels explains the confusion. Step 5: predict hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, severe hemolysis with hemoglobinuria, and kidney injury, and recognize that the low glucose may itself worsen the mental status."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-05-C04","obj":"HE-9-E.4","title":"Schistosomiasis: egg granulomas in the portal and vesical veins","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Schistosomes are blood flukes (trematodes) that live as male and female pairs inside veins. Freshwater snails release free-swimming cercariae, which penetrate intact skin during wading or bathing; a short-lived itchy rash (cercarial dermatitis) can mark the site. The young worms travel through the lungs to the portal system, mature, and then migrate to their final venous home. Adults coat themselves with host molecules and cause little harm themselves. Disease comes from their eggs."},{"type":"table","title":"The three main human schistosomes","cols":["Species","Adult worms live in","Egg spine and where eggs are found","Main disease","Geography"],"rows":[["S. mansoni","Inferior mesenteric veins","Large lateral spine; stool","Intestinal and hepatosplenic disease","Africa, Middle East, South America, Caribbean"],["S. japonicum","Superior mesenteric veins","Small or absent spine; stool","Hepatosplenic disease, often severe (high egg output)","China, Philippines, Indonesia"],["S. haematobium","Vesical (bladder) venous plexus","Terminal spine; urine","Urinary tract disease","Africa, Middle East"]],"caption":"The location of the adult pair decides which organ the eggs damage."},{"type":"p","text":"Many eggs pass through the gut or bladder wall and leave the body; the rest are swept back in venous blood or remain in the wall. Living eggs secrete soluble antigens through their shells. The host responds with a CD4 T helper 2 (Th2) response: IL-4 drives the Th2 pattern, IL-5 recruits eosinophils, and IL-13 activates macrophages and fibroblasts. Each trapped egg becomes the center of a granuloma of eosinophils, macrophages, and lymphocytes that later heals by fibrosis. Peripheral eosinophilia reflects the same IL-5 drive. The granuloma protects tissue from egg toxins, but the cumulative scarring causes chronic disease."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hepatosplenic schistosomiasis (S. mansoni and S. japonicum)","steps":["Eggs are carried in portal blood toward the liver","Eggs lodge in small presinusoidal portal venules","Th2 granulomas with eosinophils surround each egg","IL-13 drives periportal collagen (pipestem fibrosis)","Portal venous resistance rises before the sinusoids","Splenomegaly, varices, and variceal bleeding follow"],"caption":"Hepatocytes and sinusoids are spared, so liver synthetic function is usually preserved."},{"type":"p","text":"In the liver, fibrosis wraps the portal tracts in thick white collars, the pipestem (Symmers) fibrosis seen on cut section and ultrasound. Because the block is presinusoidal, the hepatocytes remain perfused and the lobular architecture is intact; this is not cirrhosis. Albumin and clotting factors stay near normal, and ascites and encephalopathy are uncommon until late, yet portal hypertension produces massive splenomegaly, hypersplenism with cytopenias, and bleeding esophageal varices. Once portosystemic collaterals open, eggs can bypass the liver and lodge in pulmonary arterioles, causing pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure."},{"type":"p","text":"In S. haematobium infection, eggs penetrate the bladder wall to reach the urine. Granulomas in the mucosa cause painless terminal hematuria, then fibrosis, calcification of the bladder wall, ureteral obstruction, and hydronephrosis. Chronic inflammation drives squamous metaplasia of the urothelium, which can progress to squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder, so S. haematobium is a recognized carcinogen. Weeks after a heavy first exposure, travelers can develop acute schistosomiasis (Katayama fever), a systemic hypersensitivity reaction to migrating worms and early egg antigens with fever, urticaria, cough, and marked eosinophilia."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: portal hypertension without liver failure","text":"A 30-year-old man from rural Egypt vomits blood. He has a very large spleen, eosinophilia, platelets of 80,000/microL, and normal albumin, bilirubin, and INR. Step 1: variceal bleeding with a large spleen means portal hypertension. Step 2: normal albumin and INR mean hepatocytes still work, which argues against cirrhosis, a sinusoidal process that destroys hepatocytes. Step 3: a presinusoidal block raises portal pressure while sparing the sinusoids. Step 4: eosinophilia and the region point to S. mansoni eggs trapped in portal venules with pipestem fibrosis. Step 5: low platelets reflect splenic sequestration (hypersplenism), not marrow failure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-05-C05","obj":"HE-9-E.5","title":"Dengue, chikungunya, and Zika: three Aedes-borne viruses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus mosquitoes bite during the day and breed in small containers of standing water near homes, so all three viruses spread in tropical and subtropical cities and can circulate together. All are enveloped, positive-sense, single-stranded RNA viruses. Dengue and Zika are flaviviruses; chikungunya is an alphavirus in the togavirus family. Their early illnesses overlap (fever, rash, aches), so the board distinction rests on what each virus targets."},{"type":"table","title":"Aedes-borne viruses compared","cols":["Feature","Dengue","Chikungunya","Zika"],"rows":[["Family","Flavivirus, 4 serotypes","Alphavirus (togavirus)","Flavivirus"],["Typical illness","High fever, pain behind the eyes, severe muscle and bone pain, rash","Abrupt high fever, severe symmetric polyarthralgia of small joints","Often asymptomatic or mild: itchy rash, nonpurulent conjunctivitis, arthralgia, low fever"],["Blood findings","Leukopenia, marked thrombocytopenia, rising hematocrit in severe disease","Lymphopenia; platelets usually only mildly low","Usually unremarkable"],["Signature complication","Severe dengue with plasma leakage, shock, and bleeding","Joint pain lasting months to years","Congenital Zika syndrome; Guillain-Barre syndrome"],["Other transmission","Transfusion, rarely vertical","Vertical near delivery","Sexual, vertical, transfusion"]],"caption":"Severe joint pain points to chikungunya, thrombocytopenia with leak to dengue, and conjunctivitis or a pregnancy risk to Zika."},{"type":"p","text":"A first dengue infection gives lasting immunity to that serotype but only brief cross-protection against the other three. Years later, antibodies from the first infection bind a second serotype without neutralizing it. The Fc portions of these antibodies attach to Fc gamma receptors on monocytes and macrophages, carrying the virus into the cells it replicates in best. This antibody-dependent enhancement raises viral load and the release of TNF and other cytokines; the viral NS1 protein also damages the endothelial glycocalyx. Infants born to immune mothers can have severe disease in a first infection because maternal antibody plays the same role."},{"type":"flow","title":"Severe dengue by antibody-dependent enhancement","steps":["First infection leaves antibodies to one serotype","Non-neutralizing antibodies bind a second serotype","Antibody-coated virus enters monocytes through Fc receptors","Viral load and cytokine release increase","Endothelial permeability rises around defervescence","Plasma leaks, hematocrit rises, and shock follows"],"caption":"The dangerous critical phase usually begins as the fever breaks, about days 3 to 7."},{"type":"p","text":"Plasma leakage explains the findings of severe dengue: a hematocrit that rises as platelets fall, pleural effusions and ascites, low albumin, and narrowing pulse pressure leading to hypovolemic shock. Thrombocytopenia comes from early marrow suppression, immune-mediated platelet destruction, and consumption, and with coagulopathy it causes petechiae, mucosal bleeding, and a positive tourniquet test. Chikungunya targets synovial fibroblasts, muscle, and macrophages in joints, and persistent viral material and inflammation explain arthralgia that can last months to years. Zika infects neural progenitor cells in the fetal brain and kills them, so infection in pregnancy (highest risk in the first trimester) causes microcephaly, subcortical calcifications, eye abnormalities, and joint contractures; in adults it can trigger Guillain-Barre syndrome."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In a patient with dengue on day 5, the fever has resolved, but the hematocrit has risen from 40% to 48% and the platelet count has fallen. Is the patient improving?","explain":"No. A rising hematocrit with falling platelets at defervescence signals plasma leakage, the start of the critical phase that can progress to shock.","check":"Compare with the antibody-dependent enhancement flow and the paragraph on plasma leakage."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-05-C06","obj":"HE-9-E.6","title":"Ebola and Marburg: filoviral hemorrhagic fever","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ebola and Marburg viruses are filoviruses: long, filamentous, enveloped viruses with a nonsegmented, negative-sense, single-stranded RNA genome. Fruit bats are the likely natural reservoir; the Egyptian fruit bat (Rousettus aegyptiacus) is an established reservoir of Marburg virus, while the Ebola reservoir is suspected but less certain. Outbreaks start when a person handles an infected bat or primate, or bushmeat, and then spread person to person through direct contact with blood, vomit, stool, and other body fluids. Health care workers and family caregivers are at high risk, and traditional burial practices spread infection because viral loads in a body remain high after death. People are not considered infectious before symptoms begin, and virus can persist in semen and other immune-privileged sites for months after recovery. The incubation period is about 2 to 21 days."},{"type":"p","text":"The virus first infects monocytes, macrophages, and dendritic cells. Viral proteins VP35 and VP24 block type I interferon production and signaling, so the innate response fails to contain replication, and infected dendritic cells cannot activate T cells well. Infected macrophages release large amounts of TNF, IL-1, IL-6, and other cytokines and express tissue factor on their surface. Lymphocytes, though rarely infected, die in large numbers by apoptosis, producing lymphopenia and weak adaptive immunity. The virus then spreads to hepatocytes, adrenal cortical cells, and endothelium."},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical course and its mechanisms","cols":["Phase","Findings","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Early (first days)","Abrupt fever, profound weakness, headache, myalgia","Cytokine release from infected macrophages"],["Gastrointestinal","Vomiting, large-volume diarrhea, abdominal pain","Infection of gut and systemic inflammation; causes hypovolemia and hypokalemia"],["Hemorrhagic","Petechiae, bleeding from gums, venipuncture sites, and gut","DIC from tissue factor, hepatic failure of clotting factor synthesis, thrombocytopenia"],["Terminal","Shock, confusion, kidney and liver failure","Vascular leak, adrenal injury, hypoperfusion"]],"caption":"Laboratory clues: early leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, AST higher than ALT, prolonged PT and aPTT, and raised D-dimer. Visible bleeding occurs in only a minority."},{"type":"p","text":"Case fatality varies by virus species and setting, averaging about 50% for Ebola and ranging from about 25% to 90% in outbreaks. Marburg disease is clinically indistinguishable from Ebola, so the history (bat caves, mines, outbreak region, contact with a sick person) and laboratory confirmation by RT-PCR separate them. Unlike dengue, filovirus disease spreads person to person and causes heavier gastrointestinal loss and liver injury."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-05-C07","obj":"HE-9-E.7","title":"Plague and cat scratch disease: bacterial lymphadenitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A lymph node filters lymph from a defined drainage area, so infection entering through the skin first reaches the regional node. Yersinia pestis and Bartonella henselae both enter through a skin breach and produce lymphadenitis in that node, but plague kills quickly by spreading beyond it, while cat scratch disease usually stays local and resolves."},{"type":"h","text":"Yersinia pestis"},{"type":"p","text":"Y. pestis is a gram-negative coccobacillus of the Enterobacterales. With Wright, Giemsa, or Wayson stain the ends take up more dye than the center, giving the bipolar safety pin appearance. Rodents (rats; in the United States, prairie dogs and ground squirrels, mostly in the Southwest) are the reservoir, and fleas are the vector. The bacteria block the flea's foregut, so the hungry flea regurgitates bacteria into each bite. Hunters handling infected animals and owners of infected cats are also exposed."},{"type":"p","text":"Y. pestis survives because its virulence factors defeat phagocytes. The F1 capsule antigen resists phagocytosis, a type III secretion system injects Yop proteins that paralyze phagocytes and suppress cytokine signaling, and the plasminogen activator Pla breaks down fibrin barriers so bacteria spread from the bite. The bacteria multiply in the draining node, usually inguinal or femoral because the legs are bitten most often. After about 2 to 8 days, fever and an exquisitely tender, swollen node appear; this bubo shows hemorrhagic necrosis, neutrophils, and sheets of bacteria, and it may suppurate. Endotoxin then drives the systemic forms."},{"type":"flow","title":"Spread of plague","steps":["Infected flea bite inoculates Y. pestis","Bacteria reach the regional node through lymphatics","Multiplication forms a painful, necrotic bubo","Bloodstream spread causes septicemic plague with DIC","Lung seeding causes secondary pneumonic plague","Respiratory droplets cause primary pneumonic plague in contacts"],"caption":"Septicemic plague can occur without a bubo; pneumonic plague is the form that spreads person to person."},{"type":"p","text":"In septicemic plague, endotoxin causes shock and DIC, and thrombosis of small vessels causes acral gangrene of fingers, toes, and nose. Pneumonic plague causes a rapidly progressive hemorrhagic pneumonia with bloody sputum and is the most lethal form; for this reason Y. pestis is a category A bioterrorism agent."},{"type":"h","text":"Bartonella henselae"},{"type":"p","text":"B. henselae is a small, fastidious gram-negative bacillus best seen with the Warthin-Starry silver stain. Cats, especially kittens, carry it in their blood without illness, and the cat flea spreads it among cats. About 3 to 10 days after a scratch or bite, a papule or pustule forms at the site, and 1 to 3 weeks later the regional node (axillary, epitrochlear, or cervical) becomes tender and enlarged. Early the node shows follicular hyperplasia; later it shows stellate necrotizing granulomas, star-shaped zones of necrosis containing neutrophils and surrounded by palisading epithelioid histiocytes. Fever is usually mild, and the node shrinks over weeks to months. In people with advanced HIV infection the same organism instead stimulates blood vessel proliferation, causing bacillary angiomatosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Plague and cat scratch disease compared","cols":["Feature","Bubonic plague","Cat scratch disease"],"rows":[["Organism and stain","Y. pestis; gram-negative coccobacillus, bipolar staining","B. henselae; gram-negative bacillus, Warthin-Starry silver stain"],["Exposure","Rodent flea bite; handling infected animals","Cat or kitten scratch or bite"],["Node","Extremely painful bubo, often inguinal","Tender node, often axillary or cervical"],["Node pathology","Hemorrhagic necrosis packed with bacteria","Stellate necrotizing granulomas with central neutrophils"],["Course","Rapid spread to blood and lungs; high mortality untreated","Usually self-limited over weeks to months"]],"caption":"The speed of illness and the node histology separate the two."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-HEME-09-05-C08","obj":"HE-9-E.8","title":"Acute lymphangitis and reactive lymph node patterns","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute lymphangitis is bacterial infection spreading along the lymphatic vessels that drain a skin wound. The usual cause is Streptococcus pyogenes (group A streptococcus), a gram-positive coccus in chains that is beta-hemolytic on blood agar. Its spreading factors, including hyaluronidase, streptokinase, and DNases, break down connective tissue matrix, fibrin, and DNA in pus, so the organism moves quickly from the wound into lymphatics. Inflamed lymphatics show through the skin as tender red streaks running proximally from the wound toward the regional nodes, for example from a hand wound toward the axilla, with tender lymphadenitis, fever, and chills. Staphylococcus aureus is a less common cause. Without control, the infection can reach the blood. A different pattern, nodular lymphangitis, with nodules along the lymphatic path, suggests organisms such as Sporothrix schenckii after a rose thorn injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Most enlarged lymph nodes are reactive, not malignant. The compartment that expands shows which arm of immunity is responding: follicles for B cell and antibody responses, the paracortex for T cell responses, and the sinuses for macrophages handling material carried in lymph."},{"type":"table","title":"Reactive lymphadenitis patterns","cols":["Pattern","Compartment","Typical causes","Key histology"],"rows":[["Follicular hyperplasia","B cell follicles in the cortex","Rheumatoid arthritis, early HIV infection, toxoplasmosis, syphilis","Many large germinal centers of varied size and shape"],["Paracortical hyperplasia","T cell zone between follicles","Viral infections such as infectious mononucleosis, some drugs (phenytoin), vaccination","Expanded paracortex with immunoblasts that can mimic lymphoma"],["Sinus histiocytosis","Medullary and subcapsular sinuses","Nodes draining a cancer or chronic inflammation","Sinuses distended by macrophages"],["Granulomatous lymphadenitis","Variable","Tuberculosis, fungi, cat scratch disease","Granulomas, caseating or necrotizing depending on cause"]],"caption":"Mixed patterns are common; the dominant pattern guides the differential diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The hardest distinction is follicular hyperplasia versus follicular lymphoma. In a normal germinal center, B cells mutate their antibody genes and switch off BCL2, an antiapoptotic protein, so cells that fail selection die and are eaten by tingible body macrophages. Reactive follicles therefore have BCL2-negative germinal centers, tingible body macrophages, and dark and light zones (polarity), and they vary in size with intact mantle zones. In follicular lymphoma, the t(14;18) translocation places BCL2 under the immunoglobulin heavy chain promoter. Neoplastic germinal center cells keep BCL2 and survive, so crowded, uniform, back-to-back follicles without tingible body macrophages efface the node, and the cells express a single light chain (kappa or lambda)."},{"type":"table","title":"Reactive node or lymphoma","cols":["Feature","Reactive","Lymphoma"],"rows":[["Clinical","Tender, soft, mobile, often small; follows infection","Painless, rubbery or firm, often fixed; persistent or growing"],["Architecture","Preserved; follicles varied in size","Effaced; follicles uniform and crowded"],["Germinal center BCL2","Negative","Positive in follicular lymphoma"],["Tingible body macrophages","Present","Absent or rare"],["Light chains","Polyclonal (kappa and lambda)","Monoclonal (one light chain)"]],"caption":"Tenderness signals rapid capsular stretch from inflammation; painless growth over weeks suggests neoplasia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A node biopsy shows closely packed, uniform follicles without tingible body macrophages. What will BCL2 staining of the germinal centers show, and why?","explain":"It will be positive, because t(14;18) keeps BCL2 switched on in follicular lymphoma cells, which then escape the apoptosis that clears normal germinal centers.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on follicular hyperplasia versus follicular lymphoma."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The organisms in this chapter follow a common rule: where the organism lives determines the organ that fails, and much of the damage is done by the host. Babesia and Plasmodium live in red cells, so they cause hemolysis, and the spleen, which normally clears them, both protects the host and enlarges. Schistosome eggs are trapped by vein anatomy, and Th2 granulomas with IL-13-driven fibrosis cause the portal hypertension and bladder disease. In dengue, antibodies made for protection enhance a second infection, and in filovirus disease, macrophages that should contain the virus become sources of cytokines and tissue factor that cause leak and DIC."},{"type":"p","text":"The lymphatic infections connect microbiology to lymph node pathology. Drainage anatomy places the bubo, the cat scratch node, and the streaks of lymphangitis, while the pattern of node enlargement shows which immune compartment responded. Histology has limits: reactive paracortical hyperplasia can mimic lymphoma and necrotizing granulomas are shared by several organisms, so exposure history, stains, clonality studies, and molecular tests are combined before a final diagnosis."}],"summary":["Babesia infects red cells without a liver stage; small rings, rare tetrads, and no pigment distinguish it from Plasmodium, and asplenia, older age, and immunosuppression allow high parasitemia and severe hemolysis.","Malarial paroxysms occur when synchronized schizonts rupture and release products that trigger TNF and IL-1; the cycle is about 48 hours for P. vivax and P. ovale and 72 hours for P. malariae.","Malarial anemia exceeds the parasitemia because the spleen also removes uninfected cells and inflammation suppresses the marrow; splenomegaly and thrombocytopenia are common.","P. falciparum invades red cells of all ages and sequesters through PfEMP1, causing cerebral malaria, severe anemia, hypoglycemia, kidney injury, hemoglobinuria, and respiratory distress; the smear shows multiple rings and banana-shaped gametocytes.","Schistosome eggs provoke Th2 granulomas with eosinophils; S. mansoni causes presinusoidal portal hypertension with pipestem fibrosis and preserved liver function, and S. haematobium causes hematuria, bladder fibrosis, and squamous cell carcinoma.","Severe dengue follows reinfection with a different serotype through antibody-dependent enhancement, with plasma leakage shown by rising hematocrit and falling platelets.","Chikungunya causes severe, persistent polyarthralgia; Zika destroys fetal neural progenitor cells, causing congenital microcephaly.","Ebola and Marburg are filoviruses from fruit bats spread by body fluids; interferon blockade, cytokine release, and macrophage tissue factor lead to vascular leak, DIC, and multiorgan failure.","Y. pestis is a bipolar-staining coccobacillus spread by rodent fleas that forms a painful bubo and spreads to blood and lungs; B. henselae from cats causes regional lymphadenopathy with stellate necrotizing granulomas.","Streptococcal lymphangitis produces red streaks toward regional nodes; reactive follicles are BCL2 negative with tingible body macrophages, while follicular lymphoma follicles are BCL2 positive and monoclonal."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology; Arthropod-Borne and Rodent-Borne Viral Diseases; sections on Yersinia and Bartonella"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases; Diseases of White Blood Cells, Lymph Nodes, Spleen, and Thymus"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to Microbes, immunity to parasites and viruses"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Immune System and Lymphoid Organs"}]}});
